<script id="bank" type="application/json">{"topics":["Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","QoS","Cloud vs On-Premises","Cisco SD-WAN","Cisco SD-Access","Campus Design & High Availability","FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","Wireless","Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","LISP & VXLAN","Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","OSPF","EIGRP","BGP","Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","Network Assurance & Monitoring","Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","Automation & Programmability","Multicast (PIM, RP)"],"questions":[{"id":"q0001","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the difference between a RIB and a FIB?","options":{"A":"The FIB is populated based on RIB content.","B":"The RIB maintains a mirror image of the FIB.","C":"The RIB is used to make IP source prefix-based switching decisions.","D":"The FIB is where all IP routing information is stored."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The difference between a RIB and a FIB lies in their functions and the type of information they handle. The Routing Information Base (RIB) is essentially the routing table where all routing information, including multiple possible routes and routing metrics, is stored. On the other hand, the Forwarding Information Base (FIB) is a more streamlined structure used by the router to make actual packet forwarding decisions. The FIB is populated based on the information contained in the RIB, selecting the best routes decided by the routing algorithms. Thus, the FIB is derived from the RIB, and this forms the core distinction between the two.","images":[],"sourcePage":1,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0002","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which QoS component alters a packet to change the way that traffic is treated in the network?","options":{"A":"policing","B":"classification","C":"marking","D":"shaping"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Marking is the QoS component that alters bits in the packet header to indicate its priority level or other attributes. This alteration allows network devices to treat the packet according to its assigned priority, thereby changing the way the traffic is handled within the network. Policing, classification, and shaping, while important QoS components, do not directly alter the packet itself to achieve this. Policing controls the rate of traffic, classification identifies traffic types, and shaping smooths traffic flows, but none of these directly modify the packet to influence its treatment like marking does.","images":[],"sourcePage":2,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0003","topic":"QoS","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the descriptions from the left onto the correct QoS components on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0003_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0003_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":3,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0004","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about Cisco Express Forwarding is true?","options":{"A":"The CPU of a router becomes directly involved with packet-switching decisions.","B":"It uses a fast cache that is maintained in a router data plane.","C":"It maintains two tables in the data plane: the FIB and adjacency table.","D":"It makes forwarding decisions by a process that is scheduled through the IOS scheduler."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cisco Express Forwarding (CEF) maintains two tables in the data plane: the Forwarding Information Base (FIB) and the adjacency table. The FIB is used to make IP destination prefix-based switching decisions, while the adjacency table maintains the layer 2 next-hop information necessary for packet forwarding. This design allows CEF to improve the efficiency and speed of packet forwarding by pre-computing the necessary information and storing it in these tables.","images":[],"sourcePage":4,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0005","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"What is a benefit of deploying an on-premises infrastructure versus a cloud infrastructure deployment?","options":{"A":"ability to quickly increase compute power without the need to install additional hardware","B":"less power and cooling resources needed to run infrastructure on-premises","C":"faster deployment times because additional infrastructure does not need to be purchased","D":"lower latency between systems that are physically located near each other"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A benefit of deploying an on-premises infrastructure versus a cloud infrastructure deployment is lower latency between systems that are physically located near each other. This is because data does not need to travel over the internet, reducing delays and improving communication speed between systems.","images":[],"sourcePage":5,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0006","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the appropriate infrastructure deployment types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0006_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0006_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":6,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0007","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"How does QoS traffic shaping alleviate network congestion?","options":{"A":"It drops packets when traffic exceeds a certain bitrate.","B":"It buffers and queues packets above the committed rate.","C":"It fragments large packets and queues them for delivery.","D":"It drops packets randomly from lower priority queues."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"QoS traffic shaping alleviates network congestion by buffering and queuing packets that exceed the committed rate. This helps smooth out bursts in traffic, allowing for more consistent data flow and preventing packet loss. Traffic shaping ensures that packets are held in a queue and sent out in a controlled manner when the network has the capacity to handle them. This is different from traffic policing, which would drop packets that exceed a certain bitrate, and other mechanisms such as packet fragmentation or random packet dropping from lower priority queues.","images":[],"sourcePage":8,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0008","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is describing QoS to a client.\nWhich two facts apply to traffic policing? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Policing should be performed as close to the source as possible.","B":"Policing adapts to network congestion by queuing excess traffic.","C":"Policing should be performed as close to the destination as possible.","D":"Policing drops traffic that exceeds the defined rate.","E":"Policing typically delays the traffic, rather than drops it."},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Traffic policing involves monitoring the data rate of a traffic flow and taking action when the rate exceeds the defined threshold. This action often includes dropping packets, thus policing drops traffic that exceeds the defined rate. Additionally, it is most effective and efficient to perform policing as close to the traffic source as possible in order to prevent excessive traffic from entering the network core and to manage bandwidth more effectively.","images":[],"sourcePage":9,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0009","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which component handles the orchestration plane of the Cisco SD-WAN?","options":{"A":"vBond","B":"vSmart","C":"vManage","D":"WAN Edge"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The component that handles the orchestration plane of the Cisco SD-WAN is vBond. vBond is responsible for authenticating the vSmart controllers and the SD-WAN routers, orchestrating connectivity between them. It is designed to be the first point of contact for SD-WAN devices and facilitates mutual discovery and initial authentication. Therefore, vBond is the correct answer.","images":[],"sourcePage":10,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0010","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What are two device roles in Cisco SD-Access fabric? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"edge node","B":"vBond controller","C":"access switch","D":"core switch","E":"border node"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"In Cisco SD-Access fabric, the Edge Node is a device that connects endpoints to the fabric network and serves as the entry point for user traffic. The Border Node is responsible for connecting the SD-Access fabric to external networks and handling traffic entering and leaving the fabric. These roles are crucial for the proper functioning and management of the SD-Access environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":11,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0011","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the role of the vSmart controller in a Cisco SD-WAN environment?","options":{"A":"It performs authentication and authorization.","B":"It manages the control plane.","C":"It is the centralized network management system.","D":"It manages the data plane."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN environment, the vSmart controller is responsible for managing the control plane. It handles tasks such as path calculation, route advertisement, and policy dissemination between the WAN Edge routers. By managing the control plane, the vSmart controller offloads data plane tasks to optimize performance.","images":[],"sourcePage":12,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0012","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"When a wired client connects to an edge switch in a Cisco SD-Access fabric, which component decides whether the client has access to the network?","options":{"A":"edge node","B":"Identity Services Engine","C":"RADIUS server","D":"control-plane node"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The component that decides whether a wired client has access to the network in a Cisco SD-Access fabric is the Identity Services Engine (ISE). ISE is responsible for providing authentication, authorization, and accounting (AAA) services. When a client connects to an edge switch, ISE identifies the user, enforces network policies, and assigns the appropriate network access based on the client's credentials. RADIUS server, while related, does not operate independently in this specific architecture; it operates through ISE.","images":[],"sourcePage":13,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0013","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"Which benefit is offered by a cloud infrastructure deployment but is lacking in an on-premises deployment?","options":{"A":"virtualization","B":"supported systems","C":"storage capacity","D":"efficient scalability"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A key benefit offered by a cloud infrastructure deployment that is often lacking in an on-premises deployment is efficient scalability. Cloud infrastructure allows for quick and easy scaling of resources in response to fluctuating demand, thanks to elastic resources that can be automatically adjusted. This contrasts with on-premises deployments, where scaling typically requires the purchase and installation of additional hardware, which is both time-consuming and costly.","images":[],"sourcePage":14,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0014","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which action is the vSmart controller responsible for in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"onboard WAN Edge nodes into the Cisco SD-WAN fabric","B":"gather telemetry data from WAN Edge routers","C":"distribute policies that govern data forwarding performed within the Cisco SD-WAN fabric","D":"handle, maintain, and gather configuration and status for nodes within the Cisco SD-WAN fabric"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN deployment, the vSmart controller is responsible for distributing policies that govern data forwarding performed within the Cisco SD-WAN fabric. The vSmart controller manages the control plane, orchestrates connectivity, and ensures the implementation of those policies across the network, which directly impacts route advertisement and path calculation.","images":[],"sourcePage":15,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0015","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Where is radio resource management performed in a Cisco SD-Access wireless solution?","options":{"A":"DNA Center","B":"control plane node","C":"wireless controller","D":"Cisco CMX"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless solution, radio resource management (RRM) is typically performed by the wireless controller. The wireless controller manages the radio resources, ensuring optimal radio conditions by dynamically assigning channels, adjusting power levels, and mitigating interference. This helps maintain the overall health and performance of the wireless network. DNA Center, the control plane node, and Cisco CMX do not directly perform radio resource management; their functions are more related to network automation, traffic control, policy enforcement, and location analytics, respectively.","images":[],"sourcePage":16,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0016","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the infrastructure types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0016_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0016_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":17,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0017","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"How does the RIB differ from the FIB?","options":{"A":"The FIB maintains network topologies and routing tables. The RIB is a list of routes to particular network destinations.","B":"The FIB includes many routes to a single destination. The RIB is the best route to a single destination.","C":"The RIB is used to create network topologies and routing tables. The FIB is a list of routes to particular network destinations.","D":"The RIB includes many routes to the same destination prefix. The FIB contains only the best route."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The RIB (Routing Information Base) contains a collection of routing information, including multiple routes to the same destination prefix. It serves as a comprehensive database of network routes, which come from different sources like routing protocols and static routes. On the other hand, the FIB (Forwarding Information Base) holds only the best route for each destination prefix, as determined by the routing protocol's decision process. This streamlined set of routes is utilized for efficient packet forwarding, ensuring that each packet reaches its destination via the most optimal path.","images":[],"sourcePage":18,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0018","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which technology is used to provide Layer 2 and Layer 3 logical networks in the Cisco SD-Access architecture?","options":{"A":"underlay network","B":"VPN routing/forwarding","C":"easy virtual network","D":"overlay network"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In the Cisco SD-Access architecture, the Overlay Network is used to provide Layer 2 and Layer 3 logical networks. The SD-Access fabric overlay is responsible for the creation of virtual networks and the segmentation of network traffic, which occurs logically without depending on the physical layout of the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":19,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0019","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the difference between CEF and process switching?","options":{"A":"CEF processes packets that are too complex for process switching to manage.","B":"Process switching is faster than CEF.","C":"CEF uses the FIB and the adjacency table to make forwarding decisions, whereas process switching punts each packet.","D":"CEF is more CPU-intensive than process switching."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"CEF (Cisco Express Forwarding) uses the Forwarding Information Base (FIB) and the adjacency table to make forwarding decisions efficiently and with minimal CPU involvement. In contrast, process switching involves punting each packet to the CPU for processing, which is slower and more CPU-intensive as it requires the CPU to be involved in every forwarding decision. Thus, CEF is faster and more efficient than process switching.","images":[],"sourcePage":20,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0020","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"What are two considerations when using SSO as a network redundancy feature? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"requires synchronization between supervisors in order to guarantee continuous connectivity","B":"the multicast state is preserved during switchover","C":"must be combined with NSF to support uninterrupted Layer 3 operations","D":"both supervisors must be configured separately","E":"must be combined with NSF to support uninterrupted Layer 2 operations"},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"When using Stateful Switchover (SSO) as a network redundancy feature, two important considerations need to be taken into account. First, SSO requires synchronization between supervisors to guarantee continuous connectivity. This synchronization ensures that the state information is dynamically maintained between redundant supervisors, allowing for a seamless transition in the event of a failure. Second, to support uninterrupted Layer 3 operations, SSO must be combined with Non-Stop Forwarding (NSF). NSF allows data packet forwarding to continue along known routes while the routing protocol information is being restored following a switchover, minimizing service outages and maintaining network stability.","images":[],"sourcePage":21,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0021","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the correct infrastructure deployment type on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0021_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0021_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":22,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0022","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access fabric, which control plane protocol is used for mapping and resolving endpoints?","options":{"A":"DHCP","B":"VXLAN","C":"SXP","D":"LISP"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access fabric, the control plane protocol used for mapping and resolving endpoints is LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol). LISP separates the identity of an endpoint from its location, allowing for better scalability and flexibility in managing network addresses and improving overall network efficiency.","images":[],"sourcePage":24,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0023","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"What are two differences between the RIB and the FIB? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"FIB is a database of routing prefixes, and the RIB is the information used to choose the egress interface for each packet.","B":"The FIB is derived from the data plane, and the RIB is derived from the FIB.","C":"The RIB is a database of routing prefixes, and the FIB is the information used to choose the egress interface for each packet.","D":"The RIB is derived from the control plane, and the FIB is derived from the RIB.","E":"The FIB is derived from the control plane, and the RIB is derived from the FIB."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"The primary purpose of the Routing Information Base (RIB) is to store routing prefixes received from various routing protocols, and it is operated by the control plane. The Forwarding Information Base (FIB), on the other hand, is derived from the RIB and is used by the data plane to determine the egress interface for each packet based on the routing information. Therefore, the RIB contains a database of routing prefixes, while the FIB contains the necessary information to forward packets, including egress interfaces. Based on these definitions, the correct differences are that the RIB is a database of routing prefixes and derived from the control plane, and the FIB is derived from the RIB.","images":[],"sourcePage":25,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0024","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which two network problems indicate a need to implement QoS in a campus network? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"port flapping","B":"excess jitter","C":"misrouted network packets","D":"duplicate IP addresses","E":"bandwidth-related packet loss"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"Excess jitter and bandwidth-related packet loss are indicators that a network needs Quality of Service (QoS) implementation. QoS helps manage network resources by prioritizing certain types of data traffic, reducing latency, jitter, and packet loss, especially in scenarios requiring consistent data flow such as voice and video communications.","images":[],"sourcePage":26,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0025","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless architecture, which device manages endpoint ID to edge node bindings?","options":{"A":"fabric control plane node","B":"fabric wireless controller","C":"fabric border node","D":"fabric edge node"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless architecture, the fabric control plane node is responsible for managing endpoint ID to edge node bindings. The Control Plane Node runs the LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) Endpoint ID database, which tracks these bindings and provides overlay reachability information. This node supports multiple types of endpoint ID lookup keys and handles registrations from edge nodes and fabric WLCs, ensuring accurate tracking and resolution of endpoint locations.","images":[],"sourcePage":27,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0026","topic":"QoS","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the QoS mechanisms from the left onto their descriptions on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0026_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0026_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":28,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0027","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which control plane protocol is used between Cisco SD-WAN routers and vSmart controllers?","options":{"A":"TCP","B":"OMP","C":"UDP","D":"BGP"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The control plane protocol used between Cisco SD-WAN routers and vSmart controllers is the Overlay Management Protocol (OMP). OMP is essential in establishing and maintaining the Viptela control plane for Cisco's SD-WAN solutions.","images":[],"sourcePage":29,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0028","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"In a three-tier hierarchical campus network design, which action is a design best-practice for the core layer?","options":{"A":"provide QoS prioritization services such as marking, queueing, and classification for critical network traffic","B":"provide redundant Layer 3 point-to-point links between the core devices for more predictable and faster convergence","C":"provide advanced network security features such as 802.1X, DHCP snooping, VACLs, and port security","D":"provide redundant aggregation for access layer devices and first-hop redundancy protocols such as VRRP"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a three-tier hierarchical campus network design, the core layer's primary responsibility is to ensure high-speed and reliable data transport. This is best achieved by providing redundant Layer 3 point-to-point links between core devices. Such redundancy allows faster and more predictable convergence, which enhances the overall network performance. Other tasks such as QoS prioritization, advanced network security features, and redundant aggregation for access layer devices are typically handled at other layers of the network, not the core layer.","images":[],"sourcePage":30,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0029","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is a VPN in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"common exchange point between two different services","B":"attribute to identify a set of services offered in specific places in the SD-WAN fabric","C":"virtualized environment that provides traffic isolation and segmentation in the SD-WAN fabric","D":"virtual channel used to carry control plane information"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A VPN in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment is a virtualized environment that provides traffic isolation and segmentation in the SD-WAN fabric. This means that it creates separate virtual networks within the same physical infrastructure, ensuring that traffic from different VPNs remains isolated from each other unless specifically allowed. This isolation can help enhance security and manage different traffic types more effectively.","images":[],"sourcePage":31,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0030","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which function does a fabric edge node perform in an SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"Connects endpoints to the fabric and forwards their traffic.","B":"Encapsulates end-user data traffic into LISP.","C":"Connects the SD-Access fabric to another fabric or external Layer 3 networks.","D":"Provides reachability between border nodes in the fabric underlay."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In an SD-Access deployment, a fabric edge node is responsible for connecting endpoints to the fabric and forwarding their traffic. This functionality involves admitting, encapsulating, and decapsulating data traffic for proper delivery within the network. The options provided include connecting endpoints (A), encapsulating end-user data into LISP (B), connecting the SD-Access fabric to external networks (C), and providing reachability between border nodes (D). Among these, the primary function of the fabric edge node is most accurately described by option A, which states that it connects endpoints to the fabric and forwards their traffic.","images":[],"sourcePage":32,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0031","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is the role of a fusion router in an SD-Access solution?","options":{"A":"acts as a DNS server","B":"provides additional forwarding capacity to the fabric","C":"performs route leaking between user-defined virtual networks and shared services","D":"provides connectivity to external networks"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A fusion router performs route leaking between user-defined virtual networks and shared services. This means it enables communication and data exchange between different segments of a network that would otherwise be isolated. This capability is essential in an SD-Access solution to ensure that various specialized services and virtual networks can interconnect and work seamlessly together.","images":[],"sourcePage":33,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0032","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which action is the vSmart controller responsible for in an SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"onboard vEdge nodes into the SD-WAN fabric","B":"gather telemetry data from vEdge routers","C":"distribute security information for tunnel establishment between vEdge routers","D":"manage, maintain, and gather configuration and status for nodes within the SD-WAN fabric"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The vSmart controller in an SD-WAN deployment is responsible for distributing security information to facilitate the establishment of IPsec tunnels between vEdge routers. This role involves using the Overlay Management Protocol (OMP) to disseminate routing information, security keys, and policy configurations to the vEdge routers, which then use this information to secure data plane communications.","images":[],"sourcePage":34,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0033","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is one fact about Cisco SD-Access wireless network deployments?","options":{"A":"The access point is part of the fabric overlay.","B":"The wireless client is part of the fabric overlay.","C":"The access point is part of the fabric underlay.","D":"The WLC is part of the fabric underlay."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In Cisco SD-Access wireless network deployments, the access points (APs) are part of the fabric overlay. This is because the fabric overlay is responsible for hosting the virtual networks where user and device traffic flows. APs form part of this virtual network infrastructure by integrating with the fabric edge nodes to build a Virtual Extensible LAN (VXLAN)-based distributed data plane for wireless clients. The overlay handles the encapsulation and routing of user traffic, ensuring that wireless traffic is properly tunneled and managed within the SD-Access architecture.","images":[],"sourcePage":35,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0034","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access solution, what is the role of a fabric edge node?","options":{"A":"to connect external Layer 3 networks to the SD-Access fabric","B":"to connect wired endpoints to the SD-Access fabric","C":"to advertise fabric IP address space to external networks","D":"to connect the fusion router to the SD-Access fabric"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access solution, the role of a fabric edge node is to connect wired endpoints to the SD-Access fabric. The fabric edge nodes are responsible for providing connectivity to end devices such as computers and IoT devices within the fabric.","images":[],"sourcePage":36,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0035","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"What are two reasons a company would choose a cloud deployment over an on-prem deployment? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Cloud costs adjust up or down depending on the amount of resources consumed. On-prem costs for hardware, power, and space are on-going regardless of usage.","B":"Cloud resources scale automatically to an increase in demand. On-prem requires additional capital expenditure.","C":"In a cloud environment, the company is in full control of access to their data. On-prem risks access to data due to service provider outages.","D":"In a cloud environment, the company controls technical issues. On-prem environments rely on the service provider to resolve technical issues.","E":"Cloud deployments require long implementation times due to capital expenditure processes. On-prem deployments can be accomplished quickly using operational expenditure processes."},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"A company might choose a cloud deployment over an on-prem deployment for several reasons. First, cloud costs adjust up or down depending on the amount of resources consumed, whereas on-premises costs for hardware, power, and space are ongoing regardless of usage. Second, cloud resources can scale automatically in response to an increase in demand, while on-premises solutions require additional capital expenditure to scale.","images":[],"sourcePage":37,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0036","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the difference between the MAC address table and TCAM?","options":{"A":"TCAM is used to make L2 forwarding decisions. CAM is used to build routing tables.","B":"Router prefix lookups happen in CAM. MAC address table lookups happen in TCAM.","C":"The MAC address table supports partial matches. TCAM requires an exact match.","D":"The MAC address table is contained in CAM. ACL and QoS information is stored in TCAM."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The MAC address table is stored in CAM (Content Addressable Memory), which is used for exact match lookups such as finding MAC addresses and their associated VLANs. TCAM (Ternary Content Addressable Memory), on the other hand, is used for more complex operations like ACL (Access Control List) and QoS (Quality of Service) lookups that require partial matches or longest-prefix matching.","images":[],"sourcePage":38,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0037","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which controller is the single plane of management for Cisco SD-WAN?","options":{"A":"vBond","B":"vSmart","C":"vManage","D":"vEdge"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"vManage is the centralized management controller for Cisco SD-WAN. It serves as the single interface for network administrators to manage, configure, monitor, and troubleshoot the SD-WAN fabric. This makes it the single plane of management in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment.","images":[],"sourcePage":39,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0038","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"A company plans to implement intent-based networking in its campus infrastructure.\nWhich design facilitates a migration from a traditional campus design to a programmable fabric design?","options":{"A":"two-tier","B":"Layer 2 access","C":"three-tier","D":"routed access"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A traditional campus design typically includes a hierarchical architecture with multiple layers for scalability and flexibility. However, to migrate to a programmable fabric design, an architecture that can support intent-based networking and automation is required. Routed access is the design where access layer switches perform routing locally, reducing complexity and allowing for a smoother transition to programmable fabrics. By routing at the access layer, you eliminate the need for a separate distribution layer, making it easier to adopt a programmable environment. This approach aligns well with the goals of intent-based networking, which is to automate and optimize the network according to business intents.","images":[],"sourcePage":40,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0039","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about a fabric access point is true?","options":{"A":"It is in local mode and must be connected directly to the fabric edge switch.","B":"It is in local mode and must be connected directly to the fabric border node.","C":"It is in FlexConnect mode and must be connected directly to the fabric border node.","D":"It is in FlexConnect mode and must be connected directly to the fabric edge switch."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A fabric access point operates in local mode and must be connected directly to the fabric edge switch. This connection is essential for establishing the CAPWAP control plane tunnel to the wireless LAN controller (WLC) and creating a VXLAN tunnel for the data plane. These connections enable the proper functioning of wireless client traffic management and application of wireless policies.","images":[],"sourcePage":41,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0040","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"A customer requests a network design that supports these requirements:\nFHRP redundancy\nmultivendor router environment\nIPv4 and IPv6 hosts\nWhich protocol does the design include?","options":{"A":"VRRP version 2","B":"VRRP version 3","C":"GLBP","D":"HSRP version 2"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct protocol to include in the design is VRRP version 3. VRRP version 3 supports both IPv4 and IPv6 hosts and provides interoperability in a multivendor router environment. Additionally, VRRP is a standardized protocol, unlike HSRP and GLBP which are Cisco proprietary and would not be suitable for a multivendor setup.","images":[],"sourcePage":42,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0041","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"While configuring an IOS router for HSRP with a virtual IP of 10.1.1.1, an engineer sees this log message.\nJan 1 12:12:12.111 : %HSRP-4-DIFFVIP1: GigabitEthernet0/0 Grp 1 active routers virtual IP address 10.1.1.1 is different to the locally configured address 10.1.1.25\nWhich configuration change must the engineer make?","options":{"A":"Change the HSRP group configuration on the local router to 1.","B":"Change the HSRP virtual address on the local router to 10.1.1.1.","C":"Change the HSRP virtual address on the remote router to 10.1.1.1.","D":"Change the HSRP group configuration on the remote router to 1."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The log message indicates that the local router has a different HSRP virtual IP address (10.1.1.25) than the expected virtual IP address (10.1.1.1). To correct this discrepancy, the HSRP virtual address on the local router needs to be changed to 10.1.1.1, ensuring consistency among the routers in the HSRP group.","images":[],"sourcePage":43,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0042","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator has designed a network with two multilayer switches on the distribution layer, which act as default gateways for the end hosts. Which two technologies allow every end host in a VLAN to use both gateways? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"VRRP","B":"GLBP","C":"VSS","D":"MHSRP","E":"HSRP"},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) allows for load balancing by using multiple virtual MAC addresses associated with a single virtual IP address, enabling every end host in a VLAN to use both gateways. MHSRP (Multiple HSRP) can be configured to provide load sharing across multiple HSRP groups, allowing each end host to use both gateways by configuring them with different active routers for each group. Therefore, both these technologies enable end hosts in a VLAN to use both default gateways.","images":[],"sourcePage":44,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0043","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. On which interfaces should VRRP commands be applied to provide first hop redundancy to PC-01 and PC-02?","options":{"A":"G0/0 and G0/1 on Core","B":"G0/0 on Edge-01 and G0/0 on Edge-02","C":"G0/1 on Edge-01 and G0/1 on Edge-02","D":"G0/0 and G0/1 on ASW-01"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol) should be configured on the interfaces that are directly connected to the subnet where the hosts (PC-01 and PC-02) reside. In this diagram, the subnet 10.104.110.0/24 is directly connected to the switch ASW-01. The devices providing first hop redundancy are usually the routers connected to this subnet. Edge-01 and Edge-02 are the routers that connect to this subnet through their G0/0 interfaces. Hence, VRRP commands should be applied on G0/0 of Edge-01 and G0/0 of Edge-02.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0043_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":45,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0044","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Under which network conditions is an outbound QoS policy that is applied on a router WAN interface most beneficial?","options":{"A":"under traffic classification and marking conditions","B":"under interface saturation conditions","C":"under all network conditions","D":"under network convergence conditions"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"An outbound QoS policy applied on a router WAN interface is most beneficial under interface saturation conditions. When a WAN interface experiences high utilization or saturation, an outbound QoS policy helps manage and prioritize the traffic being sent out. This ensures that critical traffic, such as voice or video, receives the necessary bandwidth and quality, minimizing packet loss, latency, and performance degradation during peak usage times. Additionally, this management helps maintain network performance and ensures that essential applications continue to operate efficiently even when the interface is congested.","images":[],"sourcePage":46,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0045","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure HSRP group 300 on a Cisco IOS router. When the router is functional, it must be the active HSRP router. The peer router has been configured using the default priority value. Which command set is required?","options":{"A":"standby version 2 standby 300 priority 110 standby 300 preempt","B":"standby 300 priority 110 standby 300 timers 1 110","C":"standby version 2 standby 300 priority 90 standby 300 preempt","D":"standby 300 priority 90 standby 300 preempt"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To ensure the router becomes the active HSRP router, it must have a higher priority than the default value of 100. Additionally, preemption is required to allow the router to take over the active role when it becomes functional. The correct configuration is to use HSRP version 2 (which supports group numbers up to 4095), set the priority higher than 100, and enable preemption. The required command set is: standby version 2, standby 300 priority 110, and standby 300 preempt.","images":[],"sourcePage":47,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0046","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of a fabric border node in a Cisco SD-Access environment?","options":{"A":"To collect traffic flow information toward external networks.","B":"To connect the Cisco SD-Access fabric to another fabric or external Layer 3 networks.","C":"To attach and register clients to the fabric.","D":"To handle an ordered list of IP addresses and locations for endpoints in the fabric."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A fabric border node in a Cisco SD-Access environment connects the Cisco SD-Access fabric to another fabric or external Layer 3 networks. It serves as the gateway between the SD-Access fabric and external networks, managing traffic flow into and out of the fabric.","images":[],"sourcePage":48,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0047","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a wireless Cisco SD-Access deployment, which roaming method is used when a user moves from one AP to another on a different access switch using a single WLC?","options":{"A":"Layer 3","B":"inter-xTR","C":"auto anchor","D":"fast roam"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"When a user moves from one AP to another on a different access switch within a Cisco SD-Access deployment using a single WLC, the correct roaming method is Inter-xTR. This method is used when the client roams between access points that are connected to different fabric edge nodes (access switches). It involves updating the map server with the client's location information and ensuring seamless connectivity during the transition.","images":[],"sourcePage":49,"flags":["Source text had a stray line break before 'WLC?'; rejoined with a space (no wording change)."]},{"id":"q0048","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is the recommended MTU size for a Cisco SD-Access Fabric?","options":{"A":"4464","B":"17914","C":"9100","D":"1500"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The recommended MTU size for a Cisco SD-Access Fabric is 9100. This size accommodates the VXLAN encapsulation overhead and ensures efficient packet forwarding and routing protocol adjacencies without fragmentation within the fabric domain.","images":[],"sourcePage":50,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0049","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of the fabric control plane node in a Cisco SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"It is responsible for policy application and network segmentation in the fabric.","B":"It performs traffic encapsulation and security profiles enforcement in the fabric.","C":"It holds a comprehensive database that tracks endpoints and networks in the fabric.","D":"It provides integration with legacy nonfabric-enabled environments."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access deployment, the fabric control plane node is responsible for maintaining a comprehensive database that tracks endpoints and networks within the fabric. This database, often referred to as the Host Tracking Database (HTDB), helps in facilitating endpoint identity and location services, decoupling endpoint IP or MAC addresses from their locations within the network. This ensures efficient forwarding, policy enforcement, and overall network management within the SD-Access fabric.","images":[],"sourcePage":51,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0050","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the data policy in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"list of ordered statements that define node configurations and authentication used within the SD-WAN overlay","B":"set of statements that defines how data is forwarded based on IP packet information and specific VPNs","C":"detailed database mapping several kinds of addresses with their corresponding location","D":"group of services tested to guarantee devices and links liveliness within the SD-WAN overlay"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN deployment, the data policy is a set of statements that define how data is forwarded based on IP packet information and specific VPNs. This encompasses details such as the prefix, port, protocol, and DSCP value from the packet header fields, ensuring that traffic flows are directed appropriately within the network based on these criteria.","images":[],"sourcePage":52,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0051","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"In Cisco SD-WAN, which protocol is used to measure link quality?","options":{"A":"IPsec","B":"OMP","C":"RSVP","D":"BFD"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is used to measure link quality in Cisco SD-WAN. BFD continuously monitors the data plane tunnels between routers for parameters such as loss, latency, and jitter, which are crucial for assessing link quality and ensuring optimal performance.","images":[],"sourcePage":53,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0052","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"What is used to perform QoS packet classification?","options":{"A":"the Type field in the Layer 2 frame","B":"the Options field in the Layer 3 header","C":"the TOS field in the Layer 3 header","D":"the Flags field in the Layer 3 header"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The ToS (Type of Service) field in the Layer 3 header is used for Quality of Service (QoS) packet classification. This field allows network devices to identify and prioritize packets based on their priority or required level of service. By marking packets in the ToS field, routers and switches can distinguish between different types of traffic and apply specific handling procedures to ensure that critical applications receive the necessary network resources and performance.","images":[],"sourcePage":54,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0053","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"How do cloud deployments differ from on-premises deployments?","options":{"A":"Cloud deployments require longer implementation times than on-premises deployments.","B":"Cloud deployments are more customizable than on-premises deployments.","C":"Cloud deployments have lower upfront costs than on-premises deployments.","D":"Cloud deployments require less frequent upgrades than on-premises deployments."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cloud deployments have lower upfront costs than on-premises deployments because they eliminate the need for significant investments in hardware and infrastructure. Instead, cloud services typically use a pay-as-you-go or subscription model, which reduces the initial financial burden.","images":[],"sourcePage":55,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0054","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which controller is capable of acting as a STUN server during the onboarding process of Edge devices?","options":{"A":"vBond","B":"vSmart","C":"vManage","D":"PNP Server"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The vBond controller is responsible for orchestrating the initial bring-up process of edge devices, including authentication, authorization, and assigning control plane roles. It also acts as a Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN) server to facilitate NAT traversal during the onboarding process, enabling secure communication between the edge devices and the SD-WAN fabric.","images":[],"sourcePage":56,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0055","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"How is `802.11` traffic handled in a fabric-enabled SSID?","options":{"A":"centrally switched back to WLC where the user traffic is mapped to a VXLAN on the WLC","B":"converted by the AP into 802.3 and encapsulated into VXLAN","C":"centrally switched back to WLC where the user traffic is mapped to a VLAN on the WLC","D":"converted by the AP into 802.3 and encapsulated into a VLAN"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a fabric-enabled SSID, the AP converts 802.11 traffic to 802.3 and encapsulates it into VXLAN. This process involves transitioning the wireless traffic to a wired Ethernet format (802.3) and then encapsulating it into a VXLAN for advanced network segmentation and delivery within the fabric network.","images":[],"sourcePage":57,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0056","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer is installing a new pair of routers in a redundant configuration. When checking on the standby status of each router, the engineer notices that the routers are not functioning as expected.\nWhich action will resolve the configuration error?","options":{"A":"configure matching hold and delay timers","B":"configure matching key-strings","C":"configure matching priority values","D":"configure unique virtual IP addresses"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The action that will resolve the configuration error is to configure matching virtual IP addresses. In the provided exhibit, the virtual IP address for R1 is 192.168.0.1, while for R2, it is 192.165.0.1. For HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) to function correctly, both routers must have the same virtual IP address configured for the same HSRP group. This allows the routers to correctly handle failover and redundancy operations.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0056_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":58,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0057","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nRouter1 is currently operating as the HSRP primary with a priority of 110. Router1 fails and Router2 takes over the forwarding role. Which command on Router1 causes it to take over the forwarding role when it returns to service?","options":{"A":"standby 2 priority","B":"standby 2 preempt","C":"standby 2 track","D":"standby 2 timers"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The command needed on Router1 to cause it to take over the forwarding role when it returns to service is 'standby 2 preempt'. The 'preempt' command allows Router1 to regain its role as the active router once it comes back online, provided it has a higher priority than Router2. This ensures that the router with the highest priority is always the active router in an HSRP configuration.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0057_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":60,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0058","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer has deployed a single Cisco 5520 WLC with a management IP address of 172 16.50.5/24. The engineer must register 50 new Cisco AIR-CAP2802I-E-K9 access points to the WLC using DHCP option 43. The access points are connected to a switch in VLAN 100 that uses the 172.16.100.0/24 subnet. The engineer has configured the DHCP scope on the switch as follows:\nNetwork 172.16.100.0 255.255.255.0\nDefault Router 172.16.100.1 -\nOption 43 ASCII 172.16.50.5 -\nThe access points are failing to join the wireless LAN controller. Which action resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"configure option 43 Hex F104.AC10.3205","B":"configure option 43 Hex F104.CA10.3205","C":"configure dns-server 172.16.50.5","D":"configure dns-server 172. 16.100.1"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of access points failing to join the wireless LAN controller, the DHCP option 43 needs to be configured in hexadecimal format. The correct format for DHCP option 43 for Cisco wireless LAN controllers includes the suboption code 0xf1, followed by the length of the IP addresses in hex (which is 4 for a single IP address), and the IP address itself converted to hexadecimal. The management IP address 172.16.50.5 translates to AC103205 in hexadecimal. Therefore, configuring DHCP option 43 as Hex F104.AC10.3205 will ensure that the access points can locate and join the WLC.","images":[],"sourcePage":62,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0059","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the role of vSmart in a Cisco SD-WAN environment?","options":{"A":"to establish secure control plane connections","B":"to monitor, configure, and maintain SD-WAN devices","C":"to provide secure data plane connectivity over WAN links","D":"to perform initial authentication of devices"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN environment, the vSmart controller is responsible for establishing secure control plane connections. The vSmart controller orchestrates and manages control plane operations, ensuring secure communication between SD-WAN devices. It facilitates policy distribution, routing information, and overall control plane operations, which are essential for the proper functioning and management of the SD-WAN network.","images":[],"sourcePage":63,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0060","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which action is performed by Link Management Protocol in a Cisco StackWise Virtual domain?","options":{"A":"It determines which switch becomes active or standby.","B":"It determines if the hardware is compatible to form the StackWise Virtual domain.","C":"It rejects any unidirectional link traffic forwarding.","D":"It discovers the StackWise domain and brings up SVL interfaces."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Link Management Protocol (LMP) in a Cisco StackWise Virtual domain primarily ensures the integrity of the links between switches. It verifies link integrity by establishing bidirectional traffic forwarding and rejects any unidirectional links. This process helps maintain the health and functionality of the StackWise Virtual links, ensuring that only reliable, bidirectional links are used for communication within the StackWise Virtual domain.","images":[],"sourcePage":64,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0061","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What are two reasons why broadcast radiation is caused in the virtual machine environment? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"vSwitch must interrupt the server CPU to process the broadcast packet.","B":"The Layer 2 domain can be large in virtual machine environments.","C":"Virtual machines communicate primarily through broadcast mode.","D":"Communication between vSwitch and network switch is broadcast based.","E":"Communication between vSwitch and network switch is multicast based."},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"Broadcast radiation in a virtual machine environment can be caused by two main factors. First, the Layer 2 domain can be large in virtual machine environments. This means that the broadcast traffic is sent to more devices within the network, increasing the amount of broadcast traffic and potential collisions (B). Second, virtual machines tend to communicate using broadcast mode for tasks such as address resolution or service discovery, which inherently generates a lot of broadcast traffic and contributes to broadcast radiation (C).","images":[],"sourcePage":65,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0062","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Which two GRE features are configured to prevent fragmentation? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"TCP window size","B":"IP MTU","C":"TCP MSS","D":"DF bit clear","E":"MTU ignore"},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"To prevent fragmentation in GRE tunnels, two features commonly used are IP MTU and TCP MSS. The IP MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) parameter defines the maximum size of the IP packet that can be transmitted without fragmentation. Adjusting the IP MTU value helps in preventing the IP packets from being fragmented as they traverse the GRE tunnel. TCP MSS (Maximum Segment Size) is another feature used to control packet size at the TCP layer. By setting the appropriate TCP MSS value, the size of the TCP segments can be controlled to ensure that they do not exceed the MTU of the path, thus preventing fragmentation. These two parameters work together to manage packet sizes and help avoid fragmentation in a GRE tunnel.","images":[],"sourcePage":66,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0063","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which LISP device is responsible for publishing EID-to-RLOC mappings for a site?","options":{"A":"ETR","B":"MR","C":"ITR","D":"MS"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Egress Tunnel Router (ETR) is responsible for publishing EID-to-RLOC mappings for a site. An ETR connects a site to the LISP-capable part of a core network and plays a crucial role in the LISP architecture by communicating its EID-to-RLOC mapping information to Map Servers or other LISP infrastructure components. This allows for proper routing and encapsulation of traffic in the LISP environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":67,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0064","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAssuming that R1 is a CE router, which VRF is assigned to Gi0/0 on R1?","options":{"A":"default VRF","B":"VRF VPN_A","C":"VRF VPN_B","D":"management VRF"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In the provided network diagram, R1 is connected to R2 within VPN_A. For the interface Gi0/0 on R1 to communicate in VPN_A, it should be assigned to the VRF specific to that VPN. Therefore, the correct VRF for Gi0/0 on R1 is VRF VPN_A.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0064_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":68,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0065","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What are two benefits of virtualizing the server with the use of VMs in a data center environment? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"reduced rack space, power, and cooling requirements","B":"smaller Layer 2 domain","C":"increased security","D":"speedy deployment","E":"reduced IP and MAC address requirements"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Virtualizing servers with the use of VMs in a data center environment offers the benefit of reduced rack space, power, and cooling requirements because multiple virtual servers can run on a single physical machine, reducing the need for physical hardware. Additionally, VMs allow for speedy deployment as virtual machines can be quickly provisioned, cloned, and managed compared to setting up physical servers.","images":[],"sourcePage":69,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0066","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about route targets is true when using VRF-Lite?","options":{"A":"Route targets control the import and export of routes into a customer routing table.","B":"When BGP is configured, route targets are transmitted as BGP standard communities.","C":"Route targets allow customers to be assigned overlapping addresses.","D":"Route targets uniquely identify the customer routing table."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Route targets control the import and export of routes into a customer routing table. In VRF-Lite, route targets are used to manage the import and export of overlapping addresses in the virtual routing and forwarding tables, ensuring proper route segregation and policy application within a customer's network.","images":[],"sourcePage":70,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0067","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which LISP infrastructure device provides connectivity between non-LISP sites and LISP sites by receiving non-LISP traffic with a LISP site destination?","options":{"A":"PITR","B":"map resolver","C":"map server","D":"PETR"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A Proxy Ingress Tunnel Router (PITR) is a LISP infrastructure device that provides connectivity between non-LISP sites and LISP sites by receiving non-LISP traffic with a LISP site destination. A PITR attracts traffic from non-LISP sites destined for LISP sites and encapsulates this traffic to End Tunnel Routers (ETRs) at the LISP sites.","images":[],"sourcePage":71,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0068","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement explains why Type 1 hypervisor is considered more efficient than Type2 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"Type 1 hypervisor is the only type of hypervisor that supports hardware acceleration techniques.","B":"Type 1 hypervisor relies on the existing OS of the host machine to access CPU, memory, storage, and network resources.","C":"Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on the physical hardware of the host machine without relying on the underlying OS.","D":"Type 1 hypervisor enables other operating systems to run on it."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on the physical hardware of the host machine without relying on the underlying OS. This makes it more efficient because it can directly manage the hardware resources, reducing the overhead and potential performance issues introduced by an underlying operating system. This direct access to hardware allows for better performance and resource management compared to Type 2 hypervisors, which run on top of an existing OS.","images":[],"sourcePage":72,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0069","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about VXLAN is true?","options":{"A":"VXLAN encapsulates a Layer 2 frame in an IP-UDP header, which allows Layer 2 adjacency across router boundaries.","B":"VXLAN uses the Spanning Tree Protocol for loop prevention.","C":"VXLAN extends the Layer 2 Segment ID field to 24-bits, which allows up to 4094 unique Layer 2 segments over the same network.","D":"VXLAN uses TCP as the transport protocol over the physical data center network."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) encapsulates a Layer 2 frame in an IP-UDP header, which allows Layer 2 adjacency across router boundaries. This encapsulation mechanism enables the creation of virtual Layer 2 networks over a Layer 3 infrastructure, facilitating network scalability and flexibility in data centers.","images":[],"sourcePage":73,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0070","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Which TCP setting is tuned to minimize the risk of fragmentation on a GRE/IP tunnel?","options":{"A":"MSS","B":"MTU","C":"MRU","D":"window size"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Maximum Segment Size (MSS) is a TCP setting that limits the size of the largest segment that a device is able to receive. By adjusting the MSS, it is possible to prevent larger packets from being fragmented by the GRE/IP tunnel, as each TCP segment will fit within the established limits when passing through the tunnel. This minimizes the risk of fragmentation occurring within the tunnel.","images":[],"sourcePage":74,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0071","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement describes the IP and MAC allocation requirements for virtual machines on Type 1 hypervisors?","options":{"A":"Virtual machines do not require a unique IP or unique MAC. They share the IP and MAC address of the physical server.","B":"Each virtual machine requires a unique IP address but shares the MAC address with the physical server.","C":"Each virtual machine requires a unique IP and MAC addresses to be able to reach to other nodes.","D":"Each virtual machine requires a unique MAC address but shares the IP address with the physical server."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Each virtual machine requires both a unique IP address and a unique MAC address to ensure proper communication within the network. Unique IP addresses are necessary for proper routing at Layer 3 (the network layer), while unique MAC addresses are required for correct data link layer (Layer 2) operations. This allows virtual machines to be uniquely identified and to communicate effectively with other devices on the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":75,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0072","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which two namespaces does the LISP network architecture and protocol use? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"TLOC","B":"RLOC","C":"DNS","D":"VTEP","E":"EID"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"The Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) network architecture and protocol uses two namespaces: Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs) and Routing Locators (RLOCs). EIDs are assigned to end hosts, and RLOCs are assigned to devices, primarily routers, that constitute the global routing system. These namespaces allow for traffic to be correctly identified and directed across the network. Therefore, the correct options are RLOC and EID.","images":[],"sourcePage":76,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0073","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which two entities are Type 1 hypervisors? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Oracle VM VirtualBox","B":"Microsoft Hyper-V","C":"VMware server","D":"VMware ESXi","E":"Microsoft Virtual PC"},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"Type 1 hypervisors, also known as bare-metal hypervisors, run directly on the host's hardware to control the hardware and manage guest operating systems. Microsoft Hyper-V and VMware ESXi are examples of Type 1 hypervisors as they can be installed directly on physical servers and do not require a host operating system. Oracle VM VirtualBox, VMware Server, and Microsoft Virtual PC are Type 2 hypervisors, which run on top of a host operating system.","images":[],"sourcePage":77,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0074","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the LISP components from the left onto the functions they perform on the right. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"Reference: https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/solutions/Enterprise/Data_Center/DCI/5-0/LISPmobility/DCI_LISP_Host_Mobility/LISPmobile_2.html#:~:text=%E2%80%93%20Proxy%20ITR%20(PITR)%3A%20A,devices%20deployed%20at%20LISP%20sites.","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0074_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0074_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":78,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0075","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which action is a function of VTEP in VXLAN?","options":{"A":"tunneling traffic from IPv6 to IPv4 VXLANs","B":"allowing encrypted communication on the local VXLAN Ethernet segment","C":"encapsulating and de-encapsulating VXLAN Ethernet frames","D":"tunneling traffic from IPv4 to IPv6 VXLANs"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A VTEP (VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint) is responsible for encapsulating Ethernet frames into VXLAN packets to send them across an IP network, and for de-encapsulating VXLAN packets back into Ethernet frames when they are received. This function facilitates the communication between different network segments within a VXLAN environment, effectively creating a tunnel for the Ethernet traffic. Options concerning tunneling traffic from IPv6 to IPv4 VXLANs or vice versa and allowing encrypted communication are not roles performed by VTEPs.","images":[],"sourcePage":80,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0076","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which two actions provide controlled Layer 2 network connectivity between virtual machines running on the same hypervisor? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Use a virtual switch provided by the hypervisor.","B":"Use a virtual switch running as a separate virtual machine.","C":"Use VXLAN fabric after installing VXLAN tunneling drivers on the virtual machines.","D":"Use a single routed link to an external router on stick.","E":"Use a single trunk link to an external Layer2 switch."},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"To provide controlled Layer 2 network connectivity between virtual machines running on the same hypervisor, two effective actions are using a virtual switch provided by the hypervisor and using a single trunk link to an external Layer 2 switch. A virtual switch, managed by the hypervisor, allows direct and efficient communication between virtual machines on the same host without requiring external hardware. Additionally, a single trunk link to an external Layer 2 switch facilitates network segmentation and controlled connectivity, leveraging VLANs to manage traffic effectively while maintaining Layer 2 communication. These methods ensure robust control over the traffic flow between virtual machines in a virtualized environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":81,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0077","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a Type 1 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"runs directly on a physical server and depends on a previously installed operating system","B":"runs directly on a physical server and includes its own operating system","C":"runs on a virtual server and depends on an already installed operating system","D":"runs on a virtual server and includes its own operating system"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on a physical server and includes its own operating system. This type of hypervisor is also known as a bare-metal hypervisor, as it does not rely on a previously installed operating system and instead operates directly with the physical hardware, managing the resources and virtual machines independently.","images":[],"sourcePage":82,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0078","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer configures a GRE tunnel and enters the show interface tunnel command. What does the output confirm about the configuration?","options":{"A":"The keepalive value is modified from the default value.","B":"The physical interface MTU is 1476 bytes.","C":"The tunnel mode is set to the default.","D":"Interface tracking is configured."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The exhibit confirms that the tunnel mode is set to the default, which for GRE tunnels is GRE/IP. This is evident because the output displays 'Tunnel protocol/transport GRE/IP,' and there is no indication that the tunnel mode has been changed from this default setting. Other details such as the keepalive value and the tunnel transport MTU do not alter the default tunnel mode setting.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0078_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":83,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0079","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"What it the purpose of the LISP routing and addressing architecture?","options":{"A":"It creates two entries for each network node, one for its identity and another for its location on the network.","B":"It allows LISP to be applied as a network virtualization overlay though encapsulation.","C":"It allows multiple instances of a routing table to co-exist within the same router.","D":"It creates head-end replication used to deliver broadcast and multicast frames to the entire network."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The purpose of the LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) routing and addressing architecture is to create two separate entries for each network node: one for its identity (called the Endpoint Identifier or EID) and another for its location on the network (called the Routing Locator or RLOC). This separation allows for more efficient routing and better mobility management within a network.","images":[],"sourcePage":84,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0080","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What function does VXLAN perform in a Cisco SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"policy plane forwarding","B":"control plane forwarding","C":"data plane forwarding","D":"systems management and orchestration"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access deployment, VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is primarily used for data plane forwarding. It encapsulates Ethernet frames within UDP packets, providing network virtualization and segmentation over an IP infrastructure. This allows for the creation of a scalable and flexible network fabric that supports multiple tenants and isolated network segments.","images":[],"sourcePage":85,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0081","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nMTU has been configured on the underlying physical topology, and no MTU command has been configured on the tunnel interfaces.\nWhat happens when a 1500-byte IPv4 packet traverses the GRE tunnel from host X to host Y, assuming the DF bit is cleared?","options":{"A":"The packet is discarded on router B","B":"The packet arrives on router C without fragmentation","C":"The packet arrives on router C fragmented","D":"The packet is discarded on router A"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When a 1500-byte IPv4 packet traverses the GRE tunnel, the packet will experience added overhead due to the GRE encapsulation. The GRE header and additional IP header together typically add 24 bytes, increasing the total size to 1524 bytes, assuming no additional options are present. Since the physical MTU is 1500 bytes, the packet exceeds this limit and must be fragmented to be transmitted across the GRE tunnel. Therefore, the packet arrives on router C fragmented.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0081_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":86,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0082","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which entity is responsible for maintaining Layer 2 isolation between segments in a VXLAN environment?","options":{"A":"VNID","B":"switch fabric","C":"VTEP","D":"host switch"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The VNID (VXLAN Network Identifier) is responsible for maintaining Layer 2 isolation between segments in a VXLAN environment. The VNID is a 24-bit segment ID that is used to define broadcast domains within VXLAN. It essentially tags packets with a unique identifier, ensuring that traffic is kept isolated between different segments, much like how VLAN IDs are used in traditional Ethernet networks. With the larger bit length, VNID allows for a significantly larger number of isolated segments compared to VLAN IDs.","images":[],"sourcePage":87,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0083","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is an emulated machine that has dedicated compute, memory, and storage resources and a fully installed operating system?","options":{"A":"mainframe","B":"host","C":"virtual machine","D":"container"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A virtual machine is an emulated environment that runs on a host system, providing dedicated compute, memory, and storage resources. It operates with a fully installed operating system, behaving like a distinct physical computer, which matches the description in the question.","images":[],"sourcePage":88,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0084","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the descriptions of the VSS technology from the left to the right. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0084_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0084_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":89,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0085","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which technology does VXLAN use to provide segmentation for Layer 2 and Layer 3 traffic?","options":{"A":"bridge domain","B":"VLAN","C":"VNI","D":"VRF"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) uses Virtual Network Identifier (VNI) to provide segmentation for Layer 2 and Layer 3 traffic. The VNI is a 24-bit field within the VXLAN header that uniquely identifies the VXLAN segment, similar to how VLAN IDs are used but with a much larger addressing space, allowing for over 16 million unique VXLAN segments.","images":[],"sourcePage":91,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0086","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must assign an IP address of 192.168.1.1/24 to the GigabitEthernet1 interface. Which two commands must be added to the existing configuration to accomplish this task? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Router(config-if)#ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0","B":"Router(config-vrf)#address-family ipv4","C":"Router(config-vrf)#ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0","D":"Router(config-if)#address-family ipv4","E":"Router(config-vrf)#address-family ipv6"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"To assign the IP address 192.168.1.1/24 to the GigabitEthernet1 interface, you must first configure the address under the interface itself and then define the address family under the VRF. The command 'Router(config-if)#ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0' assigns the IP address to the interface. Additionally, 'Router(config-vrf)#address-family ipv4' is needed to enable IPv4 for the VRF STAFF, which is necessary when the VRF is defined using 'vrf definition STAFF' to ensure proper handling of IPv4 traffic.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0086_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":92,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0087","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"The following system log message is presented after a network administrator configures a GRE tunnel:\n%TUN-RECURDOWN Interface Tunnel 0 temporarily disabled due to recursive routing\nWhy is Tunnel 0 disabled?","options":{"A":"Because dynamic routing is not enabled.","B":"Because the tunnel cannot reach its tunnel destination.","C":"Because the best path to the tunnel destination is through the tunnel itself.","D":"Because the router cannot recursively identify its egress forwarding interface."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Tunnel 0 is disabled because the best path to the tunnel destination is through the tunnel itself. This creates a recursive routing issue where the router tries to route the packet through the tunnel interface it is trying to establish, leading to a disabling of the tunnel interface.","images":[],"sourcePage":93,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0088","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a benefit of using a Type 2 hypervisor instead of a Type 1 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"better application performance","B":"improved security because the underlying OS is eliminated","C":"improved density and scalability","D":"ability to operate on hardware that is running other OSs"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A Type 2 hypervisor, also known as a hosted hypervisor, operates on top of an existing operating system. This allows it to run on hardware that is already running another OS, providing flexibility in environments where a pre-installed OS is necessary. Type 1 hypervisors, on the other hand, run directly on the physical hardware and generally require dedicated hardware, making integration into existing setups more complex.","images":[],"sourcePage":94,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0089","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer must simplify the IPsec configuration by enabling IPsec over GRE using IPsec profiles. Which two configuration changes accomplish this? (Choose two).","options":{"A":"Create an IPsec profile, associate the transform-set ACL, and apply the profile to the tunnel interface.","B":"Apply the crypto map to the tunnel interface and change the tunnel mode to tunnel mode ipsec ipv4.","C":"Remove all configuration related to crypto map from R1 and R2 and eliminate the ACL.","D":"Create an IPsec profile, associate the transform-set, and apply the profile to the tunnel interface.","E":"Remove the crypto map and modify the ACL to allow traffic between 10.10.0.0/24 to 10.20.0.0/24."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"To simplify the IPsec configuration for enabling IPsec over GRE using IPsec profiles, you need to remove all configurations related to the crypto map from both routers and eliminate the ACL, as crypto map configurations are not required when using IPsec profiles. You also need to create an IPsec profile, associate it with the transform-set, and apply this profile to the tunnel interface to handle IPsec policy settings directly on the GRE tunnel.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0089_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":95,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0090","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a benefit of a virtual machine when compared with a physical server?","options":{"A":"Multiple virtual servers can be deployed on the same physical server without having to buy additional hardware.","B":"Virtual machines increase server processing performance.","C":"The CPU and RAM resources on a virtual machine cannot be affected by other virtual machines.","D":"Deploying a virtual machine is technically less complex than deploying a physical server."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"One significant benefit of virtual machines over physical servers is that multiple virtual machines can be deployed on the same physical hardware without the need for additional equipment. This allows for better resource utilization and cost savings as it avoids the necessity to purchase and maintain multiple physical servers.","images":[],"sourcePage":97,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0091","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is required for a virtual machine to run?","options":{"A":"a Type 1 hypervisor and a host operating system","B":"a hypervisor and physical server hardware","C":"only a Type 1 hypervisor","D":"only a Type 2 hypervisor"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A virtual machine requires a hypervisor and physical server hardware to run. The hypervisor helps manage and allocate resources from the physical hardware to the virtual machines, allowing them to operate as if they were standalone systems. This setup applies whether using Type 1 or Type 2 hypervisors.","images":[],"sourcePage":98,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0092","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which entity is a Type 1 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"Oracle VM VirtualBox","B":"Citrix XenServer","C":"VMware server","D":"Microsoft Virtual PC"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A Type 1 hypervisor, also known as a bare-metal hypervisor, runs directly on the hardware without needing a host operating system. Citrix XenServer is a Type 1 hypervisor because it installs directly on the server hardware. In contrast, Oracle VM VirtualBox, VMware server, and Microsoft Virtual PC are all Type 2 hypervisors, which require a host operating system to run.","images":[],"sourcePage":99,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0093","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What are two benefits of virtual switching when compared to hardware switching? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"increased MTU size","B":"VM-level isolation","C":"extended 802.1Q VLAN range","D":"hardware independence","E":"increased flexibility"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"Virtual switching provides VM-level isolation which allows each virtual machine (VM) to operate as if it has its own dedicated switch port, improving security and network segmentation. Additionally, virtual switching offers increased flexibility in terms of network management and configuration since it operates at the software level, making it easier to provision, manage, and modify virtual networks to meet changing needs compared to hardware-based solutions.","images":[],"sourcePage":100,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0094","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of a virtual machine?","options":{"A":"It must run the same operating system as its host.","B":"It is deployable without a hypervisor to host it.","C":"It must be aware of other virtual machines, in order to allocate physical resources for them.","D":"It relies on hypervisors to allocate computing resources for it."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A characteristic of a virtual machine is that it relies on hypervisors to allocate computing resources for it. Hypervisors are essential for creating and managing virtual machines, as they allow multiple virtual machines to share the physical hardware resources of the host system efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":1,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0095","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which LISP component is required for a LISP site to communicate with a non-LISP site?","options":{"A":"Proxy ITR","B":"ITR","C":"ETR","D":"Proxy ETR"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A Proxy Egress Tunnel Router (PETR) is necessary for a LISP site to communicate with a non-LISP site. The PETR allows traffic that is LISP-encapsulated to be forwarded to destinations outside of the LISP environment by acting similarly to an Egress Tunnel Router (ETR) but specifically for outbound traffic targeting non-LISP destinations.","images":[],"sourcePage":2,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0096","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which two components are supported by LISP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"proxy ETR","B":"egress tunnel router","C":"route reflector","D":"HMAC algorithm","E":"spoke"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) supports components such as proxy ETR (proxy Egress Tunnel Router) and egress tunnel router (ETR). The proxy ETR acts as an intermediary to communicate with non-LISP sites, while the egress tunnel router is responsible for receiving traffic from the core network and delivering it to the destination site. The other options, such as route reflector, HMAC algorithm, and spoke, are not components supported by LISP.","images":[],"sourcePage":3,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0097","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of a VTEP in VXLAN?","options":{"A":"provide the routing underlay and overlay for VXLAN headers","B":"dynamically discover the location of end hosts in a VXLAN fabric","C":"encapsulate and de-encapsulate traffic into and out of the VXLAN fabric","D":"statically point to end host locations of the VXLAN fabric"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A VTEP (VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint) is responsible for encapsulating and de-encapsulating traffic into and out of the VXLAN fabric. This involves taking Ethernet frames from a local network, encapsulating them within VXLAN headers for transport across an IP network, and then de-encapsulating the VXLAN headers to deliver the original Ethernet frames to the destination within the VXLAN fabric.","images":[],"sourcePage":4,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0098","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration MUST be applied to R1 to enable R1 to reach the server at 172.16.0.1?","options":{"A":"interface Ethernet0/0 ip address 172.16.0.7 255.255.0.0 router ospf 44 vrf bank network 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0","B":"interface Ethernet0/0 vrf forwarding hotel ip address 172.16.0.7 255.255.0.0 router ospf 44 vrf Hotel network 172.16.0.0 0.0.255.255 area 0","C":"interface Ethernet0/0 vrf forwarding bank ip address 172.16.0.7 255.255.0.0 router ospf 44 vrf bank network 172.16.0.0 0.0.255.255 area 0","D":"interface Ethernet0/0 ip address 172.16.0.7 255.255.0.0 router ospf 44 vrf hotel network 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To enable R1 to reach the server at 172.16.0.1, the configuration must associate the Ethernet0/0 interface with the correct VRF and use the right OSPF process. The correct configuration is 'interface Ethernet0/0 vrf forwarding bank ip address 172.16.0.7 255.255.0.0' and 'router ospf 44 vrf bank network 172.16.0.0 0.0.255.255 area 0'. This ensures that the interface is part of the 'bank' VRF, has the appropriate IP address, and participates in OSPF routing for the specified network range. This configuration allows R1 to communicate with the server through R2 as expected.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0098_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":5,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0099","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration allows Customer2 hosts to access the FTP server of Customer1 that has the IP address of 192.168.1.200?","options":{"A":"ip route vrf Customer1 172.16.1.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.1.1 global ip route vrf Customer2 192.168.1.200 255.255.255.255 192.168.1.1 global ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 Vlan10 ip route 172.16.1.0 255.255.255.0 Vlan20","B":"ip route vrf Customer1 172.16.1.1 255.255.255.255 172.16.1.1 global ip route vrf Customer2 192.168.1.200 255.255.255.0 192.168.1.1 global ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 Vlan10 ip route 172.16.1.0 255.255.255.0 Vlan20","C":"ip route vrf Customer1 172.16.1.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.1.1 Customer2 ip route vrf Customer2 192.168.1.200 255.255.255.255 192.168.1.1 Customer1","D":"ip route vrf Customer1 172.16.1.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.1.1 Customer1 ip route vrf Customer2 192.168.1.200 255.255.255.255 192.168.1.1 Customer2"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To allow Customer2 hosts to access the FTP server of Customer1 with an IP address of 192.168.1.200, specific routes between the VRFs should be configured. The correct configuration will involve creating routes for both Customer1 and Customer2 VRFs with specific destinations and next hops. By creating a route from Customer2 to the FTP server at 192.168.1.200 and defining the routes between the VLAN10 and VLAN20 interfaces in the global routing table, this setup will enable the intended connectivity. Therefore, the correct answer is to configure the routes as shown in option A.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0099_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":7,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0100","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer configures a new GRE tunnel and enters the show run command. What does the output verify?","options":{"A":"The tunnel keepalive is configured incorrectly because they must match on both sites.","B":"The tunnel destination will be known via the tunnel interface.","C":"The tunnel will be established and work as expected.","D":"The default MTU of the tunnel interface is 1500 bytes."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The output verifies that the tunnel destination will be known via the tunnel interface. This means that one end of the GRE tunnel learns the tunnel destination address through OSPF, resulting in a recursive routing problem, where the tunnel tries to route traffic to its own destination through itself. This causes the tunnel to flap as the interface goes up and down repeatedly.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0100_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":9,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0101","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the virtual components from the left onto their descriptions on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0101_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0101_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":11,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0102","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which element enables communication between guest VMs within a virtualized environment?","options":{"A":"hypervisor","B":"virtual router","C":"vSwitch","D":"pNIC"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The element that enables communication between guest VMs within a virtualized environment is the virtual switch (vSwitch). The vSwitch facilitates network traffic between virtual machines (VMs) that are hosted on the same physical server. It acts at layer 2 of the OSI model, allowing multiple vNICs to connect through it without needing to send the traffic to a physical switch.","images":[],"sourcePage":12,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0103","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two methods are used to reduce the AP coverage area? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Reduce channel width from 40 MHz to 20 MHz.","B":"Reduce AP transmit power.","C":"Enable Fastlane.","D":"Increase minimum mandatory data rate.","E":"Disable 2.4 GHz and use only 5 GHz."},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"Reducing the AP transmit power directly decreases the signal strength, thereby limiting the coverage area. By increasing the minimum mandatory data rate, devices farther away with weaker signals will not meet the required data rate and will not connect. This effectively limits the coverage area to ensure that only devices closer to the AP, which can maintain the higher data rates, remain connected.","images":[],"sourcePage":13,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0104","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which antenna type should be used for a site-to-site wireless connection?","options":{"A":"patch","B":"dipole","C":"omnidirectional","D":"Yagi"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"For a site-to-site wireless connection, the most suitable antenna type would be one with high directionality and gain to ensure a strong, focused signal between the two points. A Yagi antenna is designed with elements that provide significant directionality and gain, making it ideal for point-to-point communications. This ensures that the signal is concentrated in a specific direction, reducing interference and increasing the efficiency of the connection. Therefore, the correct answer is Yagi.","images":[],"sourcePage":14,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0105","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"In a wireless network environment, what is calculated using the numerical values of the transmitter power level, cable loss, and antenna gain?","options":{"A":"SNR","B":"RSSI","C":"EIRP","D":"dBi"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Effective Isotropic Radiated Power (EIRP) is calculated using the numerical values of the transmitter power level, cable loss, and antenna gain. To determine EIRP, add the transmitter power (in dBm) to the antenna gain (in dBi) and subtract any cable losses (in dB). This calculation helps to understand the effective power radiated from an antenna which is crucial for adhering to regulatory power limits.","images":[],"sourcePage":15,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0106","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What does the LAP send when multiple WLCs respond to the CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.localdomain hostname during the CAPWAP discovery and join process?","options":{"A":"unicast discovery request to the first WLC that resolves the domain name","B":"broadcast discovery request","C":"join request to all the WLCs","D":"unicast discovery request to each WLC"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"During the CAPWAP discovery and join process, when an LAP encounters multiple WLCs that respond to the CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.localdomain hostname, it will send a unicast discovery request to each WLC. This allows each WLC to respond individually, helping the LAP to gather information from all possible controllers before making a decision on which one to join.","images":[],"sourcePage":16,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0107","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nA port channel is configured between SW2 and SW3. SW2 is not running a Cisco operating system. When all physical connections are made, the port channel does not establish.\nBased on the configuration excerpt of SW3, what is the cause of the problem?","options":{"A":"The port-channel mode should be set to auto.","B":"The port channel on SW2 is using an incompatible protocol.","C":"The port-channel trunk is not allowing the native VLAN.","D":"The port-channel interface load balance should be set to src-mac."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The cause of the problem is that the port channel on SW2 is using an incompatible protocol. SW3 is configured to use PAgP (Port Aggregation Protocol) with the 'channel-group 30 mode desirable' command. PAgP is a Cisco proprietary protocol, which means it only works between Cisco devices. Since SW2 is not running a Cisco operating system, it cannot use PAgP. Instead, the configuration should use LACP (Link Aggregation Control Protocol), an open standard supported by both Cisco and non-Cisco devices. The compatible modes for LACP are 'active' and 'passive'. Therefore, ensuring the use of LACP would resolve the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0107_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":17,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0108","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"What is a fact about Cisco EAP-FAST?","options":{"A":"It requires a client certificate.","B":"It is an IETF standard.","C":"It does not require a RADIUS server certificate.","D":"It operates in transparent mode."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cisco EAP-FAST (Flexible Authentication via Secure Tunneling) is a protocol that does not require the use of server certificates. EAP-FAST uses a Protected Access Credential (PAC) for mutual authentication between the client and the authentication server. This makes it distinct from other EAP types that commonly require certificates. The key point here is that EAP-FAST was developed by Cisco to enable secure network authentication without the complexity of certificate management.","images":[],"sourcePage":19,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0109","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which DNS lookup does an access point perform when attempting CAPWAP discovery?","options":{"A":"CISCO-CONTROLLER.local","B":"CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.local","C":"CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.local","D":"CISCO-DNA-CONTROLLER.local"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When an access point attempts to discover a CAPWAP controller, it performs a DNS lookup for CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.local. This is the standard domain name used in CAPWAP discovery processes.","images":[],"sourcePage":20,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0110","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhat are two effects of this configuration? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"It establishes a one-to-one NAT translation.","B":"The 209.165.201.0/27 subnet is assigned as the outside local address range.","C":"The 10.1.1.0/27 subnet is assigned as the inside local addresses.","D":"Inside source addresses are translated to the 209.165.201.0/27 subnet.","E":"The 10.1.1.0/27 subnet is assigned as the inside global address range."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"This configuration primarily sets up a dynamic NAT. The access-list permits the 10.1.1.0/27 subnet, meaning those addresses are designated as inside local addresses. The NAT pool 'CISCO' specifies a range of global addresses from 209.165.201.1 to 209.165.201.30. The command 'ip nat inside source list 1 pool CISCO' translates the inside local addresses (10.1.1.0/27) to the inside global addresses from the specified pool (209.165.201.0/27). Therefore, the 10.1.1.0/27 subnet is assigned as the inside local address range (C) and the inside source addresses are translated to the 209.165.201.0/27 subnet (D).","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0110_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":21,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0111","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer configures a WLAN with fast transition enabled. Some legacy clients fail to connect to this WLAN.\nWhich feature allows the legacy clients to connect while still allowing other clients to use fast transition based on their OUIs?","options":{"A":"over the DS","B":"802.11k","C":"adaptive R","D":"802.11v"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The feature that allows legacy clients to connect to a WLAN with fast transition enabled while still allowing other clients to use fast transition based on their OUIs is adaptive 802.11r. Adaptive 802.11r does not advertise the fast transition (FT) authentication key management (AKM) scheme but allows the use of it when supported clients connect. This helps in maintaining compatibility with legacy clients that do not support FT, allowing them to connect using standard WPA2, while FT-capable clients can still benefit from fast transition.","images":[],"sourcePage":22,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0112","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What are two common sources of interference for Wi-Fi networks? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"LED lights","B":"radar","C":"fire alarm","D":"conventional oven","E":"rogue AP"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"Radar systems operate in similar frequency bands as Wi-Fi networks and can cause interference, especially if they are in close proximity to Wi-Fi devices. Rogue Access Points (APs) are unauthorized devices that can disrupt a Wi-Fi network by competing for bandwidth and channel space, leading to degraded performance. These are common sources of interference for Wi-Fi networks.","images":[],"sourcePage":23,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0113","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two pieces of information are necessary to compute SNR? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"transmit power","B":"noise floor","C":"EIRP","D":"RSSI","E":"antenna gain"},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"To compute the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR), you need the noise floor and the Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI). The noise floor represents the level of background noise present in the wireless environment. The RSSI measures the power level of the received signal at the receiver. SNR is calculated as the difference between RSSI and the noise floor, making both of these metrics essential for its computation.","images":[],"sourcePage":24,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0114","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Which OSPF network types are compatible and allow communication through the two peering devices?","options":{"A":"point-to-multipoint to nonbroadcast","B":"broadcast to nonbroadcast","C":"point-to-multipoint to broadcast","D":"broadcast to point-to-point"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"OSPF network types that can communicate with each other include combinations where the timers and mechanisms for forming adjacencies are compatible. The most relevant detail here is that broadcast and non-broadcast networks can communicate if the hello and dead timers are adjusted to match. Hence, the compatible network types for communication include broadcast to non-broadcast, making this the correct choice.","images":[],"sourcePage":25,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0115","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich statement about the OSPF debug output is true?","options":{"A":"The output displays OSPF hello messages which router R1 has sent or received on interface Fa0/1.","B":"The output displays all OSPF messages which router R1 has sent or received on all interfaces.","C":"The output displays all OSPF messages which router R1 has sent or received on interface Fa0/1.","D":"The output displays OSPF hello and LSACK messages which router R1 has sent or received."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The correct option is that the output displays OSPF hello messages which router R1 has sent or received on interface Fa0/1. The 'debug ip ospf hello' command is specifically used to display OSPF hello messages. Additionally, the 'debug condition interface Fa0/1' command sets a condition to limit the debugging output to only the specified interface, which in this case is Fa0/1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0115_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":26,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0116","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about multicast RPs is true?","options":{"A":"RPs are required only when using protocol independent multicast dense mode.","B":"RPs are required for protocol independent multicast sparse mode and dense mode.","C":"By default, the RP is needed periodically to maintain sessions with sources and receivers.","D":"By default, the RP is needed only to start new sessions with sources and receivers."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"By default, the Rendezvous Point (RP) in multicast routing for Protocol Independent Multicast Sparse Mode (PIM-SM) is needed only to start new sessions with sources and receivers. This is because the RP serves as a temporary meeting place for the initial transmission of multicast data. Once the initial session is established and the necessary state is created, the data can flow directly between sources and receivers without the continuous involvement of the RP.","images":[],"sourcePage":27,"flags":["Source text had a stray line break before 'true?'; rejoined with a space (no wording change)."]},{"id":"q0117","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"To increase total throughput and redundancy on the links between the wireless controller and switch, the customer enabled LAG on the wireless controller.\nWhich EtherChannel mode must be configured on the switch to allow the WLC to connect?","options":{"A":"Active","B":"Passive","C":"On","D":"Auto"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To increase total throughput and redundancy on the links between the wireless controller and switch using Link Aggregation (LAG), the EtherChannel on the switch must be configured in 'on' mode. This mode statically sets the port channels without any negotiation protocol, ensuring compatibility with the wireless controller, as it does not support the negotiation protocols LACP or PAgP.","images":[],"sourcePage":28,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0118","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Based on this interface configuration, what is the expected state of OSPF adjacency?","options":{"A":"2WAY/DROTHER on both routers","B":"not established","C":"FULL on both routers","D":"FULL/BDR on R1 and FULL/BDR on R2"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The OSPF adjacency will not be established between R1 and R2. One of the crucial requirements for OSPF neighbors to establish adjacency is that their hello and dead intervals must match. In the given configuration, R1 has a hello interval of 2 seconds, while R2 has the default hello interval of 10 seconds. This mismatch in hello intervals will prevent OSPF adjacency from forming.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0118_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":29,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0119","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nLink1 is a copper connection and Link2 is a fiber connection. The fiber port must be the primary port for all forwarding. The output of the show spanning-tree command on SW2 shows that the fiber port is blocked by spanning tree. An engineer enters the spanning-tree port-priority 32 command on G0/1 on SW2, but the port remains blocked.\nWhich command should be entered on the ports that are connected to Link2 to resolve the issue?","options":{"A":"Enter spanning-tree port-priority 4 on SW2.","B":"Enter spanning-tree port-priority 32 on SW1.","C":"Enter spanning-tree port-priority 224 on SW1.","D":"Enter spanning-tree port-priority 64 on SW2."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To ensure that the fiber port (Link2) becomes the primary forwarding port in the spanning tree topology, the port-priority on the port connected to Link2 on the upstream switch (SW1) should be lowered. The spanning-tree protocol selects the port with the lowest priority as the root port in case of a tie on path costs. By setting the port priority of the fiber port (G0/1) on SW1 to 32, it ensures that SW2 receives a BPDU with a lower priority on that port, making it the preferred root port over the copper connection (Link1).","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0119_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":31,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0120","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which behavior can be expected when the HSRP version is changed from 1 to 2?","options":{"A":"No changes occur because the standby router is upgraded before the active router.","B":"No changes occur because version 1 and 2 use the same virtual MAC OUI.","C":"Each HSRP group reinitializes because the virtual MAC address has changed.","D":"Each HSRP group reinitializes because the multicast address has changed."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When changing HSRP from version 1 to version 2, each HSRP group reinitializes because the virtual MAC address has changed. In HSRP version 1, the virtual MAC address follows the format 0000.0C07.ACxx, where xx is the HSRP group number. HSRP version 2 uses a different MAC address format, 0000.0C9F.Fxxx, where xxx corresponds to the group number. The change in the virtual MAC address causes each group to reinitialize during the transition from version 1 to version 2.","images":[],"sourcePage":33,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0121","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich IP address becomes the active next hop for 192.168.102.0/24 when 192.168.101.2 fails?","options":{"A":"192.168.101.10","B":"192.168.101.14","C":"192.168.101.6","D":"192.168.101.18"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Based on the BGP path selection process, the next hop for 192.168.102.0/24 when 192.168.101.2 fails would be 192.168.101.10. Here's why: First, the Weight attribute is examined, and the path with the highest weight is preferred. Since the Weight is 0 for all routes, we move to the next criteria, which is Local Preference. The default Local Preference is 100 when not specified, making paths with explicit Local Preference values of 80 less preferred. Thus, the paths via 192.168.101.14 and 192.168.101.6 are disregarded. Next in line is the AS Path attribute, with preference given to the shortest AS Path length. Both 192.168.101.10 and 192.168.101.18 have a Local Preference of 100, but the AS Path for 192.168.101.18 is shorter, making it the best path. Therefore, once 192.168.101.2 fails, the next option with the shortest AS Path would be 192.168.101.10.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0121_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":34,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0122","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Which PAgP mode combination prevents an EtherChannel from forming?","options":{"A":"auto/desirable","B":"desirable/desirable","C":"desirable/auto","D":"auto/auto"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"PAgP (Port Aggregation Protocol) works by having ports in either 'auto' or 'desirable' mode. 'Auto' mode will only respond to PAgP packets and never initiate them, whereas 'desirable' mode actively attempts to form an EtherChannel. Therefore, if both ends of a potential EtherChannel are set to 'auto', neither end will initiate the negotiation process, and the EtherChannel will not form. Thus, the combination of auto/auto prevents an EtherChannel from forming.","images":[],"sourcePage":35,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0123","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"If a VRRP master router fails, which router is selected as the new master router?","options":{"A":"router with the lowest priority","B":"router with the highest priority","C":"router with the highest loopback address","D":"router with the lowest loopback address"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"If a VRRP master router fails, the router with the highest priority is selected as the new master router. In a scenario where routers have the same priority, the router with the highest IP address value takes over as the master router.","images":[],"sourcePage":36,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0124","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which two mechanisms are available to secure NTP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"IPsec","B":"IP prefix list-based","C":"encrypted authentication","D":"TACACS-based authentication","E":"IP access list-based"},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"To secure Network Time Protocol (NTP), two mechanisms are commonly used. The first mechanism is encrypted authentication, which ensures that the NTP messages are encrypted to prevent unauthorized access and manipulation. The second mechanism is IP access list-based restrictions, which allow administrators to specify which IP addresses or subnets can communicate with the NTP server, thus controlling access and enhancing security.","images":[],"sourcePage":37,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0125","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"In OSPF, which LSA type is responsible for pointing to the ASBR router?","options":{"A":"type 1","B":"type 2","C":"type 3","D":"type 4"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In OSPF (Open Shortest Path First), a Type 4 LSA (Link State Advertisement) is used to advertise the presence of an Autonomous System Boundary Router (ASBR) in the network. This LSA is generated by Area Border Routers (ABRs) to inform other OSPF routers within the same area about the existence and location of ASBRs. The primary purpose of Type 4 LSAs is to provide a route to the ASBR for other routers so they can reach external networks that the ASBR connects to. Therefore, Type 4 is responsible for pointing to the ASBR router.","images":[],"sourcePage":38,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0126","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nVLANs 50 and 60 exist on the trunk links between all switches. All access ports on SW3 are configured for VLAN 50 and SW1 is the VTP server.\nWhich command ensures that SW3 receives frames only from VLAN 50?","options":{"A":"SW1(config)#vtp mode transparent","B":"SW3(config)#vtp mode transparent","C":"SW2(config)#vtp pruning","D":"SW1(config)#vtp pruning"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To ensure that SW3 receives frames only from VLAN 50, VTP pruning should be enabled on the VTP server, which in this case is SW1. VTP pruning helps minimize unnecessary VLAN traffic by only sending traffic for VLANs that exist on the trunk links between the switches. Enabling VTP pruning on SW1 will prevent VLAN 60 traffic from being sent to SW3, as SW3 only has access ports configured for VLAN 50. This setup optimizes the network by reducing the amount of broadcast, multicast, and unknown unicast traffic traversing the trunk links.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0126_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":39,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0127","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which First Hop Redundancy Protocol maximizes uplink utilization and minimizes the amount of configuration that is necessary?","options":{"A":"GLBP","B":"HSRP v2","C":"VRRP","D":"HSRP v1"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) is designed to maximize uplink utilization by enabling load balancing across multiple active gateways. Unlike other First Hop Redundancy Protocols (FHRPs) such as HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) and VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol), which typically operate with a single active router and one or more standby routers, GLBP allows multiple gateways to be active simultaneously. This not only optimizes the use of available uplinks but also simplifies configuration because it dynamically manages the load balancing and failover process.","images":[],"sourcePage":40,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0128","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich configuration establishes EBGP neighborship between these two directly connected neighbors and exchanges the loopback network of the two routers through BGP?","options":{"A":"R1(config)#router bgp 1 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.10.2 remote-as 2 R1(config-router)#network 10.1.1.0 mask 255.255.255.0 R2(config)#router bgp 2 R2(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.10.1 remote-as 1 R2(config-router)#network 10.2.2.0 mask 255.255.255.0","B":"R1(config)#router bgp 1 R1(config-router)#neighbor 10.2.2.2 remote-as 2 R1(config-router)#network 10.1.1.0 mask 255.255.255.0 R2(config)#router bgp 2 R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.1.1.1 remote-as 1 R2(config-router)#network 10.2.2.0 mask 255.255.255.0","C":"R1(config)#router bgp 1 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.10.2 remote-as 2 R1(config-router)#network 10.0.0.0 mask 255.0.0.0 R2(config)#router bgp 2 R2(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.10.1 remote-as 1 R2(config-router)#network 10.0.0.0 mask 255.0.0.0","D":"R1(config)#router bgp 1 R1(config-router)#neighbor 10.2.2.2 remote-as 2 R1(config-router)#neighbor 10.2.2.2 update-source lo0 R1(config-router)#network 10.1.1.0 mask 255.255.255.0 R2(config)#router bgp 2 R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.1.1.1 remote-as 1 R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.1.1.1 update-source lo0 R2(config-router)#network 10.2.2.0 mask 255.255.255.0"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To establish EBGP neighborship between two directly connected neighbors and exchange the loopback network of both routers through BGP, the relevant configuration would include specifying the neighbor's IP address and the remote AS number, along with advertising the specific networks via BGP. Option A provides this configuration correctly. It configures R1 to establish a BGP session with R2 using the directly connected IP addresses (192.168.10.2 and 192.168.10.1 respectively) and advertises the loopback addresses (10.1.1.0 and 10.2.2.0 respectively). This fulfils the requirement based on the provided network topology.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0128_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":41,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0129","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich type of antenna do the radiation patterns present?","options":{"A":"Yagi","B":"patch","C":"omnidirectional","D":"dipole"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The radiation patterns presented in the exhibit correspond to a patch antenna. Patch antennas typically exhibit a directional radiation pattern which is evident in both the azimuth and elevation plane patterns shown. The azimuth pattern resembles a broad circle, indicating a broad directional range, while the elevation pattern shows more distinct lobes, typical of a patch antenna's higher directionality. This is characteristic of patch antennas, which are designed to provide higher directed gain for specific coverage areas.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0129_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":43,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0130","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Which reason could cause an OSPF neighborship to be in the EXSTART/EXCHANGE state?","options":{"A":"mismatched OSPF link costs","B":"mismatched OSPF network type","C":"mismatched areas","D":"mismatched MTU size"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A common reason for OSPF neighborship to be stuck in the EXSTART/EXCHANGE state is a mismatched MTU size. When two OSPF routers have different MTU settings, the router with the larger MTU may send packets that exceed the MTU of the neighboring router. As a result, the neighboring router ignores these packets, preventing the routers from successfully completing the exchange of OSPF Database Description (DBD) packets.","images":[],"sourcePage":44,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0131","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"When configuring WPA2 Enterprise on a WLAN, which additional security component configuration is required?","options":{"A":"PKI server","B":"NTP server","C":"RADIUS server","D":"TACACS server"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When configuring WPA2 Enterprise on a WLAN, a RADIUS server is required. This server handles the task of authenticating network users' access based on the 802.1x policy, which is essential for the enterprise mode of WPA2. TACACS, on the other hand, does not support EAP (Extensible Authentication Protocol), which is obligatory for running Enterprise mode. Therefore, the correct additional security component configuration required for WPA2 Enterprise is a RADIUS server.","images":[],"sourcePage":45,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0132","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A customer has several small branches and wants to deploy a Wi-Fi solution with local management using CAPWAP.\nWhich deployment model meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"local mode","B":"autonomous","C":"SD-Access wireless","D":"Mobility Express"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To deploy a Wi-Fi solution with local management using CAPWAP in several small branches, Mobility Express is the ideal choice. Mobility Express enables a single access point to act as both a wireless LAN controller and an access point, creating a cost-effective solution for small and mid-sized environments. This model supports CAPWAP tunnels formed between the AP acting as the WLC and other APs at the branch, thus meeting the requirement for local management without additional hardware.","images":[],"sourcePage":46,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0133","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nThe WLC administrator sees that the controller to which a roaming client associates has Mobility Role Anchor configured under Clients > Detail.\nWhich type of roaming is supported?","options":{"A":"indirect","B":"Layer 3 intercontroller","C":"intracontroller","D":"Layer 2 intercontroller"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Roaming clients typically use Layer 3 intercontroller roaming when the controller to which they associate has the Mobility Role set as 'Anchor'. In this scenario, two controllers are involved: one as the Anchor controller and the other as the Foreign controller. The client's IP address remains the same during the roam, even though it moves to a different subnet, making Layer 3 intercontroller roaming the correct answer.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0133_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":47,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0134","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the correct routing protocol types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0134_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0134_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":49,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0135","topic":"EIGRP","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature is supported by EIGRP but is not supported by OSPF?","options":{"A":"route filtering","B":"unequal-cost load balancing","C":"route summarization","D":"equal-cost load balancing"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"EIGRP supports unequal-cost load balancing, which allows traffic to be distributed across multiple paths that do not have the same metric, providing a more efficient use of network resources. This feature is not supported by OSPF, which only supports equal-cost load balancing.","images":[],"sourcePage":50,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0136","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct EBGP path attribute list, ordered from most preferred to least preferred, that the BGP best-path algorithm uses?","options":{"A":"local preference, weight, AS path, MED","B":"weight, local preference, AS path, MED","C":"weight, AS path, local preference, MED","D":"local preference, weight, MED, AS path"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The BGP path selection process prioritizes the path attributes in the following order: weight, local preference, AS path, and MED. Weight is entirely local to the router and is the first criterion utilized by BGP to select a path. Local preference is the second attribute considered, determining the preferred path for outbound traffic. The AS path follows, where the shortest path is preferred in inter-AS routing. Finally, the MED attribute is utilized to select routes between autonomous systems.","images":[],"sourcePage":51,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0137","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"A local router shows an EBGP neighbor in the Active state.\nWhich statement is true about the local router?","options":{"A":"The local router is attempting to open a TCP session with the neighboring router.","B":"The local router is receiving prefixes from the neighboring router and adding them in RIB-IN.","C":"The local router has active prefixes in the forwarding table from the neighboring router.","D":"The local router has BGP passive mode configured for the neighboring router."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"When an EBGP neighbor is in the Active state, it means that the local router is attempting to open a TCP session with the neighboring router. The term 'Active' indicates that the router is actively trying to establish a connection, but has not yet done so. This state is part of the process where the local router is continuously attempting to initiate and complete a TCP handshake with the EBGP neighbor.","images":[],"sourcePage":52,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0138","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nSwitchC connects HR and Sales to the Core switch. However, business needs require that no traffic from the Finance VLAN traverse this switch.\nWhich command meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"SwitchC(config)#vtp pruning vlan 110","B":"SwitchC(config)#vtp pruning","C":"SwitchC(config)#interface port-channel 1 SwitchC(config-if)#switchport trunk allowed vlan add 210,310","D":"SwitchC(config)#interface port-channel 1 SwitchC(config-if)#switchport trunk allowed vlan remove 110"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To ensure that no traffic from the Finance VLAN (VLAN 110) traverses SwitchC, the best approach is to explicitly remove VLAN 110 from the trunk. This can be achieved with the command SwitchC(config-if)#switchport trunk allowed vlan remove 110. This command ensures that the Finance VLAN is not allowed on the trunk ports, preventing any traffic from this VLAN from passing through SwitchC. Since the switch is in VTP transparent mode, VTP pruning commands are not applicable, and adding VLANs is unnecessary as it does not prevent the traversal of unwanted VLAN traffic.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0138_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":53,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0139","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must ensure that all traffic leaving AS 200 will choose Link 2 as the exit point.\nAssuming that all BGP neighbor relationships have been formed and that the attributes have not been changed on any of the routers, which configuration accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"R4(config-router)bgp default local-preference 200","B":"R3(config-router)bgp default local-preference 200","C":"R4(config-router)neighbor 10.2.2.2 weight 200","D":"R3(config-router)neighbor 10.1.1.1 weight 200"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To ensure that all traffic leaving AS 200 will choose Link 2 as the exit point, the local-preference BGP attribute is the correct choice to influence route selection. Local-preference is an attribute used to influence the exit path for outbound traffic from the Autonomous System (AS), and it is applied to the entire AS. By configuring a higher local-preference value, the path via Link 2 will be preferred over other paths. Weight, on the other hand, is a Cisco-specific attribute that only affects route selection on the individual router where it is configured and is not shared with other routers in the AS. Therefore, setting the local-preference to 200 on R4 ensures that all routers in AS 200 will prefer this exit point.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0139_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":55,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0140","topic":"EIGRP","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature of EIGRP is not supported in OSPF?","options":{"A":"load balancing of unequal-cost paths","B":"load balance over four equal-cost paths","C":"uses interface bandwidth to determine best path","D":"per-packet load balancing over multiple paths"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"EIGRP supports load balancing of unequal-cost paths, known as variance, which allows for more efficient use of available paths. OSPF, on the other hand, only supports load balancing over equal-cost paths and does not have a similar feature for unequal-cost path load balancing.","images":[],"sourcePage":56,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0141","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which NTP Stratum level is a server that is connected directly to an authoritative time source?","options":{"A":"Stratum 0","B":"Stratum 1","C":"Stratum 14","D":"Stratum 15"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A server that is connected directly to an authoritative time source is classified as an NTP Stratum 1 server. Stratum 0 refers to high-precision timekeeping devices like atomic clocks and GPS reference clocks, which are not used as NTP servers themselves but are directly connected to Stratum 1 servers. Therefore, Stratum 1 is the correct classification for servers directly connected to these authoritative sources.","images":[],"sourcePage":57,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0142","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer reconfigures the port-channel between SW1 and SW2 from an access port to a trunk and immediately notices this error in SW1's log:\n*Mar 1 09:47:22.245: %PM-4-ERR_DISABLE: bpduguard error detected on Gi0/0, putting Gi0/0 in err-disable sta\n                                                                                                              \nWhich command set resolves this error?","options":{"A":"SW1(config-if)#interface Gi0/0 SW1(config-if)#no spanning-tree bpdufilter SW1(config-if)#shut SW1(config-if)#no shut","B":"SW1(config-if)#interface Gi0/0 SW1(config-if)#no spanning-tree bpduguard enable SW1(config-if)#shut SW1(config-if)#no shut","C":"SW1(config-if)#interface Gi0/0 SW1(config-if)#spanning-tree bpduguard enable SW1(config-if)#shut SW1(config-if)#no shut","D":"SW1(config-if)#interface Gi0/1 SW1(config-if)#spanning-tree bpduguard enable SW1(config-if)#shut SW1(config-if)#no shut"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The error occurred because BPDU Guard is enabled on the interface and it received a BPDU, which caused it to go into the err-disable state. To resolve this, you need to disable BPDU Guard on the interface, which is done by using the command 'no spanning-tree bpduguard enable'. After that, you bring the interface down and up again using the 'shut' and 'no shut' commands to recover from the err-disable state.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0142_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":58,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0143","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"When a wireless client roams between two different wireless controllers, a network connectivity outage is experienced for a period of time.\nWhich configuration issue would cause this problem?","options":{"A":"Not all of the controllers in the mobility group are using the same mobility group name.","B":"Not all of the controllers within the mobility group are using the same virtual interface IP address.","C":"All of the controllers within the mobility group are using the same virtual interface IP address.","D":"All of the controllers in the mobility group are using the same mobility group name."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"When wireless clients roam between controllers within a mobility group, it's essential that all controllers use the same virtual interface IP address. This virtual interface IP address is used for mobility management and to tunnel client traffic between controllers. If the controllers do not share the same virtual interface IP address, the mobility tunnel might fail to establish or maintain, causing a network connectivity outage for the client during the transition.","images":[],"sourcePage":59,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0144","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the role of the RP in PIM sparse mode?","options":{"A":"The RP maintains default aging timeouts for all multicast streams requested by the receivers.","B":"The RP acts as a control-plane node only and does not receive or forward multicast packets.","C":"The RP is the multicast router that is the root of the PIM-SM shared multicast distribution tree.","D":"The RP responds to the PIM join messages with the source of a requested multicast group."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In PIM sparse mode, the role of the RP (Rendezvous Point) is to act as the root of the shared multicast distribution tree. Sources must send their multicast traffic to the RP, which then forwards it down the shared tree to the receivers. This makes the RP responsible for facilitating the initial distribution of multicast packets before any source-specific trees are formed. This central role makes the RP the root of the shared multicast distribution tree in PIM-SM.","images":[],"sourcePage":60,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0145","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Why is an AP joining a different WLC than the one specified through option 43?","options":{"A":"The AP is joining a primed WLC","B":"The APs broadcast traffic is unable to reach the WLC through Layer 2","C":"The AP multicast traffic is unable to reach the WLC through Layer 3","D":"The WLC is running a different software version"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"An AP joins a different WLC than the one specified through option 43 primarily if it has previously been associated with another WLC and has stored the information of the primary, secondary, and tertiary controllers in its nonvolatile memory. This stored information takes precedence over the DHCP option 43 configuration. This process is known as priming the access point. Therefore, the AP tries to join the controllers it has been primed for before attempting to discover new ones using option 43.","images":[],"sourcePage":61,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0146","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Company policy restricts VLAN 10 to be allowed only on SW1 and SW2. All other VLANs can be on all three switches. An administrator has noticed that VLAN 10 has propagated to SW3.\nWhich configuration corrects the issue?","options":{"A":"SW1(config)#int gi1/1 SW1(config)#switchport trunk allowed vlan 1-9,11-4094","B":"SW2(config)#int gi1/2 SW2(config)#switchport trunk allowed vlan 10","C":"SW2(config)#int gi1/2 SW2(config)#switchport trunk allowed vlan 1-9,11-4094","D":"SW1(config)#int gi1/1 SW1(config)#switchport trunk allowed vlan 10"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To ensure that VLAN 10 is only allowed on SW1 and SW2, you need to configure the trunk on SW2 to allow only the required VLANs. By configuring SW2's interface gi1/2 with the command 'switchport trunk allowed vlan 1-9,11-4094', VLAN 10 will be excluded from being allowed on the trunk link to SW3. This effectively prevents VLAN 10 from propagating to SW3, which corrects the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0146_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":62,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0147","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which First Hop Redundancy Protocol should be used to meet a design requirement for more efficient default gateway bandwidth usage across multiple devices?","options":{"A":"GLBP","B":"LACP","C":"HSRP","D":"VRRP"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) should be used to meet the design requirement for more efficient default gateway bandwidth usage across multiple devices. GLBP is specifically designed to provide load balancing among multiple gateways, allowing for the efficient utilization of default gateway bandwidth. It achieves this by using multiple virtual MAC addresses and enabling hosts to send traffic to different gateways based on a load-balancing algorithm.","images":[],"sourcePage":63,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0148","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A client device roams between access points located on different floors in an atrium. The access points are joined to the same controller and configured in local mode. The access points are in different AP groups and have different IP addresses, but the client VLAN in the groups is the same.\nWhich type of roam occurs?","options":{"A":"inter-controller","B":"inter-subnet","C":"intra-VLAN","D":"intra-controller"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"When a client device roams between access points that are connected to the same controller, even if the access points are in different AP groups and have different IP addresses, as long as the client VLAN remains the same, the roam is classified as intra-controller. This type of roam occurs within the same wireless LAN controller (WLC), which manages the handoff process smoothly without involving another controller or subnet.","images":[],"sourcePage":64,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0149","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which DHCP option helps lightweight APs find the IP address of a wireless LAN controller?","options":{"A":"Option 43","B":"Option 60","C":"Option 67","D":"Option 150"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"DHCP Option 43 is used by lightweight access points (APs) to find the IP address of wireless LAN controllers. When an AP receives an IP address from the DHCP server, it looks for Option 43 in the server response to identify the controller addresses to join.","images":[],"sourcePage":65,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0150","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nA network engineer is configuring OSPF between router R1 and router R2. The engineer must ensure that a DR/BDR election does not occur on the Gigabit Ethernet interfaces in area 0.\nWhich configuration set accomplishes this goal?","options":{"A":"R1(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R1(config-if)ip ospf network point-to-point R2(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R2(config-if)ip ospf network point-to-point","B":"R1(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R1(config-if)ip ospf network broadcast R2(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R2(config-if)ip ospf network broadcast","C":"R1(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R1(config-if)ip ospf database-filter all out R2(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R2(config-if)ip ospf database-filter all out","D":"R1(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R1(config-if)ip ospf priority 1 R2(config-if)interface Gi0/0 R2(config-if)ip ospf priority 1"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Configuring the OSPF network type as point-to-point on both Gigabit Ethernet interfaces on R1 and R2 prevents the election of a Designated Router (DR) and Backup Designated Router (BDR). The point-to-point network type assumes that there are only two OSPF routers on the link, which eliminates the need for DR/BDR elections and simplifies the OSPF adjacency process. This configuration is suitable for directly connected router links, as illustrated in the exhibit. Thus, the configuration set that accomplishes the goal is the one where OSPF network type is set to point-to-point on both interfaces.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0150_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":66,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0151","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich type of antenna does the radiation pattern represent?","options":{"A":"multidirectional","B":"directional patch","C":"omnidirectional","D":"Yagi"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The antenna 3D radiation pattern in the exhibit represents an omnidirectional antenna. This is observed from the symmetrical radiation pattern when viewed in three dimensions, suggesting that the antenna radiates equally well in all directions around its axis, typical of omnidirectional antennas.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0151_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":68,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0152","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Wireless users report frequent disconnections from the wireless network. While troubleshooting, a network engineer finds that after the user is disconnected, the connection re-establishes automatically without any input required. The engineer also notices these message logs:\nAP 'AP2' is down. Reason: Radio channel set. 6:54:04 PM\nAP 'AP4' is down. Reason: Radio channel set. 6:44:49 PM\nAP 'AP7' is down. Reason: Radio channel set. 6:34:32 PM\nWhich action reduces the user impact?","options":{"A":"enable coverage hole detection","B":"increase the AP heartbeat timeout","C":"enable BandSelect","D":"increase the dynamic channel assignment interval"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The frequent disconnections are due to the access points resetting their radio channels, as indicated by the logs showing 'radio channel set' events. Increasing the dynamic channel assignment (DCA) interval would decrease the frequency of these channel-switching events, thereby reducing the disconnections experienced by users.","images":[],"sourcePage":69,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0153","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nThe inside and outside interfaces in the NAT configuration of this device have been correctly identified.\nWhat is the effect of this configuration?","options":{"A":"NAT64","B":"dynamic NAT","C":"static NAT","D":"PAT"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The configuration shown involves the use of the 'overload' keyword in the NAT statement. This indicates Port Address Translation (PAT), which is a type of network address translation where multiple devices on a local network are mapped to a single public IP address but with different port numbers. This allows multiple devices to share a single public IP address for accessing external networks, typically the internet. Therefore, the correct answer is PAT.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0153_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":70,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0154","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the descriptions from the left onto the routing protocol they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0154_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0154_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":71,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0155","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the purpose of an RP in PIM?","options":{"A":"send join messages toward a multicast source SPT","B":"ensure the shortest path from the multicast source to the receiver","C":"receive IGMP joins from multicast receivers","D":"secure the communication channel between the multicast sender and receiver"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The purpose of a Rendezvous Point (RP) in Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) is to facilitate the joining of multicast group members with the multicast source. The RP sends join messages toward the multicast source, helping to create the Shortest Path Tree (SPT) for efficient distribution of multicast traffic. This role does not involve receiving IGMP joins from receivers, ensuring the shortest path itself, or securing the communication channel specifically.","images":[],"sourcePage":72,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0156","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command must be applied to R2 for an OSPF neighborship to form?","options":{"A":"network 20.1.1.2 255.255.0.0 area 0","B":"network 20.1.1.2 0.0.0.0 area 0","C":"network 20.1.1.2 255.255.255.255 area 0","D":"network 20.1.1.2 0.0.255.255 area 0"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct command to apply to R2 for an OSPF neighborship to form is 'network 20.1.1.2 0.0.0.0 area 0'. This command specifies an exact match for the individual IP address 20.1.1.2 with the most specific wildcard mask 0.0.0.0, ensuring that OSPF is enabled on the specific interface with that IP address. This precise method aligns with best practices in network configuration and security, as it avoids unnecessarily broad network advertisements.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0156_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":73,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0157","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which router is the designated router on the segment 192.168.0.0/24?","options":{"A":"This segment has no designated router because it is a p2p network type.","B":"Router Chicago because it has a lower router ID.","C":"Router NewYork because it has a higher router ID.","D":"This segment has no designated router because it is a nonbroadcast network type."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The segment between NewYork and Chicago is a point-to-point (P2P) network type, as indicated by the 'show ip ospf interface' output for the Serial0/0 interface on the Chicago router. In OSPF, P2P network types do not have elections for designated routers (DR) or backup designated routers (BDR). Therefore, this segment does not have a designated router.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0157_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":75,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0158","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which two commands are needed to allow for full reachability between AS 1000 and AS 2000? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"R2#no network 10.0.0.0 255.255.255.0","B":"R2#network 209.165.201.0 mask 255.255.192.0","C":"R2#network 192.168.0.0 mask 255.255.0.0","D":"R1#no network 10.0.0.0 255.255.255.0","E":"R1#network 192.168.0.0 mask 255.255.0.0"},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"To allow for full reachability between AS 1000 and AS 2000, R2 needs to remove the incorrect advertisement of the 10.0.0.0/24 network, as it actually belongs to AS 1000 and should not be announced by R2. The command 'no network 10.0.0.0 255.255.255.0' on R2 accomplishes this. Additionally, R2 should advertise its own network, which is 192.168.0.0/16, to make it known to AS 1000. The command 'network 192.168.0.0 mask 255.255.0.0' on R2 accomplishes this.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0158_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":77,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0159","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer attempts to configure a router on a stick to route packets between Clients, Servers, and Printers; however, initial tests show that this configuration is not working. Which command set resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"interface Vlan10 no ip vrf forwarding Clients ! interface Vlan20 no ip vrf forwarding Servers ! interface Vlan30 no ip vrf forwarding Printers","B":"router eigrp 1 network 10.0.0.0 255.255.255.0 network 172.16.0.0 255.255.255.0 network 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0","C":"interface Vlan10 no ip vrf forwarding Clients ip address 192.168.1.1. 255.255.255.0 ! interface Vlan20 no ip vrf forwarding Servers ip address 172.16.1.1 255.255.255.0 ! interface Vlan30 no ip vrf forwarding Printers ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0","D":"router eigrp 1 network 10.0.0.0 255.0.0.0 network 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0 network 192.168.1.0 255.255.0.0"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The problem in the current configuration is the use of Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF) which isolates the routing of different VLANs, preventing communication between them. To allow routing between the VLANs for Clients, Servers, and Printers, the VRF configuration needs to be removed, and the interfaces need to be reconfigured with their respective IP addresses. Hence, the correct command set is to remove the VRF settings and then reassign the IP addresses to each VLAN interface.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0159_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":79,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0160","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"How does SSO work with HSRP to minimize network disruptions?","options":{"A":"It enables HSRP to elect another switch in the group as the active HSRP switch.","B":"It ensures fast failover in the case of link failure.","C":"It enables data forwarding along known routes following a switchover, while the routing protocol reconverges.","D":"It enables HSRP to failover to the standby RP on the same device."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"SSO (Stateful Switchover) works with HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) by enabling failover to the standby Route Processor (RP) on the same device. This ensures that in the event the active RP fails, the standby RP can immediately take over, maintaining the HSRP state without disrupting network traffic. This makes sure that network services continue seamlessly during a switchover.","images":[],"sourcePage":80,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0161","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command when applied to the Atlanta router reduces type 3 LSA flooding into the backbone area and summarizes the inter-area routes on the Dallas router?","options":{"A":"Atlanta(config-router)#area 0 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.252.0","B":"Atlanta(config-router)#area 1 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.248.0","C":"Atlanta(config-router)#area 0 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.248.0","D":"Atlanta(config-router)#area 1 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.252.0"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In OSPF, route summarization between areas can be done on an Area Border Router (ABR) and involves creating summary routes to reduce LSA flooding. In this case, the Atlanta router is the ABR connecting OSPF Area 1 with the backbone Area 0. To effectively summarize the routes and reduce type 3 LSA flooding into the backbone area, the appropriate command should summarize the range of subnets from 192.168.0.0 through 192.168.3.0, which are all /24 subnets within Area 1. The command \"area 1 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.252.0\" on the Atlanta router will create a summary route encompassing these subnets, thus the correct command is \"Atlanta(config-router)#area 1 range 192.168.0.0 255.255.252.0\".","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0161_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":81,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0162","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure interface GigabitEthernet0/0 for VRRP group 10. When the router has the highest priority in the group, it must assume the master role.\nWhich command set must be added to the initial configuration to accomplish this task?\nInitial Configuration -interface GigabitEthernet0/0description To IDF A 38-24-044.40ip address 172.16.13.2 255.255.255.0","options":{"A":"standby 10 ip 172.16.13.254 255.255.255.0 standby 10 preempt","B":"vrrp group 10 ip 172.16.13.254 255.255.255.0 vrrp group 10 priority","C":"standby 10 ip 172.16.13.254 standy 10 priority 120","D":"vrrp 10 ip 172.16.13.254 vrrp 10 preempt"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To configure interface GigabitEthernet0/0 for VRRP group 10, ensuring that the router assumes the master role when it has the highest priority, the correct set of commands is 'vrrp 10 ip 172.16.13.254' followed by 'vrrp 10 preempt'. The 'vrrp' command is used for VRRP configurations, not 'standby', which is used for HSRP. Specifying 'preempt' ensures that the router will take over as master when it has the highest priority.","images":[],"sourcePage":82,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0163","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the routing protocols they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0163_question.png"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0163_answer.png"}],"sourcePage":83,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0164","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which two commands ensure that DSW1 becomes the root bridge for VLAN 10 and 20? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"spanning-tree mst 1 priority 4096","B":"spanning-tree mst 1 root primary","C":"spanning-tree mst vlan 10,20 priority root","D":"spanning-tree mst 1 priority 1","E":"spanning-tree mstp vlan 10,20 root primary"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"To ensure that DSW1 becomes the root bridge for VLAN 10 and 20 under MST (Multiple Spanning Tree), two commands are appropriate. The command 'spanning-tree mst 1 priority 4096' manually sets the bridge priority to 4096, which is a lower value than the default and increases its chance to be the root bridge. Additionally, the 'spanning-tree mst 1 root primary' command dynamically adjusts the priority to ensure DSW1 becomes the root if another switch has a lower bridge priority. Using these two commands together appropriately adjusts the bridge priority and designation for the root role in the spanning tree.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0164_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":85,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0165","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. R1 is able to ping the R3 fa0/1 interface. Why do the extended pings fail?","options":{"A":"The maximum packet size accepted by the command is 1476 bytes.","B":"The DF bit has been set.","C":"R3 is missing a return route to 10.99.69.0/30.","D":"R2 and R3 do not have an OSPF adjacency."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The extended pings fail because the DF (Don't Fragment) bit has been set. When the DF bit is set, it prevents the packet from being fragmented. In this case, the MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) on one of the intermediary links is likely smaller (1492 bytes) than the packet size being sent (1500 bytes). Since the packets cannot be fragmented and must traverse a link with a smaller MTU, they are discarded, resulting in a failure. To resolve this issue, the DF bit should not be set, or a smaller packet size should be used that fits within the MTU of all links.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0165_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":87,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0166","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command allows hosts that are connected to FastEthernet0/2 to access the Internet?","options":{"A":"ip nat inside source list 10 interface FastEthernet0/2 overload","B":"ip nat inside source list 10 interface FastEthernet0/1 overload","C":"ip nat outside source static 209.165.200.225 10.10.10.0 overload","D":"ip nat outside source list 10 interface FastEthernet0/2 overload"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure NAT (Network Address Translation) for allowing hosts connected to FastEthernet0/2 to access the Internet, the command 'ip nat inside source list 10 interface FastEthernet0/1 overload' is used. This command tells the router to use the access list 10, which permits traffic from the 10.10.10.0 network, and to translate this traffic as it exits through the FastEthernet0/1 interface (which is designated as the 'outside' interface) using PAT (Port Address Translation) for overload. Option B correctly implements this configuration.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0166_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":89,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0167","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What are two effects of this configuration? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"If R1 goes down, R2 becomes active but reverts to standby when R1 comes back online.","B":"If R2 goes down, R1 becomes active but reverts to standby when R2 comes back online.","C":"R1 becomes the active router.","D":"R1 becomes the standby router.","E":"If R1 goes down, R2 becomes active and remains the active device when R1 comes back online."},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"R1 becomes the active router because it has the highest priority (120) compared to R2's priority (110). Therefore, R1 will initially assume the active role. Without the preempt command configured, if R1 goes down, R2 will take over as the active router and will remain in that role even when R1 comes back online, since preemption is not enabled by default.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0167_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":90,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0168","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must ensure that all traffic entering AS 200 from AS 100 chooses Link 2 as an entry point. Assume that all BGP neighbor relationships have been formed and that the attributes have not been changed on any of the routers. Which configuration accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"R3(config)#route-map PREPEND permit 10 R3(config-route-map)#set as-path prepend 200 200 200 R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3#(config-router)#neighbor 10.1.1.1 route-map PREPEND out","B":"R4(config)#route-map PREPEND permit 10 R4(config-route-map)#set as-path prepend 100 100 100 R4(config)#router bgp 200 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.2.2.2 route-map PREPEND in","C":"R4(config)#route-map PREPEND permit 10 R4(config-route-map)#set as-path prepend 200 200 200 R4(config)#router bgp 200 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.2.2.2 route-map PREPEND out","D":"R3(config)#route-map PREPEND permit 10 R3(config-route-map)#set as-path prepend 100 100 100 R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.1.1.1 route-map PREPEND in"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To ensure that all traffic entering AS 200 from AS 100 chooses Link 2 as an entry point, AS path prepending can be used to make Link 1 less preferred. This can be achieved by increasing the AS path length for routes advertised from R4 to R2. By configuring route-map PREPEND on R4 and setting the AS path to prepend extra instances of AS 200, Link 2 will be preferred since it has a shorter AS path. Therefore, the correct answer is to configure the route-map on R4 and apply it to the neighbor relationship with R2, the correct configuration being C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0168_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":92,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0169","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which DHCP option provides the CAPWAP APs with the address of the wireless controller(s)?","options":{"A":"43","B":"66","C":"69","D":"150"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"DHCP option 43 is used to provide CAPWAP (Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) access points with the IP address of the wireless controller(s). It is a common method used in networking to ensure that wireless APs can locate and communicate with their corresponding controllers for proper management and operation.","images":[],"sourcePage":94,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0170","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich two commands ensure that DSW1 becomes root bridge for VLAN 10? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"DSW1(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 4096","B":"DSW1(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 priority root","C":"DSW2(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 61440","D":"DSW1(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 port-priority 0","E":"DSW2(config)#spanning-tree vlan 20 priority 0"},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"To ensure that DSW1 becomes the root bridge for VLAN 10, you need to set its priority to a lower value and/or increase the priority of the other switches. The command 'DSW1(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 4096' sets the priority of DSW1 to 4096, which is a lower value than the current priority values of other switches. Additionally, the command 'DSW2(config)#spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 61440' configures a high priority value for DSW2, ensuring DSW1 has the lowest priority. The other command options do not appropriately set or change the priority to achieve this outcome.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0170_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":95,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0171","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"What mechanism does PIM use to forward multicast traffic?","options":{"A":"PIM sparse mode uses a pull model to deliver multicast traffic.","B":"PIM dense mode uses a pull model to deliver multicast traffic.","C":"PIM sparse mode uses receivers to register with the RP.","D":"PIM sparse mode uses a flood and prune model to deliver multicast traffic."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"PIM sparse mode uses a pull model to deliver multicast traffic. In this mode, multicast data is only forwarded to network segments that have requested it, thus avoiding unnecessary flooding of the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":96,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0172","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nThe traceroute fails from R1 to R3.\nWhat is the cause of the failure?","options":{"A":"An ACL applied inbound on loopback0 of R2 is dropping the traffic.","B":"The loopback on R3 is in a shutdown state.","C":"Redistribution of connected routes into OSPF is not configured.","D":"An ACL applied inbound on fa0/1 of R3 is dropping the traffic."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The traceroute output shows the character sequence \"!A\" next to the IP address of 10.99.69.6. The \"!A\" indicates that the traffic has been administratively prohibited, which typically means that an Access Control List (ACL) is blocking the traffic. Given that 10.99.69.6 is the IP address of interface fa0/1 on R3, this suggests that an ACL applied inbound on fa0/1 of R3 is dropping the traffic. Therefore, the cause of the traceroute failure is an ACL applied inbound on fa0/1 of R3.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0172_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":97,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0173","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must establish eBGP peering between router R3 and router R4. Both routers should use their loopback interfaces as the BGP router ID.\nWhich configuration set accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.4.4.4 remote-as 100 R3(config-router)# neighbor 10.4.4.4 update-source Loopback0 R4(config)#router bgp 100 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.3.3.3 remote-as 200 R4(config-router)#network 10.3.3.3 update-source Loopback0","B":"R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.4 remote-as 100 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.4 update-source Loopback0 R4(config)#router bgp 100 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.3 remote-as 200 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.3 update-source Loopback0","C":"R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.4.4.4 remote-as 100 R3(config-router)#bgp router-id 10.3.3.3 R4(config)#router bgp 100 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.3.3.3 remote-as 200 R4(config-router)#bgp router-id 10.4.4.4","D":"R3(config)#router bgp 200 R3(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.4 remote-as 100 R3(config-router)#bgp router-id 10.3.3.3 R4(config)#router bgp 100 R4(config-router)#neighbor 10.24.24.3 remote-as 200 R4(config-router)#bgp router-id 10.4.4.4"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To establish eBGP peering between R3 and R4 using their loopback interfaces as the BGP router ID, the correct configuration involves using the 10.24.24.0/24 subnet that connects the two routers directly for the neighbor statements and setting the router IDs to match the loopback interfaces. Configuration D does this correctly by specifying the neighbor IPs as 10.24.24.4 and 10.24.24.3 and setting the BGP router ID to the loopback interfaces on both routers, fulfilling the requirement stated in the question.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0173_exhibit.png"}],"sourcePage":99,"flags":["Question depends on an exhibit image (see images)."]},{"id":"q0174","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is configuring GigabitEthernet1/0/0 for VRRP. When the router has the highest priority in group 5, it must assume the master role.\nWhich command set should the engineer add to the configuration to accomplish this task?\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0 description To IDF A 38-70-774-10 ip address 172.16.13.2 255.255.255.0","options":{"A":"standby 5 ip 172.16.13.254 standby 5 priority 100 standby 5 track 1 decrement 10","B":"standby 5 ip 172.16.13.254 standby 5 priority 100 standby 5 preempt","C":"vrrp 5 ip 172.16.13.254 vrrp 5 priority 100","D":"vrrp 5 ip 172.16.13.254 255.255.255.0 vrrp 5 track 1 decrement 10 vrrp 5 preempt"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To configure GigabitEthernet1/0/0 for VRRP and ensure that the router assumes the master role when it has the highest priority in group 5, the correct command set involves defining the VRRP group, setting the virtual IP address, and optionally setting the priority. Since VRRP preemption is enabled by default, there is no need to explicitly include a preempt command. The default priority for VRRP is 100, which is sufficient unless other routers in the group have higher priorities set explicitly. Therefore, the correct command set is to use the 'vrrp 5 ip 172.16.13.254' and 'vrrp 5 priority 100' commands.","images":[],"sourcePage":201,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0175","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which two security features are available when implementing NTP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"encrypted authentication mechanism","B":"symmetric server passwords","C":"clock offset authentication","D":"broadcast association mode","E":"access list-based restriction scheme"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"Two security features available when implementing NTP are an encrypted authentication mechanism and an access list-based restriction scheme. The encrypted authentication mechanism ensures that the time information exchanged between NTP servers and clients is authenticated and not tampered with. The access list-based restriction scheme allows administrators to restrict which devices can communicate with the NTP server, providing an additional layer of security by controlling access.","images":[],"sourcePage":202,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0176","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"mcq","question":"How does the EIGRP metric differ from the OSPF metric?","options":{"A":"The EIGRP metric is calculated based on bandwidth only. The OSPF metric is calculated on delay only.","B":"The EIGRP metric is calculated based on delay only. The OSPF metric is calculated on bandwidth and delay.","C":"The EIGRP metric is calculated based on bandwidth and delay. The OSPF metric is calculated on bandwidth only.","D":"The EIGRP metric is calculated based on hop count and bandwidth. The OSPF metric is calculated on bandwidth and delay."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The EIGRP metric is calculated based on bandwidth and delay. OSPF, on the other hand, uses a cost metric derived from the interface bandwidth. Therefore, the EIGRP metric incorporates both bandwidth and delay, while OSPF bases its metric solely on bandwidth.","images":[],"sourcePage":203,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0177","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer is installing a new pair of routers in a redundant configuration.\nWhich protocol ensures that traffic is not disrupted in the event of a hardware failure?","options":{"A":"HSRPv1","B":"GLBP","C":"VRRP","D":"HSRPv2"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The protocol that ensures traffic is not disrupted in the event of a hardware failure, as shown in the exhibit, is HSRPv1. The virtual MAC address in the exhibit (0000.0c07.acXX) corresponds to HSRPv1. HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) provides high network availability by routing IP traffic from hosts on Ethernet, FDDI, or Token Ring networks without relying on the availability of any single router. In the configuration shown in the exhibit, both routers are active, which is a standard redundancy setup for HSRP to handle failover scenarios. Therefore, based on the given exhibit, HSRPv1 is the correct protocol.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0177_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":204,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0178","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After an engineer configures an EtherChannel between switch SW1 and switch SW2, this error message is logged on switch SW2:\nSW2#\n09:45:32: %PM-4-ERR_DISABLE: channel-misconfig error detected on Gi0/0, putting Gi0/0 in err-disable state 09:45:32: %PM-4-ERR_DISABLE: channel-misconfig error detected on Gi0/1, putting Gi0/1 in err-disable state\nBased on the output from switch SW1 and the log message received on switch SW2, what action should the engineer take to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"Configure the same protocol on the EtherChannel on switch SW1 and SW2.","B":"Define the correct port members on the EtherChannel on switch SW1.","C":"Correct the configuration error on Interface Gi0/0 on switch SW1.","D":"Correct the configuration error on Interface Gi0/1 on switch SW1."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The error message and the EtherChannel summary on SW1 indicate that while the Port Channel (Po1) exists, no port members have been configured for it. This lack of member ports is causing SW2 to place interfaces Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 in an error-disabled state due to channel misconfiguration. To resolve this, the engineer should define the correct port members on the EtherChannel on switch SW1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0178_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":205,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0179","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Communication between London and New York is down. Which command set must be applied to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"NewYork(config)#int f0/1 NewYork(config)#switchport nonegotiate NewYork(config)#end\nNewYork#","B":"NewYork(config)#int f0/1 NewYork(config)#switchport mode trunk NewYork(config)#end\nNewYork#","C":"NewYork(config)#int f0/1 NewYork(config)#switchport trunk encap dot1q\nNewYork(config)#end NewYork#","D":"NewYork(config)#int f0/1 NewYork(config)#switchport mode dynamic desirable\nNewYork(config)#end NewYork#"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The issue is that the trunk can’t form due to a mismatch in encapsulation protocols. The exhibit shows that New York is set to use ISL encapsulation by default, while ports that need to communicate must use the same encapsulation protocol. London is configured to use dot1q encapsulation.\n\nTherefore, to address the mismatch, you must change the encapsulation method on New York to dot1q. This is achieved by the command set outlined in option C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0179_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":207,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0180","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which encryption hashing algorithm does NTP use for authentication?","options":{"A":"SSL","B":"MD5","C":"AES128","D":"AES256"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"NTP (Network Time Protocol) uses MD5 for authentication. MD5 is a hashing algorithm, whereas the other options (SSL, AES128, AES256) are encryption methods and not specifically designed for hashing used in NTP authentication.","images":[],"sourcePage":208,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0181","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The trunk between Gig1/0/1 of switch SW2 and Gig1/0/1 of switch SW1 is not operational. Which action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"Configure both interfaces to nonegotiate and ensure that the switches are in different VTP domains.","B":"Configure both interfaces in dynamic auto DTP mode and ensure that the switches are in the same VTP domain.","C":"Configure both interfaces in dynamic auto DTP mode and ensure that the switches are in different VTP domains.","D":"Configure both interfaces in dynamic desirable DTP mode and ensure that the switches are in the same VTP domain."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The current configuration shows both interfaces in dynamic auto DTP mode, where neither interface actively attempts to convert to a trunk. For a trunk to be formed, at least one side needs to be set to dynamic desirable DTP mode, which actively attempts to negotiate a trunk if the neighboring interface agrees. Therefore, configuring both interfaces in dynamic desirable DTP mode and ensuring they are in the same VTP domain will resolve the issue by actively forming a trunk link between the switches.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0181_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":209,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0182","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which two facts does the device output confirm? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The device's HSRP group uses the virtual IP address 10.0.3.242.","B":"The device is configured with the default HSRP priority.","C":"The device sends unicast messages to its peers.","D":"The standby device is configured with the default HSRP priority.","E":"The device is using the default HSRP hello timer."},"correctAnswer":["D","E"],"explanation":"The standby device is configured with the default HSRP priority, as indicated by the priority 100. The hold and hello times mentioned in the output are 10 seconds and 3 seconds respectively, aligning with the default HSRP values.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0182_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":211,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0183","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which type of antenna is shown on the radiation patterns?","options":{"A":"patch","B":"dipole","C":"omnidirectional","D":"Yagi"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The given radiation patterns suggest that the antenna is omnidirectional. The diagram shows typical radiation patterns for an omnidirectional antenna. While a dipole antenna is a specific type of omnidirectional antenna, the general pattern shown in the exhibit represents how omnidirectional antennas radiate. Omnidirectional antennas radiate equally in all directions in a plane, matching the circular pattern seen in the images.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0183_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":212,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0184","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the descriptions from the left onto the routing protocol they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0184_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0184_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":213,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0185","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. All switches are configured with the default port priority value. Which two commands ensure that traffic from PC1 is forwarded over the Gi1/3 trunk port between DSW1 and DSW2? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"DSW2(config)#interface gi1/3","B":"DSW1(config-if)#spanning-tree port-priority 0","C":"DSW2(config-if)#spanning-tree port-priority 128","D":"DSW1(config)#interface gi1/3","E":"DSW2(config-if)#spanning-tree port-priority 16"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"To ensure that traffic from PC1 is forwarded over the Gi1/3 trunk port between DSW1 and DSW2, you need to configure the port priority on DSW2. Since all switches are configured with the default port priority value, you need to set a lower port priority on the relevant interface of DSW2 to influence the spanning-tree path selection. Port priority is configured in increments of 16, and the default value is 128. Setting the port priority to a lower value on DSW2 will make its interface more preferred. Therefore, you should open the interface configuration on DSW2 and set the port priority to a lower value, such as 16.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0185_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":215,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0186","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A company has an existing Cisco 5520 HA cluster using SSO. An engineer deploys a new single Cisco Catalyst 9800 WLC to test new features. The engineer successfully configures a mobility tunnel between the 5520 cluster and 9800 WLC. Clients connected to the corporate WLAN roam seamlessly between access points on the 5520 and 9800 WLC. After a failure on the primary 5520 WLC, all WLAN services remain functional; however, clients cannot roam between the 5520 and 9800 controllers without dropping their connection. Which feature must be configured to remedy the issue?","options":{"A":"mobility MAC on the 5520 cluster","B":"mobility MAC on the 9800 WLC","C":"new mobility on the 5520 cluster","D":"new mobility on the 9800 WLC"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco wireless network with high availability (HA) using Stateful Switchover (SSO), each controller in an HA pair shares a mobility MAC address to ensure seamless roaming and communication. When the primary 5520 WLC fails and the standby takes over, the mobility MAC address should remain consistent. However, if the 9800 WLC is not configured with the mobility MAC address of the HA pair, it will not recognize the mobility peer, causing roaming issues. Therefore, configuring the mobility MAC on the 9800 WLC ensures that the mobility tunnel remains intact, enabling seamless client roaming between the 5520 cluster and the 9800 WLC.","images":[],"sourcePage":217,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0187","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration change ensures that R1 is the active gateway whenever it is in a functional state for the 172.30.110.0/24 network?","options":{"A":"R2 standby 1 priority 90 standby 1 preempt","B":"R2 standby 1 priority 100 standby 1 preempt","C":"R1 standby 1 preempt R2 standby 1 priority 90","D":"R1 standby 1 preempt R2 standby 1 priority 100"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To ensure that R1 is always the active gateway when it is functional for the 172.30.110.0/24 network, R1 should be configured with the 'preempt' command. This allows R1 to take over as the active router whenever it is available. Additionally, R2 should have a lower priority than R1, so in this case, setting R2's priority to 90 ensures that R1 will take precedence as the active gateway when both routers are functional. The correct configuration is 'R1 standby 1 preempt' and 'R2 standby 1 priority 90'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0187_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":218,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0188","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"A customer has completed the installation of a Wi-Fi 6 greenfield deployment at their new campus. They want to leverage Wi-Fi 6 enhanced speeds on the trusted employee WLAN. To configure the employee WLAN, which two Layer 2 security policies should be used? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"WPA2 (AES)","B":"802.1X","C":"OPEN","D":"WEP","E":"WPA (AES)"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"To configure the employee WLAN in a Wi-Fi 6 environment, WPA2 (AES) and 802.1X should be used. WPA2 (AES) provides robust encryption and is supported in Wi-Fi 6, ensuring a secure connection. 802.1X is an authentication framework that provides an additional layer of security by requiring devices to authenticate before accessing the network. These two options together enhance security and performance, making them suitable for a trusted employee WLAN.","images":[],"sourcePage":219,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0189","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two actions, when applied in the LAN network segment, will facilitate Layer 3 CAPWAP discovery for lightweight AP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Utilize DHCP option 43.","B":"Utilize DHCP option 17.","C":"Configure an ip helper-address on the router interface.","D":"Enable port security on the switch port.","E":"Configure WLC IP address on LAN switch"},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"Utilizing DHCP option 43 is one method to facilitate Layer 3 CAPWAP discovery for lightweight access points as it allows the AP to receive the IP address of the Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) via DHCP. Configuring an IP helper-address on the router interface helps in forwarding DHCP requests from clients in different subnets to the DHCP server, which is essential for proper AP-to-WLC communication in larger networks with multiple subnets.","images":[],"sourcePage":220,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0190","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which action resolves the EtherChannel issue between SW2 and SW3?","options":{"A":"Configure switchport mode trunk on SW2.","B":"Configure switchport nonegotiate on SW3.","C":"Configure channel-group 1 mode desirable on both interfaces.","D":"Configure channel-group 1 mode active on both interfaces."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To resolve the EtherChannel issue between SW2 and SW3, it is necessary to configure both switches to use the same negotiation protocol. SW2 is currently configured to use PAgP (Port Aggregation Protocol), a Cisco proprietary protocol. To align with this, the correct action is to set SW3 to use PAgP as well. By configuring channel-group 1 mode desirable on both interfaces, we enable PAgP on SW3, ensuring compatibility and allowing the EtherChannel to function correctly. Simply, desirable mode on SW3 matches the PAgP configuration of SW2, resolving the issue. Therefore, the answer is to configure channel-group 1 mode desirable on both interfaces, which aligns with option C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0190_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":221,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0191","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer configures OSPF and reviews the router configuration. Which interface or interfaces are able to establish OSPF adjacency?","options":{"A":"GigabitEthernet0/0 and GigabitEthernet0/1","B":"only GigabitEthernet0/1","C":"only GigabitEthernet0/0","D":"GigabitEthernet0/1 and GigabitEthernet0/1.40"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The interface or interfaces able to establish OSPF adjacency are those that are not configured as passive. Passive interfaces do not send OSPF hello packets, which are essential for forming OSPF adjacencies. According to the output, GigabitEthernet0/1 and GigabitEthernet0/1.40 are explicitly marked as passive interfaces, indicated by 'No Hellos (Passive interface).' Therefore, they cannot form OSPF adjacencies. The only interface that can establish OSPF adjacency is GigabitEthernet0/0, which is not in passive mode and thus can send and receive OSPF hello packets.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0191_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":223,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0192","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the DHCP messages that are exchanged between a client and an AP into the order they are exchanged on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0192_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0192_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":225,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0193","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two methods are used by an AP that is trying to discover a wireless LAN controller? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Cisco Discovery Protocol neighbor","B":"querying other APs","C":"DHCP Option 43","D":"broadcasting on the local subnet","E":"DNS lookup CISCO-DNA-PRIMARY.localdomain"},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"An Access Point (AP) can discover a Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) through two primary methods: DHCP Option 43 and broadcasting on the local subnet. Using DHCP Option 43, the AP gets the IP address of the WLC when it requests an IP address from the DHCP server. Broadcasting on the local subnet allows the AP to send a broadcast message to all devices on the subnet to identify available WLCs. These methods ensure that the AP can find and connect to the appropriate WLC efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":226,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0194","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What is the responsibility of a secondary WLC?","options":{"A":"It enables Layer 2 and Layer 3 roaming between itself and the primary controller.","B":"It registers the LAPs if the primary controller fails.","C":"It avoids congestion on the primary controller by sharing the registration load on the LAPs.","D":"It shares the traffic load of the LAPs with the primary controller."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A secondary Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) is responsible for registering the Lightweight Access Points (LAPs) if the primary controller fails. This ensures network continuity by providing a backup controller that can take over in the event of a primary controller failure, keeping the network operational.","images":[],"sourcePage":227,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0195","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nBased on the configuration in this WLAN security setting, which method can a client use to authenticate to the network?","options":{"A":"text string","B":"username and password","C":"RADIUS token","D":"certificate"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The configuration shows the PSK (Pre-Shared Key) authentication method is enabled. PSK generally refers to a text string that users must input to join the network. This indicates that a client can authenticate using a text string.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0195_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":228,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0196","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A client device fails to see the enterprise SSID, but other client devices are connected to it.\nWhat is the cause of this issue?","options":{"A":"The client has incorrect credentials stored for the configured broadcast SSID.","B":"The hidden SSID was not manually configured on the client.","C":"The broadcast SSID was not manually configured on the client.","D":"The client has incorrect credentials stored for the configured hidden SSID."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"If a client device fails to see the enterprise SSID while other devices are connected to it, the most likely cause is that the broadcast SSID has not been manually configured on that client. The broadcast SSID is visible and should automatically appear in the network list if it is being transmitted. If the SSID does not appear, it indicates that the client device may not have been set up to connect to that particular broadcast SSID.","images":[],"sourcePage":229,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0197","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two descriptions of FlexConnect mode for Cisco APs are true? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"APs that operate in FlexConnect mode cannot detect rogue APs.","B":"When connected to the controller, FlexConnect APs can tunnel traffic back to the controller.","C":"FlexConnect mode is used when the APs are set up in a mesh environment and used to bridge between each other.","D":"FlexConnect mode is a feature that is designed to allow specified CAPWAP-enabled APs to exclude themselves from managing data traffic between clients and infrastructure.","E":"FlexConnect mode is a wireless solution for branch office and remote office deployments."},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"FlexConnect mode is a wireless solution for branch office and remote office deployments. It allows APs to be centrally managed from the corporate office through a WAN link, without the need for a controller at each remote site. When connected to the controller, FlexConnect APs have the capability to tunnel traffic back to the controller. Additionally, FlexConnect APs can operate in a local switching mode where client data traffic is handled locally, which is ideal for locations with unreliable or slow connectivity to the central controller.","images":[],"sourcePage":230,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0198","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. How was spanning-tree configured on this interface?","options":{"A":"By entering the command spanning-tree portfast trunk in the interface configuration mode.","B":"By entering the command spanning-tree mst1 vlan 10,20,30,40 in the global configuration mode.","C":"By entering the command spanning-tree portfast in the interface configuration mode.","D":"By entering the command spanning-tree vlan 10,20,30,40 root primary in the interface configuration mode."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The exhibit shows that the port carries multiple VLANs, indicating that it is set up in trunk mode. Additionally, it is configured as an edge port, which is typically done to speed up the spanning-tree process for ports connected directly to end devices. The command used to enable PortFast on trunk ports is 'spanning-tree portfast trunk'. This configuration allows the interface to handle multiple VLANs as a trunk while also enabling PortFast to minimize the time the port spends in the spanning-tree listening and learning states. Therefore, the correct answer is achieved by entering the command 'spanning-tree portfast trunk' in the interface configuration mode.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0198_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":231,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0199","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Rapid PVST+ is enabled on all switches. Which command set must be configured on Switch1 to achieve the following results on port fa0/1?\nWhen a device is connected, the port transitions immediately to a forwarding state. The interface should not send or receive BPDUs. If a BPDU is received, it continues operating normally.","options":{"A":"Switch1(config)# spanning-tree portfast bpdufilter default Switch1(config)# interface f0/1 Switch1(config-if)# spanning-tree portfast","B":"Switch1(config)# spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default Switch 1 (config)# interface f0/1 Switch1 (config-if)# spanning-tree portfast","C":"Switch1(config)# interface f0/1 Switch1(config-if)# spanning-tree portfast","D":"Switch1(config)# interface f0/1 Switch1(config-if)# spanning-tree portfast Switch1 (config-if)# spanning-tree bpduguard enable"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The command set to achieve the desired results on Switch1 for port fa0/1 includes enabling both 'spanning-tree portfast' and 'spanning-tree portfast bpdufilter default'. The 'spanning-tree portfast' command ensures that the port transitions immediately to the forwarding state when a device is connected. The 'spanning-tree portfast bpdufilter default' command prevents the interface from sending or receiving BPDUs. If a BPDU is received, the interface continues to operate normally.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0199_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":232,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0200","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration change will force BR2 to reach 209.165.201.0/27 via BR1?","options":{"A":"Set the MED to 1 on PE2 toward BR2 outbound.","B":"Set the origin to igp on BR2 toward PE2 inbound.","C":"Set the weight attribute to 65,535 on BR1 toward PE1.","D":"Set the local preference to 150 on PE1 toward BR1 outbound."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To force BR2 to reach 209.165.201.0/27 via BR1, the appropriate action would be to manipulate the MED (Multi-Exit Discriminator) attribute. The MED is used to influence the inbound path selection for routes advertised by different autonomous systems. By setting the MED to 1 on PE2 towards BR2 outbound, BR2 will perceive the route through PE2 as having a higher MED value (which is less preferred compared to the default MED value of 0 from BR1). Therefore, BR2 will prefer the route with a lower MED value through BR1, ensuring that traffic to 209.165.201.0/27 is routed via BR1. Note that the 'always-compare-med' command may be necessary on BR2 to ensure the comparison of MEDs across different autonomous systems.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0200_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":234,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0201","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the protocols they apply to on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0201_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0201_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":236,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0202","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which set of commands on router R1 allow deterministic translation of private hosts PC1, PC2, and PC3 to addresses in the public space?","options":{"A":"RouterR1(config)#int f0/0 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#int f0/1 RouterR1(config)#ip nat outside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#access-list 1 10.10.10.0 0.0.0.255 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source list 1 interface f0/1 overload","B":"RouterR1(config)#int f0/0 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#int f0/1 RouterR1(config)#ip nat outside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#access-list 1 10.10.10.0 0.0.0.255 RouterR1(config)#ip nat pool POOL 155.1.1.101 155.1.1.103 netmask 255.255.255.0 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source list 1 pool POOL","C":"RouterR1(config)#int f0/0 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside\nRouterR1(config)#exit RouterR1(config)#int f0/1 RouterR1(config)#ip nat outside RouterR1(config)#exit RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.101 155.1.1.101 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.102 155.1.1.102 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.103 155.1.1.103","D":"RouterR1(config)#int f0/0 RouterR1(config)#ip nat outside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#int f0/1 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside RouterR1(config)#exit\nRouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.101 155.1.1.101 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.102 155.1.1.102 RouterR1(config)#ip nat inside source static 10.10.10.103 155.1.1.103"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To achieve deterministic NAT (Network Address Translation), where each private IP address is mapped to a specific public IP address consistently, static NAT must be used. The configuration includes specifying the interfaces as 'inside' and 'outside', and setting static translations for each private IP to its corresponding public IP. The correct commands for this setup are: specifying the inside interface with 'ip nat inside', the outside interface with 'ip nat outside', and the static mappings with 'ip nat inside source static ' for each host.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0202_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":237,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0203","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Communication between London and New York is down. Which command set must be applied to the NewYork switch to resolve the issue?","options":{"A":"NewYork(config)#no interface po1 NewYork(config)#interface range fa0/1-2 NewYork(config-if)#channel-group 1 mode negotiate NewYork(config-if)#end NewYork#","B":"NewYork(config)#no interface po1 NewYork(config)#interface range fa0/1-2 NewYork(config-if)#channel-group 1 mode on NewYork(config-if)#end NewYork#","C":"NewYork(config)#no interface po1 NewYork(config)#interface range fa0/1-2\nNewYork(config-if)#channel-group 1 mode passive NewYork(config-if)#end\nNewYork#","D":"NewYork(config)#no interface po1 NewYork(config)#interface range fa0/1-2 NewYork(config-if)#channel-group 1 mode auto NewYork(config-if)#end NewYork#"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The issue lies with the mismatch of etherchannel protocols on the London and New York switches. London is configured for Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) in active mode, which requires the other side to also be LACP. For the etherchannel to form, the New York switch should be set to LACP as well. Since London is in active mode, New York should be configured in either active or passive mode for LACP. Therefore, the correct command is to set the New York switch to channel-group 1 mode passive to match with London's active LACP configuration.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0203_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":239,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0204","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer is configuring an EtherChannel between Switch1 and Switch2 and notices the console message on Switch2. Based on the output, which action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"Configure more member ports on Switch1.","B":"Configure less member ports on Switch2.","C":"Configure the same port channel interface number on both switches.","D":"Configure the same EtherChannel protocol on both switches."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The issue in the EtherChannel configuration is due to a protocol mismatch. On Switch1, the EtherChannel is configured to use LACP (Link Aggregation Control Protocol), while on Switch2, no protocol is set, indicating that the EtherChannel is in 'on' mode. EtherChannel requires both sides to use the same protocol in order to establish the connection properly. Therefore, configuring the same EtherChannel protocol on both switches will resolve the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0204_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":241,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0205","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer configures NAT on R1 and enters the show command to verify the configuration. What does the output confirm?","options":{"A":"The first packet triggered NAT to add an entry to the NAT table.","B":"R1 is configured with NAT overload parameters.","C":"A Telnet session from 160.1.1.1 to 10.1.1.10 has been initiated.","D":"R1 is configured with PAT overload parameters."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The output from the command shows that NAT has been successfully configured, and one translation entry is active in the NAT table, indicating that the first packet has triggered the NAT process to create this entry. The configuration details show the mapping of internal addresses to an external address pool without the 'overload' keyword, which is required for PAT (Port Address Translation). The details also do not show evidence of a specific TCP session, such as a Telnet session. Therefore, the correct interpretation is that the first packet triggered NAT to add an entry to the NAT table.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0205_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":243,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0206","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What is the cause of the log messages?","options":{"A":"OSPF area change","B":"MTU mismatch","C":"IP address mismatch","D":"hello packet mismatch"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The cause of the log messages is an OSPF area change. The logs indicate that the interface from which the router is receiving packets is configured for a different OSPF area than expected. Specifically, the message '%OSPF-6-AREACHG' shows that the network 10.0.0.1/32 changed from area 0 to area 1, which is consistent with an OSPF area change. Mismatched area IDs between OSPF neighbors will prevent them from forming a proper adjacency, causing the neighbor down event. Therefore, the correct answer is an OSPF area change.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0206_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":245,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0207","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer configures BGP between R1 and R2. Both routers use BGP peer group CORP and are set up to use MD5 authentication. This message is logged to the console of router R1:\n`May 5 39:85:55.469: %TCP-6-BADAUTH` Invalid MD5 digest from 10.10.10.1 (29832) to 10.120.10.1\n(179) tebleid -0`\nWhich two configurations allow a peering session to form between R1 and R2? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"R1(config-router)#neighbor 10.10.10.1 peer-group CORP R1(config-router)#neighbor CORP password Cisco","B":"R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.120.10.1 peer-group CORP R2(config-router)#neighbor CORP password Cisco","C":"R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.10.10.1 peer-group CORP R2(config-router)#neighbor PEER password Cisco","D":"R1(config-router)#neighbor 10.120.10.1 peer-group CORP R1(config-router)#neighbor CORP password Cisco","E":"R2(config-router)#neighbor 10.10.10.1 peer-group CORP R2(config-router)#neighbor CORP password Cisco"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"To establish a BGP peering session with MD5 authentication, both routers must be configured with the same password for the peer group, and each router must specify the peer's IP address and associate it with the peer group. In this case, Router R1 is 10.120.10.1 and Router R2 is 10.10.10.1. Therefore, R1 should be configured with R2's IP address and the password, and R2 should be configured with R1's IP address and the password. This setup will ensure that the MD5 password match and the peering session can be established successfully.","images":[],"sourcePage":246,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","Part of the exhibit text in the question is garbled in the PDF text; kept as extracted."]},{"id":"q0208","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer implemented several configuration changes and receives the logging message on Switch1. Which action should the engineer take to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"Change Switch2 to switch port mode dynamic auto.","B":"Change the VTP domain to match on both switches.","C":"Change Switch1 to switch port mode dynamic auto.","D":"Change Switch1 to switch port mode dynamic desirable."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The engineer should change the VTP domain to match on both switches. Switch1 is configured with the VTP domain 'DATACENTER1' and Switch2 with the VTP domain 'DATACENTER2'. For trunk links to work properly, both switches must be in the same VTP domain. The discrepancy in VTP domains is causing the trunk negotiation to fail, leading to the error message on Switch1. Matching the VTP domains will resolve the issue and allow the trunk link to form correctly.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0208_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":247,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0209","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics of PIM Dense Mode from the left to the right. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0209_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0209_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":249,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0210","topic":"Wireless","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the wireless elements on the left to their definitions on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0210_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0210_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":250,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0211","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"How are the different versions of IGMP compatible?","options":{"A":"IGMPv2 is compatible only with IGMPv2.","B":"IGMPv3 is compatible only with IGMPv3.","C":"IGMPv2 is compatible only with IGMPv1.","D":"IGMPv3 is compatible only with IGMPv1"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"IGMPv2 is compatible only with IGMPv1 because IGMP versions are designed to ensure backward compatibility with the previous versions. IGMPv2 can understand and process the messages from IGMPv1, but not the other way around. This compatibility allows networks that are incrementally upgraded to IGMPv2 to still interoperate with devices running IGMPv1, ensuring a smooth transition.","images":[],"sourcePage":251,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0212","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which measurement is used from a post wireless survey to depict the cell edge of the access points?","options":{"A":"SNR","B":"Noise","C":"RSSI","D":"CCI"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI) is the measurement used to depict the cell edge of access points in a post wireless survey. RSSI measures the power level that a client device is receiving from an access point. It is commonly used to determine how strong the signal is at a given point and helps in mapping out how far the signal reliably extends from the access point. Measurement of RSSI allows for the visualization of signal strength across an area, thus identifying the boundary where the signal strength drops to a level considered the edge of the cell.","images":[],"sourcePage":252,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0213","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"If a client's radio device receives a signal strength of -67 dBm and the noise floor is -85 dBm, what is the SNR value?","options":{"A":"15 dB","B":"16 dB","C":"18 dB","D":"20 dB"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) is calculated by subtracting the noise floor from the signal strength. Given a signal strength of -67 dBm and a noise floor of -85 dBm, the calculation is: -67 - (-85) = -67 + 85 = 18 dB. Therefore, the SNR value is 18 dB.","images":[],"sourcePage":253,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0214","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which AP mode allows an engineer to scan configured channels for rogue access points?","options":{"A":"monitor","B":"bridge","C":"local","D":"sniffer"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Monitor mode allows an access point to scan all configured channels for rogue access points. In this mode, the AP listens to the radio frequencies for identification of unauthorized devices and does not transmit, making it ideal for scanning and rogue detection purposes.","images":[],"sourcePage":254,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0215","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. While troubleshooting a routing issue, an engineer issues a ping from S1 to S2. Which two actions result from the initial value of the TTL?(Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The packet reaches R2, and the TTL expires.","B":"R1 replies with a TTL exceeded message.","C":"The packet reaches R3, and the TTL expires.","D":"R2 replies with a TTL exceeded message.","E":"R3 replies with a TTL exceeded message.","F":"The packet reaches R1, and the TTL expires."},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"When the packet leaves S1, the initial Time To Live (TTL) value is 2. The TTL is decremented by 1 each time the packet passes through a router. When the packet reaches R1, the TTL is reduced to 1. When the packet reaches R2, the TTL is decremented to 0. Since the TTL has reached 0 at R2, R2 will discard the packet and reply with an ICMP Time Exceeded message to the source. Therefore, the two resulting actions are that the packet reaches R2 and the TTL expires, and R2 replies with a TTL exceeded message.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0215_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":255,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0216","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What is the wireless Received Signal Strength Indicator?","options":{"A":"the value given to the strength of the wireless signal received compared to the noise level","B":"the value of how strong the wireless signal is leaving the antenna using transmit power, cable loss, and antenna gain","C":"the value of how much wireless signal is lost over a defined amount of distance","D":"the value of how strong a wireless signal is received, measured in dBm"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI) is a measurement of how strongly a wireless signal is received, typically expressed in dBm. It represents the power level being received by the antenna and is used to estimate the quality of the connection. Therefore, the most accurate definition given in the options is the value of how strong a wireless signal is received, measured in dBm.","images":[],"sourcePage":257,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0217","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which two operational modes enable an AP to scan one or more wireless channels for rogue access points and at the same time provide wireless services to clients? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"monitor","B":"rogue detector","C":"FlexConnect","D":"sniffer","E":"local"},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"The two operational modes that enable an AP to scan one or more wireless channels for rogue access points while providing wireless services to clients are FlexConnect and local. FlexConnect mode allows an AP to provide continuous wireless services to clients while periodically scanning the other channels for rogue devices. Similarly, local mode APs provide client connectivity but can also perform off-channel scanning for a brief period to detect rogue access points.","images":[],"sourcePage":258,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0218","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer attempts to configure a trunk between switch SW1 and switch SW2 using DTP, but the trunk does not form. Which command should the engineer apply to switch SW2 to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"switchport nonegotiate","B":"no switchport","C":"switchport mode dynamic desirable","D":"switchport mode access"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Both switch SW1 and SW2 are configured with 'switchport mode dynamic auto,' which means they are both waiting for the other side to initiate the trunking process. This results in no trunk formation. To resolve this, the engineer should configure one side to actively attempt to form a trunk. 'switchport mode dynamic desirable' will assist in forming the trunk by actively trying to negotiate trunking with the other side.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0218_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":259,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0219","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is troubleshooting the AP join process using DNS. Which FQDN must be resolvable on the network for the access points to successfully register to the WLC?","options":{"A":"wlchostname.domain.com","B":"cisco-capwap-controller.domain.com","C":"ap-manager.domain.com","D":"primary-wlc.domain.com"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In the AP join process using DNS, the Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) that must be resolvable on the network for the access points to successfully register to the Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) is 'cisco-capwap-controller.domain.com'. This FQDN is specific to Cisco's CAPWAP protocol, which is used for managing access points and ensuring they can communicate with the WLC.","images":[],"sourcePage":260,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0220","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which new enhancement was implemented in Wi-Fi 6?","options":{"A":"Uplink and Downlink Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiple Access","B":"Channel bonding","C":"Wi-Fi Protected Access 3","D":"4096 Quadrature Amplitude Modulation Mode"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Wi-Fi 6 introduced several enhancements, one of which is Uplink and Downlink Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiple Access (OFDMA). OFDMA is a modulation scheme that allows multiple devices to be served simultaneously on the same frequency channel, improving efficiency and reducing latency. This is a significant improvement over previous Wi-Fi standards, which used Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiplexing (OFDM) that could only serve one device at a time on a given frequency channel. Additionally, while other options like WPA3 and 4096-QAM are related to security and modulation enhancements, they are not the defining new capabilities introduced specifically by Wi-Fi 6.","images":[],"sourcePage":261,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0221","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which device makes the decision for a wireless client to roam?","options":{"A":"wireless client","B":"wireless LAN controller","C":"access point","D":"WCS location server"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The device that makes the decision for a wireless client to roam is the wireless client itself. In 802.11 WiFi networks, the client device is in control of the roaming decision based on its own roaming algorithm. The client uses beacon frames or sends probe requests to discover access points advertising the preferred SSID and assesses signal strength to determine whether to switch to a different access point or remain connected to the current one.","images":[],"sourcePage":262,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0222","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer configures GigabitEthernet 0/1 for VRRP group 115. The router MUST assume the primary role when it has the highest priority in the group.\nWhich command set is required to complete this task?\n`interface GigabitEthernet0/1``ip address 10.10.10.2 255.255.255.0``vrrp 115 ip 10.10.10.1``vrrp 115 authentication 407441579`","options":{"A":"Router(config if)# vrrp 115 track 1 decrement 100 Router(config-if)# vrrp 115 preempt","B":"Router(config-if)# vrrp 115 priority 100","C":"Router(config-if)# vrrp 115 track 1 decrement 10 Router(config-if)# vrrp 115 preempt","D":"Router(config-if)# standby 115 priority 100 Router(config-if)# standby 115 preempt"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"","images":[],"sourcePage":263,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space.","No explanation in the source PDF."]},{"id":"q0223","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"How is MSDP used to interconnect multiple PIM-SM domains?","options":{"A":"MSDP allows a rendezvous point to dynamically discover active sources outside of its domain.","B":"MSDP SA request messages are used to request a list of active sources for a specific group.","C":"MSDP depends on BGP or multiprotocol BGP for interdomain operation.","D":"MSDP messages are used to advertise active sources in a domain."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"MSDP allows a rendezvous point to dynamically discover active sources outside of its domain. This enables multiple PIM-SM domains to exchange information about active multicast sources, facilitating the efficient forwarding of multicast traffic across domain boundaries.","images":[],"sourcePage":264,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0224","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"If the noise floor is -90 dBm and the wireless client is receiving a signal of 75’ˆ‫ ג‬dBm, what is the SNR?","options":{"A":"15","B":"1.2","C":"165’ˆ‫ג‬","D":".83"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To calculate the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR), you subtract the noise floor from the signal strength. Given a noise floor of -90 dBm and a signal strength of -75 dBm, the SNR is calculated as -75 dBm - (-90 dBm) which equals 15 dB. Therefore, the SNR in this case is 15 dB.","images":[],"sourcePage":265,"flags":["The PDF text contains garbled quote characters (shown as ג€ or similar); kept exactly as extracted.","The signal value in the question is garbled in the PDF text (looks like 75 followed by odd symbols before dBm); check the original."]},{"id":"q0225","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nThe EtherChannel between SW2 and SW3 is not operational. Which action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"Configure the channel-group mode on SW3 Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 to active.","B":"Configure the mode on SW2 Gi0/0 to trunk.","C":"Configure the channel-group mode on SW2 Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 to on.","D":"Configure the mode on SW2 Gi0/1 to access."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The EtherChannel between SW2 and SW3 is not operational because the port-channel between them requires both ends to be in either 'active' mode or a combination of 'active' and 'passive' mode for LACP to function properly. In the current configuration, SW2's Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 are set to 'active,' while SW3's Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 are set to 'passive.' To resolve the issue, the channel-group mode on SW3 Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 should be configured to 'active' to match the configuration on SW2, thereby enabling the EtherChannel to establish successfully.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0225_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":266,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0226","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich action completes the configuration to achieve a dynamic continuous mapped NAT for all users?","options":{"A":"Reconfigure the pool to use the 192.168.1.0 address range.","B":"Configure a match-host type NAT pool.","C":"Increase the NAT pool size to support 254 usable addresses.","D":"Configure a one-to-one type NAT pool."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The current configuration attempts to create a NAT pool for dynamic continuous mapped NAT. However, the pool defined in the configuration only supports 100 IP addresses (10.10.10.1 to 10.10.10.100). Given the subnet for users is 192.168.1.0/24, which contains 254 addresses, the current pool is insufficient for all users. To ensure that NAT can be performed for all users in the subnet dynamically, the NAT pool size should be increased to support 254 usable addresses.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0226_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":268,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0227","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"mcq","question":"How does EIGRP differ from OSPF?","options":{"A":"EIGRP is more prone to routing loops than OSPF.","B":"EIGRP uses more CPU and memory than OSPF.","C":"EIGRP has a full map of the topology, and OSPF only knows directly connected neighbors.","D":"EIGRP supports equal or unequal path cost, and OSPF supports only equal path cost."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"EIGRP supports both equal and unequal path cost for routing, allowing it to choose a primary route and a backup route with different metrics if needed. In contrast, OSPF only supports equal-cost multipath (ECMP) routing, where multiple routes to the same destination must have the same cost to be used simultaneously.","images":[],"sourcePage":269,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0228","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which AP mode allows a supported AP to function like a WLAN client would, associating and identifying client connectivity issues?","options":{"A":"client mode","B":"SE-connect mode","C":"sensor mode","D":"sniffer mode"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The AP mode that allows a supported AP to function like a WLAN client would, associating and identifying client connectivity issues, is client mode. In client mode, the AP acts as a client to connect to another WLAN network and can identify connectivity issues from a client's perspective.","images":[],"sourcePage":270,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0229","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A client device roams between wireless LAN controllers that are mobility peers. Both controllers have dynamic interfaces on the same client VLAN. Which type of roam is described?","options":{"A":"intra-VLAN","B":"inter-controller","C":"intra-controller","D":"inter-subnet"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"When a client device roams between wireless LAN controllers that are mobility peers and both controllers have dynamic interfaces on the same client VLAN, it is described as an inter-controller roam. This type of roaming occurs at Layer 2, meaning the client retains its IP address as it moves between access points associated with different controllers. The controllers work together to maintain the client's network session and provide seamless connectivity.","images":[],"sourcePage":271,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0230","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which component does Cisco Threat Defense use to measure bandwidth, application performance, and utilization?","options":{"A":"TrustSec","B":"Advanced Malware Protection for Endpoints","C":"NetFlow","D":"Cisco Umbrella"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"NetFlow is a critical component used by Cisco Threat Defense to measure bandwidth, application performance, and utilization. It captures and analyzes IP network traffic data, allowing for detailed insights into network behaviors and performance. This capability is essential for monitoring and securing network operations against threats and ensuring optimal performance.","images":[],"sourcePage":272,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0231","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Which IP SLA operation requires the IP SLA responder to be configured on the remote end?","options":{"A":"UDP jitter","B":"ICMP jitter","C":"TCP connect","D":"ICMP echo"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The IP SLA operation that requires the IP SLA responder to be configured on the remote end is the UDP jitter operation. The IP SLA responder is necessary to accurately measure the jitter, latency, and packet loss by generating timestamps that are sent back to the source device. This setup helps in providing precise round-trip time measurements for UDP packets, which is essential for jitter operations.","images":[],"sourcePage":273,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0232","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What does the error message relay to the administrator who is trying to configure a Cisco IOS device?","options":{"A":"The device received a valid NETCONF request and serviced it without error.","B":"The NETCONF running datastore is currently locked.","C":"A NETCONF request was made for a data model that does not exist.","D":"A NETCONF message with valid content based on the YANG data models was made, but the request failed."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The error message indicates that a NETCONF request was made for a data model that does not exist. The server responds with an empty data response, which is a standard behavior when a requested data model or leaf is not implemented on the device. Therefore, the administrator receives an empty data tag to signal the absence of the requested data model.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0232_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":274,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0233","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which three methods does Cisco DNA Center use to discover devices? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"CDP","B":"SNMP","C":"LLDP","D":"ping","E":"NETCONF","F":"a specified range of IP addresses"},"correctAnswer":["A","B","C"],"explanation":"Cisco DNA Center can use the following methods to discover devices: CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol), SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol), and LLDP (Link Layer Discovery Protocol). CDP is a Cisco proprietary protocol for discovering Cisco devices on the network. SNMP is used for network management and device discovery across different vendors. LLDP is a standardized network discovery protocol that works in multivendor environments. These methods are essential for identifying and managing network devices efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":275,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0234","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about TLS is accurate when using RESTCONF to write configurations on network devices?","options":{"A":"It is used for HTTP and HTTPS requests.","B":"It requires certificates for authentication.","C":"It is provided using NGINX acting as a proxy web server.","D":"It is not supported on Cisco devices."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Transport Layer Security (TLS) is a cryptographic protocol designed to provide secure communication over a network. When using RESTCONF to write configurations on network devices, TLS requires certificates for authentication to ensure that the communication is secure. This authentication mechanism verifies the identity of the devices involved in the communication. While NGINX can act as a proxy web server providing HTTPS, it is not specific to TLS's role in securing RESTCONF communications. Therefore, the accurate statement about TLS in this context is that it requires certificates for authentication.","images":[],"sourcePage":276,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0235","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What do Cisco DNA southbound APIs provide?","options":{"A":"interface between the controller and the consumer","B":"RESTful API interface for orchestrator communication","C":"interface between the controller and the network devices","D":"NETCONF API interface for orchestrator communication"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cisco DNA southbound APIs provide the interface between the controller and the network devices. They facilitate communication and interaction with the devices managed by the controller, enabling tasks such as configuration, monitoring, and management of the network infrastructure.","images":[],"sourcePage":277,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0236","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about an RSPAN session configuration is true?","options":{"A":"Only one session can be configured at a time.","B":"A special VLAN type must be used as the RSPAN destination.","C":"A filter must be configured for RSPAN sessions.","D":"Only incoming traffic can be monitored."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A special VLAN type must be used as the RSPAN destination. This dedicated VLAN ensures that the traffic mirrored from source ports across multiple switches is properly encapsulated and transported to the destination without interference from regular network traffic.","images":[],"sourcePage":278,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0237","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature must be configured to allow packet capture over Layer 3 infrastructure?","options":{"A":"RSPAN","B":"ERSPAN","C":"VSPAN","D":"IPSPAN"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"ERSPAN (Encapsulated Remote SPAN) allows packet capture over Layer 3 infrastructure by encapsulating the captured traffic in Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE). This encapsulated traffic can then be routed across Layer 3 domains, making it possible to monitor packets on different network segments and switches that are not directly connected. Other options like RSPAN, VSPAN, and IPSPAN do not have the capability to extend monitoring sessions across Layer 3 networks.","images":[],"sourcePage":279,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0238","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhat is the result when a technician adds the monitor session 1 destination remote vlan 223 command?","options":{"A":"The RSPAN VLAN is replaced by VLAN 223.","B":"RSPAN traffic is sent to VLANs 222 and 223.","C":"An error is flagged for configuring two destinations.","D":"RSPAN traffic is split between VLANs 222 and 223."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The command 'monitor session 1 destination remote vlan 223' will replace the existing RSPAN VLAN 222 with VLAN 223. An RSPAN session can only have one destination VLAN configured, so configuring a new destination VLAN will override the previous one.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0238_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":280,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0239","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures monitoring on SW1 and enters the show command to verify operation. What does the output confirm?","options":{"A":"RSPAN session 1 is incompletely configured for monitoring.","B":"RSPAN session 1 monitors activity on VLAN 50 of a remote switch.","C":"SPAN session 2 monitors all traffic entering and exiting port FastEthernet 0/15.","D":"SPAN session 2 only monitors egress traffic exiting port FastEthernet 0/14."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The output shows that session 1 is configured as a Remote Destination Session with source RSPAN VLAN 50, but it does not include a destination port, which is required to complete the configuration for monitoring. Without specifying a destination port, the RSPAN session cannot properly function, making the configuration incomplete. Therefore, RSPAN session 1 is incompletely configured for monitoring.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0239_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":281,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0240","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network is being migrated from IPv4 to IPv6 using a dual-stack approach. Network management is already 100% IPv6 enabled.\nIn a dual-stack network with two dual-stack NetFlow collectors, how many flow exporters are needed per network device in the flexible NetFlow configuration?","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"4","D":"8"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a dual-stack network, each network device must be able to send NetFlow data to both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. Since each flow exporter can only handle one destination, and there are two NetFlow collectors, one for IPv4 traffic and one for IPv6 traffic, two flow exporters are needed per network device in the flexible NetFlow configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":282,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0241","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer is configuring Flexible NetFlow and enters these commands. sampler NetFlow1 mode random one-out-of 100 interface fastethernet 1/0 flow-sampler NetFlow1\nWhat are two results of implementing this feature instead of traditional NetFlow? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Only the flows of top 100 talkers are exported.","B":"CPU and memory utilization are reduced.","C":"The number of packets to be analyzed are reduced.","D":"The data export flow is more secure.","E":"The accuracy of the data to be analyzed is improved."},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"Flexible NetFlow, when configured with flow sampling, reduces CPU and memory utilization and decreases the number of packets to be analyzed. By sampling one out of every 100 packets, the device processing load is significantly reduced as it no longer needs to analyze every single packet. This results in lower resource consumption and less data to process, though potentially with a slight reduction in overall accuracy compared to traditional NetFlow.","images":[],"sourcePage":283,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0242","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must add the SNMP interface table to the NetFlow protocol flow records. Where should the SNMP table option be added?","options":{"A":"under the interface","B":"under the flow record","C":"under the flow monitor","D":"under the flow exporter"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The SNMP interface table option should be added under the flow exporter configuration. The flow exporter is responsible for sending flow records to a collector, and the option interface-table command is used within the exporter configuration to include SNMP-related information in the flow records. This enables the collector to map the interface SNMP indexes to interface names, enhancing the exported flow data.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0242_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":284,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0243","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator is implementing a routing configuration change and enables routing debugs to track routing behavior during the change. The logging output on the terminal is interrupting the command typing process.\nWhich two actions can the network administrator take to minimize the possibility of typing commands incorrectly? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Configure the logging synchronous global configuration command.","B":"Configure the logging synchronous command under the vty.","C":"Increase the number of lines on the screen using the terminal length command.","D":"Configure the logging delimiter feature.","E":"Press the TAB key to reprint the command in a new line."},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"To minimize the possibility of typing commands incorrectly while receiving logging output, the network administrator should configure the logging synchronous command under the vty, which ensures that unsolicited messages are synchronized with command output, making it easier to read and continue typing commands correctly. Additionally, pressing the TAB key can help to reprint the partially typed command line, allowing the administrator to continue without needing to retype the entire command.","images":[],"sourcePage":286,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0244","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"When using TLS for syslog, which configuration allows for secure and reliable transportation of messages to its default port?","options":{"A":"logging host 10.2.3.4 vrf mgmt transport tcp port 514","B":"logging host 10.2.3.4 vrf mgmt transport udp port 514","C":"logging host 10.2.3.4 vrf mgmt transport tcp port 6514","D":"logging host 10.2.3.4 vrf mgmt transport udp port 6514"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct configuration for secure and reliable transportation of syslog messages using TLS is to use TCP port 6514. TCP is necessary for reliable transmission, and port 6514 is the standard port for syslog over TLS.","images":[],"sourcePage":287,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0245","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to this output.\nR1# *Feb 14 37:09:53.129: %LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: Line protocol on Interface GigabitEthernet0/1, changed state to up\nWhat is the logging severity level?","options":{"A":"notification","B":"emergency","C":"critical","D":"alert"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The logging severity level for a line protocol state change to up is 'notification'. According to Cisco's definitions, a notification is a normal but significant condition, corresponding to severity level 5. This type of message indicates changes such as an interface going up or down, which is informative but does not require immediate action.","images":[],"sourcePage":288,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0246","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer reviews a router's logs and discovers the following entry. What is the event's logging severity level?\nRouter#\nJan 01 38:24:04.401: %LINK-3-UPDOWN: Interface GigabitEthernet0/1, changed state to up","options":{"A":"error","B":"warning","C":"informational","D":"notification"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The event's logging severity level is 'error'. Cisco IOS uses a numeric representation for logging severity levels, where each number correlates to a specific level: 0 for emergency, 1 for alert, 2 for critical, 3 for error, 4 for warning, 5 for notification, 6 for informational, and 7 for debugging. The log entry provided contains the severity level 3 (%LINK-3-UPDOWN), indicating an error condition.","images":[],"sourcePage":289,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0247","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must configure a SPAN session.\nWhat is the effect of the configuration?","options":{"A":"Traffic received on VLANs 10, 11, and 12 is copied and sent to interface g0/1.","B":"Traffic sent on VLANs 10 and 12 only is copied and sent to interface g0/1.","C":"Traffic sent on VLANs 10, 11, and 12 is copied and sent to interface g0/1.","D":"Traffic received on VLANs 10 and 12 only is copied and sent to interface g0/1."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The configuration specifies that traffic received (indicated by 'rx') on VLANs 10, 11, and 12 is mirrored and sent to the destination interface gigabitethernet0/1. Hence, the option stating that traffic received on VLANs 10, 11, and 12 is copied and sent to interface g0/1 is correct.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0247_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":290,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0248","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nThese commands have been added to the configuration of a switch.\nWhich command flags an error if it is added to this configuration?","options":{"A":"monitor session 1 source interface port-channel 6","B":"monitor session 1 source vlan 10","C":"monitor session 1 source interface FastEthemet0/1 rx","D":"monitor session 1 source interface port-channel 7, port-channel 8"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The command 'monitor session 1 source vlan 10' would flag an error. In a SPAN (Switched Port Analyzer) session, you cannot mix source ports and source VLANs within the same session. The current configuration already includes source interfaces such as FastEthernet0/1, FastEthernet0/2, and port-channel 5, so attempting to add a VLAN source to this session would result in an error.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0248_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":291,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0249","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which method does Cisco DNA Center use to allow management of non-Cisco devices through southbound protocols?","options":{"A":"It creates device packs through the use of an SDK.","B":"It uses an API call to interrogate the devices and register the returned data.","C":"It obtains MIBs from each vendor that details the APIs available.","D":"It imports available APIs for the non-Cisco device in a CSV format."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Cisco DNA Center allows management of non-Cisco devices by creating device packs through the use of a Software Development Kit (SDK). This method enables the integration of third-party devices with the Cisco DNA Center platform, ensuring that these devices can be managed using the same systems and protocols as Cisco devices.","images":[],"sourcePage":292,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0250","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Cisco DNA Center has obtained the username of the client and the multiple devices that the client is using on the network. How is Cisco DNA Center getting these context details?","options":{"A":"Those details are provided to Cisco DNA Center by the Identity Services Engine.","B":"The administrator had to assign the username to the IP address manually in the user database tool on Cisco DNA Center.","C":"Cisco DNA Center pulled those details directly from the edge node where the user connected.","D":"User entered those details in the Assurance app available on iOS and Android devices."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The context details such as the username of the client and multiple devices are typically provided to Cisco DNA Center by the Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE). ISE integrates with Cisco DNA Center to offer enhanced management awareness and control over users and devices accessing the network. It performs policy implementation, enabling dynamic mapping of users and devices, providing context information that is necessary for various network operations and security policies. Hence, the correct answer is A.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0250_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":293,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0251","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Which command set configures RSPAN to capture outgoing traffic from VLAN 3 on interface GigabitEthernet 0/3 while ignoring other VLAN traffic on the same interface?","options":{"A":"monitor session 2 source interface gigabitethernet0/3 rx monitor session 2 filter vlan 3","B":"monitor session 2 source interface gigabitethernet0/3 rx monitor session 2 filter vlan 1 - 2, 4 - 4094","C":"monitor session 2 source interface gigabitethernet0/3 tx monitor session 2 filter vlan 3","D":"monitor session 2 source interface gigabitethernet0/3 tx monitor session 2 filter vlan 1- 2, 4 - 4094"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To configure RSPAN to capture outgoing traffic from VLAN 3 on interface GigabitEthernet 0/3, you need to use the 'tx' keyword to specify outgoing traffic and 'filter vlan 3' to capture only traffic from VLAN 3. The correct command will therefore be monitor session 2 source interface gigabitethernet0/3 tx monitor session 2 filter vlan 3, which exactly matches option C.","images":[],"sourcePage":294,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0252","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What is required to configure a second export destination for IP address 192.168.10.1?","options":{"A":"Specify a different UDP port.","B":"Specify a different TCP port.","C":"Configure a version 5 flow-export to the same destination.","D":"Specify a different flow ID.","E":"Specify a VRF."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To configure a second export destination to the same IP address 192.168.10.1, it is necessary to specify a different UDP port. This ensures that each flow export stream is unique and can be properly distinguished by the system. Using the same UDP port for multiple destinations would lead to conflicts and mismanagement of the flow data.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0252_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":295,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0253","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After implementing the configuration, 172.20.20.2 stops replying to ICMP echos, but the default route fails to be removed. What is the reason for this behavior?","options":{"A":"The threshold value is wrong.","B":"The source-interface is configured incorrectly.","C":"The destination must be 172.30.30.2 for icmp-echo.","D":"The default route is missing the track feature."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The issue is that the default route is missing the track feature. This means that when the IP SLA detects that 172.20.20.2 is not reachable, the route will not be removed because the track feature is not associated with the default route. The missing part in the configuration is the 'track 10' statement in the route command. To correct this, the command should be 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 172.20.20.2 track 10'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0253_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":296,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0254","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer is troubleshooting a connectivity issue and executes a traceroute. What does the result confirm?","options":{"A":"The destination port is unreachable.","B":"The probe timed out.","C":"The destination server reported it is too busy.","D":"The protocol is unreachable."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The result shows asterisks (*) for the third hop, indicating that the probe timed out. This suggests that the packets sent by the traceroute command did not receive a response within the expected time frame from the destination. Therefore, the correct answer is that the probe timed out.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0254_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":298,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0255","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which Cisco DNA Center application is responsible for group-based access control permissions?","options":{"A":"Provision","B":"Design","C":"Assurance","D":"Policy"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The Cisco DNA Center application responsible for group-based access control permissions is Policy. This application manages virtual networks and identity services, making it the appropriate tool for setting and enforcing policies within a network environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":299,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0256","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is concerned with the deployment of a new application that is sensitive to inter-packet delay variance. Which command configures the router to be the destination of jitter measurements?","options":{"A":"Router(config)# ip sla responder udp-connect 172.29.139.134 5000","B":"Router(config)# ip sla responder tcp-connect 172.29.139.134 5000","C":"Router(config)# ip sla responder udp-echo 172.29.139.134 5000","D":"Router(config)# ip sla responder tcp-echo 172.29.139.134 5000"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To configure the router to be the destination of jitter measurements, the appropriate command is 'ip sla responder udp-echo'. This command sets up the router to respond to UDP echo probes, which are used to measure jitter and other network performance metrics. UDP-echo is suitable for this purpose because it allows the measurement of delays between packets without establishing a complete connection, making it ideal for scenarios that are sensitive to inter-packet delay variance.","images":[],"sourcePage":300,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0257","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which NGFW mode blocks flows crossing the firewall?","options":{"A":"tap","B":"inline","C":"passive","D":"inline tap"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The inline mode in a Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW) configuration is where the firewall actively inspects and filters traffic as it passes through, allowing or blocking flows based on defined security policies. This is opposed to modes like tap, passive, or inline tap, which do not intervene directly to block traffic flows crossing the firewall.","images":[],"sourcePage":301,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0258","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"How does Cisco TrustSec enable more flexible access controls for dynamic networking environments and data centers?","options":{"A":"uses flexible NetFlow","B":"assigns a VLAN to the endpoint","C":"classifies traffic based on advanced application recognition","D":"classifies traffic based on the contextual identity of the endpoint rather than its IP address"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Cisco TrustSec enables more flexible access controls for dynamic networking environments and data centers by classifying traffic based on the contextual identity of the endpoint rather than its IP address. This allows for more granular and adaptable security policies that can adjust to varying networking conditions and requirements, rather than relying solely on traditional IP-based classification methods.","images":[],"sourcePage":302,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0259","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"The login method is configured on the VTY lines of a router with these parameters:\nThe first method for authentication is TACACS If TACACS is unavailable, login is allowed without any provided credentials\nWhich configuration accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct configuration is D. Here's why: The configuration requirement specifies that the first method for authentication should be TACACS and if TACACS is unavailable, login should be allowed without any provided credentials. The command 'aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ none' in option D accomplishes this task. This command sets the default login authentication method to use TACACS first, and if TACACS is not available, it allows login without requiring any credentials ('none' keyword). The other options either do not properly specify the fallback method, or incorrectly configure the authentication list.","images":[],"sourcePage":303,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0259_optA.webp","B":"images/q0259_optB.webp","C":"images/q0259_optC.webp","D":"images/q0259_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0260","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature does Cisco TrustSec use to provide scalable, secure communication throughout a network?","options":{"A":"security group tag ACL assigned to each port on a switch","B":"security group tag number assigned to each user on a switch","C":"security group tag number assigned to each port on a network","D":"security group tag ACL assigned to each router on a network"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cisco TrustSec uses security group tags (SGTs) to provide scalable and secure communication throughout a network. The security group tags are assigned to each port on a network. As traffic passes through the network infrastructure devices such as switches and routers, these tags are used to enforce access control policies based on the assigned SGTs. This method allows for dynamic and granular access control, ensuring secure communication across the entire network.","images":[],"sourcePage":306,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0261","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which technology provides a secure communication channel for all traffic at Layer 2 of the OSI model?","options":{"A":"SSL","B":"Cisco TrustSec","C":"MACsec","D":"IPsec"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"MACsec (Media Access Control Security) is a technology specifically designed to provide secure communication channels at Layer 2 of the OSI model, which is the data link layer. Unlike SSL and IPsec, which operate at higher layers (Layer 4 and Layer 3, respectively), MACsec encrypts traffic on Ethernet networks at Layer 2, ensuring that all traffic—including management protocols like DHCP and ARP—is protected within a local area network (LAN). This makes it the appropriate choice for Layer 2 security.","images":[],"sourcePage":307,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0262","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is configuring local web authentication on a WLAN. The engineer chooses the Authentication radio button under the Layer 3 Security options for WebPolicy.\nWhich device presents the web authentication for the WLAN?","options":{"A":"ISE server","B":"RADIUS server","C":"anchor WLC","D":"local WLC"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The device that presents the web authentication for the WLAN is the local WLC. When configuring local web authentication on a WLAN and selecting the Authentication radio button under the Layer 3 Security options for Web Policy, the local Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) hosts the web authentication portal. This portal redirects users to a web page where they can input their credentials or necessary information to gain access to the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":308,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0263","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which method does the enable secret password option use to encrypt device passwords?","options":{"A":"MD5","B":"PAP","C":"CHAP","D":"AES"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The enable secret password option in Cisco devices uses the MD5 algorithm to hash passwords. This method provides a more secure way of protecting device passwords compared to the older enable password command. Although MD5 is technically a hashing algorithm and not encryption, it is used here to generate a hashed representation of the password, which adds a layer of security.","images":[],"sourcePage":309,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0264","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"On which protocol or technology is the fabric data plane based in Cisco SD-Access fabric?","options":{"A":"VXLAN","B":"LISP","C":"Cisco TrustSec","D":"IS-IS"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The fabric data plane in Cisco SD-Access fabric is based on VXLAN. VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a tunneling technology that uses UDP encapsulation to create an overlay network. This capability allows it to support both Layer 2 and Layer 3 virtual topologies and operate over any IP-based network, providing built-in network segmentation and group-based policies.","images":[],"sourcePage":310,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0265","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the difference between the enable password and the enable secret password when service password encryption is enabled on an IOS device?","options":{"A":"The enable secret password is protected via stronger cryptography mechanisms.","B":"The enable password cannot be decrypted.","C":"The enable password is encrypted with a stronger encryption method.","D":"There is no difference and both passwords are encrypted identically."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The enable secret password is protected via stronger cryptography mechanisms. The enable secret password is hashed using the MD5 algorithm, which is stronger and provides better security. In contrast, the enable password is encrypted using the Vigenere cipher when service password encryption is enabled, which is a much weaker form of encryption. Therefore, the enable secret password offers greater protection.","images":[],"sourcePage":311,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0266","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which access control list allows only TCP traffic with a destination port range of 22-443, excluding port 80?","options":{"A":"deny tcp any any eq 80 permit tcp any any gt 21 lt 444","B":"permit tcp any any range 22 443 deny tcp any any eq 80","C":"permit tcp any any eq 80","D":"deny tcp any any eq 80 permit tcp any any range 22 443"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct access control list (ACL) needs to allow TCP traffic within the port range of 22-443, but explicitly exclude port 80. The correct ACL should first deny traffic headed to port 80 and then permit traffic within the specified range. The sequence of rules is important because ACLs operate in order and stop processing once a match is found. 'deny tcp any any eq 80' first blocks traffic on port 80, and then 'permit tcp any any range 22 443' permits traffic in the range of 22 to 443. Therefore, this ensures that port 80 is excluded while allowing other ports in the specified range.","images":[],"sourcePage":312,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0267","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator applies the following configuration to an IOS device: aaa new-model aaa authentication login default local group tacacs+\nWhat is the process of password checks when a login attempt is made to the device?","options":{"A":"A TACACS+ server is checked first. If that check fails, a local database is checked.","B":"A TACACS+ server is checked first. If that check fails, a RADIUS server is checked. If that check fails, a local database is checked.","C":"A local database is checked first. If that check fails, a TACACS+ server is checked. If that check fails, a RADIUS server is checked.","D":"A local database is checked first. If that check fails, a TACACS+ server is checked."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The command 'aaa authentication login default local group tacacs+' means that for all login attempts, the device will first check its local database for user credentials. If no matching credentials are found in the local database, then it will check the TACACS+ server. This is consistent with the configuration where 'local' is specified before 'group tacacs+'.","images":[],"sourcePage":313,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0268","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAssuming the WLC's interfaces are not in the same subnet as the RADIUS server, which interface would the WLC use as the source for all RADIUS-related traffic?","options":{"A":"the controller management interface","B":"the controller virtual interface","C":"the interface specified on the WLAN configuration","D":"any interface configured on the WLC"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When the 'RADIUS Server Overwrite interface' checkbox is enabled, the controller uses the interface specified in the WLAN configuration for all RADIUS-related traffic. This allows for configuring a specific interface for RADIUS traffic, separate from the management interface. In this case, the WLC will use the defined WLAN interface instead of the management interface as the source for RADIUS traffic.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0268_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":314,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0269","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which component of the Cisco Cyber Threat Defense solution provides user and flow context analysis?","options":{"A":"Cisco Firepower and FireSIGHT","B":"Cisco Stealthwatch system","C":"Advanced Malware Protection","D":"Cisco Web Security Appliance"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The component of the Cisco Cyber Threat Defense solution that provides user and flow context analysis is Cisco Stealthwatch system. Stealthwatch collects telemetry from every part of the network and applies advanced security analytics to the data. It creates a baseline of normal web and network activity for a network host and applies context-aware analysis to detect anomalous behaviors automatically.","images":[],"sourcePage":316,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0270","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must protect their company against ransomware attacks.\nWhich solution allows the engineer to block the execution stage and prevent file encryption?","options":{"A":"Use Cisco Firepower and block traffic to TOR networks.","B":"Use Cisco AMP deployment with the Malicious Activity Protection engine enabled.","C":"Use Cisco Firepower with Intrusion Policy and snort rules blocking SMB exploitation.","D":"Use Cisco AMP deployment with the Exploit Prevention engine enabled."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To block the execution stage and prevent file encryption in a ransomware attack, using Cisco AMP deployment with the Malicious Activity Protection engine enabled is the correct solution. This engine provides real-time detection and blocking of abnormal behaviors associated with ransomware, effectively preventing the execution and encryption stages of the attack.","images":[],"sourcePage":317,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0271","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must deny Telnet traffic from the loopback interface of router R3 to the loopback interface of router R2 during the weekend hours. All other traffic between the loopback interfaces of routers R3 and R2 must be allowed at all times.\nWhich command set accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"R3(config)#time-range WEEKEND R3(config-time-range)#periodic Saturday Sunday 00:00 to 23:59 R3(config)#access-list 150 deny tcp host 10.3.3.3 host 10.2.2.2 eq 23 time-range WEEKEND R3(config)#access-list 150 permit ip any any time-range WEEKEND\nR3(config)#interface G0/1 R3(config-if)#ip access-group 150 out","B":"R1(config)#time-range WEEKEND R1(config-time-range)#periodic weekend 00:00 to 23:59 R1(config)#access-list 150 deny tcp host 10.3.3.3 host 10.2.2.2 eq 23 time-range WEEKEND R1(config)#access-list 150 permit ip any any\nR1(config)#interface G0/1 R1(config-if)#ip access-group 150 in","C":"R3(config)#time-range WEEKEND R3(config-time-range)#periodic weekend 00:00 to 23:59\nR3(config)#access-list 150 permit tcp host 10.3.3.3 host 10.2.2.2 eq 23 time-range WEEKEND\nR3(config)#access-list 150 permit ip any any time-range WEEKEND R3(config)#interface G0/1\nR3(config-if)#ip access-group 150 out","D":"R1(config)#time-range WEEKEND R1(config-time-range)#periodic Friday Sunday 00:00 to 00:00 R1(config)#access-list 150 deny tcp host 10.3.3.3 host 10.2.2.2 eq 23 time-range WEEKEND R1(config)#access-list 150 permit ip any any R1(config)#interface G0/1 R1(config-if)#ip access-group 150 in"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct command set should create a time range for the weekend, define an access list to deny the Telnet traffic from R3 to R2 during that time, and then apply this access list on the appropriate interface of R1. B uses the correct time range for the weekend (00:00 to 23:59 on Saturday and Sunday), and the access control list correctly specifies the Telnet traffic to be denied (TCP port 23), while permitting all other traffic. Applying this access list inbound on R1's G0/1 interface ensures that traffic originating from R3 is filtered appropriately before it reaches R2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0271_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":318,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0272","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. PC-1 must access the web server on port 8080. To allow this traffic, which statement must be added to an access control list that is applied on SW2 port G0/0 in the inbound direction?","options":{"A":"permit tcp host 172.16.0.2 host 192.168.0.5 eq 8080","B":"permit tcp host 192.168.0.5 host 172.16.0.2 eq 8080","C":"permit tcp host 192.168.0.5 eq 8080 host 172.16.0.2","D":"permit tcp host 192.168.0.5 lt 8080 host 172.16.0.2"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To allow PC-1 to access the web server on port 8080, the access control list (ACL) needs to permit the traffic originating from PC-1's IP address (172.16.0.2) and targeting the web server's IP address (192.168.0.5) on port 8080. Therefore, the correct statement to allow this traffic is: permit tcp host 172.16.0.2 host 192.168.0.5 eq 8080.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0272_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":320,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0273","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"What is the result of applying this access control list?\nip access-list extended STATEFUL\n10 permit tcp any any established 20 deny ip any any","options":{"A":"TCP traffic with the URG bit set is allowed.","B":"TCP traffic with the SYN bit set is allowed.","C":"TCP traffic with the ACK bit set is allowed.","D":"TCP traffic with the DF bit set is allowed."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The access control list (ACL) permits only TCP traffic that is part of an established connection, explicitly defined by the presence of the ACK or RST bit set within the TCP header. The 'permit tcp any any established' rule allows traffic that is part of an already established TCP session, thereby filtering out new connection requests which have the SYN bit set but not the ACK or RST bit. Therefore, the correct interpretation is that TCP traffic with the ACK bit set is allowed.","images":[],"sourcePage":321,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0274","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which outbound access list, applied to the WAN interface of a router, permits all traffic except for http traffic sourced from the workstation with IP address 10.10.10.1?","options":{"A":"ip access-list extended 200 deny tcp host 10.10.10.1 eq 80 any permit ip any any","B":"ip access-list extended 10 deny tcp host 10.10.10.1 any eq 80 permit ip any any","C":"ip access-list extended NO_HTTP deny tcp host 10.10.10.1 any eq 80","D":"ip access-list extended 100 deny tcp host 10.10.10.1 any eq 80 permit ip any any"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct answer is the access list that uses the extended range (100-199) valid for extended access lists, correctly denies TCP traffic from the specific workstation (10.10.10.1) aimed at port 80 (HTTP) and then permits all other IP traffic. This configuration ensures that only the specified HTTP traffic is blocked while allowing all other traffic to pass through.","images":[],"sourcePage":322,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0275","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must create a configuration that executes the show run command and then terminates the session when user CCNP logs in.Which configuration change is required?","options":{"A":"Add the access-class keyword to the username command.","B":"Add the autocommand keyword to the aaa authentication command.","C":"Add the access-class keyword to the aaa authentication command.","D":"Add the autocommand keyword to the username command."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To automatically execute the 'show run' command and terminate the session when user CCNP logs in, the 'autocommand' keyword should be added to the username command. This can be done by adding 'username CCNP autocommand show run'. The autocommand keyword executes the specified command after the user successfully logs in and then ends the session.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0275_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":323,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0276","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures CoPP and enters the show command to verify the implementation. What is the result of the configuration?","options":{"A":"All traffic will be policed based on access-list 120.","B":"If traffic exceeds the specified rate, it will be transmitted and remarked.","C":"Class-default traffic will be dropped.","D":"ICMP will be denied based on this configuration."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The configuration shown in the exhibit applies a Control Plane Policing (CoPP) policy that specifically matches traffic identified by access-list 120. The class-map named 'CISCO' is configured to match all conditions, which in this case is only access-group 120. The policy is then applied to the control plane's input service-policy, indicating that all traffic matching the access-list 120 criteria will be policed. The exhibit shows that traffic exceeding the specified rate is dropped, which aligns with the action defined. Therefore, the correct result of the configuration is that all traffic will be policed based on access-list 120.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0276_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":324,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0277","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the threat defense solutions from the left onto their descriptions on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0277_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0277_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":326,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0278","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhat is the effect of this configuration?","options":{"A":"The device will allow users at 192.168.0.202 to connect to vty lines 0 through 4 using the password ciscotestkey.","B":"The device will authenticate all users connecting to vty lines 0 through 4 against TACACS+.","C":"The device will allow only users at 192.168.0.202 to connect to vty lines 0 through 4.","D":"When users attempt to connect to vty lines 0 through 4, the device will authenticate them against TACACS+ if local authentication fails."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The configuration shown involves setting up AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) using TACACS+ for authentication of the users connecting through vty lines 0 to 4. The command 'aaa authentication login authorizationlist tacacs+' specifies that the authorization list for login authentication will use TACACS+. The 'tacacs-server host' command defines the TACACS+ server's IP address and the 'tacacs-server key' command specifies the shared key for the TACACS+ server. The 'login authentication authorizationlist' command applied to the vty lines 0 through 4 indicates that these lines will authenticate users against the specified TACACS+ server. Therefore, the device will authenticate all users connecting to vty lines 0 through 4 against TACACS+.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0278_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":327,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0279","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which deployment option of Cisco NGFW provides scalability?","options":{"A":"inline tap","B":"high availability","C":"clustering","D":"tap"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Clustering allows multiple Cisco NGFW devices to work together as a single logical unit, enhancing performance, throughput, and redundancy. This setup ensures scalability by providing the ability to handle increased loads and traffic efficiently through the combined resources of the clustered devices.","images":[],"sourcePage":328,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0280","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the REST API authentication methods from the left onto their descriptions on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0280_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0280_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":329,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0281","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access solution, what is the role of the Identity Services Engine?","options":{"A":"It is leveraged for dynamic endpoint to group mapping and policy definition.","B":"It provides GUI management and abstraction via apps that share context.","C":"It is used to analyze endpoint to app flows and monitor fabric status.","D":"It manages the LISP EID database."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The role of the Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE) in a Cisco SD-Access solution is to be leveraged for dynamic endpoint to group mapping and policy definition. It simplifies the delivery of highly secure network access control by dynamically enforcing policies based on the identities of users and devices.","images":[],"sourcePage":330,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0282","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the solutions that compromise Cisco Cyber Threat Defense from the left onto the objectives they accomplish on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0282_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0282_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":331,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0283","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nAn engineer creates the configuration below. Drag and drop the authentication methods from the left into the order of priority on the right. Not all options are used.\nR1#sh run | i aaa -\naaa new-modelaaa authentication login default group ACE group AAA_RADIUS local-case aaa session-id common\nR1#\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0283_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0283_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":332,"flags":["In the question text two exhibit lines are run together in the PDF (\"new-modelaaa authentication\"); kept as extracted.","Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0284","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"What is provided by the Stealthwatch component of the Cisco Cyber Threat Defense solution?","options":{"A":"real-time threat management to stop DDoS attacks to the core and access networks","B":"real-time awareness of users, devices, and traffic on the network","C":"malware control","D":"dynamic threat control for web traffic"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The Stealthwatch component of the Cisco Cyber Threat Defense solution provides real-time awareness of users, devices, and traffic on the network. By collecting and analyzing data, Stealthwatch offers security teams comprehensive internal visibility, enabling them to effectively monitor network activity and respond to potential threats. This aligns directly with the function of Stealthwatch as described in its solution overview.","images":[],"sourcePage":334,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0285","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure an ACL that permits packets which include an ACK in the TCP header. Which entry must be included in the ACL?","options":{"A":"access-list 110 permit tcp any any eq 21 tcp-ack","B":"access-list 10 permit tcp any any eq 21 established","C":"access-list 110 permit tcp any any eq 21 established","D":"access-list 10 permit ip any any eq 21 tcp-ack"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To permit TCP packets that include an acknowledgment (ACK) in the TCP header, an extended access control list (ACL) should be used. Standard ACLs (numbered 1-99 and 1300-1999) do not filter traffic based on protocol type or TCP flags; they only make decisions based on source IP addresses. Extended ACLs (numbered 100-199 and 2000-2699) can filter based on IP, protocol type, source and destination IP addresses, and TCP flags. The 'established' keyword in an extended ACL checks if the ACK or RST bit is set, which is required to permit packets with the ACK flag. Hence, the correct entry would be 'access-list 110 permit tcp any any eq 21 established'.","images":[],"sourcePage":335,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0286","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"A client with IP address 209.165.201.25 must access a web server on port 80 at 209.165.200.225. To allow this traffic, an engineer must add a statement to an access control list that is applied in the inbound direction on the port connecting to the web server. Which statement allows this traffic?","options":{"A":"permit tcp host 209.165.200.225 lt 80 host 209.165.201.25","B":"permit tcp host 209.165.201.25 host 209.165.200.225 eq 80","C":"permit tcp host 209.165.200.225 eq 80 host 209.165.201.25","D":"permit tcp host 209.165.200.225 host 209.165.201.25 eq 80"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To allow the client with IP address 209.165.201.25 to access the web server on port 80 at 209.165.200.225, we need to permit traffic with a source IP of the web server and destination IP of the client. Since the traffic is being permitted inbound on the port connecting to the web server, the access control list statement should match traffic coming from the web server's IP with a source port of 80 to the client's IP. Therefore, the correct ACL statement is 'permit tcp host 209.165.200.225 eq 80 host 209.165.201.25'.","images":[],"sourcePage":336,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0287","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which standard access control entry permits traffic from odd-numbered hosts in the 10.0.0.0/24 subnet?","options":{"A":"permit 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.1","B":"permit 10.0.0.1 0.0.0.254","C":"permit 10.0.0.1 0.0.0.0","D":"permit 10.0.0.0 255.255.255.254"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To permit traffic from odd-numbered hosts in the 10.0.0.0/24 subnet, the access control entry needs to allow IP addresses with odd numbers. The correct approach is to match the IP addresses 10.0.0.1, 10.0.0.3, 10.0.0.5, etc., using a wildcard mask that differentiates between odd and even addresses. The entry 'permit 10.0.0.1 0.0.0.254' fits this requirement. The wildcard mask 0.0.0.254 applied to the base IP 10.0.0.1 will match all odd-numbered addresses in the subnet.","images":[],"sourcePage":337,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0288","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must modify the access control list EGRESS to allow all IP traffic from subnet 10.1.10.0/24 to 10.1.2.0/24. The access control list is applied in the outbound direction on router interface GigabitEthernet 0/1.\nWhich configuration command set will allow this traffic without disrupting existing traffic flows?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct answer is D. The command 'permit ip 10.1.10.0 0.0.0.255 10.1.2.0 0.0.0.255' needs to be added before the existing 'deny ip any any' rule in the access list to ensure traffic from 10.1.10.0/24 to 10.1.2.0/24 is allowed. By using the sequence number 5, this new rule is inserted before any existing rules, ensuring that it is processed first and does not disrupt existing traffic flows. Other options either do not specify a sequence number or involve creating a new access list, which is not required as the existing list just needs to be updated.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0288_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":338,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0288_optA.webp","B":"images/q0288_optB.webp","C":"images/q0288_optC.webp","D":"images/q0288_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0289","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which configuration restricts the amount of SSH traffic that a router accepts to 100 kbps?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct configuration to restrict the amount of SSH traffic that a router accepts to 100 kbps is C. In this configuration, the policy map 'CoPP_SSH' is applied to the control plane, which manages the router's processor and memory, ensuring that traffic directed to the router itself is limited to 100 kbps. The access list 'CoPP_SSH' permits TCP traffic on port 22 (used for SSH), and the 'police cir 100000' command enforces the traffic rate limit. This effectively applies a traffic policy at the control plane, protecting the router's resources.","images":[],"sourcePage":340,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0289_optA.webp","B":"images/q0289_optB.webp","C":"images/q0289_optC.webp","D":"images/q0289_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0290","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What step resolves the authentication issue?","options":{"A":"use basic authentication","B":"change the port to 12446","C":"target 192.168.100.82 in the URI","D":"restart the vsmart host"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The issue arises because the Postman request is targeted at the IP address 192.168.100.80, which corresponds to the vSmart host. However, the correct target should be the vManage host, which is responsible for managing the system. According to the show control connections command, the private IP for vManage is 192.168.100.82. Therefore, targeting 192.168.100.82 in the URI should resolve the authentication issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0290_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":343,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0291","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nSecurity policy requires all idle exec sessions to be terminated in 600 seconds.\nWhich configuration achieves this goal?","options":{"A":"line vty 0 15 absolute-timeout 600","B":"line vty 0 15 no exec-timeout","C":"line vty 0 15 exec-timeout 10 0","D":"line vty 0 4 exec-timeout 600"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To achieve the requirement of terminating all idle exec sessions in 600 seconds, the correct configuration is to set the exec-timeout to 10 minutes. This is done by using 'exec-timeout 10 0', where 10 represents minutes and 0 represents seconds. This ensures that any idle session will be terminated after 10 minutes (600 seconds) of inactivity.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0291_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":345,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0292","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must block all traffic from a router to its directly connected subnet 209.165.200.0/24. The engineer applies access control list EGRESS in the outbound direction on the GigabitEthernet0/0 interface of the router. However, the router can still ping hosts on the 209.165.200.0/24 subnet.\nWhat explains this behavior?","options":{"A":"Access control lists that are applied outbound to a router interface do not affect traffic that is sourced from the router.","B":"After an access control list is applied to an interface, that interface must be shut and no shut for the access control list to take effect.","C":"Only standard access control lists can block traffic from a source IP address.","D":"The access control list must contain an explicit deny to block traffic from the router."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Access control lists that are applied outbound to a router interface do not affect traffic that is sourced from the router. In this scenario, the ACL is applied in the outbound direction on the GigabitEthernet0/0 interface of the router. However, the ACL will only filter traffic that is being forwarded through this interface and not traffic that is generated by the router itself, such as pings to the 209.165.200.0/24 subnet.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0292_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":347,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0293","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of a next-generation firewall?","options":{"A":"only required at the network perimeter","B":"required in each layer of the network","C":"filters traffic using Layer 3 and Layer 4 information only","D":"provides intrusion prevention"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A next-generation firewall provides intrusion prevention. Unlike traditional firewalls, which primarily use basic packet filtering and stateful inspection methods based on Layer 3 and Layer 4 information, next-generation firewalls include advanced features such as deep packet inspection that goes beyond traditional capabilities. They can monitor and block threats by identifying and blocking sophisticated intrusion attempts and malware activities within a network.","images":[],"sourcePage":348,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0294","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer is investigating why guest users are able to access other guest user devices when the users are connected to the customer guestWLAN. What action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"implement P2P blocking","B":"implement MFP client protection","C":"implement Wi-Fi direct policy","D":"implement split tunneling"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of guest users being able to access other guest user devices, implementing Peer-to-Peer (P2P) blocking is necessary. P2P blocking restricts direct communication between devices connected to the same WLAN, ensuring that devices cannot communicate with each other directly, which aligns with the goal of preventing guest users from accessing other guest user devices.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0294_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":349,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0295","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer has configured Cisco ISE to assign VLANs to clients based on their method of authentication, but this is not working as expected.\nWhich action will resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"enable AAA override","B":"set a NAC state","C":"utilize RADIUS profiling","D":"require a DHCP address assignment"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of VLAN assignment based on authentication methods not working as expected, the AAA override feature should be enabled. Enabling AAA override allows the wireless LAN controller (WLC) to accept and apply the network policies returned by the RADIUS server, including VLAN assignments. This feature is critical in ensuring that the WLC can dynamically assign VLANs as specified by Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE) based on user authentication attributes.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0295_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":351,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0296","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which single security feature is recommended to provide Network Access Control in the enterprise?","options":{"A":"MAB","B":"802.1X","C":"WebAuth","D":"port security sticky MAC"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The recommended security feature for providing Network Access Control (NAC) in an enterprise environment is IEEE 802.1X. IEEE 802.1X is a standard for port-based network access control that provides authentication to devices wishing to attach to a LAN or WLAN. It is widely used for securing network access and is considered robust and reliable for enterprise environments, making it the best choice among the given options.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0296_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":353,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0297","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After configuring an IPsec VPN, an engineer enters the show command to verify the ISAKMP SA status. What does the status show?","options":{"A":"VPN peers agreed on parameters for the ISAKMP SA.","B":"Peers have exchanged keys, but ISAKMP SA remains unauthenticated.","C":"ISAKMP SA is authenticated and can be used for Quick Mode.","D":"ISAKMP SA has been created, but it has not continued to form."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The status shown in the exhibit indicates that the ISAKMP Security Association (SA) is in the QM_IDLE state and is active. This means that the Phase 1 ISAKMP (IKE) negotiations have been successfully completed, and the SA is authenticated and ready for use in Quick Mode, which is Phase 2 of the IPsec VPN establishment process. The displayed status confirms that the ISAKMP SA remains authenticated with its peer and can be used for further Quick Mode exchanges to establish additional IPsec SAs.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0297_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":354,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0298","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which two threats does AMP4E have the ability to block? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"email phishing","B":"DDoS","C":"Microsoft Word macro attack","D":"SQL injection","E":"ransomware"},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"AMP4E, or Advanced Malware Protection for Endpoints, is specifically designed to protect endpoints from a variety of threats. Among the options provided, Microsoft Word macro attacks and ransomware are both threats that typically occur directly on the endpoint. Microsoft Word macro attacks involve malicious scripts embedded in Word documents, while ransomware is a type of malware that encrypts files on the infected system, demanding payment for decryption. AMP4E can effectively detect and block these types of threats. On the other hand, email phishing is generally addressed through email security solutions, DDoS attacks target network availability, and SQL injection attacks target databases through vulnerabilities in the code, making them less relevant to endpoint-specific protection.","images":[],"sourcePage":355,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0299","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"lab","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must deny HTTP traffic from host A to host B while allowing all other communication between the hosts. Drag and drop the commands into the configuration to achieve these results. Some commands may be used more than once. Not all commands are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0299_exhibit.webp"},{"role":"question","file":"images/q0299_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0299_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":356,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0300","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure the strongest password authentication to locally authenticate on a router. Which configuration must be used?","options":{"A":"username netadmin secret 5 $1$b1Ju$kZbBS1Pyh4QzwXyZ1kSZ2","B":"username netadmin secret 9 $9$vFpMf8elb4RVV8$seZ/bDAx1uV","C":"username netadmin secret $1$b1Ju$k406689705QzwXyZ1kSZ2","D":"line Console 0 password $1$b1Ju$"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure the strongest password authentication for local authentication on a router, the best option is to use 'username netadmin secret 9'. This option utilizes the SCRYPT hashing algorithm, which is significantly stronger and more secure than the alternatives listed, such as MD5 (secret 5) or a simple password. SCRYPT provides enhanced protection against brute-force attacks due to its memory-intensive nature. Thus, for optimal security, the configuration that includes a type 9 SCRYPT hashed secret is the most robust choice.","images":[],"sourcePage":358,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0301","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of MACsec?","options":{"A":"802.1AE is built between the host and switch using the MKA protocol, which negotiates encryption keys based on the primary session key from a successful 802.1X session.","B":"802.1AE is negotiated using Cisco AnyConnect NAM and the SAP protocol.","C":"802.1AE is built between the host and switch using the MKA protocol using keys generated via the Diffie-Hellman algorithm (anonymous encryption mode).","D":"802.1AE provides encryption and authentication services."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"802.1AE, also known as MACsec, is a standard for providing encryption and authentication services at the MAC layer. It secures the data being transmitted over Ethernet by encrypting and authenticating each frame, ensuring confidentiality and integrity. This answer is straightforward and aligns with the overall purpose of MACsec without involving specifics that may lead to confusion or errors.","images":[],"sourcePage":359,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0302","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer attempts to connect to the Router1 console port.\nWhich configuration is needed to allow Telnet connections?","options":{"A":"Router1(config)# line vty 0 15 Router1(config-line)# transport output telnet","B":"Router1(config)# telnet client","C":"Router1(config)# line console 0 Router1(config-line)# transport output telnet","D":"Router1(config)# access-list 100 permit tcp any any eq telnet Router1(config)# line console 0\nRouter1(config-line)# access-class 100 out"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To allow Telnet connections, you must configure the vty (Virtual Teletype) lines on the router that are used for remote access. The command 'line vty 0 15' selects all 16 vty lines on the router, and 'transport input telnet' specifies that Telnet is allowed as an input transport method on these lines. Configuring the console line for Telnet is unnecessary and incorrect because Telnet connections are handled by vty lines, not the console line.","images":[],"sourcePage":360,"flags":["The question says \"Refer to the exhibit\" but the PDF page contains no exhibit image, so it is missing."]},{"id":"q0303","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nA network engineer must configure a password expiry mechanism on the gateway router for all local passwords to expire after 60 days. What is required to complete this task?","options":{"A":"Add the username admin privilege 15 common-criteria-policy Administrators password 0 Cisco13579! command.","B":"The password expiry mechanism is on the AAA server and must be configured there.","C":"Add the aaa authentication enable default Administrators command.","D":"No further action is required. The configuration is complete."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To configure a password expiry mechanism on the gateway router for all local passwords to expire after 60 days, it is necessary to associate the common-criteria policy with the user account. This is achieved by using the command that links the user to the specified policy. The appropriate syntax for this command is 'username [username] privilege 15 common-criteria-policy [policy-name] password 0 [password].' In this case, the correct command is 'username admin privilege 15 common-criteria-policy Administrators password 0 Cisco13579!' which applies the policy named 'Administrators' to the 'admin' user.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0303_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":361,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space.","A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0304","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich privilege level is assigned to VTY users?","options":{"A":"1","B":"7","C":"13","D":"15"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In the exhibit, the VTY lines (line vty 0 4 and line vty 5 15) are configured with a password and the login command, but there is no explicit privilege level assigned to these lines. In such cases, the default privilege level is 1. Therefore, the privilege level assigned to VTY users by default, when not explicitly set to a different level, is 1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0304_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":363,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0305","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which statements are used for error handling in Python?","options":{"A":"try/catch","B":"catch/release","C":"block/rescue","D":"try/except"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct statements for error handling in Python are 'try' and 'except'. The 'try' statement allows you to test a block of code for errors, while the 'except' statement enables you to handle the error. This is a fundamental construct in Python for managing exceptions, ensuring that programs can cope with errors gracefully and maintain robust error handling.","images":[],"sourcePage":364,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0306","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"How do agent-based versus agentless configuration management tools compare?","options":{"A":"Agentless tools use proxy nodes to interface with slave nodes.","B":"Agentless tools require no messaging systems between master and slaves.","C":"Agent-based tools do not require a high-level language interpreter such as Python or Ruby on slave nodes.","D":"Agent-based tools do not require installation of additional software packages on the slave nodes."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Agentless configuration management tools do not require any specific messaging systems between the master and slave nodes. They typically use standard communication protocols like SSH or WinRM to execute tasks directly on the slave nodes, thereby eliminating the need for specialized agents or messaging systems. This contrasts with agent-based tools, which require agents to be installed on managed nodes for communication and task execution.","images":[],"sourcePage":365,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0307","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich HTTP JSON response does the Python code output give?","options":{"A":"7.0(3)|7(4)","B":"7.61","C":"NameError: name 'json' is not defined","D":"KeyError: 'kickstart_ver_str'"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Python code sends an HTTP POST request to a specified URL with a JSON payload requesting the 'show version' command on a network device. The response JSON object contains various details about the device, including the 'kickstart_ver_str' key, which stores the version of the kickstart image.\n\nThe Python code then extracts and prints the value associated with 'kickstart_ver_str'. According to the provided JSON response, 'kickstart_ver_str' has the value '7.0(3)7(4)'. Therefore, the correct answer is 7.0(3)7(4).","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0307_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":366,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0308","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator is preparing a Python script to configure a Cisco IOS XE-based device on the network. The administrator is worried that colleagues will make changes to the device while the script is running.\nWhich operation of the ncclient manager prevents colleagues from making changes to the devices while the script is running?","options":{"A":"m.lock(config='running')","B":"m.lock(target='running')","C":"m.freeze(target='running')","D":"m.freeze(config='running')"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To prevent colleagues from making changes to the device while the script is running, the ncclient manager's lock operation should be used. Specifically, using m.lock(target='running') acquires a lock on the running configuration datastore of the device, ensuring modification protection. This lock remains in place until it is explicitly released, preventing any concurrent configuration changes by other users.","images":[],"sourcePage":367,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0309","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which outcome is achieved with this Python code?\nclient.connect (ip, port= 22, username= usr, password= pswd ) stdin, stdout, stderr = client.exec_command ( 'show ip bgp 192.168.101.0 bestpath\n' ) print (stdout)","options":{"A":"connects to a Cisco device using SSH and exports the BGP table for the prefix","B":"displays the output of the show command in a formatted way","C":"connects to a Cisco device using SSH and exports the routing table information","D":"connects to a Cisco device using Telnet and exports the routing table information"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The code connects to a device using SSH to execute the 'show ip bgp 192.168.101.0 bestpath' command and displays the output. It specifically queries the best BGP path for the given prefix.","images":[],"sourcePage":368,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0310","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which JSON syntax is derived from this data?","options":{"A":"{[{\"First Name\": \"Johnny\", \"Last Name\": \"Table\", \"Hobbies\": [\"Running\", \"Video games\"]}, {\"First Name\": \"Billy\", \"Last Name\": \"Smith\", \"Hobbies\": [\"Napping\", \"Reading\"]}]}","B":"{\"Person\": [{\"First Name\": \"Johnny\", \"Last Name\": \"Table\", \"Hobbies\": \"Running\", \"Video games\"}, {\"First Name\": \"Billy\", \"Last Name\": \"Smith\", \"Hobbies\": \"Napping\", \"Reading\"}]}","C":"{[{\"First Name\": \"Johnny\", \"Last Name\": \"Table\", \"Hobbies\": \"Running\", \"Hobbies\": \"Video games\"}, {\"First Name\": \"Billy\", \"Last Name\": \"Smith\", \"Hobbies\": \"Napping\", \"Reading\"}]}","D":"{\"Person\": [{\"First Name\": \"Johnny\", \"Last Name\": \"Table\", \"Hobbies\": [\"Running\", \"Video games\"]}, {\"First Name\": \"Billy\", \"Last Name\": \"Smith\", \"Hobbies\": [\"Napping, \"Reading]}]}"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct JSON syntax according to the provided data is: {'Person': [{'First Name': 'Johnny', 'Last Name': 'Table', 'Hobbies': ['Running', 'Video games']}, {'First Name': 'Billy', 'Last Name': 'Smith', 'Hobbies': ['Napping', 'Reading']}]}. Each person object includes their first name, last name, and a list of their hobbies, which is the correct way to represent this structure in JSON format.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0310_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":369,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0311","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which data is properly formatted with JSON?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct answer is B. For data to be properly formatted in JSON, it must follow specific syntax rules. JSON objects must have keys enclosed in double quotes, string values must also be enclosed in double quotes, and each key-value pair must be separated by a comma except the last one. In option B, all keys and string values are enclosed in double quotes, and all key-value pairs are correctly followed by a comma except the last one, which adheres to JSON formatting rules. Therefore, option B is the correctly formatted JSON data.","images":[],"sourcePage":371,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space.","The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0311_optA.webp","B":"images/q0311_optB.webp","C":"images/q0311_optC.webp","D":"images/q0311_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0312","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the output below, which Python code shows the value of the \"upTime\" key?","options":{"A":"json_data = response.json() print(json_data[\"response\"][0][\"upTime\"])","B":"json_data = response.json() print(json_data[response][0][upTime])","C":"json_data = json.loads(response.text) print(json_data[\"response\"][\"family\"][\"upTime\"])","D":"json_data = response.json() print(json_data[\"response\"][family][\"upTime\"])"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To retrieve the value of the \"upTime\" key from the given JSON structure, you need to correctly navigate through the structure using string keys and indexes. In Python, the response JSON is first converted to a dictionary using the response.json() method. The 'response' key holds a list, and you need to access the first item in that list, which is done using [0]. From there, you can access the 'upTime' key within that dictionary. Therefore, the correct code is: json_data = response.json() print(json_data['response'][0]['upTime']). Options B and D are incorrect because they attempt to use variables without correct string notation, and C is incorrect because it incorrectly navigates through the JSON structure.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0312_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":373,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0313","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which exhibit displays a valid JSON file?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The valid JSON format must adhere to specific syntax rules. Each key-value pair within an object must be separated by a comma, objects are enclosed in curly braces {}, and arrays are enclosed in square brackets []. Option D correctly represents a JSON object with the 'hostname' key as a string and the 'interfaces' key as an array of strings, enclosed in curly braces and square brackets respectively, and without an extra comma after the last value. Therefore, option D is the correct answer.","images":[],"sourcePage":375,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0313_optA.webp","B":"images/q0313_optB.webp","C":"images/q0313_optC.webp","D":"images/q0313_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0314","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhat is the JSON syntax that is formed from the data?","options":{"A":"{Name: Bob Johnson, Age: 75, Alive: true, Favorite Foods: [Cereal, Mustard, Onions]}","B":"{\"Name\" : \"Bob Johnson\", \"Age\": 75, \"Alive\": true, \"Favorite Foods\": [\"Cereal\", \"Mustard\", \"Onions\"]}","C":"{\"Name\": \"Bob Johnson\", \"Age\": 75, \"Alive\": True, \"Favorite Foods\": \"Cereal\", \"Mustard\", \"Onions\"}","D":"{\"Name\": \"Bob Johnson\", \"Age\": Seventyfive, \"Alive\": true, \"Favorite Foods\": [\"Cereal\", \"Mustard\", \"Onions\"]}"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To convert the given information to a JSON format, we need to follow JSON syntax rules precisely. All keys and string values should be enclosed in double quotes. Numerical values should not be enclosed in quotes, and booleans should be in lowercase. Arrays are denoted by square brackets. The correct JSON format is: {\"Name\": \"Bob Johnson\", \"Age\": 75, \"Alive\": true, \"Favorite Foods\": [\"Cereal\", \"Mustard\", \"Onions\"]}. This format follows all the proper syntax rules of JSON.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0314_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":377,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0315","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which JSON syntax is valid?","options":{"A":"{\"switch\": \"name\": \"dist\", \"interfaces\": [\"gig1\", \"gig2\", \"gig3\"]}","B":"{/\"switch/\": {/\"name/\": \"dist\", /\"interfaces/\": [\"gig1\", \"gig2\", \"gig3\"]}}","C":"{\"switch\": {\"name\": \"dist\", \"interfaces\": [\"gig1\", \"gig2\", \"gig3\"]}}","D":"{\"switch\": (\"name\": \"dist\", \"interfaces\": [\"gig1\", \"gig2\", \"gig3\"])}"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct JSON syntax includes key-value pairs where keys and string values are enclosed in double quotes. Additionally, nested objects should be enclosed in curly braces and arrays should be enclosed in square brackets. Option C follows this format correctly: it uses double quotes for keys and string values and correctly nests the object under the key 'switch', which contains the nested keys 'name' and 'interfaces' with their respective values.","images":[],"sourcePage":378,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0316","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is the structure of a JSON web token?","options":{"A":"three parts separated by dots: header, payload, and signature","B":"three parts separated by dots: version, header, and signature","C":"header and payload","D":"payload and signature"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A JSON Web Token (JWT) consists of three parts separated by dots: header, payload, and signature. This structure ensures secure information transmission by including metadata (header), claims (payload), and verification (signature).","images":[],"sourcePage":379,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0317","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"A response code of 404 is received while using the REST API on Cisco DNA Center to POST to this URI:\n/dna/intent/api/v1/template-programmer/project\nWhat does the code mean?","options":{"A":"The POST/PUT request was fulfilled and a new resource was created. Information about the resource is in the response body.","B":"The request was accepted for processing, but the processing was not completed.","C":"The client made a request for a resource that does not exist.","D":"The server has not implemented the functionality that is needed to fulfill the request."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A response code of 404, known as 'Not Found,' indicates that the client made a request for a resource that does not exist. This means that the server cannot find the requested resource. This is a typical response when the URI is not available or the endpoint is incorrect.","images":[],"sourcePage":380,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0318","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which two operations are valid for RESTCONF? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"PULL","B":"PUSH","C":"PATCH","D":"REMOVE","E":"ADD","F":"HEAD"},"correctAnswer":["C","F"],"explanation":"RESTCONF operations closely follow REST principles and are based on HTTP methods. The valid operations for RESTCONF include GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, and HEAD. PATCH is used for partial updates to a resource, and HEAD is used to retrieve metadata without a response body. Therefore, PATCH and HEAD are both valid operations for RESTCONF.","images":[],"sourcePage":381,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0319","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer is using XML in an application to send information to a RESTCONF-enabled device. After sending the request, the engineer gets this response message and an HTTP response code of 400. What do these responses tell the engineer?","options":{"A":"The Accept header sent was application/xml.","B":"POST was used instead of PUT to update.","C":"The Content-Type header sent was application/xml.","D":"A JSON body was used."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The HTTP response code of 400 indicates a bad request, which usually means there is an issue with the request sent to the server. In this case, the error message specifically states 'End-of-file reached in XML stream' along with an 'error-tag' of 'malformed-message'. This indicates that the server expected the request body in XML format, but it was improperly formed or incomplete. The presence of 'application/xml' in the error response suggests that the Content-Type header of the request was set to 'application/xml'. Therefore, the problem lies with the structure or completeness of the XML data sent, making the correct answer that the Content-Type header sent was 'application/xml'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0319_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":382,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0320","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is used to validate the authenticity of client and is sent in HTTP requests as a JSON object?","options":{"A":"SSH","B":"HTTPS","C":"JWT","D":"TLS"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"JSON Web Token (JWT) is used to validate the authenticity of clients and is sent in HTTP requests as a JSON object. It is a compact, URL-safe means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties. The claims in a JWT are encoded as a JSON object that is used as the payload of a JSON Web Signature (JWS) structure or as the plaintext of a JSON Web Encryption (JWE) structure, enabling the claims to be digitally signed or integrity protected with a Message Authentication Code (MAC) and/or encrypted.","images":[],"sourcePage":383,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0321","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is YANG used for?","options":{"A":"scraping data via CLI","B":"processing SNMP read-only polls","C":"describing data models","D":"providing a transport for network configuration data between client and server"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"YANG is used for describing data models. It is a data modeling language used to model configuration and state data manipulated by network management protocols like NETCONF and RESTCONF. YANG allows for the definition of data structures that are meant for network configurations, making it the correct choice.","images":[],"sourcePage":384,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0322","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which HTTP status code is the correct response for a request with an incorrect password applied to a REST API session?","options":{"A":"HTTP Status Code: 200","B":"HTTP Status Code: 302","C":"HTTP Status Code: 401","D":"HTTP Status Code: 504"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct HTTP status code for a request with an incorrect password applied to a REST API session is 401. This status code signifies 'Unauthorized', indicating that the client must authenticate itself to get the requested response. An incorrect password fits this scenario perfectly, as it means the authentication has failed, rendering the client unauthorized to access the resource.","images":[],"sourcePage":385,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0323","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which protocol does REST API rely on to secure the communication channel?","options":{"A":"HTTP","B":"SSH","C":"HTTPS","D":"TCP"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To achieve secure communication, REST APIs rely on HTTPS. HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) encrypts the communication channel, ensuring the confidentiality and integrity of the data exchanged between clients and servers. HTTP, while used for communication, does not provide the necessary security features, and other protocols like SSH and TCP do not serve the specific purpose of securing REST API communications in the same way.","images":[],"sourcePage":386,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0324","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"At which layer does Cisco DNA Center support REST controls?","options":{"A":"session layer","B":"northbound APIs","C":"EEM applets or scripts","D":"YAML output from responses to API calls"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Cisco DNA Center supports REST controls at the northbound layer. The northbound APIs in Cisco DNA Center expose specific capabilities of the platform through RESTful services. These APIs use HTTPS verbs such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE with JSON structures to facilitate network discovery and control.","images":[],"sourcePage":387,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0325","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which algorithms are used to secure REST API from brute attacks and minimize the impact?","options":{"A":"SHA-512 and SHA-384","B":"MD5 algorithm-128 and SHA-384","C":"SHA-1, SHA-256, and SHA-512","D":"PBKDF2, BCrypt, and SCrypt"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"PBKDF2, BCrypt, and SCrypt are specialized algorithms designed specifically for securely storing passwords by making brute-force attacks computationally expensive and time-consuming. Unlike SHA-512, SHA-384, MD5, and SHA-1, which are general-purpose hash functions primarily intended for data integrity, PBKDF2, BCrypt, and SCrypt include mechanisms such as key stretching to effectively increase the difficulty of attacks aimed at exploiting hash values, thus minimizing the impact of such attacks.","images":[],"sourcePage":388,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0326","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which method of account authentication does OAuth 2.0 use within REST APIs?","options":{"A":"username/role combination","B":"access tokens","C":"cookie authentication","D":"basic signature workflow"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"OAuth 2.0 primarily uses access tokens as the method of account authentication within REST APIs. Access tokens are used to provide secure access to resources without sharing credentials directly, enhancing security by allowing access to be granted and revoked without exposing a user's password.","images":[],"sourcePage":389,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0327","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which two protocols are used with YANG data models? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"TLS","B":"RESTCONF","C":"SSH","D":"NETCONF","E":"HTTPS"},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"YANG data models are typically used with the two protocols NETCONF and RESTCONF. NETCONF is a network management protocol specifically designed to handle YANG data, allowing for easy retrieval, configuration, and control of network devices. RESTCONF is a REST-like protocol that provides similar functionalities for YANG data models, offering a simpler and more easily accessible interface using HTTP methods. These two protocols are closely associated with YANG data models, aligning well with industry standards and practices.","images":[],"sourcePage":390,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0328","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is a benefit of data modeling languages like YANG?","options":{"A":"They create more secure and efficient SNMP OIDs.","B":"They provide a standardized data structure, which results in configuration scalability and consistency.","C":"They enable programmers to change or write their own applications within the device operating system.","D":"They make the CLI simpler and more efficient."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Data modeling languages like YANG provide a standardized data structure, which facilitates configuration scalability and consistency across network devices. This uniformity simplifies the management of complex networks and ensures that configurations can be easily replicated and maintained.","images":[],"sourcePage":391,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0329","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which protocol infers that a YANG data model is being used?","options":{"A":"SNMP","B":"RESTCONF","C":"REST","D":"NX-API"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The protocol that infers a YANG data model is being used is RESTCONF. RESTCONF specifically supports accessing data defined in YANG models and is designed for network management using YANG data models.","images":[],"sourcePage":392,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0330","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which method displays text directly into the active console with a synchronous EEM applet policy?","options":{"A":"event manager applet boom event syslog pattern 'UP' action 1.0 syslog priority direct msg 'logging directly to console'","B":"event manager applet boom event syslog pattern 'UP' action 1.0 gets 'logging directly to console'","C":"event manager applet boom event syslog pattern 'UP' action 1.0 string 'logging directly to console'","D":"event manager applet boom event syslog pattern 'UP' action 1.0 puts 'logging directly to console'"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To display text directly into the active console with a synchronous EEM applet policy, the correct method is to use the 'action puts' command. This command prints data directly to the local tty in a synchronous applet. The other options do not provide the functionality to print data directly to the console.","images":[],"sourcePage":393,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0331","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which two steps are required for a complete Cisco DNA Center upgrade? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"automation backup","B":"system update","C":"golden image selection","D":"proxy configuration","E":"application updates"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"A complete Cisco DNA Center upgrade requires both a system update and application updates. The system update ensures that the base system software is current, while the application updates guarantee that all applications running on Cisco DNA Center are up-to-date. These steps are critical for maintaining the stability and functionality of the network management system during and after the upgrade process.","images":[],"sourcePage":394,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0332","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which method creates an EEM applet policy that is registered with EEM and runs on demand or manually?","options":{"A":"event manager applet ondemand event none action 1.0 syslog priority critical msg 'This is a message from ondemand'","B":"event manager applet ondemand action 1.0 syslog priority critical msg 'This is a message from ondemand'","C":"event manager applet ondemand event register action 1.0 syslog priority critical msg 'This is a message from ondemand'","D":"event manager applet ondemand event manual action 1.0 syslog priority critical msg 'This is a message from ondemand'"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The method to create an EEM applet policy that can be manually triggered or run on demand involves using the 'event none' keyword. This keyword ensures that the policy does not automatically trigger based on predefined events but instead can be executed manually by an administrator using the 'event manager run' command. This registration method allows the applet to be flexible and operator-driven.","images":[],"sourcePage":395,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0333","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What does this EEM applet event accomplish?\n\"event snmp oid 1.3.6.1.3.7.6.5.3.9.3.8.7 get-type next entry-op gt entry-val 75 poll-interval 5\"","options":{"A":"Upon the value reaching 75%, a SNMP event is generated and sent to the trap server.","B":"It reads an SNMP variable, and when the value exceeds 75%, it triggers an action.","C":"It issues email when the value is greater than 75% for five polling cycles.","D":"It presents a SNMP variable that can be interrogated."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The EEM (Embedded Event Manager) applet event described monitors a specific SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) OID (Object Identifier). It uses the 'get-type' parameter to check the value of this OID and evaluates it every specified interval (poll-interval 5). The 'entry-op gt entry-val 75' condition specifies that when the value of the SNMP variable exceeds 75, an action is triggered. Therefore, it accurately describes the process of reading an SNMP variable and triggering an action when the specified condition is met.","images":[],"sourcePage":396,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0334","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is a requirement for an Ansible-managed node?","options":{"A":"It must have an SSH server running.","B":"It must be a Linux server or a Cisco device.","C":"It must support ad hoc commands.","D":"It must have an Ansible Tower installed."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Ansible, by default, requires an SSH server to be running on the managed nodes in order to establish connections and execute tasks. While alternative transport methods like WinRM for Windows nodes exist, SSH is the most common and fundamental connection method used in the majority of deployments. Therefore, having an SSH server running on the managed node is a basic requirement for effective management using Ansible.","images":[],"sourcePage":397,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0335","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which characteristic distinguishes Ansible from Chef?","options":{"A":"Ansible uses Ruby to manage configurations. Chef uses YAML to manage configurations.","B":"The Ansible server can run on Linux, Unix or Windows. The Chef server must run on Linux or Unix.","C":"Ansible pushes the configuration to the client. Chef client pulls the configuration from the server.","D":"Ansible lacks redundancy support for the primary server. Chef runs two primary servers in active/active mode."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Ansible operates using a push model where the server pushes configurations to the client machines, whereas Chef uses a pull model where the client machines pull the configurations from a central server. This key distinction highlights that Ansible initiates communication from the server to the managed nodes, while Chef requires the client nodes to actively fetch their configuration from the server.","images":[],"sourcePage":398,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0336","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the orchestration tools that they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0336_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0336_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":399,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0337","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What does the snippet of code achieve?","options":{"A":"It creates an SSH connection using the SSH key that is stored, and the password is ignored.","B":"It creates a temporary connection to a Cisco Nexus device and retrieves a token to be used for API calls.","C":"It opens an ncclient connection to a Cisco Nexus device and maintains it for the duration of the context.","D":"It opens a tunnel and encapsulates the login information, if the host key is correct."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The code snippet shown is using the ncclient library to establish an ncclient connection to a Cisco Nexus device. The parameters include the host IP address, port number, and login credentials (username and password). The connection is maintained temporarily for the duration of the `with` statement context. During this period, operations can be performed over this connection using the NETCONF protocol to manage the Cisco Nexus device.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0337_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":401,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0338","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which two characteristics define the Intent API provided by Cisco DNA Center? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"northbound API","B":"business outcome oriented","C":"device-oriented","D":"southbound API","E":"procedural"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"The Intent API provided by Cisco DNA Center is characterized by being a northbound API and being business outcome oriented. A northbound API allows external applications to communicate with and control the network, often used for integrating with business applications and controllers. Being business outcome oriented means that the API focuses on achieving specific business goals and outcomes rather than being concerned with the specifics of the underlying devices and procedures.","images":[],"sourcePage":402,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0339","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco DNA Center Plug and Play environment, why would a device be labeled unclaimed?","options":{"A":"The device has not been assigned a workflow.","B":"The device could not be added to the fabric.","C":"The device had an error and could not be provisioned.","D":"The device is from a third-party vendor."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco DNA Center Plug and Play environment, a device is labeled 'unclaimed' when it has not been assigned a workflow. This indicates that the device is recognized by the system but has not yet been configured or set up to do any specific tasks or operations within the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":403,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0340","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What does the Cisco DNA Center use to enable the delivery of applications through a network and to yield analytics for innovation?","options":{"A":"process adapters","B":"Command Runner","C":"intent-based APIs","D":"domain adapters"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Cisco DNA Center utilizes intent-based APIs to enable the delivery of applications through a network and to provide analytics for innovation. Intent-based APIs allow Cisco DNA Center to interpret and implement the intents of business and IT applications directly into network operations, ensuring efficient policy provisioning, software image management, assurance, and overall network management. These APIs facilitate automation and data analytics capabilities, which are critical for innovation and operational efficiency.","images":[],"sourcePage":404,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0341","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which devices does Cisco DNA Center configure when deploying an IP-based access control policy?","options":{"A":"all devices integrating with ISE","B":"selected individual devices","C":"all devices in selected sites","D":"all wired devices"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When deploying an IP-based access control policy, Cisco DNA Center configures all devices in the selected sites. This ensures that the policy is consistently applied across the entire scope of the site's network, affecting all devices within the defined area. This approach helps maintain a uniform security and access control policy throughout a specific network segment, simplifying management and enforcement.","images":[],"sourcePage":405,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0342","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which tool is used in Cisco DNA Center to build generic configurations that are able to be applied on devices with similar network settings?","options":{"A":"Command Runner","B":"Application Policies","C":"Template Editor","D":"Authentication Template"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The Template Editor in Cisco DNA Center is specifically designed to create and manage CLI templates. These templates allow for the building of generic configurations that can be applied to multiple devices with similar network settings, making it the appropriate tool for this task.","images":[],"sourcePage":406,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0343","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"In which part of the HTTP message is the content type specified?","options":{"A":"HTTP method","B":"body","C":"header","D":"URI"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The content type of an HTTP message is specified in the header. This information is not found in the HTTP method, body, or URI, but in the header section where various metadata about the request or response is included. The Content-Type header specifically indicates the media type of the resource and informs the recipient how to interpret the data in the body of the message.","images":[],"sourcePage":407,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0344","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must create an EEM applet that sends a syslog message in the event a change happens in the network due to trouble with an OSPF process. Which action should the engineer use? event manager applet LogMessage event routing network 172.30.197.0/24 type all","options":{"A":"action 1 syslog msg ‫ג‬€OSPF ROUTING ERROR‫ג‬€","B":"action 1 syslog send ‫ג‬€OSPF ROUTING ERROR‫ג‬€","C":"action 1 syslog pattern ‫ג‬€OSPF ROUTING ERROR‫ג‬€","D":"action 1 syslog write ‫ג‬€OSPF ROUTING ERROR‫ג‬€"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The correct action for sending a syslog message in an EEM applet is to use the syntax 'action 1 syslog msg '. In the context of the provided question, this would mean using 'action 1 syslog msg OSPF ROUTING ERROR' to send the appropriate syslog message when an OSPF process encounters trouble. Other options such as 'pattern', 'send', or 'write' are not supported syntax for this specific action.","images":[],"sourcePage":408,"flags":["The PDF text contains garbled quote characters (shown as ג€ or similar); kept exactly as extracted."]},{"id":"q0345","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer runs the code against an API of Cisco DNA Center, and the platform returns this output. What does the response indicate?","options":{"A":"The authentication credentials are incorrect.","B":"The URI string is incorrect.","C":"The Cisco DNA Center API port is incorrect.","D":"The HTTP method is incorrect."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The HTTP response code 405 indicates that the method specified in the request is not allowed for the resource identified by the URI. In the provided code, the engineer is using an HTTP GET request to obtain a token from the Cisco DNA Center API endpoint. However, the correct method for this operation should be an HTTP POST request. Therefore, the response indicates that the HTTP method used in the code is incorrect.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0345_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":409,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0346","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What are two benefits of YANG? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"It enforces the use of a specific encoding format for NETCONF.","B":"It collects statistical constraint analysis information.","C":"It enables multiple leaf statements to exist within a leaf list.","D":"It enforces configuration semantics.","E":"It enforces configuration constraints."},"correctAnswer":["D","E"],"explanation":"YANG provides a structured and standardized way to define the configuration and operational semantics of network devices, ensuring clear and consistent data model definitions. This helps in ensuring that network devices are configured correctly and consistently. Additionally, YANG allows for the definition of constraints and validation rules on configuration data, ensuring that it adheres to specific requirements related to data type, range, length, and dependencies between configuration elements. By enforcing these constraints, YANG helps prevent misconfigurations and improves the overall reliability and stability of the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":411,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0347","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the orchestration tools they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0347_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0347_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":412,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0348","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After running the code in the exhibit, which step reduces the amount of data that the NETCONF server returns to the NETCONF client, to only the interface's configuration?","options":{"A":"Use the Ixml library to parse the data returned by the NETCONF server for the interface's configuration.","B":"Create an XML filter as a string and pass it to get_config() method as an argument.","C":"Create a JSON filter as a string and pass it to the get_config() method as an argument.","D":"Use the JSON library to parse the data returned by the NETCONF server for the interface's configuration."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To reduce the amount of data that the NETCONF server returns to the NETCONF client to only the interface's configuration, an XML filter should be created and passed to the get_config() method as an argument. This will ensure that only the relevant portion of the configuration is returned, minimizing the data transfer and processing required. Using libraries to parse the data after it has been received does not reduce the amount of data initially transferred from the server.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0348_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":414,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0349","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Running the script causes the output in the exhibit. Which change to the first line of the script resolves the error?","options":{"A":"from ncclient import","B":"import manager","C":"from ncclient import *","D":"import ncclient manager"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the error, the correct way to import the necessary module is 'from ncclient import manager'. This import statement explicitly brings in the 'manager' module from the 'ncclient' package, allowing the code to access 'ncclient.manager' correctly. This avoids the AttributeError ('module' object has no attribute 'manager') caused by trying to directly use 'ncclient.manager.connect' without properly importing 'manager'. Non-specific imports, such as those using wildcard '*' or incorrect formats like 'import manager', will not address the problem adequately.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0349_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":415,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0350","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which line must be added in the Python function to return the JSON object {`cat_9k`: `FXS1932Q2SE`}?","options":{"A":"return (json.dumps({d['hostname']: d['serialNumber'] for d in json.loads(test_json)['response']}))","B":"return (json.dumps({for d in json.loads(test_json)['response']: d['hostname']: d['serialNumber']}))","C":"return (json.loads({d['hostname']: d['serialNumber'] for d in json.dumps(test_json)['response'}))","D":"return (json.loads({for d in json.dumps(test_json)['response']: d['hostname']: d['serialNumber']}))"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To return the JSON object {'cat_9k': 'FXS1932Q2SE'} from the given JSON string, the correct line to be added is: return (json.dumps({d['hostname']: d['serialNumber'] for d in json.loads(test_json) ['response']})). This line parses the JSON string into a Python dictionary using json.loads, extracts the 'hostname' and 'serialNumber' from each entry in the 'response' list, and creates a new dictionary with 'hostname' as keys and 'serialNumber' as values. Finally, json.dumps converts this dictionary back into a JSON string, which matches the required format.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0350_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":417,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0351","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which code results in the working Python script displaying a list of network devices from the Cisco DNA Center?","options":{"A":"network_device_list(dnac[‫ג‬€host‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€username‫ג‬€],dnac[‫ג‬€password‫ג‬€]) login = dnac_login(dnac) print(dnac_devices)","B":"login = dnac_login(dnac[‫ג‬€host‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€username‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€password‫ג‬€]) network_device_list(dnac, login) print(dnac_devices)","C":"login = dnac_login(dnac[‫ג‬€host‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€username‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€password‫ג‬€]) network_device_list(dnac, login) for item in dnac_devices: print(dnac_devices.item)","D":"network_device_list(dnac[‫ג‬€host‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€username‫ג‬€], dnac[‫ג‬€password‫ג‬€]) login = dnac_login(dnac) for item in dnac_devices: print(dnac_devices.item)"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct sequence of function calls in the script ensures that the user first logs in to get an authorization token and then uses this token to retrieve and display the list of network devices. The script works as follows: First, the user logs in using the dnac_login function, which returns a token. This token is then passed to the network_device_list function to retrieve the network devices. Finally, the dnac_devices table, which has been populated with the network device data, is printed. This approach correctly handles the authentication and data retrieval process, ensuring that the devices are displayed as intended.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0351_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":419,"flags":["The PDF text contains garbled quote characters (shown as ג€ or similar); kept exactly as extracted."]},{"id":"q0352","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What does the Cisco DNA REST response indicate?","options":{"A":"Cisco DNA Center has the incorrect credentials for cat3850-1","B":"Cisco DNA Center is unable to communicate with cat9000-1","C":"Cisco DNA Center has the incorrect credentials for cat9000-1","D":"Cisco DNA Center has the incorrect credentials for RouterASR-1"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The Cisco DNA REST response indicates that Cisco DNA Center has the incorrect credentials for cat9000-1 because its reachability status is 'Authentication Failed.' This status typically means that the device is reachable but the authentication credentials used were not accepted, implying incorrect credentials were provided for cat9000-1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0352_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":421,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0353","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of YANG?","options":{"A":"It is a Cisco proprietary language that models NETCONF data.","B":"It allows model developers to create custom data types.","C":"It structures data in an object-oriented fashion to promote model reuse.","D":"It provides loops and conditionals to control flow within models."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"YANG is a data modeling language developed by the IETF, not Cisco proprietary. One of its key characteristics is that it allows model developers to create custom data types. This feature enhances the expressiveness and flexibility of YANG data models, accommodating various network configuration needs. Therefore, the correct characteristic of YANG is that it allows model developers to create custom data types.","images":[],"sourcePage":423,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0354","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to construct a script that changes the routing from gateway 1 to gateway 2 from 11:00 p.m. to 12:00 a.m. (2300 to 2400) only, daily. Not all options are used, and some options may be used twice.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0354_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0354_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":424,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0355","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The IP SLA is configured in a router. An engineer must configure an EEM applet to shut down the interface and bring it back up when there is a problem with the IP SLA. Which configuration should the engineer use?","options":{"A":"event manager applet EEM_IP_SLA event track 10 state down","B":"event manager applet EEM_IP_SLA event track 10 state unreachable","C":"event manager applet EEM_IP_SLA event sla 10 state unreachable","D":"event manager applet EEM_IP_SLA event sla 10 state down"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To configure an EEM applet to monitor the IP SLA and take action when there is a problem, you use the 'event' command to specify the condition. The IP SLA tracked object's state will be either 'up' or 'down'. The correct event syntax in the EEM applet should reference the state's transition to 'down'. Therefore, the configuration 'event track 10 state down' is correct.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0355_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":425,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0356","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhat is the value of the variable list after the code is run?","options":{"A":"[1, 2], [1, 2], [1, 2]","B":"[1, 2] * 3","C":"[1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2]","D":"[3, 6]"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The code initializes a list with two integers, [1, 2]. When the list variable is multiplied by 3, it repeats the contents of the list three times, resulting in [1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2]. Therefore, the value of the variable list after the code runs is [1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2].","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0356_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":426,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0357","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich result does the Python code achieve?","options":{"A":"The code encrypts a base64 decrypted password.","B":"The code converts time to the Epoch LINUX time format.","C":"The code converts time to the \"year/month/day\" time format.","D":"The code converts time to the yyyymmdd representation."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The Python code decodes a base64-encoded string, strips any newline characters, gets the current date, and then formats this date as a string in the 'yyyymmdd' format. The operations performed in the code indeed produce a string representing the date in the 'yyyymmdd' format by multiplying the year by 10000, the month by 100, and then adding the day value.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0357_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":427,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0358","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nPOSTMAN is showing an attempt to retrieve network device information from Cisco DNA Center API. What is the issue?","options":{"A":"The URI string is incorrect.","B":"The token has expired.","C":"Authentication has failed.","D":"The JSON payload contains the incorrect UUID."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The issue here is that the URI string is incorrect. The path specified in the request is '/network-devices', but it should be '/network-device' (without the 's'). The error message 's is not a valid UUID of device' indicates that the server is interpreting 's' as an incorrect UUID due to the malformed URI. This kind of syntax error in the URI results in a 400 Bad Request status.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0358_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":428,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0359","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nRunning the script causes the output in the exhibit. What should be the first line of the script?","options":{"A":"from ncclient import manager","B":"import manager","C":"from ncclient import *","D":"ncclient manager import"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The issue in the provided script is that it tries to access the 'manager' attribute from the 'ncclient' module, which is causing the AttributeError. To correctly reference 'manager' within the 'ncclient' package, the script must explicitly import the 'manager' module from 'ncclient'. Therefore, the first line of the script should be 'from ncclient import manager'. This import statement ensures that the 'manager' module is recognized and can be used in the subsequent lines of the script.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0359_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":430,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0360","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer must create a script that appends the output of the show process cpu sorted command to a file. Which action completes the configuration?","options":{"A":"action 4.0 syslog command \"show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file\"","B":"action 4.0 cli command \"show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file\"","C":"action 4.0 cns-event \"show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file\"","D":"action 4.0 publish-event \"show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file\""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct action to append the output of the 'show process cpu sorted' command to a file using EEM (Embedded Event Manager) is to use the CLI command action. This can be done by specifying 'cli command' followed by the desired command. In this case, the command to append the output to a file should be 'show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file'. Therefore, action 4.0 should be 'cli command show process cpu sorted | append flash:high-cpu-file'. The 'cli command' option allows you to execute a CLI command, making option B the correct choice.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0360_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":431,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0361","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is an advantage of utilizing data models in a multivendor environment?","options":{"A":"lowering CPU load incurred to managed devices","B":"improving communication security with binary encoded protocols","C":"facilitating a unified approach to configuration and management","D":"removing the distinction between configuration and runtime state data"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a multivendor environment, data models provide a unified approach to configuration and management. This standardization allows for easier integration and interoperability among devices from different manufacturers, ensuring consistent and efficient management practices.","images":[],"sourcePage":432,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0362","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"How is a data modeling language used?","options":{"A":"To enable data to be easily structured, grouped, validated, and replicated.","B":"To represent finite and well-defined network elements that cannot be changed.","C":"To model the flows of unstructured data within the infrastructure.","D":"To provide human readability to scripting languages."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A data modeling language is used to enable data to be easily structured, grouped, validated, and replicated. It organizes and structures data in a consistent and standardized manner, which facilitates easier processing, management, and replication of data across different systems. It is essential in various applications such as network configurations, databases, and other data management systems where clear organization and structure are critical.","images":[],"sourcePage":433,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0363","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer is configuring OSPF on a router. The engineer wants to prevent having a route to 172.16.0.0/16 learned via OSPF in the routing table and configures a prefix list using the command `ip prefix-list OFFICE seq 5 deny 172.16.0.0/16`. Which two additional configuration commands must be applied to accomplish the goal? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"ip prefix-list OFFICE seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 le 32","B":"distribute-list prefix OFFICE in under the OSPF process","C":"distribute-list OFFICE in under the OSPF process","D":"distribute-list OFFICE out under the OSPF process","E":"ip prefix-list OFFICE seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 ge 32"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"To prevent a specific route from being learned via OSPF, the network engineer needs to create a prefix list that denies the route and permits all others, then apply this prefix list with a distribute-list command. The command 'ip prefix-list OFFICE seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 le 32' ensures all other routes are permitted. Applying 'distribute-list prefix OFFICE in under the OSPF process' will filter incoming OSPF updates based on the prefix list, ensuring the route 172.16.0.0/16 is denied and not added to the routing table.","images":[],"sourcePage":434,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0364","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the technology types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0364_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0364_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":435,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0365","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command set must be applied on R1 to establish a BGP neighborship with R2 and to allow communication from R1 to reach the networks?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"The correct command set to establish a BGP neighborship with R2 and allow communication from R1 to reach the networks is as follows: router bgp 1200 network 209.165.200.224 mask 255.255.255.224 neighbor 209.165.202.130 remote-as 1201 Explanation: For BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) configuration, R1 needs to specify its own Autonomous System (AS) number (1200), the network it will advertise (209.165.200.224 with mask 255.255.255.224), and the neighbor it will connect to, which is R2 with IP address 209.165.202.130 and its AS number (1201). This configuration ensures both the proper establishment of the BGP neighborship and the advertisement of the correct network.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0365_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":436,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0365_optA.webp","B":"images/q0365_optB.webp","C":"images/q0365_optC.webp","D":"images/q0365_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0366","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command must be configured for RESTCONF to operate on port 8888?","options":{"A":"restconf port 8888","B":"ip http restconf port 8888","C":"ip http port 8888","D":"restconf http port 8888"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To configure RESTCONF to operate on a specific port, you need to set the HTTP server port to the desired value. The command 'ip http port 8888' configures the HTTP server to listen on port 8888, which is necessary for RESTCONF communication on that port.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0366_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":438,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0367","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Why would a log file contain a * next to the date?","options":{"A":"The network device was receiving NTP time when the log messages were recorded.","B":"The network device was unable to reach the NTP server when the log messages were recorded.","C":"The network device is not configured to use NTP.","D":"The network device is not configured to use NTP time stamps for logging."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A log file will contain an asterisk (*) next to the date when the network device is not configured to use NTP. This indicates that the system clock has not been set by an authoritative time source, like NTP, and the date and time are likely incorrect.","images":[],"sourcePage":439,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0368","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"mcq","question":"What is one difference between EIGRP and OSPF?","options":{"A":"EIGRP uses the DUAL distance vector algorithm, and OSPF uses the Dijkstra link-state algorithm.","B":"OSPF uses the DUAL distance vector algorithm, and EIGRP uses the Dijkstra link-state algorithm.","C":"EIGRP uses the variance command for unequal cost load balancing, and OSPF supports unequal cost balancing by default.","D":"OSPF is a Cisco proprietary protocol, and EIGRP is an IETF open standard protocol."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"EIGRP uses the DUAL (Diffusing Update Algorithm), a distance vector protocol, which helps it determine the best path for data transmission through a network. On the other hand, OSPF uses the Dijkstra algorithm, a link-state routing protocol, to create a shortest path tree for each of its networks. This fundamental difference in the underlying algorithm of each protocol is a key distinction between EIGRP and OSPF.","images":[],"sourcePage":440,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0369","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the routing protocols they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0369_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0369_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":441,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0370","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer must configure a router to send logging messages to a syslog server based on these requirements:\nuses syslog IP address: 10.10.10.1\nuses a reliable protocol\nmust not use any well-known TCP/UDP ports\nWhich configuration must be used?","options":{"A":"logging host 10.10.10.1 transport tcp port 1024","B":"logging host 10.10.10.1 transport udp port 1024","C":"logging host 10.10.10.1 transport udp port 1023","D":"logging origin-id 10.10.10.1"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To configure a router to send logging messages to a syslog server based on the requirements provided, the configuration must ensure that a reliable protocol is used and does not utilize any well-known TCP/UDP ports. TCP is considered a reliable protocol due to its connection-oriented nature, ensuring that the data sent is received correctly. The well-known ports range from 0 to 1023, so using port 1024 falls outside of this range. Therefore, the correct configuration is 'logging host 10.10.10.1 transport tcp port 1024'.","images":[],"sourcePage":442,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0371","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to construct a script that shows all logging that occurred on the appliance from Sunday until 9:00 p.m.Thursday. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0371_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0371_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":443,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0372","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the infrastructure deployment models on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0372_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0372_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":444,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0373","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer must log in to the router via the console, but the RADIUS servers are not reachable. Which credentials allow console access?","options":{"A":"no username and only the password ‫ג‬€test123‫ג‬€","B":"no username and only the password ‫ג‬€cisco123‫ג‬€","C":"the username ‫ג‬€cisco‫ג‬€ and the password ‫ג‬€cisco‫ג‬€","D":"the username ‫ג‬€cisco‫ג‬€ and the password ‫ג‬€cisco123‫ג‬€"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct answer is that no username is needed, but the password 'cisco123' will allow console access. The configuration shows that the console line (line con 0) uses login authentication group1. In the defined AAA authentication method for group1, if the RADIUS servers are not reachable, the router will fall back to the line password. The line con 0 is configured with the password 'cisco123', so using this password will grant access to the console.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0373_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":445,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space.","The PDF text contains garbled quote characters (shown as ג€ or similar); kept exactly as extracted."]},{"id":"q0374","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the orchestration tools that they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0374_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0374_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":447,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0375","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which configuration allows administrators to configure the device through the console port and use a network authentication server?","options":{"A":"aaa new-model aaa authentication login default local aaa authorization console aaa authorization config-commands username netadmin secret 9 $9$vFpMf8elb4RVV8$seZ/bDAx1uV","B":"aaa new-model aaa authentication login default local aaa authorization console aaa authorization config-commands","C":"aaa new-model aaa authentication login default line","D":"aaa new-model aaa authentication login default group radius aaa authorization console aaa authorization config-commands"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct configuration to allow administrators to configure the device through the console port and use a network authentication server involves setting up AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) with a RADIUS server. The option that includes 'aaa authentication login default group radius' ensures that the device uses a network authentication server (RADIUS) for login. Additionally, it includes 'aaa authorization console' and 'aaa authorization config-commands' to control command authorization. Therefore, option D is the correct configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":448,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0376","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the process for moving a virtual machine from one host machine to another with no downtime?","options":{"A":"high availability","B":"disaster recovery","C":"live migration","D":"multisite replication"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The process for moving a virtual machine from one host machine to another with no downtime is called live migration. This technique allows the virtual machine to be transferred without the need to disconnect the client or application, ensuring continuous operation.","images":[],"sourcePage":449,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0377","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the infrastructure deployment models they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0377_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0377_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":450,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0378","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the routing protocols they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0378_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0378_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":451,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0379","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the tools from the left onto the agent types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0379_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0379_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":452,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0380","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must create a configuration that prevents R3 from receiving the LSA about 172.16.1.4/32. Which configuration set achieves this goal?","options":{"A":"On R3 ip access-list standard R4_L0 deny host 172.16.1.4 permit any router ospf 200 distribute-list R4_L0 in","B":"On R1 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 5 deny 172.16.1.4/32 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 le 32 router ospf 200 area 1 filter-list prefix INTO-AREA1 out","C":"On R1 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 5 deny 172.16.1.4/32 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 le 32 router ospf 200 area 1 filter-list prefix INTO-AREA1 in","D":"On R3 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 5 deny 172.16.1.4/32 ip prefix-list INTO-AREA1 seq 10 permit 0.0.0.0/0 le 32 router ospf 200 area 1 filter-list prefix INTO-AREA1 in"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To prevent R3 from receiving the LSA about 172.16.1.4/32, the correct configuration should be applied on R1 since R1 is the Area Border Router (ABR) between Area 0 and Area 1. The specific command set must filter the route when advertising routes into Area 1. This involves using a prefix-list to deny the specific route and permitting all other routes, and then applying this prefix-list as an inbound filter for Area 1. Therefore, the correct configuration should be applied on R1 with the inbound direction specified, which matches the provided configuration set in option C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0380_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":453,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0381","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer is reaching network 172.16.10.0/24 via the R1-R2-R4 path. Which configuration forces the traffic to take a path of R1-R3-R4?","options":{"A":"R1(config)#route-map RM_LOCAL_PREF permit 10 R1(config-route-map)#set local-preference 101 R1(config-route-map)#exit R1(config)#router bgp 100\nR1(config-router)#neighbor 13.13.13.3 route-map RM_LOCAL_PREF in\nR1(config-router)#end R1#clear ip bgp 13.13.13.3 soft in","B":"R1(config)#route-map RM_AS_PATH_PREPEND R1(config-route-map)#set as-path prepend 200 200 R1(config-route-map)#exit R1(config)#router bgp 100 R1(config-router)#neighbor 12.12.12.2 route-map RM_AS_PATH_PREPEND in R1(config-router)#end R1#clear ip bgp 12.12.12.2 soft in","C":"R1(config)#router bgp 100 R1(config-router)#neighbor 13.13.13.3 weight 1 R1(config-router)#end","D":"R2(config)#route-map RM_MED permit 10 R2(config-route-map)#set metric 1 R2(config-route-map)#exit R2(config)#router bgp 200 R2(config-router)#neighbor 12.12.12.1 route-map RM_MED out R2(config-router)#end R2#clear ip bgp 12.12.12.1 soft out"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To ensure that traffic takes the path through R1-R3-R4, you need to prioritize that path over the current R1-R2-R4 path. The local preference attribute is effective for making this decision within an AS. Setting the local preference to a higher value for routes learned from R3 (AS200) will cause traffic to prefer the R1-R3-R4 path. The configuration provided in option A does exactly this by setting a higher local preference value (101) for the incoming routes from neighbor 13.13.13.3 (R3). This makes the routes through R3 more preferred compared to the default local preference of 100. Hence, option A is the correct configuration.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0381_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":455,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0382","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access solution, which protocol is used by an extended node to connect to a single edge node?","options":{"A":"VXLAN","B":"IS-IS","C":"802.1Q","D":"CTS"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access solution, the protocol used by an extended node to connect to a single edge node is 802.1Q. 802.1Q is a networking standard that supports VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network) tagging on an Ethernet network, allowing for segregation and management of logical networks within the same physical network infrastructure.","images":[],"sourcePage":457,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0383","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. How does the router handle traffic after the CoPP policy is configured on the router?","options":{"A":"Traffic generated by R1 that matches access list SNMP is policed.","B":"Traffic coming to R1 that matches access list SNMP is policed.","C":"Traffic passing through R1 that matches access list SNMP is policed.","D":"Traffic coming to R1 that does not match access list SNMP is dropped."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0383_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":458,"flags":["No explanation in the source PDF."]},{"id":"q0384","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of Cisco StackWise technology?","options":{"A":"It is supported on the Cisco 4500 series.","B":"It supports devices that are geographically separated","C":"It combines exactly two devices.","D":"It uses proprietary cabling."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Cisco StackWise technology uses proprietary cabling to connect and manage multiple switches as a single unit. This characteristic allows the switches to operate as a unified system, enhancing performance, redundancy, and efficiency. The use of proprietary cabling is a defining feature of this technology, distinguishing it from other methods that can support geographically separated devices or use standard cabling.","images":[],"sourcePage":459,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0385","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is one primary REST security design principle?","options":{"A":"fail-safe defaults","B":"password hash","C":"adding a timestamp in requests","D":"OAuth"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"OAuth is a primary REST security design principle. It is an industry-standard protocol for authorization that allows applications to securely access assets on behalf of a user. It is widely used to grant third-party applications limited access to user accounts without exposing user credentials.","images":[],"sourcePage":460,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0386","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After the code is run on a Cisco IOS-XE router, the response code is 204. What is the result of the script?","options":{"A":"The configuration fails because interface GigabitEthernet2 is missing on the target device.","B":"Interface GigabitEthemet2 is configured with IP address 10.10.10.1/24.","C":"The configuration fails because another interface is already configured with IP address 10.10.10.1/24.","D":"The configuration is successfully sent to the device in cleartext."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"After running the script, the HTTP response code returned is 204, which indicates that the request to configure the device was successfully processed but no content is being returned in the response body. Given that HTTP status codes in the 2xx range signify a successful action, and 204 specifically shows no additional content, it can be concluded that the GigabitEthernet2 interface was configured with the IP address 10.10.10.1/24 as stated in the script.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0386_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":461,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0387","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which time protocol offers security features and utilizes site-local IPv6 multicast addresses?","options":{"A":"NTPv3","B":"PTP IEEE 1588v1","C":"NTPv4","D":"PTP IEEE 1588v2"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"PTP IEEE 1588v2 is the time protocol that offers security features and utilizes site-local IPv6 multicast addresses. While NTPv4 does support IPv6, it does not specifically utilize site-local IPv6 multicast addresses and is generally not known for providing advanced security features as compared to PTP IEEE 1588v2. PTP IEEE 1588v2, also known as Precision Time Protocol version 2, includes enhancements for better time synchronization accuracy and security features addressing the needs of modern networks.","images":[],"sourcePage":463,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0388","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command MUST be applied to Router1 to bring the GRE tunnel to an up/up state?","options":{"A":"Router1(config-if)#tunnel source Loopback0","B":"Router1(config-if)#tunnel mode gre multipoint","C":"Router1(config-if)#tunnel source GigabitEthernet0/1","D":"Router1(config)#interface tunnel0"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To bring the GRE tunnel to an up/up state, the configuration must specify a valid source IP address for the tunnel's source interface. The current configuration shows that neither GigabitEthernet0/1 nor any other GigabitEthernet interfaces have an IP address assigned or are in an up state, making them invalid sources for the tunnel. The Loopback0 interface has a valid IP address (192.168.10.1) and is in an up state, making it a suitable tunnel source. By configuring the tunnel source as Loopback0 with the command 'Router1(config-if)#tunnel source Loopback0', the GRE tunnel can establish a reliable source and will thus transition to an up/up state.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0388_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":464,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0389","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the infrastructure deployment models on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0389_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0389_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":466,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0390","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. VPN-A sends point-to-point traffic to VPN-B and receives traffic only from VPN-C. VPN-B sends point-to-point traffic to VPN-C and receives traffic only from VPN-A. Which configuration is applied?","options":{"A":"PE-2 vrf VPN-B address-family ipv4 unicast import route-target 100:1 export route-target 100:2","B":"PE-3 vrf VPN-B address-family ipv4 unicast import route-target 100:2 export route-target 100:2","C":"PE-2 vrf VPN-B address-family ipv4 unicast import route-target 100:2 export route-target 100:2","D":"PE-3 vrf VPN-B address-family ipv4 unicast import route-target 100:1 export route-target 100:2"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"PE-3 needs to import routes with the RT value of 100:1 and export routes with the RT value of 100:2 to ensure that VPN-B and VPN-C communicate as described. VPN-A exports routes with RT 100:1, and VPN-B must, therefore, import these routes. Similarly, VPN-B must export routes with RT 100:2 to ensure that VPN-C can import them. No other configuration aligns with the described communication flow where VPN-A sends traffic to VPN-B, and VPN-B sends traffic to VPN-C. Therefore, the configuration matching this flow is: PE-3 vrf VPN-B address-family ipv4 unicast import route-target 100:1 export route-target 100:2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0390_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":467,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0391","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"A customer wants to use a single SSID to authenticate IoT devices using different passwords. Which Layer 2 security type must be configured in conjunction with Cisco ISE to achieve this requirement?","options":{"A":"Central Web Authentication","B":"Cisco Centralized Key Management","C":"Identity PSK","D":"Fast Transition"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To authenticate IoT devices using different passwords under a single SSID, Identity PSK (Pre-Shared Key) is the suitable Layer 2 security type. Identity PSKs allow unique pre-shared keys to be created for individual devices or groups of devices, making it possible to manage and control access for different devices easily. This method provides stronger security for IoT devices, particularly in scenarios where 802.1X may not be supported. It also allows for easy revocation and management of keys, addressing the requirement effectively.","images":[],"sourcePage":469,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0392","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"In which two ways does TCAM differ from CAM? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"CAM is used to make Layer 2 forwarding decisions, and TCAM is used for Layer 3 address lookups.","B":"CAM is used by routers for IP address lookups, and TCAM is used to make Layer 2 forwarding decisions.","C":"CAM is used for software switching mechanisms, and TCAM is used for hardware switching mechanisms.","D":"The MAC address table is contained in TCAM, and ACL and QoS information is stored in CAM.","E":"The MAC address table is contained in CAM, and ACL and QoS information is stored in TCAM."},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"TCAM (Ternary Content Addressable Memory) and CAM (Content Addressable Memory) are both used in networking hardware, but they serve different purposes and are optimized for different types of searches. CAM is used to store MAC addresses and to make Layer 2 forwarding decisions, as it provides exact match lookups which are necessary for switching. On the other hand, TCAM is designed to handle more complex searches that involve multiple fields and longest-prefix matching, which is essential for Layer 3 address lookups, Access Control Lists (ACLs), and Quality of Service (QoS) policies. Therefore, the correct ways in which TCAM differs from CAM are that TCAM is used for Layer 3 address lookups and for storing ACL and QoS information, while CAM is used for storing MAC addresses and making Layer 2 forwarding decisions.","images":[],"sourcePage":470,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0393","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"When firewall capabilities are considered, which feature is found only in Cisco next-generation firewalls?","options":{"A":"malware protection","B":"stateful inspection","C":"traffic filtering","D":"active/standby high availability"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Among the features listed, malware protection is a significant functionality that stands out in Cisco's next-generation firewalls. Cisco's advanced malware protection is a notable feature that differentiates its next-generation firewalls by integrating comprehensive network security with advanced threat prevention, which is not typically found in all traditional firewall solutions.","images":[],"sourcePage":471,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0394","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer is enabling HTTPS access to the core switch, which requires a certificate to be installed on the switch signed by the corporate certificate authority. Which configuration commands are required to issue a certificate signing request from the core switch?","options":{"A":"Core-Switch(config)#crypto pki enroll Core-Switch Core-Switch(config)#ip http secure-trustpoint Core-Switch","B":"Core-Switch(config)#ip http secure-trustpoint Core-Switch Core-Switch(config)#crypto pki enroll Core-Switch","C":"Core-Switch(config)#crypto pki trustpoint Core-Switch Core-Switch(ca-trustpoint)#enrollment terminal Core-Switch(config)#crypto pki enroll Core-Switch","D":"Core-Switch(config)#crypto pki trustpoint Core-Switch Core-Switch(ca-trustpoint)#enrollment terminal Core-Switch(config)#ip http secure-trustpoint Core-Switch"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To issue a certificate signing request (CSR) from the core switch in order to enable HTTPS access, the correct sequence involves three main steps: creating a trust point, specifying enrollment method, and enrolling the trust point. The commands needed to achieve this are: 1) create the trust point using 'crypto pki trustpoint Core-Switch', 2) specify the enrollment method which is 'enrollment terminal', and 3) perform the enrollment using 'crypto pki enroll Core-Switch'. This sequence ensures that the core switch can request a certificate signed by the corporate certificate authority. Option C correctly follows these steps.","images":[],"sourcePage":472,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0395","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must create a new SSID on a Cisco 9800 wireless LAN controller. The client has asked to use a pre-shared key for authentication. Which profile must the engineer edit to achieve this requirement?","options":{"A":"Policy","B":"RF","C":"Flex","D":"WLAN"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct profile to edit is the WLAN profile. The WLAN profile is directly related to configuring SSIDs and their associated security settings, including the use of a pre-shared key for authentication. Managing SSIDs and their security features, such as PSK, falls under the WLAN profile configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":473,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0396","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is configuring a new SSID to present users with a splash page for authentication. Which WLAN Layer 3 setting must be configured to provide this functionality?","options":{"A":"Local Policy","B":"WPA2 Policy","C":"CCKM","D":"Web Policy"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To present users with a splash page for authentication in a new SSID configuration, the appropriate WLAN Layer 3 setting to be configured is the Web Policy. This policy specifically allows the use of a splash page for web-based authentication, ensuring users are redirected to the authentication page upon connecting to the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":474,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0397","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"A customer requests a design that includes GLBP as the FHRP. The network architect discovers that the members of the GLBP group have different throughput capabilities. Which GLBP load balancing method supports this environment?","options":{"A":"round robin","B":"host dependent","C":"weighted","D":"least connection"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol) load balancing method for an environment where group members have different throughput capabilities is 'weighted.' This method allows each member of the GLBP group to be assigned a weight that indicates its capacity or performance. Higher capacity devices can be given a higher weight, thereby directing more traffic to them, ensuring efficient traffic distribution in proportion to their capability.","images":[],"sourcePage":475,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0398","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which `command` set changes the neighbor state from Idle (Admin) to Active?","options":{"A":"R1(config)#router bgp 65001 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.50.2 remote-as 65001","B":"R1(config)#router bgp 65001 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.50.2 activate","C":"R1(config)#router bgp 65001 R1(config-router)#no neighbor 192.168.50.2 shutdown","D":"R1(config)#router bgp 65002 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.50.2 activate"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The command set needed to change the BGP neighbor state from 'Idle (Admin)' to 'Active' involves removing the administrative shutdown state from the BGP neighbor. The correct command to achieve this on a Cisco router is 'no neighbor [neighbor IP address] shutdown'. This command takes the BGP neighbor out of the administrative shutdown state, allowing it to attempt to establish a BGP session. Hence, the correct command set is: R1(config)#router bgp 65001 R1(config-router)#no neighbor 192.168.50.2 shutdown.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0398_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":476,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0399","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer configures a WLAN controller with increased security for web access. There is IP connectivity with the WLAN controller, but the engineer cannot start a management session from a web browser. Which action resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"Disable Adobe Flash Player.","B":"Use a private or incognito session.","C":"Use a browser that supports 128-bit or larger ciphers.","D":"Disable JavaScript on the web browser."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When a WLAN controller is configured with increased security for web access, it often requires the use of strong encryption protocols or ciphers. If the browser being used does not support 128-bit or larger ciphers, it will not be able to establish a secure connection with the controller, thus preventing the management session from starting. Ensuring the browser supports these ciphers resolves the issue.","images":[],"sourcePage":477,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0400","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What does a northbound API accomplish?","options":{"A":"programmatic control of abstracted network resources through a centralized controller","B":"access to controlled network resources from a centralized node","C":"communication between SDN controllers and physical switches","D":"controlled access to switches from automated security applications"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A northbound API accomplishes programmatic control of abstracted network resources through a centralized controller. In Software-Defined Networking (SDN) architecture, northbound APIs facilitate communication between the control plane and the application plane, enabling the centralized controller to manage and control the network resources programmatically.","images":[],"sourcePage":478,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0401","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-WAN solution, how is the health of a data plane tunnel monitored?","options":{"A":"with IP SLA","B":"ARP probing","C":"using BFD","D":"with OMP"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN solution, the health of a data plane tunnel is monitored using Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD). BFD is a lightweight protocol that quickly detects faults in the forwarding path between two systems, ensuring the reliable delivery of data across the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":479,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0402","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which solution do IaaS service providers use to extend a Layer 2 segment across a Layer 3 network?","options":{"A":"VXLAN","B":"VTEP","C":"VRF","D":"VLAN"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that provides the ability to stretch Layer 2 networking over a Layer 3 infrastructure. This is achieved using MAC-in-UDP encapsulation, which allows for the encapsulation of Layer 2 Ethernet frames within Layer 3 UDP packets. This technique is widely used by IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service) service providers to extend Layer 2 segments across different data centers and network boundaries, facilitating scalable and flexible virtual network overlays.","images":[],"sourcePage":480,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0403","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 16 use?","options":{"A":"c0:41:41:43:07:10","B":"00:05:5c:07:0c:16","C":"00:00:0c:07:ac:10","D":"05:00:0c:07:ac:16"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"By default, HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) uses a virtual MAC address format of 0000.0c07.acxx, where 'xx' represents the hexadecimal representation of the HSRP group number. For HSRP group 16, 'xx' in hexadecimal is 10. Therefore, the virtual MAC address for HSRP group 16 is 00:00:0c:07:ac:10.","images":[],"sourcePage":481,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0404","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Why was the response code generated?","options":{"A":"The resource was unreachable.","B":"Access was denied based on the user permissions.","C":"Access was denied based on the credentials.","D":"The resource is no longer available on the server."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A 403 Forbidden status code means that the server understood the request but refuses to authorize it. Unlike a 401 Unauthorized status code, which indicates that the request lacks valid authentication credentials, a 403 status code indicates that the client's identity is known, but the permissions do not allow the requested action. Therefore, access was denied based on the user permissions.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0404_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":482,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0405","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer the exhibit. Which configuration elects SW4 as the root bridge for VLAN 1 and puts G0/2 on SW2 into a blocking state?","options":{"A":"SW4(config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 priority 0 ! SW2(config)#int G0/2\nSW2(config-if)#spanning-tree cost 128","B":"SW4(config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 priority 0 ! SW2(config)#interface G0/2 SW2(config-if)#spanning-tree vlan 1 port-priority 64","C":"SW4(config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 priority 32768 ! SW2(config)#int G0/2 SW2(config-if)#spanning-tree cost 128","D":"SW4(config)#spanning-tree vlan 1 priority 32768 ! SW2(config)#interface G0/2 SW2(config-if)#spanning-tree vlan 1 port-priority 0"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Configuring SW4 with a priority of 0 ensures that SW4 becomes the root bridge for VLAN 1 because lower priority values are preferred in the election process. By setting the spanning-tree cost of the port G0/2 on SW2 to 128, it makes the path through this port less preferred compared to other paths with lower costs, which would cause G0/2 on SW2 to go into a blocking state if it is not the best path to the root bridge.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0405_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":483,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0406","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What happens when a FlexConnect AP changes to standalone mode?","options":{"A":"All client roaming continues to work.","B":"Only clients on central switching WLANs stay connected.","C":"All clients on all WLANs are disconnected.","D":"All controller-dependent activities stop working except the DFS."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"When a FlexConnect AP changes to standalone mode, all controller-dependent activities stop working except Dynamic Frequency Selection (DFS). In standalone mode, centrally switched WLANs will be disconnected, but clients on locally switched WLANs can stay connected. The main functions that depend on the controller, such as network access control and web authentication, will be disabled. DFS remains operational to comply with regulatory requirements.","images":[],"sourcePage":484,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0407","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"If the maximum power level assignment for global TPC 802.11a/n/ac is configured to 10 dBm. which power level effectively doubles the transmit power?","options":{"A":"13 dBm","B":"14 dBm","C":"17 dBm","D":"20 dBm"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In terms of dBm, an increase of 3 dBm represents a doubling of the power. This is due to the logarithmic nature of the decibel scale. For every 3 dB increase, the power is doubled. Since the current power level is 10 dBm, doubling the power would result in 10 dBm + 3 dBm = 13 dBm.","images":[],"sourcePage":485,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0408","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must create an EEM script to enable OSPF debugging in the event the OSPF neighborship goes down. Which script must the engineer apply?","options":{"A":"event manager applet ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG event syslog pattern \"%OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 6, Nbr 1.1.1.1 on Serial0/0 from FULL to DOWN\" action 1.0 cli command \"enable\" action 2.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf event\" action 3.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf adj\" action 4.0 syslog priority informational msg \"ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG\"","B":"event manager applet ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG event syslog pattern \"%OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 5, Nbr 1.1.1.1 on Serial0/0 from LOADING to FULL\" action 1.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf event\" action 2.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf adj\" action 3.0 syslog priority informational msg \"ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG\"","C":"event manager applet ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG event syslog pattern \"%OSPF-1-ADJCHG: Process 5, Nbr 1.1.1.1 on Serial0/0 from FULL to DOWN\" action 1.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf event\" action 2.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf adj\" action 3.0 syslog priority informational msg \"ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG\"","D":"event manager applet ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG event syslog pattern \"%OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 5, Nbr 1.1.1.1 on Serial0/0 from LOADING to FULL\" action 1.0 cli command \"enable\" action 2.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf event\" action 3.0 cli command \"debug ip ospf adj\" action 4.0 syslog priority informational msg \"ENABLE_OSPF_DEBUG\""},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The correct script must enable the debugging commands when the OSPF adjacency goes from FULL to DOWN. The correct syslog pattern to match this event is '%OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process x, Nbr x.x.x.x on Interface from FULL to DOWN'. OSPF adjacency change messages are at the notification level (level 5), not critical (level 1). The script must start with the 'enable' command to ensure that the subsequent debugging commands execute in privileged EXEC mode. Based on these requirements, the correct script is the one that has the syslog pattern matching 'FULL to DOWN', the OSPF notification level (5), and starts with the 'enable' command.","images":[],"sourcePage":486,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0409","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"A customer wants to provide wireless access to contractors using a guest portal on Cisco ISE. The portal is also used by employees. A solution is implemented, but contractors receive a certificate error when they attempt to access the portal. Employees can access the portal without any errors.\nWhich change must be implemented to allow the contractors and employees to access the portal?","options":{"A":"Install a trusted third-party certificate on the Cisco ISE.","B":"Install an internal CA signed certificate on the Cisco ISE.","C":"Install a trusted third-party certificate on the contractor devices.","D":"Install an internal CA signed certificate on the contractor devices."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the certificate error experienced by contractors, the Cisco ISE should be equipped with a trusted third-party certificate. This approach ensures that devices, including those used by contractors and employees, automatically recognize and trust the certificate. As a result, it prevents certificate errors without requiring manual configuration or installations on individual contractor devices, thereby providing a seamless and user-friendly authentication experience for everyone accessing the portal.","images":[],"sourcePage":487,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0410","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure a new loopback interface on a router and advertise the interface as a /24 in OSPF. Which command set accomplishes this task?","options":{"A":"R2(config)#interface Loopback0 R2(config-if)#ip address 172.22.2.1 255.255.255.0 R2(config-if)#ip ospf network broadcast R2(config-if)#ip ospf 100 area 0","B":"R2(config)#interface Loopback0 R2(config-if)#ip address 172.22.2.1 255.255.255.0 R2(config-if)#ip ospf network point-to-point R2(config-if)#ip ospf 100 area 0","C":"R2(config)#interface Loopback0 R2(config-if)#ip address 172.22.2.1 255.255.255.0 R2(config-if)#ip ospf network point-to-multipoint R2(config-if)#router ospf 100","D":"R2(config-router)#network 172.22.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0 R2(config)#interface Loopback0\nR2(config-if)#ip address 172.22.2.1 255.255.255.0 R2(config-if)#ip ospf 100 area 0"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure a new loopback interface on a router and advertise it as a /24 in OSPF, the required commands are to set up the loopback interface, assign the IP address with the /24 subnet mask, and configure the OSPF network type as point-to-point. The 'ip ospf network point-to-point' command ensures that OSPF advertises the loopback interface with its correct subnet mask. This differs from the default loopback network type which advertises the interface with a /32 mask.","images":[],"sourcePage":488,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0411","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the LISP components on the left to their descriptions on the right. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0411_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0411_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":489,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0412","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which commands are required to allow SSH connections to the router?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"To allow SSH connections to the router, extended access control lists (ACLs) should be used because they permit both source and destination IP addresses and port numbers. The provided options use two different types of ACL numbers: standard (1-99) and extended (100-199). For SSH traffic, an extended ACL is necessary to specify TCP port 22. Option B introduces ACL 101, which is appropriate for this requirement. Therefore, the correct command sequence to allow SSH connections, which includes creating an extended ACL and applying it in the QoS policy map for control plane policing (CoPP), is as follows: access-list 100 permit tcp any any eq 22, access-list 101 permit tcp any any eq 22, class-map class-ssh, match access-group 101, policy-map CoPP, class class-ssh, police 100000 conform-action transmit. This configuration ensures that SSH traffic is correctly matched and permitted.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0412_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":490,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0412_optA.webp","B":"images/q0412_optB.webp","C":"images/q0412_optC.webp","D":"images/q0412_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0413","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of a type 2 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"complicated deployment","B":"ideal for data center","C":"referred to as bare-metal","D":"ideal for client/end-user system"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A type 2 hypervisor, also known as a hosted hypervisor, runs on top of a conventional operating system rather than directly on the hardware. This makes it more suitable for client or end-user systems as it can utilize the underlying operating system's resources and is easier to install and manage for individual users. Type 2 hypervisors are generally not ideal for data centers, which typically require the more efficient, high-performance characteristics of type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisors.","images":[],"sourcePage":492,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0414","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which two features does the Cisco SD-Access architecture add to a traditional campus network? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"modular QoS","B":"software-defined segmentation","C":"identity services","D":"private VLANs","E":"SD-WAN"},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"The Cisco SD-Access architecture enhances a traditional campus network with two key features: software-defined segmentation and identity services. Software-defined segmentation allows for dynamic, policy-based traffic management and network segmentation, increasing security and efficiency. Identity services provide the ability to enforce policies based on user identity, enhancing network access control and visibility. These features distinguish the SD-Access architecture from the traditional campus network setup.","images":[],"sourcePage":493,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0415","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which congestion queuing method on Cisco IOS based routers uses four static queues?","options":{"A":"weighted fair","B":"custom","C":"low latency","D":"priority"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Priority queuing (PQ) on Cisco IOS based routers utilizes four static queues. Each queue is assigned a different priority level, with higher-priority queues being processed before lower-priority ones. This ensures that critical traffic is given precedence over less important data.","images":[],"sourcePage":494,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0416","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nAn engineer plans to use Python to convert text files that contain device information to JSON Drag and drop the code snippets from the bottom onto the blanks in the code to construct the request. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0416_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0416_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":495,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0417","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which resource is able to be shared among virtual machines deployed on the same physical server?","options":{"A":"disk","B":"VM configuration file","C":"applications","D":"operating system"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The resource that is able to be shared among virtual machines deployed on the same physical server is the disk. Disks can be partitioned and accessed by multiple virtual machines, allowing them to store data and share storage resources effectively. VM configuration files, applications, and operating systems are typically isolated to each individual virtual machine to maintain separation and avoid conflicts.","images":[],"sourcePage":497,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0418","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must set up connectivity between a campus aggregation layer and a branch office access layer. The engineer uses dynamic trunking protocol to establish this connection; however, management traffic on VLAN1 is not passing. Which action resolves the issue and allow communication for all configured VLANs?","options":{"A":"Disable Spanning Tree for the native VLAN.","B":"Allow all VLANs on the trunk links.","C":"Change both interfaces to access ports.","D":"Configure the correct native VLAN on the remote interface"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The error message in the exhibit indicates a native VLAN mismatch between two switches on the specified interfaces. VLAN1 is used on one switch while VLAN30 is used on the other. To resolve this issue and ensure communication for all configured VLANs, you need to configure the correct native VLAN on the remote interface, making sure that both sides of the trunk link agree on the native VLAN. This alignment will prevent the mismatch and allow management traffic on VLAN1, as well as other VLANs, to pass correctly.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0418_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":498,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0419","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which three resources must the hypervisor make available to the virtual machines? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Memory","B":"bandwidth","C":"IP address","D":"Processor","E":"storage","F":"secure access"},"correctAnswer":["A","D","E"],"explanation":"To create and run virtual machines, a hypervisor must allocate memory, processors, and storage. Memory (RAM) is required for each VM to perform operations and run applications. Processors (CPU cores) are essential for computation and running the VM's tasks. Storage (disk space) is necessary for holding the VM's operating system, applications, and data. These three resources are fundamental for the operation of virtual machines.","images":[],"sourcePage":499,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0420","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nRefer to the exhibit. Drag and drop the snippets into the RESTCONF request to form the request that returns this response. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0420_exhibit.webp"},{"role":"question","file":"images/q0420_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0420_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":500,"flags":["This question starts on the last page of chunk 3 (PDF page 500) and finishes here; it was left out of chunk 3.","Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0421","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must configure static NAT on R1 to allow users HTTP access to the web server on TCP port 80. The web server must be reachable through ISP 1 and ISP 2. Which command set should be applied to R1 to fulfill these requirements?","options":{"A":"ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.200.225 80 extendable ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.201.1 80 extendable","B":"ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.200.225 80 ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.201.1 80","C":"ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.200.225 80 no-alias ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.201.1 80 no-alias","D":"ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.200.225 80 ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 8080 209.165.201.1 8080"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To allow HTTP access to the web server on TCP port 80 through both ISP 1 and ISP 2, static NAT entries must be created for each public IP address corresponding to the NAT IPs of ISP 1 and ISP 2. The 'extendable' keyword in the NAT command allows the router to map a single internal address to multiple external addresses for the same port, which is necessary in this scenario. Therefore, the correct command set includes 'ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.200.225 80 extendable' and 'ip nat inside source static tcp 10.1.1.100 80 209.165.201.1 80 extendable' to achieve the required configuration for reachability through both ISPs.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0421_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":502,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0422","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer attempts to bundle interface Gi0/0 into the port channel, but it does not function as expected. Which action resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"Enable fast LACP PDUs on interface Gi0/0.","B":"Set LACP max-bundle to 2 on interface Port-channel1.","C":"Configure no shutdown on interface Gi0/0.","D":"Configure channel-group 1 mode active on interface Gi0/0."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The exhibit indicates that interface Gi0/0 is in a hot-standby (hot-sby) state, which means the port is already configured and synchronized with the LACP group but remains in standby mode because the LACP group has reached its maximum number of active ports. The LACP max-bundle setting determines the number of active links allowed in the port channel. To resolve the issue and include Gi0/0 in the active port channel, it is necessary to increase the LACP max-bundle to 2, allowing both Gi0/0 and Gi0/1 to be active simultaneously.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0422_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":504,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0423","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco StackWise Virtual environment, which planes are virtually combined in the common logical switch?","options":{"A":"management and data","B":"control, and forwarding","C":"control and management","D":"control and data"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco StackWise Virtual environment, the control and forwarding planes are virtually combined to create a common logical switch. The management plane remains separate for each switch. This allows the stack to operate as a single logical switch from a control and forwarding perspective while maintaining independent management.","images":[],"sourcePage":505,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0424","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer is enabling logging to a local buffer, to the terminal, and to a syslog server for all debugging level logs filtered by facility code 7. Which command is needed to complete this configuration snippet?","options":{"A":"logging buffered debugging","B":"logging discriminator Disc1 severity includes 7","C":"logging buffered discriminator Disc1 debugging","D":"logging discriminator Disc1 severity includes 7 facility includes fac7"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To enable logging for all debugging level logs filtered by facility code 7, the correct command is 'logging discriminator Disc1 severity includes 7 facility includes fac7'. This command includes both the severity level (7, which is the debugging level) and the specific facility code (fac7). The configuration snippet shows the use of a discriminator named Disc1, which needs to include both the desired log messages by severity and facility. This makes option D the most comprehensive and appropriate command for the given requirement.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0424_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":506,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0425","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to construct a script that adds a prefix list to a route map and sets the local preference. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0425_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0425_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":507,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0426","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What is the result of the API request?","options":{"A":"The native interface information is read from the network appliance.","B":"The information for all interfaces is read from the network appliance.","C":"The ‫ג‬€params‫ג‬€ variable reads data fields from the network appliance.","D":"The ‫ג‬€params‫ג‬€ variable sends data fields to the network appliance."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The method specified is GET, which indicates that the request is designed to retrieve information from the specified URL. The URL given is '/restconf/api/running/native/interface'. This endpoint targets the native interface configuration hierarchy on the network appliance. The params indicate that it accepts multiple JSON media types related to YANG data. Therefore, the API request retrieves information for all interfaces from the network appliance, as it is not limited to a single native interface but includes all interfaces configured within the native context.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0426_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":509,"flags":["The PDF text contains garbled quote characters (shown as ג€); kept exactly as extracted."]},{"id":"q0427","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which definition describes JWT in regard to REST API security?","options":{"A":"an encrypted JSON token that is used for authentication","B":"an encrypted JSON token that is used for authorization","C":"an encoded JSON token that is used to securely exchange information","D":"an encoded JSON token that is used for authentication"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A JSON Web Token (JWT) is an open standard (RFC 7519) that defines a compact and self-contained way for securely transmitting information between parties as a JSON object. JWTs are encoded, not encrypted, and they can be used for various purposes, including authentication and secure information exchange. In the context of REST API security, while JWTs are often used for authentication, they fundamentally serve as a means to securely exchange information, making option C the most accurate definition.","images":[],"sourcePage":510,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0428","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A client has two directly connected eBGP peering links with diverse ISPs. Both providers advertise the same public prefix 209.165.200.224/27 to R1 without any route manipulation. Traffic leaves R1 outbound via ISP1 but returns inbound via ISP2. Which configuration prevents asymmetrical routing and makes ISP1 the preferred path inbound and outbound?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0428_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":511,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty.","No explanation in the source PDF."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0428_optA.webp","B":"images/q0428_optB.webp","C":"images/q0428_optC.webp","D":"images/q0428_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0429","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must allow all users in the 10.2.2.0/24 subnet to access the internet. To conserve address space, the public interface address of209.165.201.1 must be used for all external communication.Which command set accomplishes these requirements?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"To allow users in the 10.2.2.0/24 subnet to access the Internet using the public IP address 209.165.201.1 for all external communication while conserving address space, NAT (Network Address Translation) needs to be configured appropriately. The outside NAT interface should be the interface connecting to the Internet (G0/3) and the inside NAT interface should be the one connecting to the local network (G0/2). Additionally, to handle multiple devices using a single public IP, you need to use PAT (Port Address Translation), which is indicated by the 'overload' keyword. The following command set meets these requirements: access-list 10 permit 10.2.2.0 0.0.0.255, interface G0/3 ip nat outside, interface G0/2 ip nat inside, ip nat inside source list 10 interface G0/3 overload.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0429_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":513,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0429_optA.webp","B":"images/q0429_optB.webp","C":"images/q0429_optC.webp","D":"images/q0429_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0430","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is a TLOC in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"value that identifies a specific tunnel within the Cisco SD-WAN overlay","B":"identifier that represents a specific service offered by nodes within the Cisco SD-WAN overlay","C":"attribute that acts as a next hop for network prefixes","D":"component set by the administrator to differentiate similar nodes that offer a common service"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A TLOC, or Transport Locator, in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment is an attribute that acts as a next hop for network prefixes. It functions similarly to the BGP NEXT_HOP attribute and serves as the next-hop identifier for OMP (Overlay Management Protocol) routes within the SD-WAN overlay. The TLOC identifies the interface that the SD-WAN device uses to reach a particular site, providing essential routing information for directing traffic through the SD-WAN network.","images":[],"sourcePage":515,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0431","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration must be applied to the HQ router to set up a GRE tunnel between the HQ and BR routers?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To set up a GRE tunnel between the HQ and BR routers, the configuration must specify the correct tunnel source and destination IP addresses. The HQ router's tunnel source will be its interface connected to the ISP A (GigabitEthernet0/0), and the tunnel destination will be the BR router's public IP address (which is connected to ISP B and ends with .134). Additionally, the IP address for the tunnel interface itself should be a private address not shown in the provided diagram. Therefore, the correct configuration that matches these criteria is: 'interface Tunnel1, ip address 10.111.111.1 255.255.255.0, tunnel source GigabitEthernet0/0, tunnel destination 209.165.202.134'. This matches answer C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0431_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":516,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0431_optA.webp","B":"images/q0431_optB.webp","C":"images/q0431_optC.webp","D":"images/q0431_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0432","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures the BGP adjacency between R1 and R2; however, it fails to establish. Which action resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"Change the network statement on R1 to 172.16.10.0.","B":"Change the remote-as number on R1 to 6500.","C":"Change the remote-as number for 192.168.100.11.","D":"Enable synchronization on R1 and R2."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The issue is that the remote-as number on Router R1 is incorrectly configured. In BGP, the remote-as number must match the AS number of the neighbor router for the adjacency to be established. Currently, R1 is configured with a remote-as of 5500, but it should be 6500 to match Router R2's AS. Changing the remote-as number on R1 to 6500 will resolve the issue and establish the BGP peering between R1 and R2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0432_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":518,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0433","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The trunk does not work over the back-to-back link between Switch1 interface Gig1/0/20 and Switch2 interface Gig1/0/20. Which configuration fixes the problem?","options":{"A":"Switch 1(config)#interface gig1/0/20 Switch1(config-if)#switchport mode dynamic auto","B":"Switch2(config)#interface gig1/0/20 Switch2(config-if)#switchport mode dynamic desirable","C":"Switch2(config)#interface gig1/0/20 Switch2(config-if)#switchport mode dynamic auto","D":"Switch1(config)#interface gig1/0/20 Switch1(config-if)#switchport trunk native vlan 1\nSwitch2(config)#interface gig1/0/20 Switch2(config-if)#switchport trunk native vlan 1"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The trunk link between the two switches is not working because both interfaces are configured in the 'dynamic auto' mode. In this mode, both switches wait for the other side to initiate trunking, which results in no trunk forming. By changing Switch2 to 'dynamic desirable' mode, it actively attempts to negotiate and form a trunk, thus resolving the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0433_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":520,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0434","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must configure an ERSPAN session with the remote end of the session 10.10.0.1. Which commands must be added to complete the configuration?","options":{"A":"Device(config)#monitor session 1 type erspan-source Device(config-mon-erspan-src)#destination Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#no origin ip address 10.10.0.1 Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#ip address 10.10.0.1","B":"Device(config)#monitor session 1 type erspan-source Device(config-mon-erspan-src)#destination Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#no vrf 1","C":"Device(config)#monitor session 1 type erspan-source Device(config-mon-erspan-src)#destination Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#no origin ip address 10.10.0.1\nDevice(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#ip destination address 10.10.0.1","D":"Device(config)#monitor session 1 type erspan-destination Device(config-mon-erspan-src)#source Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)#origin ip address 10.1.0.1"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The provided configuration for the ERSPAN source session is missing the specification of the destination IP address for the GRE tunnel. In an ERSPAN configuration, it is necessary to remove any potential misconfigurations and then add the correct IP destination address. The correct commands to complete the configuration are to remove the existing origin IP address and then correctly set the destination IP address. Thus, the appropriate commands to add are 'no origin ip address 10.10.0.1' and 'ip address 10.10.0.1'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0434_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":522,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0435","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure a router to leak routes between two VRFs. Which configuration must the engineer apply?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure a router to leak routes between two VRFs, you must use an extended ACL in combination with a route-map and ensure the correct syntax in the VRF configuration. The proper configuration is as follows: use 'permit ip any' in the access-list, reference the correct access-list in the route-map, and then use the route-map in the 'import ipv4 unicast map' command under the VRF configuration. With this in mind and focusing on correct referencing and syntax, the correct configuration is found in option B.","images":[],"sourcePage":524,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0435_optA.webp","B":"images/q0435_optB.webp","C":"images/q0435_optC.webp","D":"images/q0435_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0436","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which Python code snippet must be added to the script to save the returned configuration as a JSON-formatted file?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0436_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":526,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty.","No explanation in the source PDF."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0436_optA.webp","B":"images/q0436_optB.webp","C":"images/q0436_optC.webp","D":"images/q0436_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0437","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What does the output confirm about the switch's spanning tree configuration?","options":{"A":"The spanning-tree operation mode for this switch is PVST.","B":"The spanning-tree operation mode for this switch is PVST+.","C":"The spanning-tree mode stp ieee command was entered on this switch.","D":"The spanning-tree operation mode for this switch is IEEE."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The output confirms that the spanning-tree mode on the switch is standard IEEE 802.1d, as indicated by the line 'Spanning tree enabled protocol ieee' under VLAN 20. This suggests that the 'spanning-tree mode stp ieee' command was entered on the switch to configure it in IEEE mode, directly referring to the standard Spanning Tree Protocol developed by IEEE.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0437_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":528,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0438","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to construct a script that advertises the network prefix 192.168.5.0/24 into a BGP session. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0438_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0438_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":530,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0439","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the router's API output in JSON format below, which Python code will display the value of the `hostname` key?","options":{"A":"json_data = response,json() print(json_data['response'][0]['hostname'])","B":"json_data = json.loads(response.text) print(json_data['response']['family']['hostname'])","C":"json_data = json.loads(response.text) print(json_data[response][0][hostname])","D":"json_data = response.json() print(json_data['response'][family][hostname'])"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To display the value of the 'hostname' key from the given JSON output, the correct Python code should first parse the JSON data and then access the 'hostname' key nested within the 'response' list. The appropriate method for fetching and parsing JSON data from an API response in Python involves using the response.json() method provided by the requests library. Therefore, the code `json_data = response.json()` will transform the JSON response into a dictionary. Then, accessing the value of 'hostname' is done by referencing `json_data['response'][0]['hostname']`. Although the correct option A contains a typo ('response,json()' should be 'response.json()'), this is the correct approach.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0439_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":532,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0440","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which function is performed by vSmart in the Cisco SD-WAN architecture'?","options":{"A":"distribution of IPsec keys","B":"execution of localized policies","C":"redistribution between OMP and other routing protocols","D":"facilitation of NAT detection and traversal"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In the Cisco SD-WAN architecture, the vSmart controller is responsible for ensuring consistency in routing information between the SD-WAN overlay and the underlying transport network. This involves the redistribution of routes between the Overlay Management Protocol (OMP) and other routing protocols. The vSmart controller centralizes the control plane functions and handles policy distribution to orchestrate the network behavior, making option C the correct answer.","images":[],"sourcePage":533,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0441","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The administrator troubleshoots an EtherChannel that keeps moving to err-disabled. Which two actions must be taken to resolve the issue?(Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Ensure that the corresponding port channel interface on the neighbor switch is named Port-channel1.","B":"Ensure that the switchport parameters of Port-channel1 match the parameters of the port channel on the neighbor switch.","C":"Ensure that interfaces Gi1/0/2 and Gi1/0/3 connect to the same neighboring switch.","D":"Reload the switch to force EtherChannel renegotiation.","E":"Ensure that the neighbor interfaces of Gi1/0/2 and Gi1/0/3 are configured as members of the same EtherChannel."},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of EtherChannel moving to err-disabled, it is important to ensure compatibility and proper configuration on both switches involved in the EtherChannel. First, confirming that the switchport parameters of Port-channel1 match the parameters of the port channel on the neighboring switch (Option B) is crucial. This includes settings such as allowed VLANs, port modes, and encapsulation types. Secondly, it is essential to ensure that the neighbor interfaces of Gi1/0/2 and Gi1/0/3 are configured as members of the same EtherChannel (Option E). This prevents inconsistencies that could lead to misconfigurations and err-disable situations.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0441_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":534,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0442","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer must configure NETCONF. After creating the configuration, the engineer gets output from the command show line, but not from show running-config. Which command completes the configuration?","options":{"A":"Device(config)# netconf max-sessions 100","B":"Device(config)# no netconf ssh acl 1","C":"Device(config)# netconf lock-time 500","D":"Device(config)# netconf max-message 1000"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To get output from the show running-config command, it's essential to ensure that the NETCONF messages can handle the data size. The command netconf max-message sets the maximum size of the messages the NETCONF session can handle. Setting it to a higher value, such as 1000, will accommodate the larger size of the running-config output, ensuring that it can be correctly displayed.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0442_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":536,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0443","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which protocol is implemented to establish secure control plane adjacencies between Cisco SD-WAN nodes?","options":{"A":"IKE","B":"TLS","C":"IPsec","D":"ESP"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The protocol implemented to establish secure control plane adjacencies between Cisco SD-WAN nodes is Transport Layer Security (TLS). TLS is used to secure communications between SD-WAN components such as vSmart controllers, vEdge routers, and vBond orchestrators, ensuring that control plane messages are encrypted and authenticated.","images":[],"sourcePage":537,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0444","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command set is needed to configure and verify router R3 to measure the response time from router R3 to the file server located in the data center?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"To configure and verify router R3 to measure the response time from router R3 to the file server located in the data center, the correct steps involve using the IP SLA (Service Level Agreement) feature in Cisco routers, which allows you to measure network performance, including response times. The file server is in the network 10.0.1.0/24, and the router R3's network is 10.0.3.0/24. Therefore, the correct IP addresses should be used as the destination and source IPs respectively. Moreover, the SLA should be scheduled to run indefinitely. Additionally, the correct command to display the statistics of the IP SLA operation should be used, rather than a general protocol command. Considering all this, the correct command set needed is: ip sla 6 icmp-echo 10.0.1.3 source-ip 10.0.3.3 frequency 300 ip sla schedule 6 life forever start-time now show ip sla statistics 6","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0444_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":538,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","NO ANSWER KEY: the PDF has no CORRECT marker and the explanation does not state a clear option letter. correctAnswer left empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0444_optA.webp","B":"images/q0444_optB.webp","C":"images/q0444_optC.webp","D":"images/q0444_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0445","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What are two reasons for IP SLA tracking failure? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The threshold value is wrong.","B":"The destination must be 172.30.30.2 for icmp-echo.","C":"The default route has the wrong next hop IP address.","D":"A route back to the R1 LAN network is missing in R2.","E":"The source-interface is configured incorrectly."},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"The source-interface is configured incorrectly as it should be Fa1/0 based on the topology, making E a correct option. Additionally, the default route has the wrong next hop IP address. The configuration includes a default route pointing to 172.30.30.2 with lower administrative distance, which would take precedence and cause tracking to fail, confirming C as correct. Therefore, the accurate reasons for IP SLA tracking failure are E and C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0445_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":540,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0446","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command set must be added to the configuration to analyze 50 packets out of every 100?","options":{"A":"sampler SAMPLER-1 mode random 1-out-of 2 flow FLOW-MONITOR-1 interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/0 ip flow monitor SAMPLER-1 input","B":"flow monitor FLOW-MONITOR-1 record v4_r1 sampler SAMPLER-1 interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/0 ip flow monitor FLOW-MONITOR-1 sampler SAMPLER-1 input","C":"sampler SAMPLER-1 no mode random 1-out-of 2 mode percent 50 interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/0 ip flow monitor FLOW-MONITOR-1 sampler SAMPLER-1 input","D":"interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/0 ip flow monitor FLOW-MONITOR-1 sampler SAMPLER-1 input"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To analyze 50 packets out of every 100, you need to apply the flow monitor and the sampler to the interface. The command set needed is 'interface GigabitEthernet 0/0/0 ip flow monitor FLOW-MONITOR-1 sampler SAMPLER-1 input'. This ensures the sampler, which is already configured to analyze 1 out of 2 packets, or 50%, is properly applied to the interface for input traffic.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0446_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":542,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0447","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Why would an engineer use YANG?","options":{"A":"to transport data between a controller and a network device","B":"to model data for NETCONF","C":"to access data using SNMP","D":"to translate JSON into an equivalent XML syntax"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"An engineer uses YANG to model data for NETCONF. YANG is a data modeling language used to define the structure of data that can be transported by the NETCONF protocol among others. It provides a standardized way to describe network configurations and state data, allowing devices and software to communicate effectively.","images":[],"sourcePage":544,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0448","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network monitoring system uses SNMP polling to record the statistics of router interfaces. The SNMP queries work as expected until an engineer installs a new interface and reloads the router. After this action, all SNMP queries for the router fail. What is the cause of this issue?","options":{"A":"The SNMP interface index changed after reboot.","B":"The SNMP server traps are disabled for the link state.","C":"The SNMP server traps are disabled for the interface index.","D":"The SNMP community is configured incorrectly."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The cause of the SNMP queries failing after installing a new interface and reloading the router is most likely due to the SNMP interface index changing after the reboot. The interface index (ifIndex) is a unique identifying number associated with each interface on a router. When a new interface is added and the router is reloaded, the ifIndex values can change, leading to a mismatch between the SNMP queries and the current interface identifiers. This would result in the SNMP polling system being unable to correctly identify and query the interfaces.","images":[],"sourcePage":545,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0449","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which character formatting is required for DHCP Option 43 to function with current AP models?","options":{"A":"MD5","B":"Base64","C":"ASCII","D":"Hex"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"For DHCP Option 43 to function correctly with current Access Point models, the character formatting required is hexadecimal (Hex). This formatting is used to encode the information that the APs need to discover the WLAN Controller IP addresses. Hexadecimal values allow for a compact and precise representation of the necessary data, which includes IP addresses and other byte-level details essential for network configuration. Hex formatting ensures that the data can be accurately parsed and interpreted by the devices involved.","images":[],"sourcePage":546,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0450","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which benefit is realized by implementing SSO?","options":{"A":"IP first-hop redundancy","B":"communication between different nodes for cluster setup","C":"physical link redundancy","D":"minimal network downtime following an RP switchover"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Implementing SSO (Stateful Switchover) helps achieve minimal network downtime following a router processor (RP) switchover. This is accomplished by maintaining the state information between active and standby routers, hence providing seamless failover. Although the SSO acronym might be misleading, in network terminology, it specifically refers to minimizing downtime during stateful switchover, rather than single sign-on.","images":[],"sourcePage":547,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0451","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After configuring HSRP an engineer enters the show standby command. Which two facts are derived from the output? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"R2 becomes the active router after the hold time expires.","B":"If Fa0/0 is shut down, the HSRP priority on R2 becomes 80.","C":"R2 Fa1/0 regains the primary role when the link comes back up.","D":"The router with IP 10.10.1.3 is active because it has a higher IP address.","E":"R2 is using the default HSRP hello and hold timers."},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"The output of the show standby command provides several pieces of information about the current HSRP configuration. The statement 'Track interface FastEthernet0/0 state Up decrement 10' means that if the interface Fa0/0 goes down, the HSRP priority on R2 will be decremented by 10, changing the priority from 90 to 80, making option B correct. Additionally, the hello time of 3 seconds and the hold time of 10 seconds are the default values for HSRP, making option E correct.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0451_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":548,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0452","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which two parameters are examples of a QoS traffic descriptor? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"DSCP","B":"MPLS EXP bits","C":"packet size","D":"bandwidth","E":"ToS"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP) and MPLS Experimental (EXP) bits are both commonly used parameters for describing and classifying network traffic in Quality of Service (QoS) implementations. DSCP is a field in the IP header that specifies the level of service or priority for a particular packet, while MPLS EXP bits are used within the MPLS header to indicate the level of priority of the packet. These fields help network devices to apply appropriate traffic management and ensure that higher priority traffic is handled accordingly.","images":[],"sourcePage":549,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0453","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer attempts to create a configuration to allow the Blue VRF to leak into the global routing table, but the configuration does not function as expected. Which action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"Change the source network that is specified in access-list 101.","B":"Change the access-list destination mask to a wildcard.","C":"Change the access-list number in the route map.","D":"Change the route-map configuration to VRF_BLUE."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The configuration aims to allow the Blue VRF to leak into the global routing table. The access-list 101 currently specifies a subnet mask. To properly match routes in an access-list, the mask should be in wildcard format. Therefore, changing the access-list destination mask to a wildcard will resolve the issue, ensuring the configuration functions as expected.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0453_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":550,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0454","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure a multicast UDP jitter operation. Which configuration should be applied?","options":{"A":"Router(config)#ip sla 1 Router(config)#udp-jitter 192.0.2.115 65051","B":"Router(config)#ip sla 1 Router(config)#udp jitter 239.1.1.1 65051 end-point list List source-ip 192.168.1.1","C":"Router(config)#ip sla 1 Router(config)#udp-jitter 192.0.2.115 65051 num-packets 20","D":"Router(config)#ip sla 1 Router(config)#udp jitter 10.0.0.1 source-ip 192.168.1.1"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure a multicast UDP jitter operation, you need to specify a destination multicast IP address (within the range 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255). Option B includes the configuration command with a multicast address (239.1.1.1), making it the correct choice for a multicast UDP jitter operation. The command also includes an endpoint list and source IP, which aligns with the proper configuration syntax for multicast operations.","images":[],"sourcePage":551,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0455","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The network administrator must be able to perform configuration changes when all the RADIUS servers are unreachable. Which configuration allows all commands to be authorized if the user has successfully authenticated?","options":{"A":"aaa authentication login default group radius local none","B":"aaa authorization exec default group radius","C":"aaa authorization exec default group radius if-authenticated","D":"aaa authorization exec default group radius none"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The configuration statement 'aaa authorization exec default group radius if-authenticated' ensures that all commands will be authorized if the user has successfully authenticated, even when the RADIUS servers are unreachable. This is achieved by allowing the user to proceed with command execution based on successful authentication through any available method defined in AAA, making sure that the authorization still occurs based on the user's authentication status.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0455_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":552,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0456","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-WAN solution, which two functions are performed by OMP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"advertisement of network prefixes and their attributes","B":"configuration of control and data policies","C":"gathering of underlay infrastructure data","D":"delivery of crypto keys","E":"segmentation and differentiation of traffic"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN solution, OMP (Overlay Management Protocol) performs several crucial functions. One is the advertisement of network prefixes and their attributes, which ensures that all nodes in the overlay network are aware of the different network routes and their characteristics. Another key function is the delivery of crypto keys, which are essential for securing data transmission across the network. These functions help in establishing and maintaining the SD-WAN control plane, ensuring optimal routes are chosen for data transmission and maintaining secure communication.","images":[],"sourcePage":553,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0457","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"How can an engineer prevent basic replay attacks from people who try to brute force a system via REST API?","options":{"A":"Add a timestamp to the request in the API header.","B":"Use a password hash.","C":"Add OAuth to the request in the API header.","D":"Use HTTPS."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To prevent basic replay attacks via a REST API, one effective method is to add a timestamp to the request in the API header. This allows the server to compare the current timestamp to the request timestamp and ensure that the request is made within a reasonable timeframe. This method helps to ensure that old, potentially malicious requests cannot be reused or replayed, thus mitigating replay attacks.","images":[],"sourcePage":554,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0458","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"What are the main components of Cisco TrustSec?","options":{"A":"Cisco ISE and Enterprise Directory Services","B":"Cisco ISE, network switches, firewalls, and routers","C":"Cisco ISE and TACACS+","D":"Cisco ASA and Cisco Firepower Threat Defense"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The main components of Cisco TrustSec include Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE), network switches, firewalls, and routers. Cisco ISE provides the central policy management and enforcement function, while the network switches, firewalls, and routers are critical for implementing the TrustSec policies across the network infrastructure. This combination ensures secure access control and policy enforcement across the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":555,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0459","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of a WLC that is in master controller mode?","options":{"A":"Configuration on the master controller is executed on all wireless LAN controllers.","B":"The master controller is responsible for load balancing all connecting clients to other controllers.","C":"All new APs that join the WLAN are assigned to the master controller.","D":"All wireless LAN controllers are managed by the master controller."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"A characteristic of a WLC that is in master controller mode is that all new APs (Access Points) that join the WLAN are assigned to the master controller. This setting ensures that any newly added access points, which do not have a primary, secondary, or tertiary controller specified, will automatically connect to the master controller within the same subnet, facilitating easier management and configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":556,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0460","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which Cisco FlexConnect state allows wireless users that are connected to the network to continue working after the connection to the WLC has been lost?","options":{"A":"Authentication Down/Switching Down","B":"Authentication-Central/Switch-Local","C":"Authentication-Central/Switch-Central","D":"Authentication-Down/Switch-Local"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The Cisco FlexConnect state that allows wireless users that are already connected to continue working after the connection to the Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) has been lost is 'Authentication-Down/Switch-Local'. In this state, the access point (AP) goes into standalone mode and continues to locally switch traffic for existing clients. While the AP cannot authenticate new clients due to the central WLC being unreachable, it maintains the session for already authenticated users, ensuring ongoing connectivity.","images":[],"sourcePage":557,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0461","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Object tracking has been configured for VRRP-enabled routers Edge-01 and Edge-02. Which commands cause Edge-02 to preempt Edge-01 in the event that interface G0/0 goes down on Edge-01?","options":{"A":"Edge-01(config)#interface G0/1 Edge-01(config-if)#vrrp 10 track 10 decrement 30","B":"Edge-02(config)#interface G0/1 Edge-02(config-if)#vrrp 10 track 10 decrement 30","C":"Edge-02(config)#interface G0/1 Edge-02(config-if)#vrrp 10 track 10 decrement 10","D":"Edge-01(config)#interface G0/1 Edge-01(config-if)#vrrp 10 track 10 decrement 10"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To ensure that Edge-02 preempts Edge-01 when the G0/0 interface on Edge-01 goes down, the priority of Edge-01 needs to be lower than that of Edge-02. Edge-01 currently has a priority of 100, and Edge-02 has a priority of 80. By using the command `vrrp 10 track 10 decrement 30` on Edge-01's interface G0/1, the priority of Edge-01 will decrease by 30 (from 100 to 70) when the G0/0 interface goes down, making it lower than Edge-02's priority of 80. Therefore, this command will cause Edge-02 to preempt Edge-01 and become the master.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0461_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":558,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0462","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must prevent the R6 loopback from getting into Area 2 and Area 3 from Area 0. Which action must the engineer take?","options":{"A":"Apply a filter list outbound on R3 and R7.","B":"Apply a filter list inbound on R2 and R9.","C":"Apply a filter list inbound on R3 and R7.","D":"Apply a filter list outbound on R7 only."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To prevent the R6 loopback address from reaching Area 2 and Area 3 from Area 0 in an OSPF network, you need to apply a filter list inbound on R3 and R7. This method stops the specific route from being installed into the specified areas (Area 2 and Area 3) when it comes from Area 0. The filter list applied inbound ensures that the Type 3 LSAs (which include the R6 loopback address) are not propagated into these areas, thus effectively preventing their advertisement into Area 2 and Area 3.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0462_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":560,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0463","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure and validate a CoPP policy that allows the network management server to monitor router R1 via SNMP while protecting the control plane. Which two commands or command sets must be used? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"access-list 150 permit udp 10.0.1.4 0.0.0.0 host 10.0.1.2 eq snmp access-list 150 permit udp 10.0.1.4 0.0.0.0 eq snmp host 10.0.1.2 class-map match-all CoPP-management match access-group 150 policy-map CoPP-policy class CoPP-management police 8000 conform-action transmit exceed-action transmit violate-action drop control-plane Service-policy input CoPP-policy","B":"show ip interface brief","C":"show quality-of-service-profile","D":"access-list 150 permit udp 10.0.1.4 0.0.0.0 host 10.0.1.2 eq snmp class-map match-all CoPP-management match access-group 150 policy-map CoPP-policy class CoPP-management police 8000 conform-action transmit exceed-action transmit violate-action transmit control-plane Service-policy input CoPP-policy","E":"show policy-map control-plane"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"To configure and validate a CoPP policy that allows the network management server to monitor router R1 via SNMP while protecting the control plane, you need to define and implement proper access control lists, class maps, and policy maps. Option A includes these elements and additionally incorporates an essential protection mechanism by dropping violating traffic with 'violate-action drop'. To validate the CoPP policy, the 'show policy-map control-plane' command from option E will be necessary, as it allows you to see the applied policies and their statistics on the control plane. Therefore, the correct choices are options A and E.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0463_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":562,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option D was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0464","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"A vulnerability assessment highlighted that remote access to the switches is permitted using unsecure and unencrypted protocols. Which configuration must be applied to allow only secure and reliable remote access for device administration?","options":{"A":"line vty 0 15 login local transport input all","B":"line vty 0 15 login local transport input ssh","C":"line vty 0 15 login local transport input telnet ssh","D":"line vty 0 15 login local transport input none"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To ensure secure and reliable remote access for device administration, it is necessary to use encrypted protocols. SSH (Secure Shell) is a protocol that provides secure remote access by encrypting the data being transmitted, unlike Telnet, which sends data in clear text and is unsecure. The configuration 'line vty 0 15 login local transport input ssh' restricts remote access to only SSH, ensuring that all remote administrative sessions are encrypted and secure.","images":[],"sourcePage":564,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0465","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature is used to propagate ARP, broadcast, and link-local frames across a Cisco SD-Access fabric to address connectivity needs for silent hosts that require reception of traffic to start communicating?","options":{"A":"Multisite Fabric","B":"Native Fabric Multicast","C":"SDA Transit","D":"Layer 2 Flooding"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct answer is Layer 2 Flooding. Layer 2 Flooding in a Cisco SD-Access fabric is used to propagate ARP, broadcast, and link-local frames. This is essential to ensure connectivity for silent hosts, which need to receive traffic in order to start communicating.","images":[],"sourcePage":565,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0466","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which function does a fabric wireless LAN controller perform in a Cisco SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"manages fabric-enabled APs and forwards client registration and roaming information to the Control Plane Node","B":"coordinates configuration of autonomous nonfabric access points within the fabric","C":"performs the assurance engine role for both wired and wireless clients","D":"is dedicated to onboard clients in fabric-enabled and nonfabric-enabled APs within the fabric"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access deployment, a fabric wireless LAN controller (WLC) manages fabric-enabled access points (APs) and forwards client registration and roaming information to the Control Plane Node. This function is integral to maintaining the seamless integration of wireless clients within the fabric, ensuring that client movements and registrations are properly tracked and managed within the system.","images":[],"sourcePage":566,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0467","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must permit traffic from these networks and block all other traffic. An informational log message should be triggered when traffic enters from these prefixes. Which access list must be used?","options":{"A":"access-list acl_subnets permit ip 10.0.32.0 0.0.7.255 access-list acl_subnets deny ip any log","B":"access-list acl_subnets permit ip 10.0.32.0 255.255.248.0 log","C":"access-list acl_subnets permit ip 10.0.32.0 0.0.7.255 log","D":"access-list acl_subnets permit ip 10.0.32.0 0.0.0.255 log"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The given networks range from 10.0.32.0/24 to 10.0.39.0/24. To summarize these networks in a single access control list (ACL) entry, the wildcard mask used should cover all these networks. The range spans 8 contiguous subnets, which translates to a wildcard mask of 0.0.7.255. Therefore, the ACL should permit traffic from the 10.0.32.0 to 10.0.39.255 networks and log any permitted traffic. Configuration C achieves this with the correct wildcard mask and includes logging, which aligns with the requirement for triggering an informational log message. Implicit deny at the end of the ACL ensures all other traffic will be blocked. Hence, option C is the correct choice.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0467_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":567,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0468","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which Python code snippet prints the descriptions of disabled interfaces only?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To print the descriptions of disabled interfaces only, we need to check if the 'enabled' attribute of each interface is 'false'. If it is 'false', we print the description of that interface. From the given options, answer B achieves this correctly by checking if 'interface[\"enabled\"]' is not equal to 'true'. This ensures that only disabled interfaces (those with 'enabled' set to 'false') have their descriptions printed. Therefore, the correct answer is B.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0468_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":569,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0468_optA.webp","B":"images/q0468_optB.webp","C":"images/q0468_optC.webp","D":"images/q0468_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0469","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Which measure is used by an NTP server to indicate its closeness to the authoritative time source?","options":{"A":"stratum","B":"time zone","C":"latency","D":"hop count"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"An NTP (Network Time Protocol) server uses the measure of 'stratum' to indicate its closeness to the authoritative time source. The stratum level defines the number of hops or steps away from the reference clock. A stratum 1 server is directly connected to an authoritative time source like an atomic clock or GPS, whereas higher stratum numbers indicate servers that are further away from the primary source.","images":[],"sourcePage":570,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0470","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"When is the Design workflow used in Cisco DNA Center?","options":{"A":"in a greenfield deployment, with no existing infrastructure","B":"in a greenfield or brownfield deployment, to wipe out existing data","C":"in a brownfield deployment, to modify configuration of existing devices in the network","D":"in a brownfield deployment, to provision and onboard new network devices"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Design workflow in Cisco DNA Center is used in a greenfield deployment, where there is no existing infrastructure. This is because the Design workflow is responsible for creating the network’s structure and framework from scratch, including defining the physical topology, network settings, and device type profiles. In scenarios where there is already an existing infrastructure (brownfield deployment), other workflows such as Discovery would be more appropriate for incorporating existing configurations and devices into Cisco DNA Center.","images":[],"sourcePage":571,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0471","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"What are two characteristics of VXLAN? (Choose two)","options":{"A":"It lacks support for host mobility.","B":"It uses VTEPs to encapsulate and decapsulate frames.","C":"It allows for up to 16 million VXLAN segments.","D":"It extends Layer 2 and Layer 3 overlay networks over a Layer 2 underlay.","E":"It has a 12-bit network identifier."},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"VXLAN uses VTEPs (VXLAN Tunnel Endpoints) to encapsulate and decapsulate Ethernet frames, allowing overlay networks to be extended over existing layer 3 networks. Additionally, VXLAN allows for up to 16 million VXLAN segments by using a 24-bit segment ID, which greatly surpasses the 12-bit limit of traditional VLANs, thus providing significant scalability.","images":[],"sourcePage":572,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0472","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures OSPF and wants to verify the configuration. Which configuration is applied to this device?","options":{"A":"R1(config)#interface Gi0/1 R1(config-if)#ip ospf enable R1(contig-if)#ip ospf network broadcast R1(config-if)#no shutdown","B":"R1(config)#router ospf 1 R1(config-router)#network 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 area 0 R1(config-router)#no passive-interface Gi0/1","C":"R1(config)#interface Gi0/1 R1(config-if)#ip ospf 1 area 0 R1(config-if)#no shutdown","D":"R1(config)#router ospf 1 R1(config-router)#network 192.168.50.0 0.0.0.255 area 0"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The exhibit indicates that the OSPF process is enabled by interface configuration. This is evident from the phrases 'Attached via Interface Enable' and 'Enabled by interface config'. The configuration that matches this description is configuring OSPF directly on the interface with the command 'ip ospf 1 area 0' under the interface configuration mode. Therefore, the correct configuration involves accessing the interface configuration mode, enabling OSPF on that interface, and ensuring the interface is not shut down, which corresponds to option C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0472_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":573,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0473","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of a control-plane node in a Cisco SD-Access solution?","options":{"A":"to run a mapping system that manages endpoint to network device relationships","B":"to implement policies and communicate with networks outside the fabric","C":"to connect external Layer 3 networks to the SD-Access fabric","D":"to connect APs and wireless endpoints to the SD-Access fabric"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A control-plane node in a Cisco SD-Access solution runs a mapping system that manages endpoint to network device relationships. This involves tracking and associating endpoints to fabric nodes, which helps in managing the network efficiently by decoupling the endpoint's identity from its location in the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":574,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0474","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"What is a characteristic of a Type 2 hypervisor?","options":{"A":"It eliminates the need for an underlying operating system.","B":"Problems in the base operating system can affect the entire system.","C":"Its main task is to manage hardware resources between different operating systems.","D":"It is completely independent of the operating system."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A Type 2 hypervisor runs on a host operating system, and any issues or problems in the base operating system can affect the performance and stability of the entire system, including the hypervisor and its virtual machines. This dependency on the underlying operating system distinguishes Type 2 hypervisors from Type 1 hypervisors, which run on bare metal without the need for a host operating system.","images":[],"sourcePage":575,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0475","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is the purpose of a data modeling language?","options":{"A":"to describe the structure and meaning of exchanged data","B":"to standardize the procedures that are executed when parsing sent and received data","C":"to establish a framework to process data by using an object-oriented programming approach","D":"to specify the rules for transcoding between text and binary data encodings"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The purpose of a data modeling language is to describe the structure and meaning of exchanged data. Data modeling languages are used to define how data is structured, its relationships, and constraints, which enable systems and applications to interchange data in a meaningful way.","images":[],"sourcePage":576,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0476","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which IPv4 packet field carries the QoS IP classification marking?","options":{"A":"ID","B":"TTL","C":"FCS","D":"ToS"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The IPv4 packet field that carries the QoS IP classification marking is the ToS (Type of Service) field. This field is used to specify the priority of the packet and its quality of service, which can help in managing traffic and ensuring efficient data transmission.","images":[],"sourcePage":577,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0477","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Which two solutions are used for backing up a Cisco DNA Center Assurance database? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"bare metal server","B":"remote server","C":"NFS share","D":"local server","E":"non-linux server"},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"The two solutions used for backing up a Cisco DNA Center Assurance database are a remote server and an NFS share. A remote server allows the creation and storage of backup files, and an NFS (Network File System) share provides the necessary support for file-based backup storage. Other server types such as bare metal, local, or non-Linux servers do not fulfill the specific requirements for these backup tasks.","images":[],"sourcePage":578,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0478","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Communication between R2 and R3 over FastEthenet1/1 falls. What is the root cause of the failure?","options":{"A":"The subnet mask is different between the two interfaces.","B":"The interface of R3 is not operational.","C":"The wrong type of cable is connected between the two interfaces.","D":"IP CEF is disabled on R3."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The root cause of the failure is that the subnet mask is different between the two interfaces. For successful direct communication, both interfaces on either end of the link must be in the same subnet. In the provided exhibit, R2 and R3 are using incompatible subnet masks (one side uses /30 while the other uses /29), which leads to issues with ARP resolution and ultimately causes the failure in communication.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0478_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":579,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0479","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What is one characteristic of the Cisco SD-Access control plane?","options":{"A":"It allows host mobility only in the wireless network.","B":"It is based on VXLAN technology.","C":"Each router processes every possible destination and route.","D":"It stores remote routes in a centralized database server."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The Cisco SD-Access control plane involves the use of VXLAN technology. VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is essential for network virtualization and provides a framework for overlaying Layer 2 networks over a Layer 3 infrastructure, which is a characteristic fundamental to the operation of the Cisco SD-Access control plane.","images":[],"sourcePage":580,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0480","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"A customer transitions a wired environment to a Cisco SD-Access solution. The customer does not want to integrate the wireless network with the fabric. Which wireless deployment approach enables the two systems to coexist and meets the customer requirement?","options":{"A":"Deploy the wireless network over the top of the fabric.","B":"Implement a Cisco DNA Center to manage the two networks.","C":"Deploy a separate network for the wireless environment.","D":"Deploy the APs in autonomous mode."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"When transitioning a wired environment to Cisco SD-Access and not integrating the wireless network into the fabric, the correct deployment approach is to deploy the wireless network over the top of the fabric. This allows the wireless network to coexist independently without being impacted by the SD-Access configuration of the wired environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":581,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0481","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 14 use?","options":{"A":"04:17:01:05:7c:0e","B":"00:05:0c:07:ac:14","C":"00:00:0c:07:ac:0e","D":"00:05:5e:19:0c:14"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The virtual MAC address used by HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) is determined by the HSRP group number. The format for HSRP MAC addresses is 00:00:0C:07:AC:XX, where XX is the hexadecimal representation of the HSRP group number. For group 14, the hexadecimal value of 14 is '0E'. Thus, the correct virtual MAC address for HSRP group 14 is 00:05:0C:07:AC:14.","images":[],"sourcePage":582,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0482","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which LISP component decapsulates messages and forwards them to the map server responsible for the egress tunnel routers?","options":{"A":"Router Locator","B":"Map Resolver","C":"Proxy ETR","D":"Ingress Tunnel Router"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The Map Resolver (MR) in LISP is responsible for accepting encapsulated Map-Request messages from Ingress Tunnel Routers (ITRs), decapsulating those messages, and then forwarding them to the Map Server (MS) responsible for the Egress Tunnel Routers (ETRs) that are authoritative for the requested Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs).","images":[],"sourcePage":583,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0483","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must design a wireless network for a school system based on these requirements:\nThe network must be able to triangulate client location based on RSSI.\nEach client must be able to sustain 5 Mbps of throughput at all times. Each classroom has up to 30 clients.\nPrimary coverage is 5 GHz.\nWhich design should be used?","options":{"A":"Place APs in a grid orientation throughout the building, located as close as possible to the center of each classroom.","B":"Mount one AP in the center of each classroom.","C":"Space APs evenly on both sides of the hallways.","D":"Place APs near exterior walls and corners of the building, and fill in the center area with a staggered pattern."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In designing a wireless network to sustain 5 Mbps throughput per client and to triangulate client location based on RSSI, placing APs in a grid orientation throughout the building, located as close as possible to the center of each classroom, is the most appropriate design. This ensures a more even distribution of coverage and sufficient signal overlap for accurate triangulation, while also maintaining the primary coverage in the 5 GHz band and handling a high density of up to 30 clients per classroom.","images":[],"sourcePage":584,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0484","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command MUST be applied to R2 for an OSPF neighborship to form?","options":{"A":"network 20.1.1.0 0.0.0.0 area 0","B":"network 20.1.1.2 0.0.0.0 area 0","C":"network 20.0.0.2 0.0.0.3 area 0","D":"network 20.0.0.2 0.0.0.0 area 0"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To form an OSPF neighborship, router R2 needs to include its directly connected interface's IP address in the OSPF network statement with the correct wildcard mask. The IP address 20.1.1.2 with a wildcard mask of 0.0.0.0 in option B specifies the exact interface that should participate in OSPF. This is necessary to match the corresponding network statement on R1, which covers all interfaces. Therefore, for OSPF neighborship to form between R1 and R2, the command 'network 20.1.1.2 0.0.0.0 area 0' must be applied on R2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0484_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":585,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0485","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is one characteristic of VXLAN?","options":{"A":"It supports a maximum of 4096 VLANs","B":"It supports multitenant segments","C":"It uses STP to prevent loops in the underlay network","D":"It uses the Layer 2 header to transfer packets through the network underlay"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"VXLAN, or Virtual Extensible LAN, is a network virtualization technology that supports multitenant segments. This allows multiple tenants to share the same physical infrastructure while maintaining logical separation. Unlike traditional VLANs which are limited to 4096 segments, VXLAN can support up to 16 million segments. Therefore, the primary characteristic of VXLAN relevant to the question is its ability to support multitenant segments.","images":[],"sourcePage":587,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0486","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of vBond in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment?","options":{"A":"initiating connections with SD-WAN routers automatically","B":"pushing of configuration toward SD-WAN routers","C":"onboarding of SD-WAN routers into the SD-WAN overlay","D":"gathering telemetry data from SD-WAN routers"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"vBond's function in a Cisco SD-WAN deployment is to onboard SD-WAN routers into the SD-WAN overlay. This involves establishing initial secure communications channels, authenticating the routers, and helping integrate them into the overlay network. It does not push configurations or gather telemetry data; those tasks are performed by other components in the SD-WAN architecture.","images":[],"sourcePage":588,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0487","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network administrator configured RSPAN to troubleshoot an issue between switch 1 and switch2. The switches are connected using interface GigabitEthernet 1/1. An external packet capture device is connected to swich2 interface GigabitEthernet 1/2. Which two commands must be added to complete this configuration? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"switch2(config-if)# switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30,40,50,60,70-80","B":"switch2(config)# monitor session 1 source remote vlan 70 switch2(config)# monitor session 1 destination interface GigabitEthernet1/2","C":"switch1(config)# interface GigabitEthernet 1/1 switch1 (config-if)# switchport mode access switch1 (config-if)# switchport access vlan 10 switch2(config)# interface GigabitEthernet 1/1\nswitch2(config-if)# switchport mode access switch2(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10","D":"switch2(config)# monitor session 2 destination vlan 10","E":"switch2(config)# monitor session 1 source remote vlan 70 switch2(config)# monitor session 1 destination interface GigabitEthernet1/1"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"Two commands need to be added to ensure RSPAN works between switch1 and switch2 while allowing the external packet capture device to capture the desired traffic. First, on switch2, the allowed VLANs must include VLAN 70, as it is used for RSPAN. This is crucial since switch1 uses VLAN 70 as the remote span VLAN, but switch2's allowed VLANs currently exclude it. This is addressed by the command to allow VLAN 70 on switch2. Second, the monitor session on switch2 must specify the source remote VLAN 70 and the destination interface where the packet capture device is connected, which is GigabitEthernet1/2. Therefore, the two necessary commands are to adjust the allowed VLANs on switch2 to include VLAN 70 and to configure the monitor session appropriately on switch2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0487_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":589,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0488","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which function does a Cisco SD-Access extended node perform?","options":{"A":"provides fabric extension to nonfabric devices through remote registration and configuration","B":"performs tunneling between fabric and nonfabric devices to route traffic over unknown networks","C":"used to extend the fabric connecting to downstream nonfabric enabled Layer 2 switches","D":"in charge of establishing Layer 3 adjacencies with nonfabric unmanaged node"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The primary function of a Cisco SD-Access extended node is to extend the fabric by connecting to downstream non-fabric enabled Layer 2 switches. This allows the integration of devices that are not inherently part of the SD-Access fabric into the network, thereby extending the network's fabric capabilities.","images":[],"sourcePage":591,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0489","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which router is elected the IGMP Querier when more than one router is in the same LAN segment?","options":{"A":"The router with the shortest uptime.","B":"The router with the longest uptime.","C":"The router with the highest IP address.","D":"The router with the lowest IP address."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol), the election of the IGMP Querier in a LAN segment with multiple routers is based on the IP address. Specifically, the router with the lowest IP address in the subnet is elected as the IGMP Querier. This ensures a consistent and predictable method of electing a query router, which helps in managing multicast group memberships efficiently. Therefore, the correct option is the router with the lowest IP address.","images":[],"sourcePage":592,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0490","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the switching mechanisms they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0490_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0490_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":593,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0491","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"mcq","question":"In which two ways does the routing protocol OSPF differ from EIGRP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"OSPF provides shorter convergence time than EIGRP.","B":"OSPF supports only equal-cost load balancing EIGRP supports unequal-cost load balancing.","C":"OSPF is distance vector protocol. EIGRP is a link-state protocol.","D":"OSPF supports an unlimited number of hops EIGRP supports a maximum of 255 hops.","E":"OSPF supports unequal-cost load balancing EIGRP supports only equal-cost load balancing."},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"OSPF supports only equal-cost load balancing, while EIGRP supports both equal and unequal-cost load balancing. Additionally, OSPF supports an unlimited number of hops, whereas EIGRP supports a maximum of 255 hops.","images":[],"sourcePage":594,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0492","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer must configure the router to use the ISE-Servers group for authentication. If both ISE servers are unavailable, the local username database must be used. If no usernames are defined in the configuration, then the enable password must be the last resort to log in. Which configuration must be applied to achieve this result?","options":{"A":"aaa authorization exec default group ISE-Servers local enable","B":"aaa authentication login error-enable aaa authentication login default group enable local ISE-Servers","C":"aaa authentication login default group ISE-Servers local enable","D":"aaa authentication login default group enable local ISE-Servers"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The router configuration requires setting up authentication using a group of TACACS+ servers (ISE-Servers), followed by the local username database if the TACACS+ servers are unavailable, and finally the enable password as the last resort. The correct command to achieve this order is 'aaa authentication login default group ISE-Servers local enable'. This command specifies that the router should first attempt authentication using the ISE-Servers group, then use the local database, and if that fails, fall back to the enable password.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0492_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":595,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0493","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"mcq","question":"When using BFD in a network design, which consideration must be made?","options":{"A":"BFD is used with dynamic routing protocols to provide subsecond convergence.","B":"BFD is used with first hop routing protocols to provide subsecond convergence.","C":"BFD is used with NSF and graceful to provide subsecond convergence.","D":"BFD is more CPU-intensive than using reduced hold timers with routing protocols."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"BFD is used with dynamic routing protocols to provide subsecond convergence. This allows for fast detection of link failures and achieves quicker reconvergence of routing protocols such as OSPF, EIGRP, and BGP. This capability is essential in minimizing downtime and improving network reliability.","images":[],"sourcePage":596,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0494","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command filters the ERSPAN session packets only to interface GigabitEthernet1?","options":{"A":"source ip 10.10.10.1","B":"filter access-group 10","C":"destination ip 10.10.10.1","D":"source interface gigabitethernet1 ip 10.10.10.1"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To filter the ERSPAN session packets only to interface GigabitEthernet1, the command that must be used is one that applies the access list specifically to the ERSPAN session. The command 'filter access-group 10' associates the access-list 10, which permits traffic only from the IP address 10.10.10.1, to the ERSPAN session. This effectively filters the ERSPAN packets to include only those from interface GigabitEthernet1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0494_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":597,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0495","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Traffic is not passing between SW1 and SW2. Which action fixes the issue?","options":{"A":"Configure switch port mode to ISL on S2","B":"Configure LACP mode on S1 to active","C":"Configure PAgP mode on S1 to desirable","D":"Configure LACP mode on S1 to passive"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To establish an EtherChannel link using Port Aggregation Protocol (PAgP), both ends of the link need to be set to compatible modes. Currently, on Switch 1 (S1), the interfaces are in 'on' mode, which does not negotiate EtherChannel parameters but forces the channel to be created. On Switch 2 (S2), the interfaces are in 'desirable' mode, which actively tries to form an EtherChannel with the other side. Since 'on' mode on S1 does not participate in PAgP negotiation, S2 in 'desirable' mode cannot establish the EtherChannel. Configuring PAgP mode on S1 to 'desirable' will allow both sides to actively negotiate and establish the EtherChannel, resolving the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0495_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":598,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0496","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which Python code snippet must be added to the script to store the changed interface configuration to a local JSON-formatted file?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To store the changed interface configuration to a local JSON-formatted file, you need to write the updated configuration, which is a string, directly to a file. The correct code snippet is: OutFile = open(\"ifaces.json\", \"w\") OutFile.write(UpdatedConfig) OutFile.close() This writes the string contained in UpdatedConfig to the file \"ifaces.json\". Using json.dump() would be incorrect since it is used for serializing Python dictionaries, not strings. Similarly, using Response.json() is incorrect because Response is a string, not a JSON object. Thus, option B is the correct answer.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0496_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":600,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0496_optA.webp","B":"images/q0496_optB.webp","C":"images/q0496_optC.webp","D":"images/q0496_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0497","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures a new HSRP group. While reviewing the HSRP status, the engineer sees the logging message generated on R2.\nWhat is the cause of the message?","options":{"A":"The HSRP configuration has caused a routing loop","B":"The same virtual IP address has been configured for two HSRP groups","C":"A PC is on the network using the IP address 10.10.1.1","D":"The HSRP configuration has caused a spanning-tree loop"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The error message indicates a duplicate IP address on the network. In the context of HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol), this happens when two different HSRP groups are configured with the same virtual IP address. Each HSRP group should have a unique virtual IP, and the message is warning that another device on the network is using the same IP address as the HSRP group's virtual IP, leading to a conflict. Ensuring unique virtual IP addresses for different HSRP groups will resolve the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0497_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":602,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0498","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration must be implemented to establish EBGP peering between R1 and R2?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To establish EBGP (External Border Gateway Protocol) peering between R1 and R2, the configurations must accurately specify the neighbor IP address and the remote AS (Autonomous System) numbers. For Router R2, it should be configured to peer with R1's Ethernet0 IP address (131.108.1.1) and correctly specify AS300 as the remote AS. Similarly, Router R1 must be configured to peer with R2's Ethernet0 IP address (131.108.1.2) and specify AS320 as the remote AS. Therefore, the correct configuration is as follows: R2 configuration: router bgp 320 neighbor 131.108.1.1 remote-as 300 R1 configuration: router bgp 300 neighbor 131.108.1.2 remote-as 320 Hence, the correct answer is C.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0498_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":604,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0498_optA.webp","B":"images/q0498_optB.webp","C":"images/q0498_optC.webp","D":"images/q0498_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0499","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the switching architectures on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0499_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0499_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":606,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0500","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"A server running Linux is providing support for virtual machines along with DNS and DHCP services for a small business. Which technology does this represent?","options":{"A":"container","B":"Type 1 hypervisor","C":"Type 2 hypervisor","D":"hardware pass-thru"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The server running Linux along with providing DNS and DHCP services implies that it has an operating system already installed. In this context, a Type 2 hypervisor, which runs on top of an existing operating system, is the most suitable technology. Type 1 hypervisors run directly on the host hardware without a primary OS, which does not match the given scenario.","images":[],"sourcePage":607,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0501","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the deployment types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0501_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0501_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":608,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0502","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the routing protocols they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0502_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0502_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":609,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0503","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which features does Cisco EDR use to provide threat detection and response protection?","options":{"A":"containment, threat intelligence, and machine learning","B":"firewalling and intrusion prevention","C":"container-based agents","D":"cloud analysis and endpoint firewall controls"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Cisco Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) utilizes features like containment, threat intelligence, and machine learning to provide threat detection and response protection. Containment helps in isolating threats, threat intelligence offers information on emerging threats, and machine learning aids in analyzing and identifying potential threats based on patterns and behaviors.","images":[],"sourcePage":610,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0504","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Only administrators from the subnet 10.10.10.0/24 are permitted to have access to the router. A secure protocol must be used for the remote access and management of the router instead of clear-text protocols. Which configuration achieves this goal?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Option A is the correct configuration to achieve secure remote access to the router only from subnet 10.10.10.0/24. The access-list 23 command permits access only from this subnet. The 'access-class 23 in' command applies this access list to incoming VTY lines, ensuring only devices from these IP addresses can attempt to connect. Finally, the 'transport input ssh' command ensures that only the SSH protocol, which is secure, is allowed for remote access, thereby disabling less secure protocols such as Telnet.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0504_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":611,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0504_optA.webp","B":"images/q0504_optB.webp","C":"images/q0504_optC.webp","D":"images/q0504_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0505","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is configuring Local WebAuth on a Cisco Wireless LAN Controller. According to RFC 5737, which virtual IP address must be used in this configuration?","options":{"A":"172.20.10.1","B":"192.168.0.1","C":"1.1.1.1","D":"192.0.2.1"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"According to RFC 5737, the blocks of IP addresses 192.0.2.0/24 (TEST-NET-1), 198.51.100.0/24 (TEST-NET-2), and 203.0.113.0/24 (TEST-NET-3) are reserved for documentation purposes. The IP address 192.0.2.1 falls within the 192.0.2.0/24 range and is intended for such purposes. Therefore, the correct virtual IP address for configuring Local WebAuth on a Cisco Wireless LAN Controller is 192.0.2.1.","images":[],"sourcePage":613,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0506","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The OSPF neighborship fails between two routers. What is the cause of this issue?","options":{"A":"The OSPF process is stopped on the neighbor router.","B":"The OSPF router ID is missing on this router.","C":"The OSPF router ID is missing on the neighbor router.","D":"There is an MTU mismatch between the two routers."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The OSPF neighborship fails because of an MTU mismatch between the two routers. The exhibit shows that the OSPF state is stuck in EXSTART, which often indicates issues with exchanging Database Description (DBD) packets. This state can be caused by mismatched Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) settings between the routers. The log indicates an MTU size of 9100, which is indicative of a jumbo frame setting, and this mismatch prevents proper OSPF adjacency formation.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0506_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":614,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0507","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is one benefit of adopting a data modeling language?","options":{"A":"augmenting the use of management protocols like SNMP for status subscriptions","B":"refactoring vendor and platform specific configurations with widely compatible configurations","C":"augmenting management process using vendor centric actions around models","D":"deploying machine-friendly codes to manage a high number of devices"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Adopting a data modeling language allows for the refactoring of vendor and platform-specific configurations into widely compatible configurations. This standardization enables multiple types of devices and platforms to be managed in a consistent and interoperable manner, facilitating greater flexibility and ease of management across diverse network environments.","images":[],"sourcePage":615,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0508","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"In Cisco DNA Center what is the integration API?","options":{"A":"southbound consumer-facing RESTful API, which enables network discovery and configuration management","B":"westbound interface, which allows the exchange of data to be used by ITSM, IPAM and reporting","C":"an interface between the controller and the network devices, which enables network discovery and configuration management","D":"northbound consumer-facing RESTful API which enables network discovery and configuration management"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In Cisco DNA Center, the integration API is referred to as the northbound consumer-facing RESTful API. This API enables network discovery and configuration management, allowing external systems to interact with Cisco DNA Center for these purposes.","images":[],"sourcePage":616,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0509","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must configure a new WLAN that allows a user to enter a passphrase and provides forward secrecy as a security measure. Which Layer 2 WLAN configuration is required on the Cisco WLC?","options":{"A":"WPA3 Enterprise","B":"WPA2 Personal","C":"WPA2 Enterprise","D":"WPA3 Personal"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"WPA3 Personal is the correct Layer 2 WLAN configuration because it allows a user to enter a passphrase and provides forward secrecy as a security measure. WPA3 enhances the security of WPA2 by adding features such as Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) which maintains forward secrecy, ensuring that even if one session key gets compromised, it does not affect the security of past sessions.","images":[],"sourcePage":617,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0510","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"How does an on-premises infrastructure compare to a cloud infrastructure?","options":{"A":"On-premises offers faster deployment than cloud.","B":"On-premises requires less power and cooling resources than cloud.","C":"On-premises offers lower latency for physically adjacent systems than cloud.","D":"On-premises can increase compute power faster than cloud."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"On-premises infrastructure typically offers lower latency for physically adjacent systems compared to cloud infrastructure because the systems are located within the same physical environment, minimizing the distance and network delays. This is not necessarily the case with cloud infrastructure, where data may need to travel greater distances and be subject to more network hops, resulting in higher latency.","images":[],"sourcePage":618,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0511","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the orchestration tools that they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0511_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0511_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":619,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0512","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"An engineer must create a script to append and modify device entries in a JSON-formatted file. The script must work as follows:\nUntil interrupted from the keyboard, the script reads in the hostname of a device, its management IP address operating system type, and CLI remote access protocol.\nAfter being interrupted, the script displays the entered entries and adds them to the JSON-formatted file, replacing existing entries whose hostname matches.\nThe contents of the JSON-formatted file are as follows:\nDrag and drop the statements onto the blanks within the code to complete the script. Not all options are used.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0512_exhibit.webp"},{"role":"question","file":"images/q0512_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0512_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":620,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0513","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures HSRP and enters the show standby command. Which two facts about the network environment are derived from the output? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"If the local device fails to receive a hello from the active router for more than 5 seconds, it becomes the active router.","B":"If a router with a higher IP address and same HSRP priority as the active router becomes available that router becomes the new active router 5 seconds later.","C":"The virtual IP address of the HSRP group is 10.1.1.1.","D":"The hello and hold timers are set to custom values.","E":"The local device has a higher priority setting than the active router."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"The virtual IP address of the HSRP group is 10.1.1.1, as indicated clearly in the output. The hello timer and hold timer are set to custom values of 3 seconds and 10 seconds respectively, which differ from the default values of 3 seconds for hello and 10 seconds for hold, hence indicating they have been customized.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0513_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":623,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0514","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Cisco IOS routers R1 and R2 are interconnected using interface Gi0/0. Which configuration allows R1 and R2 to form an OSPF neighborship on interface Gi0/0?","options":{"A":"R2(config)#interface Gi0/0 R2(config-if)#ip ospf cost 1","B":"R1(config)#router ospf 1 R1(config-if)#network 172.20.0.0 0.0.0.255 area 1","C":"R1(config)#router ospf 1 R1(config-router)#no passive-interface Gi0/0","D":"R2(config)#router ospf 1 R2(config-router)#passive-interface Gi0/0"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In OSPF, if an interface is configured as passive, it prevents the router from sending OSPF Hello packets and forming OSPF adjacencies on that interface. From the exhibit, R1 has Gi0/0 set as a passive interface as indicated by 'No hellos (Passive interface)'. To allow R1 and R2 to form an OSPF neighborship on interface Gi0/0, it is necessary to change this configuration. Therefore, the command required is to remove the passive-interface setting on Gi0/0 for R1, enabling it to send Hello packets and establish an OSPF adjacency. Hence, the correct answer is to configure 'no passive-interface Gi0/0' on R1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0514_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":625,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0515","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. After configuring the BGP network an engineer verifies that the path between Server1 and Server2 is functional. Why did RouterSF choose the route from RouterDAL instead of the route from RouterCHI?","options":{"A":"BGP is not running on RouterCHI","B":"There is a static route in RouterSF for 10.0.0.0/24","C":"The route from RouterDAL has a lower MED","D":"The Router-ID for Router DAL is lower than the Router-ID for RouterCHI"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"RouterSF chose the route from RouterDAL instead of the route from RouterCHI because the Router-ID for RouterDAL is lower than the Router-ID for RouterCHI. In BGP, if all other path selection criteria are equal, the path with the lowest Router-ID is selected as the best path. This is evident from the Router IDs shown in the parentheses, where RouterDAL has a Router-ID of 192.168.2.2, which is numerically lower than RouterCHI's Router-ID of 192.168.3.2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0515_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":627,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0516","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the LISP components on the left to the correct description on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0516_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0516_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":629,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0517","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command set must be added to permit and log all traffic that comes from 172.20.10.1 in interface `GigabitEthernet0/1` without impacting the functionality of the access list?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To permit and log all traffic from the IP address 172.20.10.1 on interface GigabitEthernet0/1 without altering the current ACL functionality, you should use the command set provided in option A. This method will add a new rule to the access list without disturbing the existing rules, as the IP address 172.20.10.1 does not overlap with the network 172.16.0.0/20 specified in the second rule. The commands are as follows: Router(config)#access-list 100 permit ip host 172.20.10.1 any log Router(config)#interface GigabitEthernet0/1 Router(config-if)#access-group 100 in.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0517_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":630,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0517_optA.webp","B":"images/q0517_optB.webp","C":"images/q0517_optC.webp","D":"images/q0517_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0518","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer checks connectivity between two routers. The engineer can ping the remote endpoint but cannot see an ARP entry. Why is there no ARP entry?","options":{"A":"When VRFs are used, ARP protocol must be enabled in each VRF.","B":"The ping command must be executed in the global routing table.","C":"Interface FastEthernet0/0 is configured in VRF CUST-A, so the ARP entry is also in that VRF.","D":"When VRFs are used, ARP protocol is disabled in the global routing table."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The lack of an ARP entry is explained by the configuration of Interface FastEthernet0/0 in VRF CUST-A. When an interface is a part of a VRF, the ARP entries are maintained within that VRF. This is why the ARP entry is not visible in the global context but exists in the VRF context. The connectivity test using the ping command through the VRF CUST-A targets another endpoint within the same VRF, demonstrating successful ping results but isolating the ARP entry within that VRF, making it invisible in the global ARP table.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0518_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":632,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0519","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which option must be used to support a WLC with an IPv6 management address and 100 Cisco Aironet 2800 Series access points that will use DHCP to register?","options":{"A":"43","B":"52","C":"60","D":"82"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"For a Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) with an IPv6 management address and when using DHCP for registering Cisco Aironet 2800 Series access points, the correct option to use is 52. Option 52 is specifically designed to provide the IPv6 management interface addresses to the access points, making it suitable for this configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":633,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0520","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration set implements Control Plane Policing for SSH and Telnet?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct answer is D. Control Plane Policing (CoPP) is used to manage traffic that is directed to the router's control plane, and it requires applying a service policy in the input direction to manage incoming traffic. In configuration option D, 'class-map match-any class-control' is used to match access-group 100 (telnet) or 101 (SSH), and the 'service-policy input CoPP' command correctly applies the policy to incoming traffic. This setup correctly filters and controls Telnet and SSH traffic entering the control plane based on the specified access control lists.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0520_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":634,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0520_optA.webp","B":"images/q0520_optB.webp","C":"images/q0520_optC.webp","D":"images/q0520_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0521","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A customer deploys a new wireless network to perform location-based services using Cisco DNA Spaces. The customer has a single WLC located on-premises in a secure data center. The security team does not want to expose the WLC to the public Internet. Which solution allows the customer to securely send RSSI updates to Cisco DNA Spaces?","options":{"A":"Deploy a Cisco DNA Spaces connector as a VM","B":"Perform tethering with Cisco DNA Center","C":"Replace the WLC with a cloud-based controller","D":"Implement Cisco Mobility Services Engine"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The customer has a single WLC located on-premises and wishes to avoid exposing it to the public Internet for security reasons. Deploying a Cisco DNA Spaces connector as a VM allows secure communication between the WLC and Cisco DNA Spaces. The VM acts as an intermediary that can encrypt traffic and provide secure updates without exposing the WLC directly to the internet. This solution aligns with the security requirements and technical constraints described.","images":[],"sourcePage":636,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0522","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer entered the command no spanning-tree bpduguard enable on interface `Fa1/0/7`. What is the effect of this command onFa1/0/7?","options":{"A":"It remains in err-disabled state until the errdisable recovery cause failed-port-state command is entered in the global configuration mode","B":"It remains in err-disabled state until the no shutdown command is entered in the interface configuration mode","C":"It remains in err-disabled state until the shutdown/no shutdown command is entered in the interface configuration mode","D":"It remains in err-disabled state until the spanning-tree portfast bpduguard disable command is entered in the interface configuration mode."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"When an interface on a switch is placed in an err-disabled state due to a BPDU guard, it requires manual intervention to recover. The typical method to recover an err-disabled interface is to issue a shutdown command followed by a no shutdown command in the interface configuration mode. This sequence of commands resets the interface, allowing it to recover from the err-disabled state and bring the interface back up.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0522_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":637,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0523","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which design principle states that a user has no access by default to any resource, and unless a resource is explicitly granted, it should be denied?","options":{"A":"least privilege","B":"fail-safe defaults","C":"economy of mechanism","D":"complete mediation"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The principle that states a user has no access by default to any resource, and unless a resource is explicitly granted, it should be denied, is called 'fail-safe defaults.' This design principle ensures that the default action is to deny access, reinforcing security by requiring explicit permission for access. The 'least privilege' principle, on the other hand, refers to granting the minimum necessary permissions to a user for completing their tasks, but it does not inherently imply an access-denied default state.","images":[],"sourcePage":639,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0524","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A customer wants to connect a device to an autonomous Cisco AP configured as a WGB. The WGB is configured properly: however, it fails to associate to a CAPWAP-enabled AP. Which change must be applied in the advanced WLAN settings to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"Enable Aironet IE.","B":"Enable passive client.","C":"Disable AAA override.","D":"Disable FlexConnect local switching."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"When a Workgroup Bridge (WGB) needs to associate with a CAPWAP-enabled access point, it is essential to enable the Aironet Information Element (IE) in the advanced WLAN settings. The Aironet IE is a Cisco proprietary information element that includes necessary information for the WGB to properly identify and associate with the correct WLAN and VLAN for its wired clients. Without enabling Aironet IE, the WGB will fail to recognize and connect to the CAPWAP-enabled AP. Therefore, ensuring that Aironet IE is enabled resolves the issue of WGB association failure.","images":[],"sourcePage":640,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0525","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 32 use?","options":{"A":"04:19:01:05:2e:32","B":"05:5e:5c:ac:0c:32","C":"00:00:0c:07:ac:20","D":"00:5e:0c:07:ac:20"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"By default, HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) uses a virtual MAC address format of 00:00:0c:07:ac:xx, where 'xx' represents the group number in hexadecimal. For HSRP group 32, the group number in hexadecimal is 20, resulting in the virtual MAC address 00:00:0c:07:ac:20.","images":[],"sourcePage":641,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0526","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which two Cisco SD-WAN components exchange OMP information? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"WAN Edge","B":"vBond","C":"vManage","D":"vAnalytics","E":"vSmart"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"In the Cisco SD-WAN architecture, the Overlay Management Protocol (OMP) is used to exchange control plane information such as routing updates, policies, and security information. OMP runs between vSmart controllers and WAN edge routers. This allows vSmart controllers to manage and distribute network-wide policies and configurations to the WAN edge routers. Therefore, the two components that exchange OMP information are the WAN Edge and the vSmart.","images":[],"sourcePage":642,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0527","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"What does the number in an NTP stratum level represent?","options":{"A":"The number of hops it takes to reach the authoritative time source","B":"The amount of offset between the device clock and true time","C":"The number of hops it takes to reach the primary time server","D":"The amount of drift between the device clock and true time"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The number in an NTP stratum level represents the number of hops it takes to reach the primary time server. Stratum levels in NTP define the hierarchy of servers relative to a primary reference clock. A stratum 1 server is directly connected to an authoritative time source, stratum 2 servers are connected to stratum 1, and so on. Therefore, the stratum number indicates how many steps away a server is from the primary time source.","images":[],"sourcePage":643,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0528","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Clients report that they cannot connect to this SSID using the provided PSK. Which action will resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"Apply the correct interface to this WLAN","B":"Apply the changes this SSID","C":"Select the PSK under authentication key management","D":"Define the correct Radio Policy."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The issue is that clients are unable to connect to the SSID using the provided Pre-Shared Key (PSK). The exhibit shows that the security policy is currently set to [WPA2][Auth(802.1X)]. For clients to connect using a PSK, the authentication method should be set to PSK under the Authentication Key Management section rather than 802.1X. This change will allow clients to use the pre-shared key for authentication. Therefore, the correct action is to select the PSK under authentication key management.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0528_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":644,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0529","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which QoS queuing method transmits packets out of the interface in the order the packets arrive?","options":{"A":"custom","B":"weighted-fair","C":"FIFO","D":"priority"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The QoS queuing method that transmits packets out of the interface in the order the packets arrive is First In First Out (FIFO). In this method, the first packet that arrives at the queue is the first one to be transmitted. This approach ensures that packets are sent in the exact order they arrive without any prioritization or weighting.","images":[],"sourcePage":645,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0530","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which two results occur if Cisco DNA Center loses connectivity to devices in the SD-Access fabric? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Users lose connectivity","B":"Already connected users are unaffected but new users cannot connect","C":"All devices reload after detecting loss of connection to Cisco DNA Center","D":"User connectivity is unaffected","E":"Cisco DNA Center is unable to collect monitoring data in Assurance"},"correctAnswer":["D","E"],"explanation":"If Cisco DNA Center loses connectivity to devices in the SD-Access fabric, the user connectivity is unaffected because the network continues to operate as usual. Cisco DNA Center primarily serves for management, control, and monitoring; its loss does not disrupt the operation of the network. However, during this period of downtime, Cisco DNA Center is unable to collect monitoring data in Assurance, affecting the ability to monitor and manage the network efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":646,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0531","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The connection between SW1 and SW2 is not operational. Which two actions resolve the issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"configure switchport nonegotiate on SW1","B":"configure switchport nonegotiate on SW2","C":"configure switchport mode access on SW2","D":"configure switchport mode trunk on SW2","E":"configure switchport mode dynamic desirable on SW2"},"correctAnswer":["D","E"],"explanation":"The issue between SW1 and SW2 is that both interfaces are set to 'dynamic auto', meaning neither switch actively initiates the trunk formation. To resolve this, you can either set one side to 'dynamic desirable' so it actively tries to form a trunk, or set the interface on SW2 directly to trunk mode. By configuring 'switchport mode dynamic desirable' on SW2, it will initiate trunk negotiations with SW1, whose Negotiation of Trunking is off. Alternatively, configuring 'switchport mode trunk' on SW2 directly sets the interface to trunk mode without needing negotiation. These changes ensure proper trunking between SW1 and SW2.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0531_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":647,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0532","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A company has an internal wireless network with a hidden SSID and RADIUS-based client authentication for increased security. An employee attempts to manually add the company network to a laptop, but the laptop does not attempt to connect to the network. The regulatory domains of the access points and the laptop are identical. Which action resolves this issue?","options":{"A":"Ensure that the \"Connect even if this network is not broadcasting\" option is selected.","B":"Change the security type to WPA2-Personal AES.","C":"Use the empty string as the hidden SSID network name.","D":"Limit the enabled wireless channels on the laptop to the maximum channel range that is supported by the access points."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of the laptop not attempting to connect to the network, you need to ensure that the 'Connect even if this network is not broadcasting' option is selected. This action is necessary because the network has a hidden SSID. When a network does not broadcast its SSID, devices need explicit instructions to connect to it. Selecting this option informs the laptop to connect to the hidden network, allowing it to connect even though the SSID is not being broadcasted.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0532_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":649,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0533","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"How do the RIB and the FIB differ?","options":{"A":"RIB is derived from the control plane, and the FIB is derived from the RIB.","B":"FIB is derived from the control plane, and the RIB is derived from the data plane.","C":"RIB contains the interface for a destination, and the FIB contains the next hop information.","D":"FIB contains routes learned through a dynamic routing protocol and the RIB contains routes that are static or directly connected."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Routing Information Base (RIB) is derived from the control plane and contains all the routing information received from different routing protocols. The Forwarding Information Base (FIB) is derived from the RIB and is used to forward packets, containing the optimized routes that are actually used for forwarding traffic. This separation allows the control plane to handle the routing protocols and the data plane to handle packet forwarding efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":651,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0534","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What does a YANG model provide?","options":{"A":"standardized data structure independent of the transport protocols","B":"creation of transport protocols and their interaction with the OS","C":"user access to interact directly with the CLI of the device to receive or modify network configurations","D":"standardized data structure that can be used only with NETCONF or RESTCONF transport protocols"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"YANG provides a standardized data structure that is independent of transport protocols. It is a data modeling language used to model configuration and state data manipulated by network configuration protocols, but it is not restricted to any specific transport protocol.","images":[],"sourcePage":652,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0535","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"A system must validate access rights to all its resources and must not rely on a cached permission matrix. If the access level to a given resource is revoked but is not reflected in the permission matrix, the security is violated. Which term refers to this REST security design principle?","options":{"A":"economy of mechanism","B":"complete mediation","C":"separation of privilege","D":"least common mechanism"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The term that refers to ensuring that a system validates access rights to all its resources and does not rely on a cached permission matrix is called 'complete mediation'. This principle means that every access attempt must be checked to ensure permissions are current and accurate, preventing outdated permissions from being used, thus maintaining security integrity.","images":[],"sourcePage":653,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0536","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command set completes the ERSPAN session configuration?","options":{"A":"monitor session 11 type erspan-destination destination interface GigabitEthemet4 source erspan-id 11 ip address 10.10.10.10","B":"monitor session 12 type erspan-destination destination interface GigabitEthernet4 source erspan-id 12 ip address 10.10.10.10","C":"monitor session 11 type erspan-destination destination interface GigabitEthernet4 source erspan-id 12 ip address 10.100.10.10","D":"monitor session 12 type erspan-destination destination interface GigabitEthernet4 source erspan-id 11 ip address 10.10.10.10"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct command set to complete the ERSPAN session configuration is 'monitor session 12 type erspan-destination destination interface GigabitEthernet4 source erspan-id 12 ip address 10.10.10.10.' For the ERSPAN configuration, the ERSPAN ID (erspan-id) must match on both devices, and the IP address specified in the erspan-destination command should correspond to the IP address given in the source configuration.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0536_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":654,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0537","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"\"HTTP/1.1 204 No Content\" is returned when the curl `-i -X DELETE` command is issued. Which situation has occurred?","options":{"A":"The command succeeded in deleting the object.","B":"The object was located at the URI, but it could not be deleted","C":"The object could not be located at the URI path.","D":"The URI was invalid."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The HTTP 204 No Content status code indicates that the request was successful and that the server has fulfilled the request, but there is no additional content to send in the response. This means that the DELETE command successfully deleted the object.","images":[],"sourcePage":655,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0538","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the tools from the left onto the agent types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0538_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0538_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":656,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0539","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which configuration is required to summarize the Area 2 networks that are advertised to Area 0?","options":{"A":"RouterB(config)# router ospf 1 RouterB(config-router)# area 2 range 192.168.36.0 255.255.252.0","B":"RouterB(config)# router ospf 1 RouterB(config-router)# network 192.168.38.0 255.255.255.0","C":"RouterB(config)# router ospf 1 RouterB(config-router)# network 192.168.38.0 255.255.252.0","D":"RouterB(config)# router ospf 1 RouterB(config-router)# area 2 range 192.168.36.0 255.255.255.0"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To summarize the Area 2 networks that are advertised to Area 0, you need to create a summary address that aggregates the three /24 subnets: 192.168.36.0/24, 192.168.37.0/24, and 192.168.38.0/24. The appropriate summary address for these networks is 192.168.36.0 with a subnet mask of 255.255.252.0 (/22), which covers the entire range from 192.168.36.0 to 192.168.39.255. Configuring the command 'area 2 range 192.168.36.0 255.255.252.0' on Router B achieves this summary.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0539_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":657,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0540","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the router's API output in JSON format below, which Python code will display the value of the \"role\" key?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To extract the value associated with the 'role' key in the given JSON data, the correct Python code needs to properly access nested structures. The value of 'role' is nested within an object inside a list in the 'response' key. Presuming 'response' is an instance of a 'requests' library response, the correct approach involves accessing the first element of this list and then the 'role' key within that object. In the given answers, option B correctly does this by first converting the response into JSON format using response.json() and then accessing the 'role' key within the first element of the 'response' list: json_data = response.json() followed by print(json_data['response'][0]['role']). Therefore, option B is the correct answer.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0540_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":658,"flags":["The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty.","The PDF has no CORRECT marker for image options; the answer letter is taken from the explicit sentence in the explanation text."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0540_optA.webp","B":"images/q0540_optB.webp","C":"images/q0540_optC.webp","D":"images/q0540_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0541","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What is the recommended minimum SNR for voice applications on wireless networks?","options":{"A":"10","B":"15","C":"20","D":"25"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The recommended minimum Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) for voice applications on wireless networks is 25 dB. Voice applications typically require a higher SNR compared to data applications to ensure clarity and reduce the likelihood of packet loss or errors that could degrade the call quality.","images":[],"sourcePage":659,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0542","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What are two results of the NAT configuration? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Packets with a destination of 200.1.1.1 are translated to 10.1.1.1 or .2, respectively.","B":"A packet that is sent to 200 1.1.1 from 10.1.1.1 is translated to 209.165.201.1 on R1.","C":"R1 is performing NAT for inside addresses and outside address.","D":"R1 looks at the destination IP address of packets entering S0/0 and destined for inside hosts.","E":"R1 processes packets entering E0/0 and S0/0 by examining the source IP address."},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"Based on the NAT configuration shown in the exhibit, two outcomes are clear. First, a static NAT translation is set up, meaning that a packet sent from 10.1.1.1 will be translated to 209.165.201.1, and traffic in the opposite direction will be translated back, hence one-to-one mapping (B). Second, R1 examines the destination IP address of packets entering the Serial 0/0 interface to perform address translation correctly, which is typical behavior in such configurations to determine the appropriate internal host (D). The other options either misinterpret the translation direction or mischaracterize the role of R1 in this configuration context.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0542_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":660,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0543","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"A network engineer must configure a switch to allow remote access for all feasible protocols\nOnly a password must be requested for device authentication and all idle sessions must be terminated in 30 minutes.\nWhich configuration must be applied?","options":{"A":"line vty 0 15 password cisco transport input telnet ssh exec-timeout 30 0","B":"line vty 0 15 password cisco transport input all exec-timeout 0 30","C":"username cisco privilege 15 cisco line vty 0 15 transport input telnet ssh login local exec-timeout 0 30","D":"line console 0 password cisco exec-timeout 30 0"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The configuration ensures that only a password is requested for device authentication and all idle sessions are terminated in 30 minutes. Even though 'all feasible protocols' is stated, in the context of VTY lines, the only practical protocols are Telnet and SSH. Line console configuration (Option D) does not allow remote access, and Options B and C have an incorrect exec-timeout configuration. Thus, Option A is the correct choice as it meets all specified requirements accurately.","images":[],"sourcePage":662,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0544","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the switching architectures on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0544_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0544_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":663,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0545","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures a trunk between SW1 and SW2 but tagged packets are not passing. Which action fixes the issue?","options":{"A":"Configure SW1 with dynamic auto mode on interface FastEthernet0/1.","B":"Configure the native VLAN to be the same VLAN on both switches on interface FastEthernet0/1.","C":"Configure SW2 with encapsulation dot1q on interface FastEthernet0/1.","D":"Configure FastEthernet0/1 on both switches for static trunking."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To resolve the issue of tagged packets not passing through the trunk between SW1 and SW2, the encapsulation protocol must be consistent on both ends of the trunk link. SW1 is configured with dot1q encapsulation, while SW2 is configured with ISL encapsulation. Configuring SW2 with dot1q encapsulation on interface FastEthernet0/1 will ensure that both switches use the same trunk encapsulation protocol, allowing the trunk to form correctly and tagged packets to pass through. Therefore, the correct action is to configure SW2 with encapsulation dot1q on interface FastEthernet0/1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0545_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":664,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0546","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"When does a Cisco StackWise primary switch lose its role?","options":{"A":"when a switch with a higher priority is added to the stack","B":"when a stack member fails","C":"when the priority value of a stack member is changed to a higher value","D":"when the stack primary is reset"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"A Cisco StackWise primary switch retains its role unless certain events occur that trigger a reelection process. One of these events is when the stack primary is reset. Resetting the stack primary will cause the entire stack to undergo a reelection process to determine the new primary switch. This process ensures that control plane functions and stack-wide coordination are maintained, thus causing the current primary switch to lose its role.","images":[],"sourcePage":666,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0547","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. A network engineer troubleshoots an issue with the port channel between SW1 and SW2. Which command resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"SW2(config-if)#switchport mode trunk","B":"SW1(config-if)#channel-group 10 mode active","C":"SW1(config-if)#channel-group 10 mode desirable","D":"SW2(config-if)#channel-group 10 mode on"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The issue in the network is related to the Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) not being enabled on the remote port, as indicated by the log messages (%EC-5-L3DONTBNDL2: Gi0/1 suspended: LACP currently not enabled on the remote port). For LACP to form a port channel, both ends of the link must be configured correctly. LACP can operate in two modes: active and passive. Configuring SW1 with 'channel-group 10 mode active' initiates LACP negotiation, which can resolve the issue if SW2's configuration is compatible. Configuring with 'desirable' mode is incorrect because it is used for PAgP, a different protocol. Therefore, the correct command to resolve the issue is 'SW1(config-if)#channel-group 10 mode active'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0547_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":667,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0548","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the automation characteristics from the left onto the appropriate tools on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0548_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0548_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":668,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0549","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"How do stratum levels relate to the distance from a time source?","options":{"A":"Stratum 0 devices are connected directly to an authoritative time source.","B":"Stratum 1 devices are connected directly to an authoritative time source.","C":"Stratum 15 devices are connected directly to an authoritative time source.","D":"Stratum 15 devices are an authoritative time source."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Stratum levels refer to the hierarchical layers in the Network Time Protocol (NTP) system, which helps in synchronizing time across a network. Stratum 0 devices are the authoritative time sources, such as atomic clocks, GPS clocks, or radio clocks. Stratum 1 devices are directly connected to Stratum 0 devices and act as primary time servers within the network. Therefore, Stratum 1 devices are one level away from the authoritative time source, making them the correct answer to how stratum levels relate to the distance from the time source.","images":[],"sourcePage":669,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0550","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A customer has a wireless network deployed within a multi-tenant building. The network provides client access, location-based services and is monitored using Cisco DNA Center. The security department wants to locate and track malicious devices based on threat signatures. Which feature is required for this solution?","options":{"A":"malicious rogue rules on Cisco DNA Center","B":"malicious rogue rules on the WLC","C":"Cisco aWIPS policies on the WLC","D":"Cisco aWIPS policies on Cisco DNA Center"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Cisco Advanced Wireless Intrusion Prevention System (aWIPS) provides intrusion detection and prevention capabilities specifically designed for wireless networks. By configuring aWIPS policies on Cisco DNA Center, you can detect and mitigate threats, including rogue devices and malicious activity, within the wireless environment. This feature allows for the identification and tracking of malicious devices based on threat signatures, meeting the security department's requirements in a multi-tenant building scenario where the network is managed through Cisco DNA Center.","images":[],"sourcePage":670,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0551","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless environment, which device is responsible for hosting the anycast gateway?","options":{"A":"fusion router","B":"control plane node","C":"fabric border node","D":"fabric edge node"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless environment, the fabric edge node is responsible for hosting the anycast gateway. The anycast gateway is used for routing within the fabric, and each edge node maintains its own gateway for the devices connected to it to ensure efficient and scalable routing.","images":[],"sourcePage":671,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0552","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. The port channel between the switches does not work as expected. Which action resolves the issue?","options":{"A":"Interface Gi0/1 on Switch1 must be configured as desirable.","B":"Trunking must be enabled on both interfaces on Switch2.","C":"Interface Gi0/0 on Switch2 must be configured as passive.","D":"Interface Gi0/1 on Switch2 must be configured as active."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"For an EtherChannel to form properly using LACP, one side must be configured as 'active' while the other can be either 'active' or 'passive.' In the provided configuration, Switch1 has Gi0/1 set to 'passive' and Switch2 has Gi0/1 also set to 'passive.' Since both sides are set to 'passive,' they will not initiate the EtherChannel process. Changing the Gi0/1 interface on Switch2 to 'active' will allow the EtherChannel to form correctly, ensuring both links are aggregated into the channel group. Therefore, configuring Interface Gi0/1 on Switch2 as 'active' resolves the issue.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0552_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":672,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0553","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which set of commands is required to configure and verify the VRF for Site 1 Network A on router R1?","options":{"A":"R1#ip routing R1#(config)#ip vrf 100 R1#(config-vrf)#rd 100:1 R1#(config-vrf)# address family ipv4 ! R1(config)#interface Gi0/2 R1(config-if)#ip address 10.0.1.1 255.255.255.0 R1#show ip route","B":"R1#ip routing R1#(config)#ip vrf 100 ! R1(config)#interface Gi0/2 R1(config-if)#ip address 10.0.1.1 255.255.255.0 R1#showip route","C":"R1#ip routing R1#(config)#ip vrf 100 ! R1(config)#interface Gi0/2 R1(config-if)#ip vrf forwarding 100 R1(config-if)#ip address 10.0.1.1 255.255.255.0\nR1#show ip vrf","D":"R1#ip routing R1#(config)#ip vrf 100 ! R1(config)#interface Gi0/2 R1(config-if)#ip address 10.0.1.1 255.255.255.0 R1#show ip vrf"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The correct set of commands to configure and verify the VRF for Site 1 Network A on router R1 is as follows: First, enable IP routing on the router. Then, define the VRF with the command 'ip vrf 100'. After that, assign the VRF to the interface by using the command 'ip vrf forwarding 100' on the appropriate interface, in this case, Gi0/2. Assign the IP address to the interface after setting the VRF. Finally, verify the VRF configuration using the command 'show ip vrf'. This sequence ensures that the VRF is properly configured and verified on the router interface.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0553_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":674,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0554","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"How does Protocol Independent Multicast function?","options":{"A":"In sparse mode, it establishes neighbor adjacencies and sends hello messages at 5-second intervals.","B":"It uses the multicast routing table to perform the multicast forwarding function.","C":"It uses unicast routing information to perform the multicast forwarding function.","D":"It uses broadcast routing information to perform the multicast forwarding function."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Protocol Independent Multicast (PIM) functions by using unicast routing information to perform its multicast forwarding function. It relies on the unicast routing table to conduct reverse path forwarding (RPF) checks, meaning that it does not establish a separate multicast routing table. This feature allows PIM to operate independently of any specific unicast routing protocol.","images":[],"sourcePage":676,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0555","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which VXLAN component is used to encapsulate and decapsulate Ethernet frames?","options":{"A":"VNI","B":"GRE","C":"VTEP","D":"EVPN"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) environment, the component responsible for encapsulating and decapsulating Ethernet frames is the VTEP (Virtual Tunnel Endpoint). VTEPs serve as the bridge between the physical network and the VXLAN overlay network, encapsulating outgoing Ethernet frames into VXLAN packets and decapsulating incoming VXLAN packets back into Ethernet frames.","images":[],"sourcePage":677,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0556","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Assuming all links are functional, which path does PC1 take to reach DSW1?","options":{"A":"PC1 goes from ALSW1 to DSW2 to CORE to DSW1.","B":"PC1 goes from ALSW1 to DSW2 to ALSW2 to DSW1.","C":"PC1 goes from ALSW1 to DSW2 to DSW1.","D":"PC1 goes from ALSW1 to DSW1."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In the topology shown, DSW2 serves as the root bridge because it has the lowest priority of 24576. According to the Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) rules, all ports on the root bridge (DSW2) must be in the forwarding state. The best path calculation to reach the root bridge from any non-root switch will depend on the lowest cost path. The cost is based on the speed of the connection: 10 Gbps links have a lower cost than 1 Gbps links. Therefore, the path that PC1 would take to reach DSW1 is through ALSW1 to DSW2 and then to DSW1. This path is chosen because ALSW1 to DSW2 is a 1 Gbps link (cost=4) and DSW2 to DSW1 is a 10 Gbps link (cost=2) which totals 6, compared to the direct ALSW1 to DSW1 path which would have a higher cost or may even be blocked by STP. Hence, the correct answer is that PC1 goes from ALSW1 to DSW2 to DSW1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0556_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":678,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0557","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 22 use?","options":{"A":"c0:41:99:98:06:16","B":"00:00:0c:07:ac:16","C":"00:00:0c:07:ac:22","D":"c0:07:0c:ac:00:22"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) uses a standardized format for its virtual MAC addresses. The format is 00:00:0c:07:ac:xx, where 'xx' represents the HSRP group number in hexadecimal format. In this case, for HSRP group 22, the hexadecimal representation of 22 is still 22, so the correct virtual MAC address will be 00:00:0c:07:ac:22.","images":[],"sourcePage":679,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0558","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Both controllers are in the same mobility group. Which result occurs when client 1 roams between APs that are registered to different controllers in the same WLAN?","options":{"A":"The client database entry moves from controller A to controller B","B":"A CAPWAP tunnel is created between controller A and controller B","C":"Client 1 uses an EoIP tunnel to contact controller A","D":"Client 1 contacts controller B by using an EoIP tunnel"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"When a client roams between access points (APs) that are registered to different controllers within the same Wireless Local Area Network (WLAN) and mobility group, the client database entry is transferred from the original controller to the new controller. This process is known as Inter Controller-Layer 2 (Intercontroller-L2) Roaming. In this type of roaming, because both controllers have interfaces within the same client subnet, they exchange mobility control messages to update the client's database entry, enabling seamless connectivity for the roaming client.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0558_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":680,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0559","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Where in Cisco DNA Center is documentation of each API call organized by its functional area?","options":{"A":"Developer Toolkit","B":"platform management","C":"platform bundles","D":"Runtime Dashboard"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The Developer Toolkit provides documentation about each API call, organized according to functional areas of the Intent API in Cisco DNA Center. This toolkit is designed to help developers understand and effectively use the APIs provided by Cisco DNA Center.","images":[],"sourcePage":681,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0560","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must protect the CPU of the router from high rates of NTP, SNMP, and SSH traffic. Which two configurations must be applied to drop these types of traffic when it continuously exceeds 320 kbps? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"R1(config-pmap)#class CLASS-CoPP-Management R1(config-pmap-c)#police 32 conform-action transmit exceed-action drop violate-action transmit","B":"R1(config)#policy-map POLICY-CoPP R1(config-pmap)#class CLASS-CoPP-Management R1(config-pmap-c)#police 320000 conform-action transmit exceed-action drop violate-action drop","C":"R1(config)#policy-map POLICY-CoPP R1(config-pmap)#class CLASS-CoPP-Management\nR1(config-pmap-c)#police 320000 conform-action transmit exceed-action transmit violate-action drop","D":"R1(config)#control-plane R1(config-cp)# service-policy output POLICY-CoPP","E":"R1(config)#control-plane R1(config-cp)# service-policy input POLICY-CoPP"},"correctAnswer":["B","E"],"explanation":"To protect the CPU from high rates of NTP, SNMP, and SSH traffic, it is necessary to configure rate limiting (policing) and apply the policy to the control plane's input. The class-map defines traffic matching criteria, and the policy-map applies policer actions. Configuration B sets a rate of 320 kbps with the excess traffic being dropped, addressing continuous traffic exceeding the defined rate. Configuration E applies the policy to the control-plane input, ensuring all relevant traffic entering the router is regulated.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0560_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":682,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option A was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option B was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0561","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which free application has the ability to make REST calls against Cisco DNA Center?","options":{"A":"API Explorer","B":"REST Explorer","C":"Postman","D":"Mozilla"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Postman is a free application that is widely used for making REST calls, including those to Cisco DNA Center. It is specifically designed to help developers build, test, and iterate on APIs, making it an ideal tool for this purpose.","images":[],"sourcePage":683,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0562","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"If AP power level is increased from 25 mW to 100 mW, what is the power difference in dBm?","options":{"A":"6 dBm","B":"14 dBm","C":"17 dBm","D":"20 dBm"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The power difference in dBm between 25 mW and 100 mW can be calculated using the formula: Power (dBm) = 10 * log10(Power in mW). First, convert each power value to dBm: 25 mW = 10 * log10(25) ≈ 14 dBm, and 100 mW = 10 * log10(100) = 20 dBm. The difference is 20 dBm - 14 dBm = 6 dBm.","images":[],"sourcePage":684,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0563","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"What is the result when an active route processor fails in a design that combines NSF with SSO?","options":{"A":"The standby route processor temporarily forwards packets until route convergence is complete.","B":"An NSF-aware device immediately updates the standby route processor RIB without churning the network.","C":"An NSF-capable device immediately updates the standby route processor RIB without churning the network.","D":"The standby route processor immediately takes control and forwards packets along known routes"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In a design that combines NSF (Nonstop Forwarding) with SSO (Stateful Switchover), the standby route processor takes control immediately and continues forwarding packets along known routes. NSF ensures that the data plane continues forwarding packets using the current CEF (Cisco Express Forwarding) tables, and SSO maintains the state information and configuration to ensure minimal disruption. Therefore, the correct result of an active route processor failure is that the standby route processor immediately takes control and forwards packets using the existing forwarding information, without waiting for a complete route convergence.","images":[],"sourcePage":685,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0564","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which command is required to verify NETCONF capability reply messages?","options":{"A":"show netconf rpc-reply","B":"show netconf | section rpc-reply","C":"show netconf schema | section rpc-reply","D":"show netconf xml rpc-reply"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The correct command to verify NETCONF capability reply messages would be 'show netconf xml rpc-reply'. This command specifically includes 'rpc-reply' in the context of NETCONF and returns XML-formatted replies, which is crucial for verifying the structure and contents of the reply messages. The other options, especially those referring to 'schema', relate to viewing the schema structure rather than verifying actual RPC replies.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0564_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":686,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines).","The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0565","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left to the table types on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0565_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0565_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":688,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0566","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Which A record type should be configured for access points to resolve the IP address of @ wireless LAN controller using ONS?","options":{"A":"CISCO.CONTROLLER localdomain","B":"CISCO.CAPWAP.CONTROLLER localdomain","C":"CISCO-CONTROLLER localdomain","D":"CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.localdomain"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The configuration for the A record type to enable access points to resolve the IP address of a wireless LAN controller using DNS typically follows the convention CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.localdomain. CAPWAP (Control And Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) is the protocol used for communication between the access points and the wireless LAN controller, making 'CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.localdomain' the appropriate choice.","images":[],"sourcePage":689,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0567","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the configuration models on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0567_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0567_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":690,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0568","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which activity requires access to Cisco DNA Center CLI?","options":{"A":"provisioning a wireless LAN controller","B":"creating a configuration template","C":"upgrading the Cisco DNA Center software","D":"graceful shutdown of Cisco DNA Center"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Access to Cisco DNA Center CLI is required for a graceful shutdown of Cisco DNA Center. While many tasks such as provisioning a wireless LAN controller, creating configuration templates, and upgrading the Cisco DNA Center software can be managed through the graphical user interface (GUI), a graceful shutdown specifically requires direct access to the CLI to ensure that all processes are terminated properly and the shutdown is conducted smoothly.","images":[],"sourcePage":691,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0569","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer must configure an ERSPAN tunnel that mirrors traffic from Linux1 on Switch1 to Linux2 on Switch2. Which command must be added to the destination configuration to enable the ERSPAN tunnel?","options":{"A":"(config-mon-erspan-dst-src)# erspan-id 172.16.10.10","B":"(config mon erspan-dst-src)# erspan-id 110","C":"(config-mon-erspan-dst-src)# no shut","D":"(config-mon-erspan-dst-src)# origin ip address 172.16.10.10"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To configure an ERSPAN tunnel, the ERSPAN ID must be the same on both the source and the destination devices. This ensures that the traffic is correctly mirrored from the source to the destination. In this case, the source on Switch1 uses ERSPAN ID 110. To match this, the destination configuration on Switch2 must also use ERSPAN ID 110.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0569_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":692,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0570","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"What are two characteristics of Cisco SD-Access elements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The border node is required for communication between fabric and nonfabric devices.","B":"Traffic within the fabric always goes through the control plane node.","C":"Fabric endpoints are connected directly to the border node.","D":"The control plane node has the full RLOC-to-EID mapping database.","E":"The border node has the full RLOC-to-EID mapping database."},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Cisco SD-Access elements have distinct characteristics. The border node is essential for communication between fabric and non-fabric devices, acting as a gateway. Additionally, the control plane node maintains the entire RLOC-to-EID mapping database, enabling efficient host tracking and routing within the fabric. These functions are crucial for the operational dynamics of Cisco SD-Access.","images":[],"sourcePage":693,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0571","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"When is an external antenna used inside a building?","options":{"A":"only when using Mobility Express","B":"only when using 2.4 GHz","C":"when it provides the required coverage","D":"only when using 5 GHz"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"An external antenna is used inside a building when it provides the required coverage. The purpose of an external antenna is to enhance the signal strength and ensure that the wireless network effectively reaches the needed areas. This usage is not limited to any specific technology or frequency but is based on the physical coverage requirements of the building.","images":[],"sourcePage":694,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0572","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. R1 and R2 are directly connected, but the BGP session does not establish. Which action must be taken to build an eBGP session?","options":{"A":"Configure no neighbor 192.168.12.1 shutdown under R2 BGP process.","B":"Configure neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as 65002 under R1 BGP process.","C":"Configure ip route 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 192.168.12.1 on R2.","D":"Configure neighbor 192.168.12.1 activate under R2 BGP process."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"The BGP session between R1 and R2 is not establishing because the neighbor 192.168.12.1 is administratively down, as indicated by the Idle (Admin) state in the output. This state occurs when the BGP neighbor relationship is manually shut down. To resolve this issue and build an eBGP session, the correct action is to bring the neighbor back up by removing the shutdown command associated with the neighbor statement under the BGP process on R2. Therefore, the command 'no neighbor 192.168.12.1 shutdown' must be configured under R2’s BGP process.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0572_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":695,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0573","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A company requires a wireless solution to support its main office and multiple branch locations. All sites have local Internet connections and a link to the main office for corporate connectivity. The branch offices are managed centrally. Which solution should the company choose?","options":{"A":"Cisco DNA Spaces","B":"Cisco Unified Wireless Network","C":"Cisco Mobility Express","D":"Cisco Catalyst switch with embedded controller"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Cisco Unified Wireless Network is the correct solution to support a company's main office and multiple branch locations with central management. This solution provides seamless wireless connectivity across all sites while enabling centralized management, which is crucial for maintaining consistent policies and configurations across the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":696,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0574","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which QoS feature uses the IP Precedence bits in the ToS field of the IP packet header to partition traffic into different priority levels?","options":{"A":"marking","B":"shaping","C":"policing","D":"classification"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"","images":[],"sourcePage":697,"flags":["No explanation in the source PDF."]},{"id":"q0575","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which two commands are required on router R1 to block FTP and allow all other traffic from the Branch 2 network? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"access-list 101 deny tcp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.0.101.3 eq ftp access-list 101 deny tcp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.0.101.3 eq ftp-data access-list 101 permit ip any any","B":"access-list 101 deny tcp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.0.101.3 eq ftp-data access-list 101 permit ip any any","C":"interface GigabitEthernet0/0 ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252 ip access-group 101 out","D":"access-list 101 deny tcp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.0.101.3 eq ftp access-list 101 permit ip any any","E":"interface GigabitEthernet0/0 ip address 10.0.101.1 255.255.255.252 ip access-group 101 in"},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"To block FTP traffic from the Branch 2 network while allowing all other traffic, the correct approach requires two steps: defining the access control list (ACL) to block FTP and then applying this ACL to the appropriate interface on the router R1. The ACL defined in option A correctly denies both FTP control (port 21) and FTP data (port 20) traffic from the Branch 2 network (10.0.2.0/24) to the FTP server (10.0.101.3). It ends with a permit statement to allow all other traffic. Option C involves applying this ACL to the GigabitEthernet0/0 interface, which is likely the correct outbound interface facing SW1, to control traffic leaving R1 towards the FTP server and other resources. This combination effectively blocks the specified FTP traffic while allowing other traffic to pass.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0575_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":698,"flags":["A line wrap inside the option C was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0576","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"A company recently decided to use RESTCONF instead of NETCONF, and many of their NETCONF scripts contain the operation <edit-config> (operation=\"create\"). Which RESTCONF operation must be used to replace these statements?","options":{"A":"PUT","B":"CREATE","C":"GET","D":"POST"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To replace the NETCONF operation=\"create\" with a RESTCONF operation, the correct method to use is POST. The POST method is used to create a new resource, which aligns with the 'create' operation in NETCONF. PUT, on the other hand, is typically used for both creating and replacing resources. Given the operation is specifically \"create\", POST is the appropriate choice.","images":[],"sourcePage":700,"flags":["A line break in the middle of a sentence in the question text was joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0577","topic":"Network Access Security (802.1X, ISE, WLAN Security)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer is configuring RADIUS-Based Authentication with EAP MS-CHAPv2 is configured on a client device. Which outer method protocol must be configured on the ISE to support this authentication type?","options":{"A":"LDAP","B":"EAP-FAST","C":"EAP-TLS","D":"PEAP"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"For RADIUS-Based Authentication with EAP MS-CHAPv2, the correct outer method protocol to be configured on the Identity Services Engine (ISE) is PEAP (Protected Extensible Authentication Protocol). PEAP uses a digital certificate to authenticate the server, creating a secure tunnel, and then uses MS-CHAPv2 for client authentication within that tunnel.","images":[],"sourcePage":701,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0578","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must protect the password for the VTY lines against over-the-shoulder attacks. Which configuration should be applied?","options":{"A":"line vty 0 15 password XD822j","B":"service password-encryption","C":"username netadmin secret 7 $1$42J31k98867Pyh4QzwXyZ4","D":"username netadmin secret 9 $9$vFpMf8elb4RVV8$seZ/bDA"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To protect the password for the VTY lines against over-the-shoulder attacks, the appropriate configuration is to use the 'service password-encryption' command. This command ensures that any clear-text passwords set using the 'line vty 0 15 password' command are encrypted in the configuration file. Although the encryption provided is relatively weak, it is sufficient to prevent someone from easily reading the password off the screen when viewing the configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":702,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0579","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Hosts FC1, PC2; and PC3 must access resources on Server1. An engineer configures NAT on Router R1 to enable the communication and enters the show command to verify operation. Which IP address is used by the hosts when they communicate globally to Server1?","options":{"A":"random addresses in the 155.1.1.0/24 range","B":"155.1.1.1","C":"their own address in the 10.10.10.0/24 range","D":"155.1.1.5"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"When hosts on the 10.10.10.0/24 network communicate to an external server, their addresses are translated by the NAT device (Router R1). The 'Inside Global Address' is the IP address that represents one or more inside local IP addresses to the outside world. In this case, the inside global address used is 155.1.1.1, which is evident from the NAT translation table on Router R1. Thus, the IP address used by the hosts when they communicate globally to Server1 is 155.1.1.1.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0579_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":703,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0580","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"Using the EIRP formula, what parameter is subtracted to determine the EIRP value?","options":{"A":"transmitter power","B":"antenna cable loss","C":"antenna gain","D":"signal-to-noise ratio"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To determine the Effective Isotropic Radiated Power (EIRP) value, the antenna cable loss is subtracted. The EIRP formula typically accounts for the transmitter power plus the antenna gain, and subtracts the losses incurred from the antenna cable. This gives a measure of the strength of the signal as it would appear if radiated isotropically from an ideal antenna.","images":[],"sourcePage":704,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0581","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"What is one main REST security design principle?","options":{"A":"separation of privilege","B":"password hashing","C":"confidential algorithms","D":"OAuth"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"OAuth is a main REST security design principle. It is an open standard for access delegation, commonly used for token-based authentication and authorization, allowing third-party services to exchange information on behalf of the user without exposing their credentials.","images":[],"sourcePage":705,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0582","topic":"BGP","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. R1 has a BGP neighborship with a directly connected router on interface Gi0/0. Which command set is applied between the iterations of show ip bgp 2.2.2.2?","options":{"A":"R1(config)#no ip route 192.168.50.2 255.255.255.255 Gi0/0","B":"R1(config)#ip route 2.2.2.2 255.255.255.255 192.168.50.2","C":"R1(config)#router bgp 65002 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.50.2 shutdown","D":"R1(config)#router bgp 65001 R1(config-router)#neighbor 192.168.50.2 shutdown"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"A route learned from BGP showing RIB failure usually indicates that there's another route with a lower administrative distance already in the routing table. A static route would have a lower administrative distance than a BGP route. In this scenario, the command that best fits is the one that installs a static route for 2.2.2.2 via 192.168.50.2. This is why the correct solution is to configure the static route `R1(config)#ip route 2.2.2.2 255.255.255.255 192.168.50.2`.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0582_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":706,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0583","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"After a redundant route processor failure occurs on a Layer 3 device, which mechanism allows for packets to be forwarded from a neighboring router based on the MOST recent tables?","options":{"A":"BFD","B":"RP failover","C":"NSF","D":"RPVST+"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Nonstop Forwarding (NSF) is the correct mechanism that allows packets to be forwarded from a neighboring router based on the most recent tables after a redundant route processor failure occurs on a Layer 3 device. NSF works by keeping the forwarding plane active while the control plane recovers, ensuring minimal packet loss and continued forwarding of IP packets.","images":[],"sourcePage":707,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0584","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 15 use?","options":{"A":"c0:42:31:98:86:0f","B":"05:af:1c:0f:ac:15","C":"00:00:0c:07:ac:0f","D":"05:5e:ac:07:0c:0f"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol) uses a virtual MAC address that is determined based on the group number. The default virtual MAC address for HSRP is 00:00:0c:07:ac:XX, where XX corresponds to the HSRP group number in hexadecimal. For HSRP group 15, XX would be 0F in hexadecimal. Therefore, the correct MAC address is 00:00:0c:07:ac:0f.","images":[],"sourcePage":708,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0585","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"An engineer must provide wireless coverage in a square office. The engineer has only one AP and believes that it should be placed it in the middle of the room.\nWhich antenna type should the engineer use?","options":{"A":"directional","B":"polarized","C":"omnidirectional","D":"Yagi"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"For providing wireless coverage in a square office from a single access point positioned in the middle of the room, an omnidirectional antenna is the best choice. This type of antenna radiates signal equally in all directions, ensuring even coverage throughout the entire office space.","images":[],"sourcePage":709,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0586","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Which technology reduces the implementation of STP and leverages both unicast and multicast?","options":{"A":"VLAN","B":"VPC","C":"VXLAN","D":"VSS"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is the technology that reduces the implementation of Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) and leverages both unicast and multicast. VXLAN encapsulates Layer 2 frames within Layer 3 packets, thus enabling Layer 2 segments to be extended across a Layer 3 infrastructure. This reduces the need for STP because the Layer 2 domains are isolated and interconnected over a Layer 3 transport. Additionally, VXLAN can use multicast for handling broadcast, unknown unicast, and multicast (BUM) traffic, which traditional VLANs would handle at Layer 2 using STP.","images":[],"sourcePage":710,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0587","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A customer has recently implemented a new wireless infrastructure using WLC-5520s at a site directly next to a large commercial airport. Users report that they intermittently lose Wi-Fi connectivity, and troubleshooting reveals it is due to frequent channel changes. Which two actions fix this issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Enable DFS channels because they are immune to radar interference.","B":"Restore the DCA default settings because this automatically avoids channel interference.","C":"Remove UNII-2 and Extended UNII-2 channels from the 5 Ghz channel list.","D":"Disable DFS channels to prevent interference with Doppler radar.","E":"Configure channels on the UNII-2 and the Extended UNII-2 sub-bands of the 5 Ghz band only."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"A customer at a site next to a large commercial airport experiences frequent Wi-Fi connectivity interruptions due to frequent channel changes. This issue is typically caused by radar interference which triggers Dynamic Frequency Selection (DFS) and forces access points to change channels to avoid radar. Removing UNII-2 and Extended UNII-2 channels from the 5 GHz channel list will help as these channels are shared with radar systems. Additionally, disabling DFS channels will prevent the access points from frequently changing channels due to radar detection requirements. These actions will stabilize the Wi-Fi connection in this environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":711,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0588","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nWhich GRE tunnel configuration command is missing on R2?","options":{"A":"tunnel source 172.16.1.0","B":"tunnel source 200.1.1.1","C":"tunnel destination 200.1.1.1","D":"tunnel source 192.168.1.2"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"For a GRE tunnel configuration, both the source and the destination IP addresses need to be specified. On R1, the tunnel source is 100.1.1.1 (which is FastEthernet1/0) and the tunnel destination is 200.1.1.1. For the tunnel to function properly, R2 should have a mirror configuration. While the R2 configuration correctly specifies the tunnel destination as 100.1.1.1, it is missing the tunnel source command. The appropriate tunnel source for R2 would be its own FastEthernet1/0 interface, which uses the address 200.1.1.1. Therefore, the correct missing command on R2 is 'tunnel source 200.1.1.1'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0588_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":712,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0589","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"The Gig0/0 interface of two routers is directly connected with a 1G Ethernet link. Which configuration must be applied to the interface of both routers to establish an OSPF adjacency without maintaining a DR/BDR relationship?","options":{"A":"interface Gig0/0 ip ospf network non-broadcast","B":"interface Gig0/0 ip ospf network point-to-multipoint","C":"interface Gig0/0 ip ospf network point-to-point","D":"interface Gig0/0 ip ospf network broadcast"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To establish an OSPF adjacency without maintaining a DR/BDR relationship on a 1G Ethernet link, you should configure the interface as an OSPF point-to-point network. This is because the point-to-point configuration treats the link as a direct connection between the two routers, eliminating the need for a DR/BDR election, which is typically used in multi-access networks.","images":[],"sourcePage":714,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0590","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit.\nAn engineer configures VRRP and issues the show commands to verify operation. What does the engineer confirm about VRRP group 1 from the output?","options":{"A":"Communication between VRRP members is encrypted using MD5.","B":"There is no route to 10.10.1.1/32 in R2's routing table.","C":"R1 is primary if 10.10.1.1/32 is in its routing table.","D":"If R1 reboots, R2 becomes the primary virtual router until R2 reboots."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In the configuration, Router 1 (R1) has a VRRP priority of 110, which is higher than Router 2's (R2) default priority of 100. Additionally, R1 is tracking the reachability of the IP route 10.10.1.1/32. If this route is reachable, R1 maintains its priority of 110, ensuring it remains the primary router. However, if the route becomes unreachable, R1's priority is decremented by 20, making it 90, which is lower than R2’s priority, thus making R2 the primary router. Therefore, as long as the route 10.10.1.1/32 is present in R1's routing table, R1 stays the primary VRRP router.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0590_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":715,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0591","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the Cisco SD-Access solution areas from the left onto the protocols they use on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0591_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0591_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":717,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0592","topic":"Routing Fundamentals & Path Control","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the routing protocols they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0592_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0592_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":719,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0593","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nAn engineer is working with the Cisco DNA Center API. Drag and drop the methods from the left onto the actions that they are used for on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0593_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0593_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":721,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0594","topic":"QoS","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the characteristics from the left onto the QoS components they describe on the right.\nSelect and Place:","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0594_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0594_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":723,"flags":["In the answer image the component names (left side) are missing, so the match between each description and its QoS component cannot be read from the image.","Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0595","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An administrator troubleshoots intermittent connectivity from internal hosts to an external public server. Some internal hosts can connect to the server while others receive an ICMP Host Unreachable message, and these hosts change over time. What is the cause of this issue?","options":{"A":"The NAT ACL and NAT pool share the same name.","B":"The translation does not use address overloading.","C":"The NAT ACL does not match all internal hosts.","D":"The NAT pool netmask is excessively wide."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The issue arises because the NAT pool is not using address overloading. The NAT pool consists of just two IP addresses (198.51.100.5 and 198.51.100.6) and cannot handle all internal hosts needing to access the external server, leading to the exhaustion of the NAT pool as indicated by the error message 'NAT pool might be exhausted'. By enabling address overloading, many internal hosts could be mapped to a single or limited number of external IP addresses, thus resolving the intermittent connectivity issues.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0595_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":724,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0596","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"What is the function of the LISP map resolver?","options":{"A":"to connect a site to the LISP-capable part of a core network publish the EID-to-RLOC mappings for the site, and respond to map-request messages","B":"to advertise routable non-LISP traffic from one address family to LISP sites in a different address family","C":"to send traffic to non-LISP sites when connected to a service provider that does not accept nonroutable EIDs as packet sources","D":"to decapsulate map-request messages from ITRs and forward the messages to the MS"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The function of the LISP map resolver (MR) is to decapsulate map-request messages from ingress tunnel routers (ITRs) and forward these messages to the map server (MS). This is critical for ensuring that the map-request reaches the correct MS responsible for handling the requested endpoint identifiers (EIDs).","images":[],"sourcePage":726,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0597","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An attacker can advertise OSPF fake routes from 172.16.20.0 network to the OSPF domain and black hole traffic. Which action must be taken to avoid this attack and still be able to advertise this subnet into OSPF?","options":{"A":"Configure 172.16.20.0 as a stub network.","B":"Configure graceful restart on the 172.16.20.0 interface.","C":"Configure a passive interface on R2 toward 172.16.20.0.","D":"Apply a policy to filter OSPF packets on R2."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"Configuring a passive interface on R2 toward 172.16.20.0 ensures that OSPF advertisements for this network are still seen in the OSPF domain, but the router will not form OSPF neighbor relationships through that interface. This effectively prevents an attacker from advertising fake OSPF routes into the network via the 172.16.20.0 interface, thus enhancing security while still allowing the network to be advertised in OSPF.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0597_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":727,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0598","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What is the result when a switch that is running PVST+ is added to this network?","options":{"A":"Both switches operate in the PVST+ mode.","B":"Spanning tree is disabled automatically on the network.","C":"Both switches operate in the Rapid PVST+ mode.","D":"DSW2 operates in Rapid PVST + and the new switch operates in PVST+."},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"When a switch running Rapid PVST+ (as indicated by the 'protocol rstp' in the output) is connected to a switch operating in PVST+, the Rapid PVST+ switch adjusts its operation but only on the specific interfaces where it detects the PVST+ BPDUs. The interfaces connected to the PVST+ switch will revert to sending 802.1D BPDUs (indicated by 'P2p Peer(STP)'), while the rest of the interfaces will continue to operate in Rapid PVST+ mode. Therefore, DSW2 continues running Rapid PVST+ on other interfaces, and only the necessary interfaces will operate in PVST+ mode to ensure compatibility with the newly added switch.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0598_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":729,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0599","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which protocol is responsible for data plane forwarding in a Cisco SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"IS-IS","B":"OSPF","C":"VXLAN","D":"LISP"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"VXLAN is responsible for data plane forwarding in a Cisco SD-Access deployment. It encapsulates Ethernet frames and provides a means to extend Layer 2 networks across Layer 3 infrastructures.","images":[],"sourcePage":730,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0600","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. Which Python code parses the response and prints \"18:32:21.474 UTC Sun Mar 10 2019\"?","options":{"A":"print(response['result'][0]('simple_time'])","B":"print(response['result']['body']( 'simple_time'])","C":"print(response['body']['simple_time'])","D":"print(response['jsonrpc']['body'][ 'simple_time'])"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To correctly access the nested 'simple_time' key in the expected JSON response structure, the correct code should be: print(response['result'][0]['simple_time']). This option correctly accesses the nested JSON object by using the appropriate keys and properly balanced brackets.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0600_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":731,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0601","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer configures the trunk and proceeds to configure an ESPAN session to monitor VLANs 10, 20, and 30. Which command must be added to complete this configuration?","options":{"A":"Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)# no vrf 1","B":"Device(config-mon-erspan-src)# no filter vlan 30","C":"Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst)# mtu 1460","D":"Device(config-mon-erspan-src-dst}# erspan-id 6"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To complete the configuration of an ERSPAN session to monitor VLANs 10, 20, and 30, the command 'no filter vlan 30' must be added. In an ERSPAN session configuration, you cannot mix source VLANs and filter VLANs. Since the goal is to monitor VLANs 10, 20, and 30, the filtering of VLAN 30 should be removed to allow its monitoring. Therefore, the command 'Device(config-mon-erspan-src)# no filter vlan 30' is necessary.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0601_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":732,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space."]},{"id":"q0602","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"An administrator is configuring NETCONF using the following XML string. What must the administrator end the request with?\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" ?>\n<rpc message-id=\"9.0\">\n<notification-on >=\"\" <=\"\" p=\"\"> <\/notification-on>","options":{"A":"<\/rpc>]]>]]>","B":"<rpc message-id=\"9.0\"><notification-off/>","C":"<\/rpc-reply>","D":"<\/rpc>"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"NETCONF (Network Configuration Protocol) uses XML for communication between a client and a server for configuration purposes. All NETCONF requests must end with the specific sequence ']]>]]>' which indicates the end of the request to the device. This sequence is processed by the device to terminate the RPC (Remote Procedure Call) request. Therefore, to correctly format and terminate your request, you need the closing tag followed by the ']]>]]>' sequence.","images":[],"sourcePage":733,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0603","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Which Python snippet should be used to store the devices data structure in a JSON file?","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To store the devices data structure in a JSON file in Python, the correct approach is to use the json.dump() function, which serializes a Python object to a JSON formatted stream and writes it to a file. The appropriate snippet should open the file in write mode, use json.dump() to write the data structure to the file, and then close the file. The correct snippet is: OutFile = open('devices.json','w') json.dump(Devices, OutFile) OutFile.close()","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0603_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":734,"flags":["The explanation continued on the next PDF page; the two pieces were joined with a space.","The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0603_optA.webp","B":"images/q0603_optB.webp","C":"images/q0603_optC.webp","D":"images/q0603_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0604","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"A large campus network has deployed two wireless LAN controllers to manage the wireless network WLC1 and WLC2 have been configured as mobility peers. A client device roams from AP1 on WLC1 to AP2 on WLC2, but the controller's client interfaces are on different VLANs. How do the wireless LAN controllers handle the inter-subnet roaming?","options":{"A":"WLC1 marks the client with an anchor entry in its own database. The database entry is copied to the new controller and marked with a foreign entry on WLC2.","B":"WLC2 marks the client with an anchor entry in its own database. The database entry is copied to the new controller and marked with a foreign entry on WLC1.","C":"WLC1 marks the client with a foreign entry in its own database. The database entry is copied to the new controller and marked with an anchor entry on WLC2.","D":"WLC2 marks the client with a foreign entry in its own database. The database entry is copied to the new controller and marked with an anchor entry on WLC1."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"When a client roams between controllers that are on different VLANs in a large campus network, the original controller where the client was initially connected (WLC1) marks the client with an anchor entry in its own database. The database entry is then copied to the new controller (WLC2) and is marked with a foreign entry. This process allows the roam to remain transparent to the wireless client, and the client can maintain its original IP address.","images":[],"sourcePage":736,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0605","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"By default, which virtual MAC address does HSRP group 25 use?","options":{"A":"04:30:83:88:4c:19","B":"00:00:0c:07:ac:25","C":"05:5c:5e:ac:0c:25","D":"00:00:0c:07:ac:19"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"By default, the virtual MAC address used by HSRP group 25 is determined by converting the HSRP group number (25) to a hexadecimal value, which results in 19. Therefore, the correct MAC address format is 00:00:0c:07:ac:XX, where XX is replaced by the hexadecimal value 19, making the correct answer 00:00:0c:07:ac:19.","images":[],"sourcePage":737,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0606","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco Catalyst switch equipped with two supervisor modules an administrator must temporarily remove the active supervisor from the chassis to perform hardware maintenance on it. Which mechanism ensures that the active supervisor removal is not disruptive to the network operation?","options":{"A":"VRRP","B":"HSRP","C":"NSF/NSR","D":"SSO"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"Stateful Switchover (SSO) ensures that the active supervisor's removal is not disruptive to the network operation in a Cisco Catalyst switch with two supervisor modules. SSO enables the standby supervisor to take over control without resetting the entire system, maintaining layer 2 state information seamlessly. This mechanism minimizes downtime efficiently in the course of hardware maintenance.","images":[],"sourcePage":738,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0607","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to create an EEM script that adds an entry to a locally stored text file with a timestamp when a configuration change is made. Not all options are used.","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0607_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0607_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":739,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0608","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"Which function does a fabric AP perform in a Cisco SD-Access deployment?","options":{"A":"It updates wireless clients' locations in the fabric.","B":"It connects wireless clients to the fabric.","C":"It manages wireless clients' membership information in the fabric.","D":"It configures security policies down to wireless clients in the fabric."},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access deployment, a fabric Access Point (AP) primarily functions to connect wireless clients to the fabric. The fabric AP ensures that wireless clients can communicate within the network by serving as the intermediary that bridges the wireless access to the fabric network architecture.","images":[],"sourcePage":740,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0609","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. What is the effect of introducing the sampler feature into the Flexible NetFlow configuration on the router?","options":{"A":"NetFlow updates to the collector are sent 50% less frequently.","B":"Every second IPv4 packet is forwarded to the collector for inspection.","C":"CPU and memory utilization are reduced when compared with what is required for full NetFlow.","D":"The resolution of sampling data increases, but it requires more performance from the router."},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"The sampler feature in Flexible NetFlow configuration reduces the number of packets that need to be analyzed, thereby reducing CPU and memory utilization on the router. This results in less resource consumption compared to full NetFlow analysis, which requires examining every single packet. The deterministic 1 out of 2 mode specified in the configuration indicates that only every second packet is analyzed, leading to the reduced resource usage.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0609_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":741,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0610","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP\n-\nDrag and drop the snippets onto the blanks within the code to construct a script that configures a loopback interface with an IP address. Not all options are used.","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0610_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0610_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":743,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0611","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"","options":{"A":"","B":"","C":"","D":""},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"To enable a successful login to router R1 and to support NETCONF/YANG data modeling, the configuration must include setting up AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) using the 'aaa new-model' command. The username must be created with privilege 15 and a secret password specified. Additionally, the 'netconf-yang' command is required to support NETCONF/YANG, as it registers NETCONF capabilities on the device. Therefore, the correct configuration is: 'netconf-yang', 'username admin privilege 15 secret cisco123', 'aaa new-model', and 'aaa authorization exec default local'.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0611_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":745,"flags":["The question text is empty in the PDF text: the question is only inside the exhibit image (the first image), which is shown above the options.","The answer options (A to D) are images, in the order they appear in the PDF; the option text is empty."],"optionImages":{"A":"images/q0611_optA.webp","B":"images/q0611_optB.webp","C":"images/q0611_optC.webp","D":"images/q0611_optD.webp"}},{"id":"q0612","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the exhibit. An engineer has configured an IP SLA for UDP echos. Which command is needed to start the IP SLA to lest every 30 seconds and continue until stopped?","options":{"A":"ip sla schedule 100 life forever","B":"ip sla schedule 30 start-time now life forever","C":"ip sla schedule 100 start-time now life 30","D":"ip sla schedule 100 start-time now life forever"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To start the IP SLA operation immediately and ensure it continues running indefinitely, the correct command is 'ip sla schedule 100 start-time now life forever'. This command specifies the operation ID (100), sets the start time to 'now', and allows the operation to run continuously ('life forever'). The frequency of the operation has already been set to 30 seconds in the initial configuration, so it does not need to be included in this command. Therefore, D is the correct answer.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0612_exhibit.webp"}],"sourcePage":747,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0613","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"What is the calculation that is used to measure the radiated power of a signal after it has gone through the radio, antenna cable, and antenna?","options":{"A":"mW","B":"ERIP","C":"dBm","D":"dBi"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"To measure the radiated power of a signal after it has gone through the radio, antenna cable, and antenna, the correct calculation is Effective Isotropic Radiated Power (EIRP). EIRP takes into account the power output of the radio, the losses in the cable, and the gain of the antenna to determine the radiated power.","images":[],"sourcePage":748,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0614","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which Quality of Service (QoS) mechanism is used to identify traffic flow and to use DSCP, IP Precedence values, and MPLS EXP bits to create different priority levels?","options":{"A":"Policing","B":"Marking","C":"Queueing","D":"Classification"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Marking is the Quality of Service (QoS) mechanism that involves modifying specific fields within a packet or frame header to indicate the packet's priority level. This is done using DSCP (Differentiated Services Code Point), IP Precedence values, and MPLS EXP (Multiprotocol Label Switching Experimental) bits. This mechanism allows traffic to be identified and appropriately prioritized according to these marked values, thereby creating different priority levels in the network.","images":[],"sourcePage":749,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0615","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"What are two valid modes that Cisco Express Forwarding can operate in? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Central CEF mode","B":"Dense CEF mode","C":"Sparse CEF mode","D":"Distributed CEF mode","E":"Routed CEF mode"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"The two valid modes that Cisco Express Forwarding (CEF) can operate in are Central CEF mode and Distributed CEF mode. In Central CEF mode, the CEF forwarding information base (FIB) and adjacency tables are stored on the route processor, which performs the express forwarding. In Distributed CEF mode, line cards maintain their own copies of the FIB and adjacency tables, allowing them to perform express forwarding independently, thus offloading the main processor from the switching operation. This mode is typically used in high-performance routing environments.","images":[],"sourcePage":750,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0616","topic":"Cloud vs On-Premises","type":"mcq","question":"You need to weigh the pros and cons of deploying a premise-based data center versus using a cloud-based data center deployment. What is an advantage of using a premise-based solution? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Lower application latency for end users","B":"Easily scalable","C":"Lower capital costs","D":"Reduced deployment times","E":"Increased control over the environment"},"correctAnswer":["A","E"],"explanation":"Using a premise-based data center has the advantage of potentially lower application latency for end users because the data can be processed closer to where it is generated or used, reducing the travel time over the internet. Additionally, it provides increased control over the environment, as organizations can manage and customize the hardware, software, security measures, and compliance requirements directly.","images":[],"sourcePage":751,"flags":["A line wrap inside the question text was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0617","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which Quality of Service (QoS) mechanism allows for the creation of multiple levels of QoS policy, providing a more granular degree of traffic management?","options":{"A":"Policing","B":"H-QoS","C":"Congestion avoidance","D":"Dual Policy"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"Hierarchical Quality of Service (H-QoS) allows for the creation of multiple levels of QoS policy, providing a more granular degree of traffic management. This enables application of different QoS rules at various layers, improving the overall management and prioritization of network traffic.","images":[],"sourcePage":752,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0618","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Which Quality of Service (QoS) mechanism allows the network administrator to control the maximum rate of traffic received or sent on a given interface?","options":{"A":"Policing","B":"Marking","C":"Queueing","D":"Classification"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Policing is a Quality of Service (QoS) mechanism that allows a network administrator to control the maximum rate of traffic received or sent on a given interface. It limits the bandwidth by dropping or remarking packets that exceed the specified rate, thereby ensuring that traffic does not exceed configured levels.","images":[],"sourcePage":753,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0619","topic":"QoS","type":"mcq","question":"Refer to the following two images regarding QoS Traffic Shaping and Traffic Policing:\nImage A:\nImage B:\nBased on the images, which of the following are true? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Image A depicts the result of Traffic Shaping","B":"Image A depicts the result of Traffic Policing","C":"Image B depicts the result of Traffic Shaping","D":"Image B depicts the result of Traffic Policing"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Traffic Shaping, depicted in Image A, smooths out the traffic rate by holding packets in buffers to ensure the rate does not exceed a specific threshold. This results in a controlled bandwidth usage over time. Traffic Policing, depicted in Image B, enforces a traffic rate limit by discarding packets or marking them as lower priority if they exceed the threshold. This can result in a sudden drop to zero traffic if the rate limit is exceeded.","images":[{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0619_exhibit1.webp"},{"role":"exhibit","file":"images/q0619_exhibit2.webp"}],"sourcePage":754,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0620","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access fabric architecture, which of the following are valid device roles (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Control Plane Node","B":"Access routing device","C":"Edge Node","D":"Border Node","E":"Distributed Node"},"correctAnswer":["A","C","D"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access fabric architecture, the valid device roles are Control Plane Node, Edge Node, and Border Node. The Control Plane Node is responsible for the LISP database and mapping, the Edge Node is where endpoints are connected, and the Border Node interfaces with networks external to the fabric. These roles are fundamental to the operation of the SD-Access architecture.","images":[],"sourcePage":756,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0621","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are valid statements when configuring Nonstop Forwarding (NSF) with Stateful Switchover (SSO) on a Cisco device? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"supports multicast routing protocols","B":"Supports IPv4 and IPv6","C":"Nonstop Forwarding requires SSO to also be configured","D":"HSRP is not supported with NSF/SSO","E":"Improper implementation of NSF/SSO can result in routing loops"},"correctAnswer":["C","E"],"explanation":"Nonstop Forwarding (NSF) requires Stateful Switchover (SSO) to be configured because NSF depends on the high availability features provided by SSO to maintain network stability during switchover events. Additionally, improper implementation of NSF/SSO can result in routing loops because any misconfiguration or inconsistency in the state information between the active and standby processors can cause network instability and potential routing issues.","images":[],"sourcePage":757,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0622","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless network, which device is used as an entry and exit point in and out of the fabric?","options":{"A":"fabric edge node","B":"control plane node","C":"fabric border node","D":"fabric access points"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access wireless network, the fabric border node serves as the entry and exit point in and out of the fabric. The fabric border node, or border node, connects the SD-Access fabric to external networks such as the Internet or other non-fabric networks. This device is responsible for routing and enforcing policies at the edge of the fabric, ensuring that data entering or exiting the fabric is properly managed and secured.","images":[],"sourcePage":758,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0623","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"The Overlay Management Protocol (OMP) is used as the control plane protocol and forms peers between the VSmart Controller and the SD-WAN edge devices. OMP is responsible for advertising which three types of routes in the SD-WAN network? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"OMP routes","B":"TLOCs","C":"MP-BGP","D":"LISP routes","E":"Service routes"},"correctAnswer":["A","B","E"],"explanation":"The Overlay Management Protocol (OMP) in an SD-WAN network is responsible for advertising three types of routes. OMP routes, also known as vRoutes, are prefixes learned from the local site via connected interfaces and various routing protocols, redistributed into OMP to be carried across the overlay network. TLOCs, or Transport Locators, refer to the next-hop transport locations, including various attributes like IP addresses, site ID, and encryption details. Service routes represent services such as firewalls or IPS that are connected to the local-site network and advertised to other sites for service insertion. These types of routes are critical for the operation and management of the SD-WAN network.","images":[],"sourcePage":759,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0624","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are the three components of the three-tier hierarchical networking model used in the classical Cisco networks design? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Distribution","B":"Core","D":"Access","E":"Leaf","F":"Spine"},"correctAnswer":["A","B","D"],"explanation":"The three components of the three-tier hierarchical networking model used in classical Cisco network design are Distribution, Core, and Access. In this model, the Core layer is responsible for fast switching, the Distribution layer aggregates data and enforces network policies, and the Access layer provides connectivity to end devices.","images":[],"sourcePage":760,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0625","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are the two components of the two-tier modern data center design? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Distribution","B":"Core","D":"Access","E":"Leaf","F":"Spine"},"correctAnswer":["E","F"],"explanation":"The two components of the two-tier modern data center design are Leaf and Spine. This architecture simplifies the traditional three-tier setup by collapsing the core and distribution layers into a single 'spine' layer. The leaf switches provide connectivity to servers and storage, and the spine switches interconnect the leaf switches, enabling efficient and high-speed data transfer.","images":[],"sourcePage":761,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0626","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-WAN network, which VPN Identifier is reserved for carrying out-of-band network management traffic?","options":{"A":"VPN 0","B":"VPN 1","C":"VPN 512","D":"VPN 514"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"VPN 512 is reserved for carrying out-of-band network management traffic in a Cisco SD-WAN network. This specific VPN is utilized for the management and communication of the Viptela devices within the overlay network, ensuring that all management traffic is segregated from data plane traffic and handled appropriately.","images":[],"sourcePage":762,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0627","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-WAN network, which VPN Identifier is reserved as the transport VPN, carrying control traffic?","options":{"A":"VPN 0","B":"VPN 1","C":"VPN 512","D":"VPN 514"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-WAN network, VPN 0 is reserved as the transport VPN, carrying control traffic via the configured WAN transport interfaces. Initially, VPN 0 contains all of a device's interfaces except for the management interface, and all interfaces are disabled. This makes it the correct choice for the transport VPN.","images":[],"sourcePage":763,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0628","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which Cisco SD-WAN component acts as a single pane of glass for management and offers centralized fault, performance, accounting, and configuration management?","options":{"A":"vBond","B":"vEdge","C":"vSmart","D":"vManage"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The Cisco SD-WAN component that acts as a single pane of glass for management and offers centralized fault, performance, accounting, and configuration management is vManage. It is specifically designed to handle these tasks by providing a unified interface for managing the SD-WAN environment, ensuring administrators can monitor and configure the network efficiently.","images":[],"sourcePage":764,"flags":["A line wrap inside the explanation was joined without a space (a hyphenated command or word split across lines)."]},{"id":"q0629","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"You need to implement a First Hop Redundancy Protocol (FHRP) in a dual stack (IPv4 and IPV6) environment that utilizes devices from multiple different vendors. Which protocol best meets these needs?","options":{"A":"HSRP","B":"GLBP","C":"VRRPv1","D":"VRRPv2"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"VRRPv2 is designed to operate in vendor-agnostic environments, which is essential when using devices from multiple different vendors. While VRRPv2 primarily supports IPv4, it is still the most appropriate choice among the given options because HSRP and GLBP are proprietary to Cisco, and VRRPv1 is outdated and less commonly used. Although VRRPv3 supports both IPv4 and IPv6, the question suggests that this version is not among the provided options. Therefore, VRRPv2 best meets the requirements for a dual-stack environment in a multi-vendor scenario.","images":[],"sourcePage":765,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0630","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"A wireless client roams from one Access Point to another Access Point using a different switch in a Cisco SD-Access network. If ONLY a single Wireless Lan Controller in involved, what roaming methods is being used?","options":{"A":"L3 roaming","B":"inter-xTR","C":"auto anchor","D":"bridged roaming"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access network, when a wireless client roams from one Access Point to another Access Point that is connected through a different switch and only a single Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) is involved, the roaming method being used is known as Inter-xTR. In this context, xTR stands for access-switch that acts as a fabric edge node. This type of roaming updates the map server with the client's location information along with the local client database.","images":[],"sourcePage":766,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0631","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access network where VXLAN is used for encapsulating data packets, what is the minimum MTU setting that devices should be configured with?","options":{"A":"1492","B":"1500","C":"1518","D":"1550"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access network where VXLAN is used for encapsulating data packets, VXLAN adds a 50-byte overhead to the original frame. This means the Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) needs to be increased by 50 bytes over the standard Ethernet MTU of 1500 bytes to accommodate the encapsulated packets. Therefore, the minimum MTU setting that devices should be configured with is 1550 bytes.","images":[],"sourcePage":767,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0632","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which Cisco Locator/ID Separation Protocol (LISP) device receives packets from remote site facing devices and either decapsulates the LISP packets or routes them natively?","options":{"A":"ITR","B":"ETR","C":"MS","D":"MR"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct answer is ETR (Egress Tunnel Router). An ETR is responsible for receiving packets from core-facing interfaces (i.e., interfaces that face the transport infrastructure), and it can either decapsulate LISP packets or route them natively to local Endpoint Identifiers (EIDs) at the site. In contrast, an ITR (Ingress Tunnel Router) encapsulates packets for transmission to remote LISP sites or forwards them natively to non-LISP sites, but it does not decapsulate them.","images":[],"sourcePage":768,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0633","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the use of Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) in a Cisco SD-WAN environment are true?","options":{"A":"BFD cannot be disabled on SD-WAN routers.","B":"OSPFv3 is not supported with BFD.","C":"In addition to link failure detection, it is also used to measure loss and latency used by application aware routing.","D":"Is not typically enabled for OMP.","E":"Does not support BGP."},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) in a Cisco SD-WAN environment is used not only for link failure detection but also for measuring loss and latency, which are key metrics for application-aware routing. Additionally, BFD is typically enabled for OMP (Overlay Management Protocol) to ensure optimal performance and fault detection across overlay tunnels. While it provides essential link statistics, it is not true that BFD cannot be disabled on SD-WAN routers or that it does not support BGP.","images":[],"sourcePage":769,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0634","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements are TRUE regarding the Link Management Protocol (LMP) when used in the Cisco Stackwise virtual link? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"It determines the switch priority.","B":"It negotiates the version of the virtual header","C":"It verifies link integrity via bidirectional forwarding","D":"It performs auto discovery of other active Stackwise switches"},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"The Link Management Protocol (LMP) is responsible for negotiating the version of the virtual header between switches in a Cisco Stackwise virtual link, ensuring compatibility and smooth communication. Additionally, LMP verifies link integrity by establishing bidirectional traffic forwarding, which ensures that any links not meeting this criteria are rejected. These tasks are essential for maintaining a reliable and functional Stackwise virtual link environment.","images":[],"sourcePage":770,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0635","topic":"VRF, GRE & IPsec Tunnels","type":"mcq","question":"You have configured router R1 with multiple VRF’s in order to support multiple customer VPN networks. If you wanted to see the best path for the `10.2.1.0.24` route in VRF Green, what command would you use?","options":{"A":"show ip route vrf Green 10.2.1.0","B":"show ip route 10.2.1.0 vrf Green","C":"show route all 10.2.1.0","D":"show ip route 10.2.1.0 Green"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"To view the best path for a specific route in a particular VRF, the correct command format is 'show ip route vrf [VRF_NAME] [IP_ADDRESS]'. In this context, 'show ip route vrf Green 10.2.1.0' is the appropriate command for viewing the route information for the 10.2.1.0 network in the 'Green' VRF.","images":[],"sourcePage":771,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0636","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are benefits from implementing the use of VXLAN’s in a network? (Choose two)","options":{"A":"Increased scalability since VXLAN extends the IF field to 24 bits, providing up to 16 million unique ID values.","B":"Makes the implementation of Spanning Tree more efficient.","C":"Can be used to replace layer 3 routing protocols and increase routing efficiency at layer 2.","D":"Supports Equal Cost Multi-pathing (ECMP) so that load balancing over multiple links can be used."},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Implementing VXLANs in a network provides two major benefits. First, increased scalability is achieved as VXLAN extends the IF field to 24 bits, allowing for up to 16 million unique ID values. This is a significant improvement over traditional VLANs, which are limited to 4096 IDs. Second, VXLAN supports Equal Cost Multi-pathing (ECMP), which enhances load balancing by allowing multiple path options across the network. This capability utilizes multiple pathways to distribute traffic evenly, improving network efficiency and redundancy.","images":[],"sourcePage":772,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0637","topic":"Packet Forwarding (RIB, FIB, CEF, TCAM)","type":"mcq","question":"Which component of TCP defines the maximum packet size that a host interface is able to accept on ingress?","options":{"A":"MTU","B":"PATH MTU","C":"Window size","D":"MRU"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The Maximum Receive Unit (MRU) defines the largest packet size that an interface can receive on ingress. This parameter ensures that packets exceeding this size are either fragmented or dropped to prevent errors and buffer overflows. MRU operates at a level concerning interface capabilities rather than TCP's flow control mechanisms, which are managed by the window size.","images":[],"sourcePage":773,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0638","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are examples of Type 2 hypervisors? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"VMware ESXi","B":"Oracle VirtualBox","C":"Oracle Solaris Zones","D":"Microsoft Hyper-V","E":"Microsoft Virtual PC"},"correctAnswer":["B","C","E"],"explanation":"Type 2 hypervisors, also known as hosted hypervisors, run on top of an existing operating system and rely on the host OS for their operations. Examples of Type 2 hypervisors include Oracle VirtualBox which runs on various operating systems like Windows, Linux, and Mac OS X, and distributes resources to virtual machines. Microsoft Virtual PC is another example that requires a host operating system like Windows. Oracle Solaris Zones, however, is a container-based virtualization technology, not a hypervisor. Therefore, another example of a Type 2 hypervisor is not included in the options given. Based on the provided options, Oracle VirtualBox and Microsoft Virtual PC are accurate examples of Type 2 hypervisors. It is necessary to select just these two options to accurately answer the question.","images":[],"sourcePage":774,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0639","topic":"Wireless","type":"mcq","question":"EIRP (Effective Isotropic Radiated Power) is the actual amount of signal leaving the antenna. It is a measurement value in db and is based on which three components? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Transmit Power","B":"RSSI","C":"Cable Loss","D":"Antenna Gain","E":"SNR"},"correctAnswer":["A","C","D"],"explanation":"To determine EIRP (Effective Isotropic Radiated Power), you need to consider three key components: Transmit Power, Cable Loss, and Antenna Gain. Transmit Power refers to the initial power output from the transmitter. Cable Loss represents the reductions in signal strength through the transmission medium (cables). Antenna Gain measures the increase in signal strength provided by the antenna's design. These three factors together define the actual signal strength emitted from the antenna, making them crucial for calculating EIRP.","images":[],"sourcePage":775,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0640","topic":"OSPF","type":"mcq","question":"A switch is attached to router R1 on its gig 0/0 interface. Fort security reasons, you want to prevent R1 from sending OSPF hellos to the switch. Which command should be enabled to accomplish this?","options":{"A":"R1(config-router)#ip ospf hello disable","B":"R1(config-router)#ip ospf hello-interval 0","C":"R1(config)#passive-interface Gig 0/0","D":"R1(config-router)#passive-interface Gig 0/0"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To prevent a router from sending OSPF hello packets on a specific interface, you need to configure the interface as a passive interface within the OSPF process. The correct command is entered in the router OSPF configuration mode, indicating the specific interface you want to set as passive. Therefore, the correct command is 'R1(config-router)#passive-interface Gig 0/0'.","images":[],"sourcePage":776,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0641","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"What are some of the key differences between HSRPv1 and HSRPv2? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"HSRPv1 uses the multicast address of 224.0.0.102 while HSRPv2 uses 225.0.0.2.","B":"HSRP uses a group range of 0-255, while HSRP uses a group range of 0-4095.","C":"HSRPv1 uses seconds based timers, while HSRPv2 uses milliseconds based timers.","D":"HSRPv1 provides support for IPv6, while HSRPv2 supports IPv4 only."},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"HSRPv1 uses a group range of 0-255, while HSRPv2 uses a group range of 0-4095. In HSRPv1, timers are based on seconds, whereas HSRPv2 can use milliseconds based timers.","images":[],"sourcePage":777,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0642","topic":"Layer 2 Switching (VLAN, STP, EtherChannel)","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are valid Port Aggregation Protocol (PAgP) modes? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"On","B":"Active","C":"Passive","D":"Auto","E":"Desirable"},"correctAnswer":["D","E"],"explanation":"Port Aggregation Protocol (PAgP) is a Cisco proprietary protocol used for the automatic creation of EtherChannel links. The valid PAgP modes are 'Auto' and 'Desirable'. 'Auto' allows the interface to respond to PAgP packets but does not initiate them, while 'Desirable' actively tries to initiate the formation of an EtherChannel. Other modes like 'On' are manual settings that do not use the PAgP protocol.","images":[],"sourcePage":778,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0643","topic":"FHRP (HSRP, VRRP, GLBP)","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are TRUE statements regarding the Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) feature? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Pre-emption is enabled by default","B":"The router priority is a configurable value from 0-4095","C":"MD5 authentication is supported with VRRP","D":"Secondary IP addresses are supported with VRRP","E":"VRRP can only be used with Cisco devices"},"correctAnswer":["A","D"],"explanation":"Pre-emption is enabled by default in VRRP, allowing a higher priority router to take over from a lower priority router if it becomes available. Additionally, VRRP supports the use of secondary IP addresses, allowing the virtual router to manage multiple IP addresses on an interface. The router priority, however, is not configurable from 0-4095, but rather from 0-255, and MD5 authentication is no longer supported in the standard VRRP protocol as per the latest RFCs. VRRP is also an open standard that can be used with devices from vendors other than Cisco.","images":[],"sourcePage":779,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0644","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"You want to securely implement the Network Time Protocol (NTP) on your network. What two mechanisms are available to secure NTP? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"IPSec communication","B":"MD5 authentication keys","C":"Role based access control (RBAC)","D":"access-group configuration"},"correctAnswer":["A","B"],"explanation":"To secure the Network Time Protocol (NTP) on a network, IPSec communication and MD5 authentication keys are two effective mechanisms. IPSec communication ensures that the data exchanged between NTP servers is encrypted and secured against interception or tampering. MD5 authentication keys provide a method to authenticate NTP messages, ensuring that only messages from trusted sources are accepted.","images":[],"sourcePage":780,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0645","topic":"EIGRP","type":"mcq","question":"Which Cisco EIGRP K-values are set to zero by default? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Bandwidth","B":"Load","C":"Total Delay","D":"Reliability","E":"MTU"},"correctAnswer":["B","D","E"],"explanation":"The default EIGRP K-values set to zero by Cisco are Load, Reliability, and MTU. In EIGRP, the K-values are used to determine the metric for routing decisions. By default, Cisco sets the K-values for Load (K2), Reliability (K4), and MTU (K5) to zero, meaning they are not used in the metric calculation. Bandwidth (K1) and Total Delay (K3) have a default value of 1 and are used in the metric computation by default.","images":[],"sourcePage":781,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0646","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"mcq","question":"What are the four stages of obtaining an IP address lease from a DHCP server?","options":{"A":"Discover, Offer, Release, Renew","B":"Discover, Obtain, Request, Renew","C":"Determine, Obtain, Release, Acknowledge","D":"Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"The four stages of obtaining an IP address lease from a DHCP server are Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledge. In the Discover phase, the client sends a broadcast message to identify available DHCP servers. During the Offer phase, a DHCP server responds with an offer to provide an IP address. In the Request phase, the client accepts one of the offers and requests the IP address. Finally, in the Acknowledge phase, the DHCP server acknowledges the request and leases the IP address to the client.","images":[],"sourcePage":782,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0647","topic":"Multicast (PIM, RP)","type":"mcq","question":"A new multicast server is being added to an existing PIM Sparse mode network. Which device in this network must the new server register with before its multicast traffic can be dispersed throughout the network?","options":{"A":"IGMP Querier","B":"Local PIM router","C":"Local IGMP switch","D":"Rendezvous Point (RP)"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"In PIM Sparse mode networks, a multicast source must register with the Rendezvous Point (RP) before its multicast traffic can be dispersed throughout the network. The RP acts as a central point where sources register and from which multicast traffic is distributed to interested receivers. This ensures that multicast traffic is only forwarded to parts of the network where there are interested receivers, optimizing bandwidth usage.","images":[],"sourcePage":783,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0648","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"You want to create a policy that allows all TCP traffic in the port range of 20 to 110, except for telnet traffic, which should be dropped. Which of the following access control lists will accomplish this?","options":{"A":"deny tcp any any eq 22 permit tcp any any gt 20 lt 110","B":"permit tcp any any range 22 443 deny tcp any any eq 23","C":"deny tcp any any eq 23 permit tcp any any","D":"deny tcp any any eq 23 permit tcp any any range 20 110"},"correctAnswer":["D"],"explanation":"To create a policy that allows all TCP traffic in the port range of 20 to 110, except for telnet traffic (port 23), which should be dropped, you need to first deny TCP traffic on port 23 and then allow TCP traffic in the specified range. The ACL should deny the specific unwanted traffic (telnet) and then permit the rest of the required range. The correct ACL is to 'deny tcp any any eq 23' followed by 'permit tcp any any range 20 110'. This ensures that telnet traffic is blocked while allowing all other specified TCP traffic.","images":[],"sourcePage":784,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0649","topic":"Cisco SD-Access","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco SD-Access network architecture, what is the role of the Fabric Edge Node?","options":{"A":"It manages endpoint to device relationships","B":"It connects external layer 3 networks to the SDA fabric","C":"It connects wired endpoints to the SDA fabric","D":"It connects wireless endpoints to the SDA fabric"},"correctAnswer":["C"],"explanation":"In a Cisco SD-Access network architecture, the Fabric Edge Node connects wired endpoints to the SDA fabric. The Fabric Edge Node operates at the access layer, where it provides connectivity for wired devices, such as computers and IP phones, to the network. This forms the crucial point of integration for wired endpoints into the SD-Access network structure.","images":[],"sourcePage":785,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0650","topic":"Infrastructure Security (ACLs, AAA, Hardening)","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following are features typically only found in a Next Generation (NextGen) firewall? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Network Address Translation (NAT)","B":"Secure remote access VPN (RA VPN)","C":"Deep packet inspection","D":"reputation based malware detection","E":"IPSec site-to-site VPN"},"correctAnswer":["C","D"],"explanation":"Next Generation (NextGen) firewalls possess distinct features that set them apart from traditional firewalls. One key feature is deep packet inspection, which allows these firewalls to analyze and understand the contents of packets beyond the traditional packet header information. Another feature typically found in NextGen firewalls is reputation-based malware detection, which utilizes advanced methods to identify and block malicious activities based on the reputation of files or IP addresses. These capabilities enable NextGen firewalls to provide comprehensive protection by monitoring and analyzing traffic in a more sophisticated manner than traditional firewalls.","images":[],"sourcePage":786,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0651","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"JSON web tokens (JWT) are used to secure JSON based communications. Which of the following fields make up a JWT? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Header","B":"Trailer","C":"Payload","D":"Sequence number","E":"Signature"},"correctAnswer":["A","C","E"],"explanation":"A JSON Web Token (JWT) is comprised of three parts. The Header, which includes information about how the JWT is encoded and which algorithm is used. The Payload, which contains the claims or the data you want to transmit, such as user information. The Signature, which is created using the header, the payload, and a secret key, ensuring the token's integrity and authenticity. These three elements collectively make up a JWT.","images":[],"sourcePage":787,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0652","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Ansible is being used in a network for configuration and management automation. Which of the following are TRUE statements regarding Ansible? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Requires an agent on the end device.","B":"Utilizes the concept of playbooks to execute the configuration.","C":"Uses a pull model, where the end devices pull configuration files from the Ansible server.","D":"Utilizes SSH."},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"Ansible utilizes the concept of playbooks to execute the configuration, allowing users to define a series of tasks and their desired states in a YAML format. Additionally, Ansible operates in an agentless manner and primarily uses SSH for communication with remote devices, which means there is no need to install an agent on the end devices.","images":[],"sourcePage":788,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0653","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco Software Defined Networking (SDN) architecture, what is used to describe the API communication between the SDN controller and the network elements (routers and switches) that it manages?","options":{"A":"Southbound API","B":"Northbound API","C":"Westbound API","D":"Eastbound API."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"In a Cisco Software Defined Networking (SDN) architecture, the Southbound API is used to describe the API communication between the SDN controller and the network elements (routers and switches) that it manages. This API allows the controller to directly communicate with the hardware to manage and control their configuration and operation.","images":[],"sourcePage":789,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0654","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"In a Cisco VXLAN based network, which of the following best describes the main function of a VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint (VTEP)?","options":{"A":"A device that performs VXLAN encapsulation and decapsulation.","B":"It is a 24 bit segment ID that defines the broadcast domain.","C":"It is the Logical interface where the encapsulation and de-encapsulation occurs.","D":"It is a device that performs tunneling using GRE."},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"A VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint (VTEP) is a device that performs VXLAN encapsulation and decapsulation. This means that it takes standard Ethernet frames, encapsulates them in VXLAN headers, and forwards them over an IP network. When incoming VXLAN packets are received, the VTEP decapsulates them back to standard Ethernet frames. This encapsulation and decapsulation process allows VXLAN to extend layer 2 segments over a layer 3 network, facilitating network scalability and flexibility.","images":[],"sourcePage":790,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0655","topic":"Campus Design & High Availability","type":"mcq","question":"Two Cisco switches are logically configured as a single switch using Cisco Stackwise technology. This will result in virtually combining which two planes? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Data Plane","B":"Control Plane","C":"Forwarding Plane","D":"Management Plane","E":"Bearer Plane"},"correctAnswer":["B","D"],"explanation":"When two Cisco switches are configured with Cisco StackWise technology, they are virtually combined to operate as a single logical switch. This configuration effectively merges the control plane and the management plane. The control plane is responsible for the decision-making processes of the network, including routing and switching decisions. The management plane handles the administrative interface, including configuration and monitoring tasks. Therefore, combining these two planes is essential for the integrated operation of the switches in a StackWise configuration.","images":[],"sourcePage":791,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0656","topic":"IP Services (DHCP, NAT, NTP, DNS)","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nPlease drag and drop the options provided in the left to configure NTP in client mode.","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0656_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0656_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":792,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0657","topic":"Hypervisors & Virtual Machines","type":"mcq","question":"Please select the correct option that shows the correct combination for the Type 1 Hypervisor.","options":{"A":"Hardware; Hypervisor; Guest OS","B":"Hardware; Host OS; Hypervisor; Guest OS","C":"Host OS; Hypervisor; Guest OS","D":"Hardware; Host OS; Guest OS"},"correctAnswer":["A"],"explanation":"Type 1 hypervisors, also known as bare-metal hypervisors, run directly on the underlying physical hardware without requiring a host operating system. The structure follows: Hardware; Hypervisor; Guest OS. This setup allows for better performance and efficiency compared to Type 2 hypervisors, which require a host OS. Therefore, the correct combination for a Type 1 hypervisor is Hardware; Hypervisor; Guest OS.","images":[],"sourcePage":793,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0658","topic":"Wireless","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP -\nDrag and drop the definitions on the left to their respective technological names on the right.","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0658_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0658_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":794,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0659","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"mcq","question":"Select the prerequisites for configuring LISP from the below options. (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Determine the type of LISP deployment you intend to deploy","B":"One can directly deploy LISP without determining the type.","C":"LISP configuration requires the datak9 license.","D":"LISP configuration requires the advanced ip services license."},"correctAnswer":["A","C"],"explanation":"To configure LISP, it is essential to determine the type of LISP deployment you intend to deploy because this will decide the necessary functionality, hardware, software, and additional support required for the deployment. Additionally, the LISP configuration requires the datak9 license, which is a prerequisite for enabling the LISP features.","images":[],"sourcePage":795,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0660","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"Select the benefits of implementing Cisco DNA Center. (Choose all that apply.)","options":{"A":"Simplified management","B":"Automatic VPN tunnelling","C":"One click Configuration","D":"Policy Driven Provisioning","E":"Ensure Network & Appliance performance"},"correctAnswer":["A","C","D"],"explanation":"Implementing Cisco DNA Center provides several key benefits. Firstly, it offers simplified management, which makes network administration more straightforward and efficient. Secondly, one-click configuration allows users to quickly and easily configure devices through templates, significantly speeding up deployment times. Finally, policy-driven provisioning ensures that network policies are consistently and automatically applied during the provisioning process, enhancing network security and performance. While ensuring network and appliance performance is a desirable goal, it is not a guaranteed benefit of Cisco DNA Center, and automatic VPN tunnelling is not a primary feature.","images":[],"sourcePage":796,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0661","topic":"Network Assurance & Monitoring","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator need to configure Netflow on the devices in his network. He has Source IP Address, Destination IP Address, Source Port number & Destination port number. What additional information do he need to configure Netflow.","options":{"A":"Layer 3 Protocol type","B":"Encryption type","C":"ToS (Type of Service) byte","D":"Input Logical Interface","E":"Hashing Algorithm","F":"Transform-set details"},"correctAnswer":["A","C","D"],"explanation":"To configure NetFlow, a network administrator needs to provide the Layer 3 protocol type, Type of Service (ToS) byte, and Input Logical Interface in addition to the Source IP address, Destination IP address, Source Port number, and Destination Port number. The Layer 3 protocol type helps identify the protocol used in the network layer, the ToS byte is used for prioritization and quality of service decisions, and the Input Logical Interface identifies the specific interface through which packets enter the network device.","images":[],"sourcePage":797,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0662","topic":"Automation & Programmability","type":"mcq","question":"A network administrator is configuring a configuration management tool for some network devices that does not support agent. Select what option will you pick from the below options to successfully configure configuration management tool for that device.","options":{"A":"Agent based Configuration","B":"Agent Less Configuration","C":"Proxy-agent Configuration","D":"None of the above"},"correctAnswer":["B"],"explanation":"The correct approach to configuring a configuration management tool for devices that do not support agents is to use an agentless configuration. An agentless configuration does not require any software or agent to be installed on the managed devices, which makes it suitable for devices that do not support agents. This method leverages existing protocols like SSH or WinRM to manage the devices remotely and is ideal for the scenario described in the question.","images":[],"sourcePage":798,"flags":[]},{"id":"q0663","topic":"LISP & VXLAN","type":"lab","question":"DRAG DROP\n-\nDrag and drop the definitions in the left to their respective Terminology in the right.","options":{},"correctAnswer":[],"explanation":"","images":[{"role":"question","file":"images/q0663_question.webp"},{"role":"answer","file":"images/q0663_answer.webp"}],"sourcePage":799,"flags":["Drag-and-drop item: the solution exists only as an image (see images with role 'answer'); not transcribed to text."]},{"id":"q0664","topic":"Cisco SD-WAN","type":"mcq","question":"Select the devices from the below options that can be fart of Cisco SDWAN Solution. (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"ISR 2900","B":"ASR 1000","C":"IR8300","D":"FTD 1120","E":"ASR 9000"},"correctAnswer":["B","C"],"explanation":"In the context of Cisco SDWAN solutions, the relevant devices typically include the Integrated Services Router (ISR) and the Aggregation Services Router (ASR) series. The ISR 2900 is a well-known router that supports advanced networking services relevant to SDWAN. The ASR 1000 series is also commonly used within SDWAN solutions due to its robust performance and advanced features. While other devices like the IR8300 and the ASR 9000 are powerful in their own right, they are not specifically highlighted as core components of Cisco's SDWAN solutions in comparison to the ISR 2900 and ASR 1000 series.","images":[],"sourcePage":800,"flags":[]}],"images":{"images/q0057_exhibit.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0084_answer.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0084_question.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0074_answer.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0016_question.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0026_question.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0081_exhibit.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0016_answer.png":"data:image/png;base64,","images/q0021_question.png":"data:image/png;base64,","i